Should the court grant the motion to dismiss the indictment?', 'Should the court grant the motion to dismiss the indictment?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14621.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":null,"batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"2","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"An indictment rests only on grand-jury testimony that includes hearsay and evidence traced to an unlawful search. Is dismissal of the indictment the remedy?","review_truth":"Grand jury taint is not automatic dismissal; suppression may be a trial remedy, but a facially valid indictment usually stands.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CRIMINAL-BA2-GRAND-JURY-01","title":"Violation is not remedy","signal":"A choice proves police misconduct, but the call asks whether the indictment is dismissed.","student_move":"Separate the wrong from the remedy. Then apply the grand-jury Gold Key.","tiny_rule":"Grand jury taint usually does not dismiss a facially valid indictment.","trap":"Unlawful search = automatic dismissal.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-GRAND-JURY-REMEDY-01","statement":"A facially valid indictment is not dismissed merely because the grand jury heard hearsay or evidence traceable to an unlawful search; those defects point to trial suppression, not automatic indictment dismissal.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Motion to dismiss an indictment based on what the grand jury heard.","authority":"Costello v. United States; United States v. Calandra; United States v. Blue.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-UNDERCOVER-NONCUSTODIAL-01","statement":"A suspect’s voluntary conversation with someone he does not know is an agent is not custodial police interrogation for Miranda purposes.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Undercover social conversation before indictment or custody.","authority":"Illinois v. Perkins.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-GRAND-JURY-REMEDY-01","statement":"When the call asks to dismiss an indictment, do not stop at ''the government violated a right.'' Ask whether dismissal is the remedy.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"Motion to dismiss indictment + answers naming unlawful search, hearsay, or counsel violation.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"Violation vs remedy","prompt":"A grand jury heard evidence derived from an unlawful search. The defendant moves to dismiss the indictment. What is the first C3 question?","answer":"Does the violation supply dismissal as the remedy?"},{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"Grand jury indictment challenge","prompt":"Does hearsay before a grand jury automatically invalidate a facially valid indictment?","answer":"No."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"Dominant search trap","prompt":"Choice says: ''Yes, because of the unlawful search.'' The call asks dismissal. What trap is this?","answer":"Violation_vs_remedy."},{"drill_type":"wrong_frame","target_skill":"Undercover conversation","prompt":"Suspect brags to an undercover agent in a café. What frame should you resist?","answer":"Custodial Miranda/right-to-counsel unless custody or charge facts appear."}]}'),
  ('94f22dc0-861b-58a9-b2fd-1347209f255e', '17026_manger_prop_bundles', 'CONTRACTS', 'Gap-Fillers, Interpretation, and the Parol Evidence Rule', 'IV', 'UCC agreement-source hierarchy: private course of dealing versus general trade usage versus default gap-fillers.', 'Esther’s homeschool-supply business bought 40 children’s Bible-story puppet-stage kits from Barnabas under a signed contract. Barnabas also agreed to paint each stage front with a lion-and-lamb design and give Esther’s staff a 20-minute assembly lesson. Most of the contract price was allocated to the puppet-stage kits themselves. The signed writing required each kit to include a “festival prop bundle,” but did not define the phrase. In four prior contracts between Esther and Barnabas for similar Bible-story kits, Barnabas included eight hand-painted prop pieces per kit under that same phrase. Barnabas now offers testimony that the broader toy-theater trade usually treats three prop pieces as a festival prop bundle. Esther offers the prior-contract evidence.', 'Esther’s homeschool-supply business bought 40 children’s Bible-story puppet-stage kits from Barnabas under a signed contract. Barnabas also agreed to paint each stage front with a lion-and-lamb design and give Esther’s staff a 20-minute assembly lesson. Most of the contract price was allocated to the puppet-stage kits themselves. The signed writing required each kit to include a “festival prop bundle,” but did not define the phrase. In four prior contracts between Esther and Barnabas for similar Bible-story kits, Barnabas included eight hand-painted prop pieces per kit under that same phrase. Barnabas now offers testimony that the broader toy-theater trade usually treats three prop pieces as a festival prop bundle. Esther offers the prior-contract evidence.

Should the court admit Esther’s prior-contract evidence to interpret “festival prop bundle”?', 'Should the court admit Esther’s prior-contract evidence to interpret “festival prop bundle”?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17026.md","source_question_id":"17026","variant_slug":"manger_prop_bundles","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"52040300","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"core diagnostic","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A mostly-goods mixed contract uses an undefined term. The same parties’ prior contracts used that term one way; general trade usage points another way. Is the parties’ prior-course evidence admissible to interpret the term?","review_truth":"In a goods-dominant UCC deal, the parties’ prior course of dealing can explain an undefined term, and it does not lose automatically to general trade usage or default gap-fillers.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CONTRACTS-REMEDY-COURSE-DEALING-01","title":"Course of dealing before defaults","signal":"Undefined UCC term + prior same-party transactions.","student_move":"Do not jump straight to trade usage or gap-fillers.","tiny_rule":"Same-party course of dealing can explain the phrase; general usage and defaults do not automatically override it.","trap":"Source-order reversal.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-MANGER-BUNDLES-01","statement":"A mixed goods/services contract follows the goods side when the sale-of-goods purpose predominates; small setup, painting, configuration, or training services do not automatically make the whole deal a services contract.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Most of the price or contract object is the tangible goods, with services attached.","authority":"UCC 2-102; Bonebrake v. Cox, 499 F.2d 951.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-MANGER-BUNDLES-02","statement":"For UCC meaning evidence, use agreement sources before defaults: express terms, course of performance, course of dealing, usage of trade, then gap-fillers. Course of dealing beats general usage of trade if they cannot reasonably be read together.","type":"rule","trigger":"Undefined contract phrase plus prior dealings and industry evidence.","authority":"UCC 2-202; UCC 1-303.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-MANGER-BUNDLES-01","statement":"Read this array as a source-order ladder: source law first, private party history second, general trade usage third, defaults last.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"An undefined phrase plus prior same-party contracts plus general trade usage.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"source-order reversal","prompt":"Undefined UCC term; prior same-party contracts and default gap-filler both appear. Which source comes first?","answer":"Prior course of dealing before default gap-filler."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"mixed contract source law","prompt":"A contract includes goods, setup, and a short training session; most price is goods. Does the service piece automatically make the deal common law?","answer":"No. Goods predominate."},{"drill_type":"clash","target_skill":"course of dealing vs usage of trade","prompt":"Same-party prior contracts define a phrase one way; general trade usage defines it another. Which source is stronger if they conflict?","answer":"Course of dealing."}]}'),
  ('38ff81e1-1a8e-5d27-acf0-a540e6c1af1f', '17045_scripture_banner_cloth', 'EVIDENCE', 'Opinion and specialized-knowledge testimony', 'Trade terminology / expert testimony', NULL, 'Lydia, a wholesaler of specialty textiles, entered into a valid written contract with Barnabas, who operated a chain of Christian craft shops that specialized in handmade Scripture banners. The contract called for Lydia to supply “3,600 yards of sanctuary-quality banner cloth,” with deliveries to be made over four months.

Two weeks after Lydia’s first delivery, Barnabas notified her that the cloth was unacceptable because the bolts were only 48 inches wide and were made from a cotton-polyester blend rather than all linen. Lydia later brought an action against Barnabas for breach of contract.

At trial, Lydia offered the testimony of Ruth. Ruth testified that she was the head seamstress for a well-known Bible-pageant company, that she was an expert in designing Scripture banners for pageants and retreats, and that she had worked in Christian stage and banner design for more than 30 years. She testified further that, during that time, she had purchased large quantities of cloth and was familiar with terminology used in the wholesale textile industry. Ruth stated that when the phrase “sanctuary-quality banner cloth” is used in the wholesale textile business, it means 45-to-54-inch bolts of either linen or cotton-polyester blend suitable for hanging display banners.

For purposes of trial, the parties specifically agreed to be bound by the terminology of the wholesale textile industry.', 'Lydia, a wholesaler of specialty textiles, entered into a valid written contract with Barnabas, who operated a chain of Christian craft shops that specialized in handmade Scripture banners. The contract called for Lydia to supply “3,600 yards of sanctuary-quality banner cloth,” with deliveries to be made over four months.

Two weeks after Lydia’s first delivery, Barnabas notified her that the cloth was unacceptable because the bolts were only 48 inches wide and were made from a cotton-polyester blend rather than all linen. Lydia later brought an action against Barnabas for breach of contract.

At trial, Lydia offered the testimony of Ruth. Ruth testified that she was the head seamstress for a well-known Bible-pageant company, that she was an expert in designing Scripture banners for pageants and retreats, and that she had worked in Christian stage and banner design for more than 30 years. She testified further that, during that time, she had purchased large quantities of cloth and was familiar with terminology used in the wholesale textile industry. Ruth stated that when the phrase “sanctuary-quality banner cloth” is used in the wholesale textile business, it means 45-to-54-inch bolts of either linen or cotton-polyester blend suitable for hanging display banners.

For purposes of trial, the parties specifically agreed to be bound by the terminology of the wholesale textile industry.

Upon proper objection by Barnabas’s attorney, Ruth’s testimony as to the meaning of the phrase should be:', 'Upon proper objection by Barnabas’s attorney, Ruth’s testimony as to the meaning of the phrase should be:', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17045.md","source_question_id":"17045","variant_slug":"scripture_banner_cloth","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"32020801","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A sales contract uses a trade phrase. A witness experienced in that wholesale trade explains what the phrase means. Should the testimony be admitted as trade-terminology evidence?","review_truth":"Trade-term testimony is admitted because it explains the trade meaning of contract language; do not swap in end-use expertise, party stipulation, or opinion-form myths.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"EVI-ANCHOR-TRADE-TERMS-01","title":"Trade term, not craft expertise","signal":"A contract phrase is explained by a witness familiar with the trade’s vocabulary.","student_move":"Name the testimony purpose before evaluating expert labels.","tiny_rule":"Trade-term testimony explains trade meaning.","trap":"Choosing the witness’s end-use expertise or the parties’ stipulation as the reason.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-TRADE-TERMS-01","statement":"Trade terminology or usage evidence may be admitted to explain the trade meaning of a contract term. The reason is the trade-meaning function, not an express agreement by the parties.","type":"rule","trigger":"A contract phrase is explained by someone familiar with the relevant wholesale trade.","authority":"UCC 1-303(c)-(d); Restatement (Second) of Contracts 222.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-OPINION-01","statement":"Opinion form alone does not exclude qualified specialized-knowledge testimony.","type":"rule","trigger":"An answer says to exclude testimony simply because it is an opinion.","authority":"FRE 702; FRE 704.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-EVIDENCE-PURPOSE-LABEL-01","statement":"On an admissibility call, first name what the testimony is offered to prove. Pick the answer that labels that use, not the answer that repeats a tempting background fact.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"Three choices say admitted, but for different reasons.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"offered purpose","prompt":"A witness familiar with wholesale terminology explains a contract phrase. Choice says admitted because the witness is expert in using the product. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut. Wrong purpose."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"opinion testimony","prompt":"A qualified specialized-knowledge witness gives an opinion that helps explain a disputed term. Exclude merely because it is opinion?","answer":"No."},{"drill_type":"CALL_FOCUS","target_skill":"true fact versus legal reason","prompt":"Parties agree to use trade terminology. Is that the core reason a trade-term witness''s testimony is admitted?","answer":"No. The core reason is trade terminology explaining the contract term."}]}'),
  ('f18b8385-44a4-5658-90a6-9ba3c9eabc18', '17058_advent_honey_parcel', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Conveyance by Deed — Requirements for Valid Deed', 'B1', 'deed validity at delivery vs downstream recording/consideration/custody facts', 'Paul owned a narrow riverfront parcel where he kept beehives for the honey cakes his family sold each Advent season. During a family potluck, Paul signed a deed that described the parcel and used words of present conveyance, but the space for the grantee’s name was empty. Paul handed the paper to Martha and asked her to keep it with his private papers. He did not authorize Martha to fill in any name. After Paul died, Martha typed her own name into the blank space and recorded the instrument.', 'Paul owned a narrow riverfront parcel where he kept beehives for the honey cakes his family sold each Advent season. During a family potluck, Paul signed a deed that described the parcel and used words of present conveyance, but the space for the grantee’s name was empty. Paul handed the paper to Martha and asked her to keep it with his private papers. He did not authorize Martha to fill in any name. After Paul died, Martha typed her own name into the blank space and recorded the instrument.

Who owns the parcel?', 'Who owns the parcel?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17058.md","source_question_id":"17058","variant_slug":"advent_honey_parcel","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"84040201","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"discriminator","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A signed deed for land is delivered for safekeeping with the grantee line blank and no authority to fill it in. The holder later names herself and records it. Who owns the land?","review_truth":"A deed fails if the grantee is missing at delivery and the later filler had no authority; recording does not fix that defect.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RP-REMED-BLANK-GRANTEE-GATE","title":"Blank grantee gate","signal":"The stem says the grantee line was blank when the deed was delivered.","student_move":"Freeze the validity gate before considering recording or consideration.","tiny_rule":"No grantee at delivery plus no authority to fill means no title passes to the later filler.","trap":"Signature or recording feels complete.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-REAL_PROPERTY-BLANK-GRANTEE-01","statement":"A deed must identify an existing or ascertainable grantee when delivered. A later unauthorized insertion plus recording does not supply that missing validity gate.","type":"threshold","trigger":"signed deed, blank grantee line, no authority to complete, later self-insertion","authority":"Cornell LII Wex, Deed; Alan White, Deeds and Recording Acts; Sanders v. Kirk; Tumansky v. Woodruff.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-REAL_PROPERTY-DEED-GATE-01","statement":"Run the deed-validity gate before downstream facts. Signature, custody, recording, and consideration matter only after the instrument can operate as a deed.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"answer choices offer signed/recorded/no-consideration reasons while the stem flags a blank grantee","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim detection","prompt":"A signed deed is held by a custodian with no authority. Choice: custody automatically gives authority to complete any blank. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut: tiered_absolute / NOT_TRUE."},{"drill_type":"Issue-Sense","target_skill":"wrong-element detection","prompt":"The stem flags a blank grantee. Choice: deed required no consideration. What is the skipped move?","answer":"It answers payment, not grantee identity."},{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"deed-validity gate","prompt":"What must be true of the grantee when a deed is delivered?","answer":"The grantee must be identified or ascertainable; unauthorized later insertion does not cure."},{"drill_type":"Silver Key navigation","target_skill":"gate before downstream facts","prompt":"Which comes first: recording or deed validity?","answer":"Deed validity first."}]}'),
  ('fb41d3b9-7d84-518e-b995-aae30e952ed5', '17084_five_loaves_bank_records', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Fourth Amendment', 'Constitutional Protection of Accused Persons', 'Fourth Amendment protected interest in bank records vs no protected interest under third-party doctrine', 'Esther runs a private business called Five Loaves Puzzle Nights, selling Christian-themed escape-room tickets and devotion journals. Federal agents suspected Esther of wire fraud after several customers complained that their Holy Land mystery weekend tickets were never honored. Without obtaining a warrant or subpoena, agents contacted Esther''s bank and requested copies of her business checking-account statements, deposit slips, and canceled checks for the past 14 months. The bank complied. The records showed deposits matching the customer payments under investigation. Esther moves to suppress the bank records, arguing that the government violated her Fourth Amendment rights.', 'Esther runs a private business called Five Loaves Puzzle Nights, selling Christian-themed escape-room tickets and devotion journals. Federal agents suspected Esther of wire fraud after several customers complained that their Holy Land mystery weekend tickets were never honored. Without obtaining a warrant or subpoena, agents contacted Esther''s bank and requested copies of her business checking-account statements, deposit slips, and canceled checks for the past 14 months. The bank complied. The records showed deposits matching the customer payments under investigation. Esther moves to suppress the bank records, arguing that the government violated her Fourth Amendment rights.

Should the court grant the motion to suppress?', 'Should the court grant the motion to suppress?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17084.md","source_question_id":"17084","variant_slug":"five_loaves_bank_records","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"75070200","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard anchor","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Agents got a suspect''s bank records from the bank without a warrant or subpoena. The suspect moves to suppress under the Fourth Amendment. Should suppression be granted?","review_truth":"Bank records voluntarily conveyed to a bank are third-party business records; no Fourth Amendment suppression remedy follows from the government obtaining them.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CRIM-REM-BANK-THIRD-PARTY-01","title":"Bank records: interest first, process second","signal":"Agents obtain account records from a bank.","student_move":"Ask whether the defendant has a Fourth Amendment interest before arguing warrant, subpoena, or suppression.","tiny_rule":"Miller bank records are bank business records; no Fourth Amendment suppression right belongs to the depositor.","trap":"Treating personal finance privacy as Fourth Amendment privacy.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-BANK-THIRD-PARTY-01","statement":"Bank account records are the bank''s business records. A customer generally has no Fourth Amendment privacy interest in information voluntarily conveyed to the bank in ordinary transactions.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"Government obtains checks, deposit slips, account statements, or bank records from the bank.","authority":"United States v. Miller, 425 U.S. 435 (1976); Smith v. Maryland, 442 U.S. 735 (1979).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-SUPPRESSION-THRESHOLD-01","statement":"On a suppression call, first ask whether this defendant has a Fourth Amendment interest in the record. If no protected interest exists, warrant and subpoena phrasing is bait.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The call asks whether to suppress records obtained from someone else.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"Bank-record third-party doctrine","prompt":"Agents obtain deposit slips and canceled checks from a suspect''s bank. Does the suspect have a Fourth Amendment privacy interest in the bank records?","answer":"No."},{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"Suppression threshold","prompt":"Before asking whether agents needed a warrant or subpoena, what threshold question should you ask?","answer":"Whether this defendant has a Fourth Amendment interest in the records."},{"drill_type":"wrong_source","target_skill":"Correct result, wrong reason","prompt":"No suppression because bank records are excluded by statute. Name the defect.","answer":"Wrong source; the rule is constitutional third-party doctrine."}]}'),
  ('1f3fa045-ed4c-5db8-9180-60feb0e11559', '17110_sticker_press_guaranty', 'CONTRACTS', 'Types of Agreements', 'Unilateral guaranty formation', 'performance before death vs notice/death trap', 'Mary later failed to pay a commercial print shop for Scripture-memory sticker books. The shop points to a note Esther had sent to Paul, the shop’s owner: “If you print and deliver Mary’s sticker-book order on credit, up to $18,000, by Friday, I will guarantee payment.” Paul received the note on Tuesday morning and delivered $18,000 of the order to Mary on credit that afternoon. Esther died three days later.', 'Mary later failed to pay a commercial print shop for Scripture-memory sticker books. The shop points to a note Esther had sent to Paul, the shop’s owner: “If you print and deliver Mary’s sticker-book order on credit, up to $18,000, by Friday, I will guarantee payment.” Paul received the note on Tuesday morning and delivered $18,000 of the order to Mary on credit that afternoon. Esther died three days later.

Assuming no writing-defense issue, what is the best formation analysis?', 'Assuming no writing-defense issue, what is the best formation analysis?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17110.md","source_question_id":"17110","variant_slug":"sticker_press_guaranty","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"core","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A guarantor says, “If you extend credit to the debtor, I guarantee payment.” The creditor extends the requested credit before the guarantor dies. Did the guaranty form before death?","review_truth":"Requested credit can accept a unilateral guaranty; death after that performance does not undo formation.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CON-FORM-GUARANTY-PERFORMANCE-01","title":"Performance accepts the guaranty offer.","signal":"If you extend credit, I guarantee payment.","student_move":"Find whether the credit was extended before death or revocation.","tiny_rule":"Requested credit extension can accept.","trap":"Treating later notice as formation.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-PERFORMANCE-GUARANTY-01","statement":"A guaranty offer that says “if you extend credit, I guarantee payment” invites acceptance by performance. The creditor accepts by extending the requested credit before the guarantor’s death.","type":"rule","trigger":"If-credit-then-I-guarantee wording plus credit extended before death.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 36, 50, 54; Davis v. Wells, 104 U.S. 159.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-STICKER-PRESS-01","statement":"For formation calls, lock the time line before the doctrine: offer, requested performance, death, default. If performance came before death, later notice language is a trap.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"A choice makes death or notice decisive even though requested performance already happened.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim detection","prompt":"No guaranty formed because all guaranties are gratuitous promises.","answer":"CUT: tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"CALL","target_skill":"call focus","prompt":"A guarantor dies after the creditor performs the requested act. Is death a formation blocker?","answer":"No. Performance before death accepted."},{"drill_type":"CLASH","target_skill":"timing axis","prompt":"Offer, performance, death, notice. Which fact matters?","answer":"Performance before death."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"performance acceptance","prompt":"If-credit-then-guarantee note; creditor extends credit before death.","answer":"Guaranty formed."}]}'),
  ('a5998a3f-acec-541a-88d4-a3776205ea22', '17151_carol_program', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Defenses', 'General Principles', 'general intent vs specific intent defense scope', 'At Lydia’s private Advent carol workshop in a rented community hall, Martha voluntarily drinks several cups of spiked wassail. Later, while the group is stacking hymn sheets, she becomes rowdy. Stephen, the hall coordinator, asks her to leave. Martha angrily swings a rolled-up carol program at Stephen and strikes him in the face. Battery in the jurisdiction requires intentional offensive contact but no further purpose or objective. Martha argues that she was too drunk to form criminal intent.', 'At Lydia’s private Advent carol workshop in a rented community hall, Martha voluntarily drinks several cups of spiked wassail. Later, while the group is stacking hymn sheets, she becomes rowdy. Stephen, the hall coordinator, asks her to leave. Martha angrily swings a rolled-up carol program at Stephen and strikes him in the face. Battery in the jurisdiction requires intentional offensive contact but no further purpose or objective. Martha argues that she was too drunk to form criminal intent.

Is voluntary intoxication a defense to the battery charge?', 'Is voluntary intoxication a defense to the battery charge?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17151.md","source_question_id":"17151","variant_slug":"carol_program","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"72060101","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A voluntarily intoxicated person intentionally makes offensive contact. The charged battery requires intentional contact only, with no further objective. Is voluntary intoxication a defense?","review_truth":"Voluntary intoxication can matter for specific intent, but it does not excuse a general-intent battery.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CRIM-INTOX-GENERAL-INTENT-01","title":"Voluntary intoxication: specific-intent only","signal":"The stem says no further purpose or objective.","student_move":"Classify the offense before applying intoxication.","tiny_rule":"Voluntary intoxication does not excuse general intent.","trap":"Treating drunkenness, blackout, or no memory as a universal intent defense.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-GENERAL-INTENT-INTOX-01","statement":"Voluntary intoxication may matter for a specific-intent element, but it is not a defense to a general-intent crime. General-intent battery requires intent to make the offensive contact, not a further objective.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Stem says the offense requires intentional contact but no further purpose or objective.","authority":"Cornell Wex intoxication; CALCRIM No. 3426; People v. Hood / People v. Atkins line of authority","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-GENERAL-INTENT-INTOX-01","statement":"When two answers reach the same outcome, test the reason against the stem before keeping it. A right result with a reason the stem contradicts is still a cut.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"One No answer says strict liability while the stem says intentional contact.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"general intent vs specific intent","prompt":"A voluntarily drunk defendant commits a general-intent battery. Is intoxication a defense?","answer":"No."},{"drill_type":"cut_reason","target_skill":"same-result wrong-reason","prompt":"The stem says battery requires intentional contact. A choice says no defense because battery is strict liability. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut: contradiction."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_spotting","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"A choice says intoxication negates any intent formed while drunk. Name the mold.","answer":"tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"timing_axis","target_skill":"memory vs intent","prompt":"A choice turns on whether the defendant remembers the event later. What is the better axis?","answer":"Intent at the moment of contact."}]}'),
  ('c5d8eff4-1545-5283-b105-601a7c0c13f6', '17167_manger_lights_portal', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Federal Powers', 'Necessary and Proper Clause', NULL, 'Congress enacts a valid federal rule governing interstate shipments of portable Christmas-pageant lighting rigs sold by private vendors. To administer that shipment rule, Congress creates a federal serial-number portal for those rigs. Lydia, who ships the rigs through Barnabas Pageant Supply, argues that the portal is unconstitutional because federal inspectors could enforce the shipment rule by using paper invoices instead.', 'Congress enacts a valid federal rule governing interstate shipments of portable Christmas-pageant lighting rigs sold by private vendors. To administer that shipment rule, Congress creates a federal serial-number portal for those rigs. Lydia, who ships the rigs through Barnabas Pageant Supply, argues that the portal is unconstitutional because federal inspectors could enforce the shipment rule by using paper invoices instead.

Which answer best states the constitutional result?', 'Which answer best states the constitutional result?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17167.md","source_question_id":"17167","variant_slug":"manger_lights_portal","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"42010104","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Congress validly regulates interstate shipments and adds an administrative portal to help run that regulation. Is the portal invalid merely because it is not indispensable?","review_truth":"Necessary and Proper means useful or appropriate to executing an enumerated power, not absolutely indispensable and not freestanding.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-NP-01","title":"Necessary means useful, not indispensable","signal":"The stem says Congress could have used another method.","student_move":"Reject indispensability language; check for enumerated-power connection.","tiny_rule":"Useful/proper means plus enumerated-power hook survives.","trap":"Turning necessary into only possible.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONLAW-MANGER-LIGHTS-01","statement":"Necessary and Proper does not mean absolutely indispensable; Congress may use appropriate means connected to executing an enumerated power.","type":"distinction","trigger":"challenger says Congress could have used another administrative method","authority":"McCulloch v. Maryland; United States v. Darby; United States v. Comstock","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONLAW-MANGER-LIGHTS-02","statement":"The Necessary and Proper Clause is not freestanding; the means must execute an enumerated or otherwise vested federal power.","type":"threshold","trigger":"answer says valid even with no enumerated-power connection","authority":"Kinsella v. United States ex rel. Singleton","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONLAW-MANGER-LIGHTS-01","statement":"In a 2-valid/2-invalid constitutional array, do not stop at the result word; read the reason after it and keep the answer that preserves both required links.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"two answers say valid and two say invalid","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"Necessary and Proper not indispensable","prompt":"Congress uses an administrative method to run a valid commerce regulation. The challenger says another method would work. Does the method have to be indispensable?","answer":"No. It must be an appropriate means connected to executing the enumerated power."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"read result plus reason","prompt":"Two choices say valid and two say invalid. What must you read next?","answer":"The reason after the result word."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"tiered absolute","prompt":"Classify: ''Federal tracking portals are never proper.''","answer":"NOT_TRUE, tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"freestanding-power trap","prompt":"Classify: ''Valid even with no connection to an enumerated power.''","answer":"NOT_TRUE, flat_misstatement."}]}'),
  ('db6ecffb-475e-595b-9a9e-ba34369a2be6', '17173_road_to_emmaus_puzzle_quest', 'CONTRACTS', 'Reliance damages', 'A2', NULL, 'Mary, a professional puzzle designer, agreed to design and run a “Road to Emmaus” puzzle quest for Daniel’s private Bible-themed birthday party for a fee of $12,600. Mary completed the entire puzzle quest, including the final resurrection-clue round. Comparable event designers would reasonably have charged $20,400 for the same work. Daniel refuses to pay. Mary sues for $20,400, arguing that she relied on the contract by doing puzzle-design work worth that amount. What is Mary’s proper contract recovery?', 'Mary, a professional puzzle designer, agreed to design and run a “Road to Emmaus” puzzle quest for Daniel’s private Bible-themed birthday party for a fee of $12,600. Mary completed the entire puzzle quest, including the final resurrection-clue round. Comparable event designers would reasonably have charged $20,400 for the same work. Daniel refuses to pay. Mary sues for $20,400, arguing that she relied on the contract by doing puzzle-design work worth that amount. What is Mary’s proper contract recovery?

What is Mary’s proper contract recovery?', 'What is Mary’s proper contract recovery?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17173.md","source_question_id":"17173","variant_slug":"road_to_emmaus_puzzle_quest","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"core diagnostic","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A service provider fully performs a fixed-price services contract; reasonable value is higher; the other side refuses to pay. What recovery?","review_truth":"Full performance plus an unpaid fixed contract price points to the bargain price; reasonable value is not a bigger reliance recovery.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-REMEDY-MENU-01","title":"Bargain price beats reasonable-value bait","signal":"Full performance + agreed price + higher reasonable value.","student_move":"Start with the bargain number before considering reliance or restitution labels.","tiny_rule":"If the contract price is fixed and fully earned, recover that price.","trap":"Picking the higher market value or the spread.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-BARGAIN-PRICE-01","statement":"Full performance plus an unpaid fixed contract price means the bargain price is the recovery. Do not relabel higher reasonable value as reliance or restitution to get more.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The stem gives both a fixed contract price and a higher reasonable value after full performance.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 344, 347, 349, 373(2).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-REMEDY-MENU-01","statement":"When the choices are all dollar amounts, classify the menu before choosing: contract price, market value, zero, or spread.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"The stem supplies two dollar amounts and the choices recombine them.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"remedy_measure_menu","target_skill":"Classify dollar choices before choosing.","prompt":"A fully performing painter agreed to $9,000. The work was worth $14,000. The owner refuses to pay. Choices are $9,000, $14,000, $5,000, or $0. Which number starts as the anchor?","answer":"$9,000, the contract price."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"Hear absolute remedy language.","prompt":"Cut this answer: ''$14,000, because reliance damages always use reasonable value.''","answer":"Cut as tiered_absolute; ''always'' overclaims and reasonable value is not automatic reliance."},{"drill_type":"wrong_element_recovery","target_skill":"Separate bad label from proper recovery.","prompt":"Why is ''nothing, because reliance is unavailable'' not responsive when the plaintiff fully performed?","answer":"Because the call asks proper recovery; the contract price remains available."},{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"Recall fixed-price full-performance anchor.","prompt":"State the tiny rule for fixed price + full performance + higher market value.","answer":"Recover the fixed contract price; do not use higher reasonable value to exceed it."}]}'),
  ('c21a5c11-c784-5270-b8aa-9379e7dc461f', '19177_manna_wagon_disposal', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Congressional power', 'Property Clause', 'specific Property Clause source vs wrong constitutional labels', 'In an effort to improve the position of United States companies in the world mobile-disaster-relief catering market, Congress voted to sell twelve surplus federal field-kitchen trailers nicknamed ''manna wagons'' to Lydia''s Ark Logistics, Inc., for $7 each. If Timothy Relief Systems, a competitor of Lydia''s Ark Logistics, challenges the constitutionality of the congressional action in an appropriate proceeding, the sale will probably be found:', 'In an effort to improve the position of United States companies in the world mobile-disaster-relief catering market, Congress voted to sell twelve surplus federal field-kitchen trailers nicknamed ''manna wagons'' to Lydia''s Ark Logistics, Inc., for $7 each. If Timothy Relief Systems, a competitor of Lydia''s Ark Logistics, challenges the constitutionality of the congressional action in an appropriate proceeding, the sale will probably be found:

If a competitor challenges the constitutionality of the congressional sale, the sale will probably be found:', 'If a competitor challenges the constitutionality of the congressional sale, the sale will probably be found:', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19177.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"manna_wagon_disposal","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard anchor","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Congress sells surplus federal property to one private company at a nominal price. A competitor challenges. Is the sale valid because Congress has constitutional power to dispose of United States property?","review_truth":"Federal property plus congressional disposal points to the Property Clause.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CONLAW-REMEDY-PROPERTY-SOURCE-01","title":"Property Clause source match","signal":"Congress disposes of property belonging to the United States.","student_move":"Name the actor, object, and action; match them to the specific power source.","tiny_rule":"Federal property + dispose/sell = Property Clause.","trap":"Policy/fairness answers distract from the source of power.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONLAW-PROPERTY-DISPOSE-01","statement":"When Congress sells or otherwise disposes of property belonging to the United States, the Property Clause is the power source.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"Congress + federal property + sell/dispose","authority":"U.S. Const. art. IV, § 3, cl. 2","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONLAW-ATTAINDER-PUNISHMENT-02","statement":"A bill of attainder requires legislative punishment of an identified person or group without judicial trial.","type":"threshold","trigger":"Answer says bill of attainder but stem gives no punishment","authority":"Nixon v. Administrator of General Services, 433 U.S. 425","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONLAW-FOURTEENTH-STATE-03","statement":"The Fourteenth Amendment Equal Protection Clause is the state-action source; do not use it as the direct source for Congress.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Congress/federal actor + Fourteenth Amendment answer","authority":"U.S. Const. amend. XIV, § 1","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONLAW-NO-GENERAL-POLICE-04","statement":"Congress has enumerated powers, not a general police power; find the specific federal power that fits.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"Answer says general police powers of Congress","authority":"United States v. Lopez, 514 U.S. 549","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONLAW-MANNA-WAGONS-01","statement":"For a Con Law source-choice array, match actor + object + action before debating policy.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Congress + federal property + sale","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"Property Clause trigger","prompt":"Congress sells surplus federal equipment to a private buyer. What power source should you check first?","answer":"Property Clause."},{"drill_type":"Wrong-frame cut","target_skill":"Bill-of-attainder threshold","prompt":"A choice says bill of attainder, but the stem gives a subsidy or sale. What fact is missing?","answer":"Punishment."},{"drill_type":"Actor matching","target_skill":"Fourteenth Amendment source","prompt":"The actor is Congress and the answer says Fourteenth Amendment equal protection. What is the mismatch?","answer":"The Fourteenth Amendment Equal Protection Clause is the state-action source."},{"drill_type":"Right-result wrong-source","target_skill":"No general federal police power","prompt":"An answer says a congressional act is valid under general police powers. What is the recovery move?","answer":"Find the specific enumerated power; Congress lacks a general police power."}]}'),
  ('b46fcfce-1468-51c9-ac93-ae57de52d44c', '19194_loaves_pages', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Equal Protection', 'Rational basis — sexual orientation / federal law / equal dignity', 'Equal-protection classification purpose vs benefit-entitlement frame', 'A federal statute provides that, for every federal tax, employee-benefit, grant, and insurance program, the word ''spouse'' means only a husband or wife of the opposite sex, and ''marriage'' means only a legal union between one man and one woman. Stephen and Barnabas, who run a little gospel-poster shop called Loaves & Pages, are legally married under their state’s law. After Stephen takes a federal park-service job, he tries to enroll Barnabas for spousal health coverage available to opposite-sex spouses of federal employees. The agency denies the enrollment solely because Barnabas is not an opposite-sex spouse. Stephen and Barnabas challenge the statute under the Equal Protection component of the Fifth Amendment Due Process Clause.', 'A federal statute provides that, for every federal tax, employee-benefit, grant, and insurance program, the word ''spouse'' means only a husband or wife of the opposite sex, and ''marriage'' means only a legal union between one man and one woman. Stephen and Barnabas, who run a little gospel-poster shop called Loaves & Pages, are legally married under their state’s law. After Stephen takes a federal park-service job, he tries to enroll Barnabas for spousal health coverage available to opposite-sex spouses of federal employees. The agency denies the enrollment solely because Barnabas is not an opposite-sex spouse. Stephen and Barnabas challenge the statute under the Equal Protection component of the Fifth Amendment Due Process Clause.

Under Equal Protection analysis, how should a court evaluate the federal statute’s refusal to recognize their state-law marriage for federal benefits?', 'Under Equal Protection analysis, how should a court evaluate the federal statute’s refusal to recognize their state-law marriage for federal benefits?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19194.md","source_question_id":"19194","variant_slug":"loaves_pages","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"44040305","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A federal law defines marriage/spouse as opposite-sex only and denies a federal benefit to a same-sex spouse who is lawfully married under state law. Under Equal Protection, does the federal definition survive?","review_truth":"A federal marriage-recognition law cannot deny federal benefits to state-law same-sex spouses when the operative purpose/effect is to demean their lawful marriage status.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-WINDSOR-BARE-ANIMUS","title":"Federal benefits + state-law same-sex marriage","signal":"Federal law refuses to recognize a same-sex spouse whom state law recognizes.","student_move":"Lock the Equal Protection call; avoid both strict-scrutiny overpromotion and benefits-entitlement wrong frame.","tiny_rule":"Bare animus/equal-dignity injury is not a legitimate interest.","trap":"Rational-basis deference plus tradition language.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONLAW-WINDSOR-DOMA-01","statement":"When a federal marriage-recognition law denies federal benefits to same-sex spouses who are lawfully married under state law for the purpose/effect of marking those marriages as lesser, it fails Fifth Amendment equal-protection principles; bare animus is not a legitimate interest.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"Federal definition of spouse/marriage excludes state-law same-sex spouses from federal benefits.","authority":"United States v. Windsor, 570 U.S. 744 (2013); Romer v. Evans, 517 U.S. 620 (1996).","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONLAW-WINDSOR-SCRUTINY-02","statement":"Do not turn Windsor into a strict-scrutiny/suspect-classification answer; Windsor invalidated DOMA without expressly making sexual orientation a suspect classification.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Answer says sexual orientation is a suspect classification under Windsor-style facts.","authority":"United States v. Windsor, 570 U.S. 744 (2013).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONLAW-BENEFIT-FRAME-01","statement":"When the call is Equal Protection, do not answer whether the benefit exists as a free-standing right; answer whether the government may draw this line.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"Answer talks about Congress’s benefits power or no constitutional right to a benefit.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"Windsor/Romer bare animus","prompt":"Federal law denies federal recognition to a same-sex spouse lawfully married under state law. What tiny anchor decides the item?","answer":"Bare animus/equal-dignity injury is not a legitimate governmental interest; Windsor/Romer controls."},{"drill_type":"Silver Key call-focus","target_skill":"wrong-frame cut","prompt":"The call asks Equal Protection, but one answer says Congress controls federal benefits. What is the cut?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE misfit: benefits authority does not answer whether the classification violates Equal Protection."},{"drill_type":"Strict-scrutiny trap","target_skill":"tier calibration","prompt":"An answer says Windsor requires strict scrutiny because sexual orientation is a suspect class. What is the recovery move?","answer":"Do not overpromote Windsor; it invalidated DOMA without expressly declaring suspect-class status."}]}'),
  ('b10bb9e5-2808-53eb-8529-79fa10d1bfd1', '19213_road_to_emmaus_breezeway', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Duty - third-party crime', NULL, 'Lydia operated Road to Emmaus Puzzles, a for-profit Bible-themed puzzle-room business. She leased a narrow mural breezeway behind the shop as the exit path for paying teams. For six years, teams had used the breezeway at night without fights, robberies, threats, or similar security incidents. After Esther finished a paid puzzle session and walked through the breezeway, an unknown stranger entered the breezeway and attacked her. Esther sued Lydia for negligence, claiming Lydia should have posted security guards at the breezeway.', 'Lydia operated Road to Emmaus Puzzles, a for-profit Bible-themed puzzle-room business. She leased a narrow mural breezeway behind the shop as the exit path for paying teams. For six years, teams had used the breezeway at night without fights, robberies, threats, or similar security incidents. After Esther finished a paid puzzle session and walked through the breezeway, an unknown stranger entered the breezeway and attacked her. Esther sued Lydia for negligence, claiming Lydia should have posted security guards at the breezeway.

Which duty analysis is most accurate?', 'Which duty analysis is most accurate?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19213.md","source_question_id":"19213","variant_slug":"road_to_emmaus_breezeway","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium_high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A paying customer is randomly attacked by an unknown stranger in a business exit area after years with no similar trouble. Did the business have a duty to post security guards?","review_truth":"A business is not an insurer against random third-party crime; guard duty needs a foreseeability trigger beyond mere customer status.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-SECURITY-DUTY-01","title":"Customer status is not guard-duty by itself","signal":"A business customer is attacked by an unknown stranger, and the answer says duty exists merely because the person was a customer.","student_move":"Ask what specific duty is claimed. If it is guards/security, look for prior similar danger, created risk, imminent danger, or comparable notice.","tiny_rule":"Status opens the premises-duty lane; foreseeability triggers the security-guard duty.","trap":"Treating invitee/customer status as automatic insurance against crime.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-THIRD-PARTY-SECURITY-01","statement":"A business is not an insurer against random third-party crime. Customer status matters, but guard/security duty needs a foreseeability trigger such as prior similar incidents, created risk, imminent danger, or comparable notice.","type":"distinction","trigger":"A customer is attacked by an unknown third party and sues for missing security guards.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 344 cmt. f; Restatement (Second) of Torts § 314A business-invitee principles, lawyer-confirmation only.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-SECURITY-CALL-01","statement":"Lock the exact duty before reading the choices: the question is not whether Esther was a customer, but whether these facts triggered a guard-duty against a random third-party attack.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"A choice says duty exists merely because the plaintiff bought or completed something at the business.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"A business must prevent all crimes on its premises.","answer":"Cut: tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"issue_sense","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"Duty exists because the plaintiff had just paid for the service.","answer":"Cut: status fact only; not guard-duty trigger."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"third-party crime duty","prompt":"No prior similar incidents; unknown stranger attack; guard-duty claim.","answer":"No guard duty absent foreseeability trigger."}]}'),
  ('6fe56962-7dc5-5e64-8e4b-b736eb8a6d6c', '19228_prayer_card_study_bible', 'EVIDENCE', 'III.B.2 — Marital Communications Privilege', 'Marital Communications Privilege', 'communication substance and confidentiality vs medium/refusal traps', 'In a later federal proceeding, the government subpoenas Ruth to authenticate and read a folded prayer-card she alone found while packing her personal study Bible for Naomi’s Psalms class. Daniel, Ruth’s husband, had tucked the card into the Bible during their marriage, and the card said, “I slid the silver coins into the olive-wood nativity crate.” Daniel asserts the marital communications privilege.', 'In a later federal proceeding, the government subpoenas Ruth to authenticate and read a folded prayer-card she alone found while packing her personal study Bible for Naomi’s Psalms class. Daniel, Ruth’s husband, had tucked the card into the Bible during their marriage, and the card said, “I slid the silver coins into the olive-wood nativity crate.” Daniel asserts the marital communications privilege.

How should the court rule?', 'How should the court rule?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19228.md","source_question_id":"19228","variant_slug":"prayer_card_study_bible","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"35030202","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"core diagnostic","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A husband privately leaves his wife a written note during marriage. The government later subpoenas the wife to read it. Does marital communications privilege protect it despite written format?","review_truth":"A confidential marital communication can be written; the privilege follows the private spouse-to-spouse communication, not the medium or the witness spouse’s refusal.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"EVI-PRIV-OBJECT-NOT-MEDIUM-01","title":"Privilege Object, Not Medium","signal":"The answer says a writing, text, card, email, or document is not a communication.","student_move":"Ask whether the object contains a confidential spouse-to-spouse communication.","tiny_rule":"Written can still be communication.","trap":"format/object confusion","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-MARITAL-WRITTEN-COMMUNICATION-01","statement":"Marital communications privilege protects a confidential communication between spouses during marriage; the communication can be written or electronic, not only spoken.","type":"distinction","trigger":"private written message between spouses during marriage","authority":"FRE 501; SEC v. Lavin; Wolfle v. United States; Trammel v. United States","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-EVIDENCE-PRAYER-CARD-01","statement":"When privilege answers fight over spoken words, document format, and refusal to testify, ask what object the privilege protects.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"one answer says document; one says spoken; one says spouse refuses","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"written marital communication","prompt":"A husband privately emails his wife during marriage. Is the email excluded from privilege merely because it is written?","answer":"No. Written can still be a confidential marital communication."},{"drill_type":"Trap spotting","target_skill":"format/object distinction","prompt":"Choice says: ''not privileged because the note is a document.'' Name the trap.","answer":"Format/object confusion."},{"drill_type":"Wrong-frame recovery","target_skill":"communications vs testimonial privilege","prompt":"Choice turns on whether the witness spouse refuses to testify. What call did it answer?","answer":"Adverse spousal testimony, not confidential marital communication."}]}'),
  ('188794ba-145e-532e-bc84-a934ba47edf5', '19250_scripture_card_printer', 'CONTRACTS', 'Conditions — Express conditions', 'V-A2', 'objective commercial performance vs subjective personal dislike', 'To stock her private Bible-conference booth, Lydia leased from Stephen a portable printer that made Scripture-verse cards. The lease made rent due only if Lydia was "satisfied that the printer produces 3,200 finished cards per hour." Before payment came due, Naomi timed three runs; each run produced 3,360 finished cards per hour. Lydia then refused to pay, saying she was honestly dissatisfied because she disliked the printer''s purple case.', 'To stock her private Bible-conference booth, Lydia leased from Stephen a portable printer that made Scripture-verse cards. The lease made rent due only if Lydia was "satisfied that the printer produces 3,200 finished cards per hour." Before payment came due, Naomi timed three runs; each run produced 3,360 finished cards per hour. Lydia then refused to pay, saying she was honestly dissatisfied because she disliked the printer''s purple case.

Which statement best describes the parties'' rights?', 'Which statement best describes the parties'' rights?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19250.md","source_question_id":"19250","variant_slug":"scripture_card_printer","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"trap repair","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A rent condition depends on satisfaction that equipment meets a measurable output rate; the equipment exceeds that rate, but the renter honestly dislikes a nonperformance feature. Must the renter pay?","review_truth":"A satisfaction clause about measurable commercial performance uses an objective standard; meeting the metric satisfies the condition despite an honest personal gripe.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-SATISFACTION-OBJECT-01","title":"Satisfaction About What?","signal":"A party says ''I was not satisfied'' after a measurable performance condition was met.","student_move":"Underline the object of satisfaction, then compare it to the facts.","tiny_rule":"Commercial/mechanical satisfaction is objective; personal taste is subjective.","trap":"honest-dislike wrong frame","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-SATISFACTION-COMMERCIAL-01","statement":"When a satisfaction condition concerns commercial utility, mechanical fitness, or measurable performance, use an objective reasonable-person standard if practicable. Honest personal dissatisfaction controls only taste/aesthetic satisfaction clauses.","type":"distinction","trigger":"satisfaction clause tied to a measurable output or performance metric","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 228; commercial/mechanical utility cases applying objective reasonableness.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-SATISFACTION-COMMERCIAL-01","statement":"Before honoring ''not satisfied,'' underline what the clause says the party must be satisfied about. If the object is a measurable output, match the answer to the metric, not the buyer''s mood.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"satisfaction clause plus test results","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"objective vs subjective satisfaction","prompt":"A satisfaction clause asks whether equipment produces 900 units per hour; tests show 940; buyer dislikes the color. Which standard governs?","answer":"Objective commercial standard."},{"drill_type":"Overclaim cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Cut the phrase: ''any satisfaction clause gives unlimited subjective discretion.''","answer":"tiered_absolute; NOT_TRUE."},{"drill_type":"Silver Key navigation","target_skill":"object-of-satisfaction scan","prompt":"Before accepting ''I was not satisfied,'' what do you underline?","answer":"What the contract says the party must be satisfied about."}]}'),
  ('6850b1ed-ae86-55d0-9ba1-7e51e54d907b', '19263_chapel_handbells', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Property Crimes', 'Other Crimes', 'What passed because of the deception: ownership/title, possession only, or lawful custody?', 'At a private Christian music expo, Lydia tells Paul, a collector of antique chapel handbell sets, that she is the purchasing manager for a well-known Christian retreat center and is buying seven handbell sets for its chapel choir. Relying on that statement, Paul signs bills of sale transferring ownership of all seven sets to Lydia and accepts Lydia''s personal check. Lydia is not affiliated with the retreat center, the check is drawn on a closed account, and she has no intention of paying. She loads the handbells into her van and leaves with the signed bills of sale.', 'At a private Christian music expo, Lydia tells Paul, a collector of antique chapel handbell sets, that she is the purchasing manager for a well-known Christian retreat center and is buying seven handbell sets for its chapel choir. Relying on that statement, Paul signs bills of sale transferring ownership of all seven sets to Lydia and accepts Lydia''s personal check. Lydia is not affiliated with the retreat center, the check is drawn on a closed account, and she has no intention of paying. She loads the handbells into her van and leaves with the signed bills of sale.

Of which crime is Lydia most likely guilty?', 'Of which crime is Lydia most likely guilty?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19263.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"chapel_handbells","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"73030300","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"hard anchor-assisted taxonomy","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"Paul signed bills of sale transferring ownership.","distilled_core_question":"A buyer lies about current status, the seller signs a bill of sale passing ownership, and the buyer leaves intending not to pay. What theft crime?","review_truth":"Title or ownership by a knowingly false present-fact lie is false pretenses; possession-only deception is larceny by trick; lawful custody then conversion is embezzlement.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CRIM-02","title":"Property-crime taxonomy: title, possession, custody","signal":"Choices name larceny by trick, embezzlement, and false pretenses.","student_move":"Find what the victim transferred.","tiny_rule":"Title/ownership by deceit = false pretenses; possession-only by deceit = larceny by trick; lawful custody then conversion = embezzlement.","trap":"Students stop at the visible lie instead of classifying the transfer.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-TITLE-PRESENT-FACT-01","statement":"False pretenses is the theft lane for title or ownership obtained by a knowingly false present or past fact. Larceny by trick is possession-only deception; embezzlement is lawful possession followed by conversion.","type":"distinction","trigger":"deception plus bill of sale/title/ownership transfer","authority":"Cornell Wex false pretenses and larceny-by-trick entries; official BarMatrix explanation.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-TITLE-AXIS-01","statement":"In a theft-taxonomy array, ask what moved first: title/ownership, possession only, or lawful custody. That axis picks the crime before the bad-check fact distracts you.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"multiple property-crime labels in the answer choices","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"axis_flash","target_skill":"title_vs_possession","prompt":"Seller signs a bill of sale after a buyer lies about current status. Which lane?","answer":"False pretenses."},{"drill_type":"contrast_pair","target_skill":"larceny_by_trick_vs_false_pretenses","prompt":"Borrowed by lie versus sold by lie: classify each.","answer":"Borrowed by lie = larceny by trick; sold by lie = false pretenses."},{"drill_type":"wrong_basis_cut","target_skill":"bad_check_as_intent_evidence","prompt":"A bad check appears in a false-pretenses stem. What does it usually do?","answer":"It supports fraudulent intent; it does not replace the present-fact lie."}]}'),
  ('c8b21293-2650-5cad-abb6-550828aa4835', '19266_carol_banquet_jmol', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Judgment as a Matter of Law', 'Sufficiency of Evidence', NULL, 'In a federal jury trial, Daniel sues Lydia, a private event planner, claiming she breached an agreement to reserve a rented community hall for his Christmas carol banquet. Daniel rests after telling the story of the banquet, but he introduces no evidence that Lydia ever agreed to make the reservation. Lydia moves for judgment as a matter of law on the agreement element. No witness, message, receipt, or admission supports Daniel on that element.', 'In a federal jury trial, Daniel sues Lydia, a private event planner, claiming she breached an agreement to reserve a rented community hall for his Christmas carol banquet. Daniel rests after telling the story of the banquet, but he introduces no evidence that Lydia ever agreed to make the reservation. Lydia moves for judgment as a matter of law on the agreement element. No witness, message, receipt, or admission supports Daniel on that element.

What standard should the court apply?', 'What standard should the court apply?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19266.md","source_question_id":"19266","variant_slug":"carol_banquet_jmol","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"92080201","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"After a plaintiff has been fully heard at trial and has no evidence on a required element, what Rule 50 standard governs JMOL?","review_truth":"Rule 50 JMOL turns on whether a reasonable jury has a legally sufficient evidentiary basis on the targeted issue, not sympathy, bad faith, or automatic jury submission.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-REM-JMOL-EVIDENCE-GATE-01","title":"JMOL = evidence gate, not moral gate","signal":"Party rests; motion targets one element.","student_move":"Ask whether the record contains evidence a reasonable jury could use on that element.","tiny_rule":"No evidence on an essential element means no legally sufficient jury basis.","trap":"Bad faith, sympathy, or jury-autopilot standards.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-JMOL-STANDARD-01","statement":"JMOL asks whether, after a party has been fully heard on an issue, a reasonable jury would have a legally sufficient evidentiary basis to find for that party on that issue.","type":"threshold","trigger":"Party rests; opponent moves for JMOL on a specific element.","authority":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 50(a)(1)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-JMOL-PROCEDURAL-LENS-01","statement":"On a JMOL motion, stay on trial evidence for the targeted element; do not drift to sympathy, filing conduct, or automatic jury submission.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"Call asks what standard the court should apply to JMOL.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"procedural_frame","prompt":"A plaintiff rests with no evidence on causation. Defendant moves for JMOL. Cut an answer saying JMOL turns on plaintiff bad faith.","answer":"Cut as fabricated_rule / procedural_frame."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"A choice says JMOL must be denied automatically because all civil trials go to the jury. What is the mold?","answer":"tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"Rule 50 standard","prompt":"State the JMOL standard after a party has been fully heard on an issue.","answer":"Whether a reasonable jury would have a legally sufficient evidentiary basis to find for that party on that issue."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"sympathy vs evidence","prompt":"A judge thinks the plaintiff is sympathetic, but the record has no evidence on an element. Does sympathy supply a jury basis?","answer":"No."}]}'),
  ('5c812d1c-4336-530e-b090-222b3615100b', '19286_five_loaves_press', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Discovery Sanctions', 'Lost ESI', 'prejudice-only curative measures vs intent-based severe sanctions', 'Mary owns Five Loaves Press, a private company that prints Bible-verse memory-card kits for homeschool groups. Ruth sues Five Loaves Press in federal court over a ruined Lent-season print run. Before the complaint was filed, Five Loaves Press knew litigation was likely and its lawyer told the company to preserve project records. The company kept using a project-board app that automatically erased production notes every 14 days. Several ''manna batch'' notes about the disputed print run disappeared and cannot be restored from backups or replaced through other discovery. Ruth shows that losing the notes prejudices her proof about when the print run failed. The record shows careless preservation, but not a deliberate plan to keep the notes from Ruth in the lawsuit.', 'Mary owns Five Loaves Press, a private company that prints Bible-verse memory-card kits for homeschool groups. Ruth sues Five Loaves Press in federal court over a ruined Lent-season print run. Before the complaint was filed, Five Loaves Press knew litigation was likely and its lawyer told the company to preserve project records. The company kept using a project-board app that automatically erased production notes every 14 days. Several ''manna batch'' notes about the disputed print run disappeared and cannot be restored from backups or replaced through other discovery. Ruth shows that losing the notes prejudices her proof about when the print run failed. The record shows careless preservation, but not a deliberate plan to keep the notes from Ruth in the lawsuit.

Which sanction framework should the court use?', 'Which sanction framework should the court use?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19286.md","source_question_id":"19286","variant_slug":"five_loaves_press","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"95070407","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium_high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"The record shows no deliberate plan to keep the notes from Ruth.","distilled_core_question":"ESI that should have been preserved was lost, cannot be restored, and caused prejudice, but there was no intent to deprive. What sanction track applies?","review_truth":"Prejudice from lost ESI without intent gets cure-only measures; intent is the gate for adverse inference and case-ending sanctions.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-LOST-ESI-01","title":"Lost ESI sanction ladder","signal":"ESI lost after preservation duty, prejudice shown, no intent.","student_move":"Choose cure-the-prejudice measures; cut adverse inference and case-ending sanctions.","tiny_rule":"Prejudice cures; intent punishes.","trap":"Treating lost evidence as automatically unfavorable.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-LOST-ESI-01","statement":"Lost ESI has a sanction ladder: prejudice without intent allows only curative measures no greater than necessary; adverse inference, presumption, dismissal, or default requires intent to deprive.","type":"distinction","trigger":"lost ESI + prejudice + no deliberate deprivation","authority":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(e)(1)-(2)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-LOST-ESI-01","statement":"Read the choices by sanction severity keyed to mental state: no intent in the stem pushes you away from unfavorable-presumption and case-ending answers.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"one answer cures prejudice; two answers punish as if intent existed","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"Rule 37(e) sanction ladder","prompt":"Lost ESI, prejudice, no intent. What sanction lane?","answer":"Measures no greater than necessary to cure prejudice."},{"drill_type":"Overclaim cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"The court must enter default whenever any ESI is lost.","answer":"Cut: automatic case-ending sanction overclaim."},{"drill_type":"Answer-array read","target_skill":"sanction severity keyed to mental state","prompt":"One answer cures prejudice; two impose severe punishment. The stem says no intent. Which way should you lean?","answer":"Cure prejudice."},{"drill_type":"Fabricated-rule cut","target_skill":"broad discovery exclusion","prompt":"The court may do nothing because ESI is not discoverable.","answer":"Cut: fabricated categorical exclusion."}]}'),
  ('547dc6a8-250b-5391-a499-e9b3fe84398d', '19336_nativity_warehouse_rescue', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Defenses', 'General Principles', 'majority reasonable appearance versus alter-ego actual rights', 'Ruth is volunteering in a privately run Christian bookstore’s Christmas-toy sorting warehouse. From the doorway, she sees Daniel yank Timothy backward by the collar and drive him into a stack of boxed nativity sets. Ruth does not know that Timothy had swung a metal tape dispenser at Daniel moments earlier and that Daniel is using reasonable force to stop Timothy from continuing. Ruth reasonably believes she is watching Daniel unlawfully attack Timothy, so she swats Daniel’s forearm hard enough to make him let go. In truth, Daniel had a valid self-defense claim against Timothy. The jurisdiction follows the majority rule on defense of others. Ruth is charged with battery.', 'Ruth is volunteering in a privately run Christian bookstore’s Christmas-toy sorting warehouse. From the doorway, she sees Daniel yank Timothy backward by the collar and drive him into a stack of boxed nativity sets. Ruth does not know that Timothy had swung a metal tape dispenser at Daniel moments earlier and that Daniel is using reasonable force to stop Timothy from continuing. Ruth reasonably believes she is watching Daniel unlawfully attack Timothy, so she swats Daniel’s forearm hard enough to make him let go. In truth, Daniel had a valid self-defense claim against Timothy. The jurisdiction follows the majority rule on defense of others. Ruth is charged with battery.

Under the majority rule, does Ruth have a valid defense-of-others claim?', 'Under the majority rule, does Ruth have a valid defense-of-others claim?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19336.md","source_question_id":"19336","variant_slug":"nativity_warehouse_rescue","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"72060203","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium_high_anchor_dependent","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A defender reasonably but mistakenly protects the apparent victim; the apparent attacker was actually acting in self-defense. Under the majority defense-of-others rule, does the defender still have the defense?","review_truth":"Majority defense of others protects a reasonable intervener from a reasonable mistake about who was legally entitled to use force.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CRIM-ANCHOR-DEFENSE-OTHERS-01","title":"Defense of others: reasonable appearance beats hidden truth","signal":"Intervener reasonably but mistakenly protects the apparent victim.","student_move":"Do not ask who was actually entitled to self-defense until you know the rule frame.","tiny_rule":"Majority rule = reasonable belief; alter ego = actual rights.","trap":"Hidden-truth answer feels precise but answers the wrong rule frame.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIM-DEFENSE-OTHERS-01","statement":"Majority defense of others is judged by the intervener’s reasonable belief. A reasonable mistake about who was entitled to use force does not forfeit the defense.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Stem says majority rule and gives both hidden actual facts and reasonable apparent facts.","authority":"Model Penal Code § 3.05(1)(b)-(c); representative reasonable-belief statutes; modern majority rule summaries.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIM-DEFENSE-OTHERS-01","statement":"When a defense-of-others stem gives hidden actual facts and apparent reasonable facts, name the axis before choosing: actual-rights rule or reasonable-appearance rule.","type":"clash","trigger":"Call says majority rule; one choice uses actual facts and one uses reasonable belief.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"Defense-of-others majority rule","prompt":"A defender reasonably but mistakenly protects the apparent victim. Does the reasonable mistake defeat the defense under the majority rule?","answer":"No. Majority rule uses reasonable belief."},{"drill_type":"CLASH axis","target_skill":"Actual hidden facts versus reasonable appearance","prompt":"One answer says the apparent attacker actually had self-defense. Another says the intervener reasonably believed force was needed. What is the axis?","answer":"Alter-ego actual rights versus majority reasonable appearance."},{"drill_type":"CUT fabricated rule","target_skill":"Extra-prerequisite detection","prompt":"Defense of others applies only if the person defended asked for help. What mold is this?","answer":"fabricated_rule; NOT_TRUE after the Gold Key."},{"drill_type":"CUT fabricated rule","target_skill":"Extra-prerequisite detection","prompt":"Defense of others fails because the defender did not witness the start of the fight. What mold is this?","answer":"fabricated_rule; NOT_TRUE after the Gold Key."}]}'),
  ('387ce434-0464-558d-a8a2-43e17d64fb24', '19379_hymn_candle_invoice_van', 'EVIDENCE', 'III.B.2 — Marital Communications Privilege', 'Marital Communications Privilege', 'adverse spousal testimonial privilege vs confidential marital communications privilege', 'In a federal wire-fraud prosecution against Timothy over online sales from his small Christian-craft booth, the prosecutor plans to call Lydia. Timothy and Lydia finalized their divorce eighteen months ago. Years earlier, while they were married, the two were sitting alone in Timothy’s parked delivery van after a city winter fair when Timothy quietly told Lydia, “I forged the online invoices for those hymn-themed candle orders.” Timothy objects to Lydia’s testimony about that private statement on spousal-privilege grounds.', 'In a federal wire-fraud prosecution against Timothy over online sales from his small Christian-craft booth, the prosecutor plans to call Lydia. Timothy and Lydia finalized their divorce eighteen months ago. Years earlier, while they were married, the two were sitting alone in Timothy’s parked delivery van after a city winter fair when Timothy quietly told Lydia, “I forged the online invoices for those hymn-themed candle orders.” Timothy objects to Lydia’s testimony about that private statement on spousal-privilege grounds.

How should the court rule?', 'How should the court rule?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19379.md","source_question_id":"19379","variant_slug":"hymn_candle_invoice_van","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"35030202","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"discriminator","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"divorced now; private statement during marriage","distilled_core_question":"Former spouse is called in a federal criminal case to testify about a private statement made during the marriage. Does divorce defeat the privilege objection?","review_truth":"Divorce ends the adverse-testimonial route, not the confidential-communications route for private statements made during marriage.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-EVIDENCE-SPOUSAL-19379-01","title":"Do not collapse the two spousal privileges","signal":"Divorced spouse asked about a private statement from the marriage.","student_move":"Separate testimonial privilege from communications privilege before choosing sustain or overrule.","tiny_rule":"Current marriage matters for testimonial privilege; private communication during marriage matters for communications privilege.","trap":"Divorce ends everything.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-SPOUSAL-01","statement":"Spousal testimonial privilege and confidential marital communications privilege are separate. Testimonial privilege requires a current marriage; communications privilege can protect confidential private communications made during the marriage even after divorce.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Divorced former spouse + private statement made during marriage.","authority":"FRE 501; Trammel v. United States; Pereira v. United States.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-EVIDENCE-SPOUSAL-01","statement":"When the answer set splits sustain/overrule and testimonial/communications, pick the route that matches the timing facts: divorced now, private communication then.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Two sustain answers with different privilege labels.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"spousal privilege distinction","prompt":"A divorced former spouse is called to testify about a private statement from the marriage. Which privilege route is live?","answer":"Confidential marital communications privilege."},{"drill_type":"Overclaim cut","target_skill":"Ear: tiered_absolute","prompt":"Cut the defect: ''Divorce ends every spousal privilege.''","answer":"Tiered absolute; divorce does not end every spousal privilege."},{"drill_type":"Wrong-frame clash","target_skill":"Issue-Sense: bait_doctrine","prompt":"Two answers say sustain. One says adverse testimonial privilege; one says confidential marital communications privilege. The parties are divorced, but the statement was private and made during marriage. Which route wins?","answer":"Confidential marital communications privilege."}]}'),
  ('72a48c04-e965-5332-a035-431e62baad85', '19381_shepherd_olive_press_deed', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Conveyance by Deed — Requirements for Valid Deed', 'B1', 'recording as validity condition vs recording as notice/priority protection', 'Naomi owned a small olive-press parcel behind her bakery. After Lydia completed a private hymn-calligraphy commission for Naomi, Naomi signed and handed Lydia a deed conveying the parcel to Lydia. The deed contained all required deed terms, and Lydia accepted it. Lydia tucked the deed inside a Psalms hymnal and never recorded it. No later buyer, lender, creditor, or other third party claims the parcel. Naomi later says the deed did not work because no recorder’s stamp was ever placed on it.', 'Naomi owned a small olive-press parcel behind her bakery. After Lydia completed a private hymn-calligraphy commission for Naomi, Naomi signed and handed Lydia a deed conveying the parcel to Lydia. The deed contained all required deed terms, and Lydia accepted it. Lydia tucked the deed inside a Psalms hymnal and never recorded it. No later buyer, lender, creditor, or other third party claims the parcel. Naomi later says the deed did not work because no recorder’s stamp was ever placed on it.

Who owns the olive-press parcel?', 'Who owns the olive-press parcel?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19381.md","source_question_id":"19381","variant_slug":"shepherd_olive_press_deed","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"calibration","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"No third party claims the parcel.","distilled_core_question":"Grantor signed, delivered, and grantee accepted a valid deed, but the grantee never recorded it. No third party claims the land. Who owns it?","review_truth":"Recording protects against outsiders; it is not required for a valid deed to pass title between grantor and grantee.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RP-ANCHOR-RECORDING-VALIDITY-01","title":"Recording vs Validity","signal":"Valid deed delivered and accepted, but not recorded.","student_move":"Ask: original parties or outsider priority?","tiny_rule":"No outsider: recording is not required for title to pass between grantor and grantee.","trap":"Treating the recorder’s office as the title-transfer event.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-REAL_PROPERTY-RECORDING-VALIDITY-01","statement":"A valid deed delivered and accepted passes title between grantor and grantee even if unrecorded; recording protects notice and priority against outsiders.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Valid deed + delivery + acceptance + no recording + no third-party claimant.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-REAL_PROPERTY-RECORDING-VALIDITY-01","statement":"When three choices make missing recording decide ownership, first ask whether the stem has an outsider. No outsider means the array is testing validity between the original parties.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"All distractors point to nonrecording.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"Recording vs validity","prompt":"A valid deed is delivered and accepted but not recorded. No third party appears. Who owns?","answer":"The grantee owns; recording is not required between grantor and grantee."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"3-vs-1 recording trap","prompt":"Three choices give the grantor title because the deed was not recorded. One gives the grantee title because delivery and acceptance occurred. What is the array testing?","answer":"Recording vs validity."},{"drill_type":"trigger_fact","target_skill":"Outsider check","prompt":"What fact would change this into a recording-act priority question?","answer":"A later buyer, lender, creditor, or other protected third-party claimant."}]}'),
  ('2c26c157-512d-509e-b5fc-7b272d09e2e2', '19386_scripture_audio_cap', 'CONTRACTS', 'Third-Party Beneficiary Contracts', 'VII-A', 'same-contract cap travels with intended-beneficiary right vs nonparty escape from the cap', 'Hannah spent $26,000 replacing unusable Scripture-memory audio tracks after Lydia, a freelance sound engineer, delivered corrupted masters. The work had been commissioned under Peter''s written service contract with Lydia. That contract required Lydia to prepare the tracks for Hannah''s private tutoring program and stated, "Lydia''s total liability for any audio-work claim is capped at $12,000." Hannah sues Lydia as an intended beneficiary and seeks $26,000.', 'Hannah spent $26,000 replacing unusable Scripture-memory audio tracks after Lydia, a freelance sound engineer, delivered corrupted masters. The work had been commissioned under Peter''s written service contract with Lydia. That contract required Lydia to prepare the tracks for Hannah''s private tutoring program and stated, "Lydia''s total liability for any audio-work claim is capped at $12,000." Hannah sues Lydia as an intended beneficiary and seeks $26,000.

Which argument is Lydia''s strongest response?', 'Which argument is Lydia''s strongest response?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19386.md","source_question_id":"19386","variant_slug":"scripture_audio_cap","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"53070100","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"core diagnostic","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"The beneficiary promise and liability cap are in the same written contract.","distilled_core_question":"A written service contract directly benefits a named third party and caps liability. The third party sues as an intended beneficiary for more than the cap. Is the beneficiary limited by the cap?","review_truth":"A third-party beneficiary can enforce the contract only as the contract gives it; the beneficiary does not get the benefit without the cap.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-TPB-PACKAGE-01","title":"Third-party beneficiary package deal","signal":"A nonparty enforces a contract that also limits the claim.","student_move":"Do not split the contract into benefit-only and cap-free pieces.","tiny_rule":"TPB right travels with the contract terms.","trap":"Nonparty status feels like freedom from the cap.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-TPB-CAP-01","statement":"An intended beneficiary takes the contract as written. If the same contract creates the beneficiary''s right and caps liability, the beneficiary''s claim is capped.","type":"rule","trigger":"A nonparty sues as an intended beneficiary and seeks more than a limit in the same contract.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 302, 309.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-TPB-CAP-01","statement":"In a no-recovery / capped-recovery / full-recovery / assignment answer set, compare the source of the right with the source of the limit. Same source means package answer.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"A beneficiary claims more than a contract limit.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"A choice says intended beneficiaries can never enforce service contracts. What is the visible breaker?","answer":"The categorical word ''never'' overclaims."},{"drill_type":"CLASH","target_skill":"source-of-right/source-of-limit axis","prompt":"A beneficiary sues under a contract that also caps the promisor''s liability. What axis decides cap vs full recovery?","answer":"Whether the right and the cap come from the same contract."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"TPB scope","prompt":"State the Gold Key for an intended beneficiary and a contract cap.","answer":"An intended beneficiary takes the contract as written; same-contract caps apply."},{"drill_type":"ISSUE-SENSE","target_skill":"TPB vs assignment","prompt":"The stem says the plaintiff sues as an intended beneficiary. A choice says recovery requires assignment. What is the mold?","answer":"Bait doctrine / wrong frame."}]}'),
  ('aa0bdc43-9381-5cdf-9cb6-fb3eb072ffeb', '19430_harp_instructor_olive_grove', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Conveyance by Deed — Requirements for Valid Deed', 'B1', 'ascertainable role-holder vs formalities/entity traps', 'Lydia owned a fenced olive grove outside Capernaum. Before leaving for a psalm-singing festival, she signed and delivered a deed stating, “I convey the olive grove to the harp instructor of Capernaum Hymn School today.” At that moment, Daniel was the only harp instructor of Capernaum Hymn School. Daniel accepted the deed.', 'Lydia owned a fenced olive grove outside Capernaum. Before leaving for a psalm-singing festival, she signed and delivered a deed stating, “I convey the olive grove to the harp instructor of Capernaum Hymn School today.” At that moment, Daniel was the only harp instructor of Capernaum Hymn School. Daniel accepted the deed.

Who owns the olive grove?', 'Who owns the olive grove?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19430.md","source_question_id":"19430","variant_slug":"harp_instructor_olive_grove","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"84040201","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"calibration","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A deed conveys land to a role-holder; one person held that role at delivery and accepted. Who owns?","review_truth":"A role description can identify the grantee if the person holding the role is ascertainable at delivery.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RP-DEED-ROLE-GRANTEE-01","title":"Role Description as Grantee","signal":"Deed conveys to “the [role holder] of [organization].”","student_move":"Ask whether one person fit that role at delivery.","tiny_rule":"Role description is enough if it identifies one person at delivery.","trap":"Do not require the personal name or grantee signature.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-REAL_PROPERTY-ASCERTAINABLE-GRANTEE-01","statement":"A deed does not fail merely because the grantee is described by role rather than personal name. If one person fits the description at delivery, that person is ascertainable and can take title.","type":"rule","trigger":"Deed says “to the [office/role holder]” and the stem says one person held that role at delivery.","authority":"Vineyard v. O’Connor, 36 S.W. 424 (Tex. 1896), as cited in ConocoPhillips Co. v. Hahn.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-REAL_PROPERTY-GRANTEE-SIGNATURE-01","statement":"A grantee generally need not sign the deed to take title; delivery by the grantor and acceptance by the grantee do the work.","type":"rule","trigger":"Answer says the named recipient loses because the recipient did not sign.","authority":"Tex. Prop. Code § 5.021 requires the conveyance to be subscribed and delivered by the conveyor or authorized agent.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-REAL_PROPERTY-ROLE-GRANTEE-01","statement":"When a deed names a role, separate the role-holder from the organization named inside the role phrase before checking formalities.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Choice set includes the person, the grantor, and the organization as possible owners.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"ascertainable grantee by role","prompt":"A deed says “to the choir director of a school today.” One person held that role at delivery. Who is the grantee?","answer":"The role-holder."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"fabricated deed formalities","prompt":"Cut the choice: “Grantor keeps title because the grantee did not sign the deed.”","answer":"NOT_TRUE; fabricated_rule; grantee signature generally not required."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"role-holder vs entity","prompt":"A deed says “to the treasurer of Mercy Hall.” Does that automatically make Mercy Hall the grantee?","answer":"No; first ask who the role-holder was."}]}'),
  ('8b887187-31c3-5c86-8525-494ad62dd6e6', '19434_hymn_hands_stimulator', 'TORTS', 'Products liability', 'Failure to warn - learned intermediary', NULL, 'Before Lydia played handbells at a community hymn festival, her physician, Stephen, prescribed a prescription-only wrist nerve stimulator to control hand tremors. The stimulator carried a rare risk of finger numbness that could make fine hand movement difficult. Before Stephen ordered it, Barnabas Biotech sent Stephen an updated professional safety notice that clearly described the risk, symptoms, and alternative treatments. Stephen read the notice, discussed the risk with Lydia, and ordered the stimulator because alternatives were unsuitable. Lydia later suffered finger numbness and sued Barnabas Biotech, arguing that Barnabas did not send the same warning directly to her. The jurisdiction follows the learned-intermediary rule.

Call:', 'Before Lydia played handbells at a community hymn festival, her physician, Stephen, prescribed a prescription-only wrist nerve stimulator to control hand tremors. The stimulator carried a rare risk of finger numbness that could make fine hand movement difficult. Before Stephen ordered it, Barnabas Biotech sent Stephen an updated professional safety notice that clearly described the risk, symptoms, and alternative treatments. Stephen read the notice, discussed the risk with Lydia, and ordered the stimulator because alternatives were unsuitable. Lydia later suffered finger numbness and sued Barnabas Biotech, arguing that Barnabas did not send the same warning directly to her. The jurisdiction follows the learned-intermediary rule.

Call:

Is Lydia likely to prevail on a strict products liability claim for failure to warn against Barnabas Biotech?', 'Is Lydia likely to prevail on a strict products liability claim for failure to warn against Barnabas Biotech?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19434.md","source_question_id":"19434","variant_slug":"hymn_hands_stimulator","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium_high_anchor","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A prescription-only product had a clear risk warning sent to the prescribing physician, who read it and discussed it with the patient. The patient suffered the warned-of risk and sues because the manufacturer did not warn her directly. Does the failure-to-warn claim succeed?","review_truth":"In a learned-intermediary jurisdiction, an adequate warning to the prescribing physician can defeat a direct-to-patient failure-to-warn theory for a prescription product.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-LI-01","title":"Warned risk is not warning defect","signal":"The answer says the plaintiff suffered the exact risk described.","student_move":"Ask whether the call is about injury or about warning duty.","tiny_rule":"Injury completes harm; it does not prove the warning was defective.","trap":"Wrong-element answer on failure-to-warn claims.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-LEARNED-INTERMEDIARY-01","statement":"For prescription products, an adequate warning to the prescribing healthcare provider can satisfy the manufacturer''s warning duty; the manufacturer is not automatically required to warn the patient directly.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Prescription-only product + professional warning to prescriber + jurisdiction follows learned-intermediary rule.","authority":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Products Liability § 6(d); learned-intermediary case law.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-WARNED-RISK-01","statement":"On a failure-to-warn call, an answer saying the warned-of risk happened proves injury, not warning defect.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"Choice points to injury by the described risk instead of the adequacy or audience of the warning.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"Failure-to-warn claim. The answer says the plaintiff suffered the exact risk listed in the warning. What filter breaks?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE: it proves injury, not warning defect."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"An answer says prescription products are exempt from all products liability claims. What mold?","answer":"tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"learned intermediary","prompt":"Prescription-only product, adequate warning to prescribing physician, no direct patient warning. What Gold Key?","answer":"Learned intermediary: adequate warning to prescriber can satisfy the manufacturer''s warning duty."},{"drill_type":"PREDICT","target_skill":"call lock","prompt":"A patient sues for failure to warn after the doctor received and discussed a warning. What is the precise call?","answer":"Whether the manufacturer breached the warning duty, not whether the patient was injured."}]}'),
  ('0cb5c216-ffe2-5b1c-bb99-f3e3c2da0744', '19448_praise_night_tshirt_cannon', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Duty - negligent entrustment', 'control/supply versus formal ownership', 'At a rented civic auditorium, Lydia organized a private Christian music night. A vendor left a compressed-air T-shirt cannon for authorized staff to use between songs. Lydia was not the cannon''s owner, but under the event agreement she had the key and authority to decide who could handle it during the event. During setup, Lydia handed the cannon to Barnabas, a visibly intoxicated friend who had never used one, so he could "make the praise-team singers laugh." Barnabas fired wildly, knocked over two music stands, and then struck Ruth, a visiting photographer, in the face. Ruth sues Lydia for negligence.', 'At a rented civic auditorium, Lydia organized a private Christian music night. A vendor left a compressed-air T-shirt cannon for authorized staff to use between songs. Lydia was not the cannon''s owner, but under the event agreement she had the key and authority to decide who could handle it during the event. During setup, Lydia handed the cannon to Barnabas, a visibly intoxicated friend who had never used one, so he could "make the praise-team singers laugh." Barnabas fired wildly, knocked over two music stands, and then struck Ruth, a visiting photographer, in the face. Ruth sues Lydia for negligence.

Is Ruth likely to establish a negligent entrustment duty against Lydia?', 'Is Ruth likely to establish a negligent entrustment duty against Lydia?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19448.md","source_question_id":"19448","variant_slug":"praise_night_tshirt_cannon","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A person with authority to control a dangerous chattel gives it to a visibly intoxicated, inexperienced user, who injures a third party. Can the controller face negligent entrustment liability even though she is not the owner?","review_truth":"For negligent entrustment, control/supply plus known unsafe user beats formal title.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-NE-001","title":"Control Beats Title","signal":"Non-owner had authority over who could use the chattel.","student_move":"Ask who supplied or permitted use, then ask whether the user was visibly unsafe.","tiny_rule":"For negligent entrustment, supplier/control can be enough; title is not the gate.","trap":"Owner-only answer.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-NEGLIGENT-ENTRUSTMENT-01","statement":"Negligent entrustment looks to control or supply of the chattel plus known unsafe use; formal legal title is not the gate.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Defendant is not owner but has key, permission authority, or control and hands the chattel to an impaired or inexperienced user.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 308, 390; Delana v. CED Sales, Inc.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-NEGLIGENT-ENTRUSTMENT-01","statement":"Lock the target defendant first: the call asks about Lydia''s duty, so an answer focused only on Barnabas''s operation is adjacent-call bait.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The injured plaintiff sues the person who handed over the chattel, not the person who physically used it.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"Negligent entrustment makes the entrustor strictly liable for any injury caused by the chattel.","answer":"CUT: tiered_absolute / wrong_standard."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"control versus title","prompt":"The defendant is not the owner but has authority to decide who uses the chattel.","answer":"Use Gold Key: supplier/control can satisfy the negligent entrustment gate."},{"drill_type":"CALL_FOCUS","target_skill":"target defendant","prompt":"The injured plaintiff sues the person who handed over the chattel, but a choice says the user alone operated it.","answer":"Lock the call to the entrustor''s duty; do not answer only the user''s negligence."}]}'),
  ('961ae7f9-0caf-5951-94cf-4179781ff7c7', '19499_jericho_chariot_night', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Inchoate Crimes and Parties', 'Inchoate Crimes', 'minimum required participants vs extra participants', 'A state criminalizes an unlicensed paid chariot race between two racers. Peter and John agree to drive such a race at a private Bible-themed harvest fair. Lydia, Ruth, and Barnabas agree to rent a fairground track, sell admission wristbands, and handle the cashbox and livestream fees. Prosecutors charge all five with conspiracy to conduct the illegal chariot race. The defendants invoke Wharton’s Rule.', 'A state criminalizes an unlicensed paid chariot race between two racers. Peter and John agree to drive such a race at a private Bible-themed harvest fair. Lydia, Ruth, and Barnabas agree to rent a fairground track, sell admission wristbands, and handle the cashbox and livestream fees. Prosecutors charge all five with conspiracy to conduct the illegal chariot race. The defendants invoke Wharton’s Rule.

What is the best response?', 'What is the best response?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19499.md","source_question_id":"19499","variant_slug":"jericho_chariot_night","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"71040200","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-high","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A two-person target offense has two required participants, but three extra people join the plan. Does Wharton’s Rule bar conspiracy?","review_truth":"Wharton’s Rule does not bar conspiracy when extra participants beyond the target offense’s minimum join the agreement.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CRIMINAL-WHARTON-COUNT","title":"Wharton Count Check","signal":"Wharton’s Rule plus extra people in the agreement","student_move":"Count required participants first; count actual agreement participants second.","tiny_rule":"Extra participants defeat Wharton’s Rule.","trap":"Stopping at the two-party offense and missing the extra-party exception.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-WHARTON-EXTRA-PARTY-01","statement":"Wharton’s Rule can bar conspiracy only when the target offense already requires the same minimum parties. If extra people beyond that minimum join the agreement, the conspiracy charge survives.","type":"exception","trigger":"two-person target offense plus organizers or other extra participants","authority":"Iannelli v. United States, 420 U.S. 770 (1975)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-WHARTON-COUNT-01","statement":"When Wharton’s Rule appears, count twice: how many people the target offense needs, then how many people joined the plan.","type":"clash","trigger":"a two-party offense with extra planners, sellers, organizers, or facilitators","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"Wharton''s Rule extra-party exception","prompt":"Two required participants plus one unnecessary helper join the plan. Does Wharton''s Rule bar conspiracy?","answer":"No. Extra participants defeat the bar."},{"drill_type":"clash_axis","target_skill":"participant counting","prompt":"Name the axis: two-party offense, five-person plan.","answer":"Minimum required participants vs actual agreement participants."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Wharton''s Rule always bars conspiracy whenever the target offense involves any agreement.","answer":"Cut as overclaim."},{"drill_type":"wrong_element","target_skill":"red-herring fact","prompt":"The organizers expected payment, so Wharton''s Rule bars conspiracy.","answer":"Wrong fact axis; payment is not the trigger."}]}'),
  ('395b28ab-4aa7-5fae-8a9d-35bb89de4779', '19502_manna_festival_finals_tax', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Constitutional Law', 'Separation of Powers', 'taxing power revenue frame vs imported constitutional defect frames', 'After several costly nationwide flood-control projects, Congress enacted the Manna Festival Finals Revenue Act as part of a broader plan to retire federal disaster-recovery debt. For six years, the Act imposed a 40% excise tax on the price of paid admission wristbands to final-round artisan food competitions. Covered events included the Manna Bread Cup, the Loaves-and-Fishes Fish-Fry Crown, the Ruth Harvest Pie Finals, and similar paid championship food contests.', 'After several costly nationwide flood-control projects, Congress enacted the Manna Festival Finals Revenue Act as part of a broader plan to retire federal disaster-recovery debt. For six years, the Act imposed a 40% excise tax on the price of paid admission wristbands to final-round artisan food competitions. Covered events included the Manna Bread Cup, the Loaves-and-Fishes Fish-Fry Crown, the Ruth Harvest Pie Finals, and similar paid championship food contests.

This federal tax is probably', 'This federal tax is probably', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19502.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"manna_festival_finals_tax","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"42010101","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"2","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Congress imposes a high temporary federal excise tax on paid admission to one category of final-round events to raise revenue. Is the tax likely constitutional despite possible economic harm to the taxed events?","review_truth":"A facially revenue-raising federal excise tax is usually valid even if it hurts the taxed activity; do not jump to strict scrutiny or equal protection without a trigger.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-TAX-REVENUE-01","title":"Tax-power frame before scrutiny frame","signal":"Federal excise tax + revenue purpose + answer choices invoking strict scrutiny or equal protection.","student_move":"Ask whether the stem gives a protected trigger. If not, stay in the revenue-tax frame.","tiny_rule":"Bad economic effects do not by themselves invalidate a facial revenue tax.","trap":"Picking the dramatic constitutional standard instead of the tax-power answer.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONLAW-TAX-REVENUE-01","statement":"A federal excise tax that is facially revenue-raising is usually valid even if it burdens, discourages, or economically harms the taxed activity.","type":"rule","trigger":"federal excise tax + revenue/debt purpose + argument that the tax hurts the taxed activity","authority":"U.S. Const. art. I, §8; Sonzinsky v. United States; Fernandez v. Wiener","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONLAW-TAX-CLASS-02","statement":"Ordinary economic and tax classifications do not get strict scrutiny unless the stem gives a suspect class, fundamental right, or other protected trigger.","type":"burden","trigger":"tax classification + compelling-interest or equal-protection answer with no protected trigger","authority":"Regan v. Taxation With Representation; FCC v. Beach Communications","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONLAW-TAX-ARRAY-01","statement":"When a Con Law tax array mixes compelling interest, rational relation, equal protection, and economic-impact language, first lock the question as taxing power before liking the strongest-sounding constitutional phrase.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"revenue tax stem with multiple constitutional standards in the answer set","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"strict-scrutiny trigger discipline","prompt":"A federal excise tax funds debt retirement. One answer says it is valid because of a compelling national interest. What is the first C3 cut?","answer":"Cut the strict-scrutiny frame unless the stem gives a protected trigger."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"economic-impact non sequitur","prompt":"A tax may reduce attendance at the taxed events. Does that alone defeat the tax''s revenue relationship?","answer":"No. Economic burden does not erase the revenue-tax frame."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"taxing-power Gold Key","prompt":"State the Gold Key for a facial revenue excise tax with adverse economic consequences.","answer":"A facial revenue tax is usually valid even if it burdens or discourages the taxed activity."},{"drill_type":"ANSWER_ARRAY","target_skill":"tax array navigation","prompt":"The array contains compelling interest, rational relation, equal protection, and adverse economic consequences. What frame should you lock first?","answer":"Taxing power."}]}'),
  ('662ba579-8e62-574b-9b19-cf3fb2dae6c2', '19554_road_to_damascus_cashbox', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Property Crimes', 'Other Crimes', 'custody versus lawful possession versus title', 'Lydia owns a private Bible-themed escape room called Road to Damascus. For one evening, she assigns Peter to the entry table for the “shipwreck puzzle” room. Peter may take visitors’ $18 admission fees and drop the bills through the slot of Lydia’s wooden ark-shaped cash box. During his shift, Peter pulls $360 from the box and hides it in his costume satchel, intending to keep it.', 'Lydia owns a private Bible-themed escape room called Road to Damascus. For one evening, she assigns Peter to the entry table for the “shipwreck puzzle” room. Peter may take visitors’ $18 admission fees and drop the bills through the slot of Lydia’s wooden ark-shaped cash box. During his shift, Peter pulls $360 from the box and hides it in his costume satchel, intending to keep it.

What is the best common-law classification?', 'What is the best common-law classification?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19554.md","source_question_id":"19554","variant_slug":"road_to_damascus_cashbox","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"73030100","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A worker may collect business payments and put them in the owner’s cash box; he pockets some intending to keep it. What common-law theft offense is it?","review_truth":"A low-level worker who only handles the owner’s cash has custody; pocketing it is larceny, not embezzlement.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CRIM-CUSTODY-POSSESSION-01","title":"Custody is not possession","signal":"Low-level worker handles owner cash.","student_move":"Ask whether the worker had custody only, lawful possession, title by deception, or no taking.","tiny_rule":"Custody + pocketing owner cash = larceny.","trap":"Worker theft automatically means embezzlement.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIM-CUSTODY-POSSESSION-01","statement":"A low-level worker who merely handles the owner’s cash usually has custody, not lawful possession. If the worker pockets it intending to keep it, the common-law classification is larceny.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Worker is authorized to collect or handle money for the owner, then secretly pockets it.","authority":"Lawyer-confirmation only: common-law larceny and embezzlement authorities.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIM-THEFT-CLASSIFICATION-01","statement":"On a theft-classification array, do not start with the job title. Sort the property path: custody, lawful possession, title, or no offense.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"The answer choices are competing common-law theft labels.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"Choice says all worker theft from a business is embezzlement. Name the mold.","answer":"tiered_absolute"},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"custody versus possession","prompt":"A low-level worker collects owner cash and pockets it. Classify.","answer":"larceny"},{"drill_type":"ISSUE_SENSE","target_skill":"wrong element","prompt":"Choice says no theft because the worker could handle the money. What element is missing?","answer":"permission to keep or appropriate the money"}]}'),
  ('395da092-7f26-5e9c-8323-6d898d6e35a3', '19607_cana_hills_bethany_flats', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Equal Protection', 'Rational basis — non-fundamental right / education', 'heightened-review trigger vs default rational basis', 'The State of Galilee funds its public schools with a statewide foundation grant plus whatever each local district raises through local property taxes. In Cana Hills, a lakeside district with expensive homes and commercial property, the public schools spend about $22,400 per student each year. In Bethany Flats, an old cannery district with a small tax base, the public schools spend about $7,300 per student each year. Mary, Peter, and Lydia, parents of children in Bethany Flats, bring an Equal Protection challenge. They argue that the funding plan denies their children an equal public education. The state responds that the plan preserves local control of schools and rationally connects local tax revenue to local school spending.', 'The State of Galilee funds its public schools with a statewide foundation grant plus whatever each local district raises through local property taxes. In Cana Hills, a lakeside district with expensive homes and commercial property, the public schools spend about $22,400 per student each year. In Bethany Flats, an old cannery district with a small tax base, the public schools spend about $7,300 per student each year. Mary, Peter, and Lydia, parents of children in Bethany Flats, bring an Equal Protection challenge. They argue that the funding plan denies their children an equal public education. The state responds that the plan preserves local control of schools and rationally connects local tax revenue to local school spending.

Under Equal Protection analysis, which standard of review applies, and how should the court likely rule?', 'Under Equal Protection analysis, which standard of review applies, and how should the court likely rule?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19607.md","source_question_id":"19607","variant_slug":"cana_hills_bethany_flats","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"44040305","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard anchor","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A state public-school funding plan creates large district spending gaps because local property-tax bases differ. Parents in the lower-funded district sue under Equal Protection. What review applies, and does the plan likely survive?","review_truth":"School-funding disparities tied to local property-tax wealth get rational basis review under federal Equal Protection unless a real suspect-class, quasi-suspect-class, or fundamental-right trigger is present.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-TIER-TRIGGER-01","title":"Tier trigger before tier label","signal":"Answer names strict or intermediate scrutiny.","student_move":"Ask what trigger the answer supplies.","tiny_rule":"No valid trigger means default rational basis.","trap":"Picking the strongest-sounding tier because the facts feel unfair.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONLAW-SCHOOL-FUNDING-01","statement":"Public education is not a federal fundamental right, and poverty/local district wealth is not a suspect class for school-funding disparities; rational basis applies.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"public-school funding gap caused by local property-tax bases","authority":"San Antonio Independent School District v. Rodriguez, 411 U.S. 1 (1973)","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONLAW-DISPARATE-IMPACT-01","statement":"A facially neutral policy’s racial disparate impact does not by itself trigger heightened Equal Protection scrutiny; look for discriminatory purpose or a racial classification.","type":"threshold","trigger":"neutral funding formula plus racial overlap language","authority":"Washington v. Davis, 426 U.S. 229 (1976)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONLAW-SCHOOL-FUNDING-01","statement":"In a scrutiny-tier array, read each heightened-review answer by its trigger, not by how morally serious the inequality feels.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Strict / intermediate / rational-basis answer spread","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"school-funding tier trigger","prompt":"A local-property-tax public-school funding plan creates richer and poorer districts. Is education a federal fundamental right for strict scrutiny?","answer":"No. Rational basis applies under the school-funding Gold Key."},{"drill_type":"Silver Key answer-array scan","target_skill":"tier trigger before tier label","prompt":"Three choices name strict or intermediate scrutiny and one names rational basis. What is the first move?","answer":"Check the trigger each heightened-review choice names."},{"drill_type":"Disparate-impact threshold","target_skill":"impact vs purpose","prompt":"A neutral public policy disproportionately affects racial-minority communities. Does impact alone trigger heightened Equal Protection scrutiny?","answer":"No. Look for discriminatory purpose or a racial classification."}]}'),
  ('f4e3156d-a251-589d-bf50-78621933b904', '19721_jonah_whale_float', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Motions', 'Motions to dismiss', 'claim sufficiency vs amount-in-controversy / notice pleading distractions', 'Mary, a freelance scenic painter and citizen of State F, sued MannaJoy Props, Inc., a company incorporated in State G with its principal place of business in State H, in federal district court. Mary demanded $130,000 for false imprisonment. Her complaint stated that “Mary was shut inside the whale-belly compartment of a Jonah parade float from late cleanup until sunrise when a MannaJoy technician negligently latched the service hatch without realizing Mary was still painting inside.” Mary suffered no physical harm from being in the float. MannaJoy filed a motion to dismiss. How should the court rule?', 'Mary, a freelance scenic painter and citizen of State F, sued MannaJoy Props, Inc., a company incorporated in State G with its principal place of business in State H, in federal district court. Mary demanded $130,000 for false imprisonment. Her complaint stated that “Mary was shut inside the whale-belly compartment of a Jonah parade float from late cleanup until sunrise when a MannaJoy technician negligently latched the service hatch without realizing Mary was still painting inside.” Mary suffered no physical harm from being in the float. MannaJoy filed a motion to dismiss. How should the court rule?

How should the court rule?', 'How should the court rule?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19721.md","source_question_id":"19721","variant_slug":"jonah_whale_float","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"92080104","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A federal complaint claims false imprisonment but pleads negligent confinement. On a motion to dismiss, should the court dismiss for failure to state a claim?","review_truth":"A motion-to-dismiss answer must match the defect in the complaint; negligent confinement does not state false imprisonment.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CIV-12B6-INTENT-01","title":"Complaint label vs complaint facts","signal":"Claim label says an intentional tort, but the pleaded facts say negligence.","student_move":"Use the claim label and the pleaded fact together; do not stop at notice.","tiny_rule":"A complaint can defeat itself by pleading facts inconsistent with an element of the selected claim.","trap":"Notice pleading or amount-in-controversy distractors.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-JONAH-WHALE-FLOAT-01","statement":"False imprisonment requires intentional confinement. A complaint that pleads only negligent confinement defeats that claim at the motion-to-dismiss stage.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Complaint labels false imprisonment but says the confinement happened negligently.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §35; Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(b)(6); Ashcroft v. Iqbal.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-JONAH-WHALE-FLOAT-02","statement":"Amount in controversy is not a likely-verdict test. The pleaded amount controls unless it is legally certain the claim is below the jurisdictional threshold.","type":"threshold","trigger":"Answer choice talks about unlikely recovery above $75,000.","authority":"28 U.S.C. §1332; St. Paul Mercury Indem. Co. v. Red Cab Co.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-JONAH-WHALE-FLOAT-01","statement":"On a motion-to-dismiss array, match the reason to the motion. Same outcome plus wrong procedural reason is a trap.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"Two choices talk about amount in controversy while one choice talks about failure to state a claim.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"same outcome wrong reason","prompt":"A motion should be granted. One answer grants on the wrong jurisdictional reason; another grants on failure to state a claim. Which survives?","answer":"The answer with the correct motion ground survives."},{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"false imprisonment intent","prompt":"Complaint says false imprisonment but pleads accidental or negligent confinement. Does it state false imprisonment?","answer":"No. False imprisonment requires intentional confinement."},{"drill_type":"Issue-Sense","target_skill":"notice pleading lure","prompt":"Complaint gives notice but pleads facts defeating one element. Is notice enough?","answer":"No. Notice does not cure facts that defeat the claim."},{"drill_type":"Anchor contrast","target_skill":"amount-in-controversy legal-certainty rule","prompt":"Is federal amount in controversy defeated merely because recovery over $75,000 is unlikely?","answer":"No. The likely recovery is not the standard."}]}'),
  ('b02ac29a-e5bf-5b67-8343-809e2e5a20c2', '19793_dove_candle_ledgers', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Appeals and Finality', 'Collateral Order Narrowness', 'review now vs review after final judgment', 'Mary sued Peter in federal district court over a private contract for dove-shaped Christmas Eve candles sold through Peter’s Light of the World craft booth. During discovery, the judge ordered Peter to produce nonprivileged wax-batch logs, shipping receipts, and supplier invoices stored in a rented warehouse. Peter says paying workers to locate, box, and scan the papers will be expensive, so he seeks an immediate appeal under the collateral order doctrine.', 'Mary sued Peter in federal district court over a private contract for dove-shaped Christmas Eve candles sold through Peter’s Light of the World craft booth. During discovery, the judge ordered Peter to produce nonprivileged wax-batch logs, shipping receipts, and supplier invoices stored in a rented warehouse. Peter says paying workers to locate, box, and scan the papers will be expensive, so he seeks an immediate appeal under the collateral order doctrine.

Is the court of appeals likely to hear the appeal now?', 'Is the court of appeals likely to hear the appeal now?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19793.md","source_question_id":"19793","variant_slug":"dove_candle_ledgers","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"93110101","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A party is ordered to produce ordinary nonprivileged documents in discovery and says compliance is expensive. Is that discovery order immediately appealable under the collateral order doctrine?","review_truth":"Ordinary discovery orders generally wait for final-judgment review; cost does not make them collateral orders.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-COLLATERAL-ORDER-NARROWNESS-01","title":"Finality Gate: Ordinary Discovery Waits","signal":"Immediate appeal from a discovery order.","student_move":"Treat cost as bait; check whether later review is enough.","tiny_rule":"Collateral order is narrow; ordinary discovery orders usually wait.","trap":"Turning expense or separateness into immediate appeal.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-COLLATERAL-ORDER-NARROWNESS-01","statement":"Collateral-order review is narrow. Ordinary discovery orders generally wait for final judgment; expense alone is not effective unreviewability.","type":"threshold","trigger":"Immediate appeal from a discovery order under the collateral order doctrine.","authority":"28 U.S.C. §1291; Cohen; Mohawk; Cunningham.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-DOVE-CANDLE-LEDGERS-01","statement":"In an appealability array, sort answers as review-now or review-later. Cut the extreme words first, then ask whether final judgment review is enough.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Immediate appeal + discovery order + collateral order doctrine.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim absolutes","prompt":"Classify: ''An expensive discovery order automatically qualifies as a collateral order.''","answer":"NOT_TRUE; tiered_absolute; cost is not automatic appealability."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"collateral order narrowness","prompt":"A party must produce ordinary nonprivileged records and wants immediate appeal because production is costly. Appeal now?","answer":"No. Ordinary discovery waits for final judgment."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"review now vs review later","prompt":"Two choices say yes-now, two choices say no-now. What axis should you name?","answer":"Review now vs review after final judgment."},{"drill_type":"recovery","target_skill":"one-prong bait","prompt":"Why is ''separate from the merits'' not enough?","answer":"It is only one collateral-order requirement."}]}'),
  ('df8697a4-1b71-5eb3-a89b-77bb3ebb3166', '19840_red_sea_bridge_bolts', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Discovery', 'Attorney-Client Privilege', 'fact itself vs communication about the fact', 'At Lydia''s privately owned Christian bookstore, Ruth was injured when a portable wooden "Red Sea crossing" bridge used for a paid Bible-story craft night shifted under her. Ruth sues Lydia in federal court for negligence. In an interrogatory, Ruth asks Lydia to identify who tightened the bridge''s bolts earlier that day. Lydia refuses, saying the answer is privileged because she gave that name to her lawyer during a confidential meeting seeking legal advice.', 'At Lydia''s privately owned Christian bookstore, Ruth was injured when a portable wooden "Red Sea crossing" bridge used for a paid Bible-story craft night shifted under her. Ruth sues Lydia in federal court for negligence. In an interrogatory, Ruth asks Lydia to identify who tightened the bridge''s bolts earlier that day. Lydia refuses, saying the answer is privileged because she gave that name to her lawyer during a confidential meeting seeking legal advice.

Is the identity of the person who tightened the bolts protected from discovery on that ground?', 'Is the identity of the person who tightened the bolts protected from discovery on that ground?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19840.md","source_question_id":"19840","variant_slug":"red_sea_bridge_bolts","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"95070405","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Can a party refuse to identify a relevant fact in an interrogatory just because the party told that fact to a lawyer while seeking legal advice?","review_truth":"Privilege can protect the lawyer conversation, but it does not hide the underlying fact.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-DISC-PRIV-01","title":"Fact, not chat","signal":"Discovery asks for a fact; privilege objection points to a lawyer conversation.","student_move":"Separate the underlying fact from the communication that reported it.","tiny_rule":"Facts do not become privileged by being told to counsel.","trap":"Every fact discussed with a lawyer becomes privileged.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-UNDERLYING-FACTS-01","statement":"Attorney-client privilege protects confidential legal-advice communications, not the underlying facts. A fact does not become privileged just because the client told it to a lawyer.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The discovery request asks for a fact, but the refusal says the fact was told to counsel.","authority":"Upjohn Co. v. United States, 449 U.S. 383 (1981); Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(1); Fed. R. Civ. P. 33(a)(2).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-FACT-NOT-CHAT-01","statement":"Before reading privilege choices, name the object of discovery. If the other side asks for the fact itself, do not let an answer switch you to the lawyer conversation.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"Interrogatory asks for an identity or event fact; objection says the fact was told to counsel.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"Overclaim detection","prompt":"A choice says every fact discussed with counsel becomes privileged. Name the mold.","answer":"tiered_absolute"},{"drill_type":"ISSUE_SENSE","target_skill":"Wrong-frame detection","prompt":"A privilege choice turns on whether the lawyer later repeated the fact in court, but the stem asks whether the fact was privileged in the first place. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut as NOT_RESPONSIVE / bait_doctrine."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"Underlying facts rule","prompt":"A client tells a lawyer the name of a witness. Can the client refuse to disclose the witness''s name solely for that reason?","answer":"No. The fact is not privileged merely because it was communicated to counsel."},{"drill_type":"CALIBRATION","target_skill":"Residual after three named cuts","prompt":"You cut every-fact, never-civil, and wrong-frame waiver. What confidence band?","answer":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED high; commit after read-check."}]}'),
  ('72e5bbb0-ceb0-5ae2-b29d-0de3882c03a2', '19904_psalm_orchard_foreclosure_notice', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Mortgages', 'Foreclosure - parties and priorities', 'process threshold versus sale-scope merits', 'Ruth owned a private orchard cottage where she made Scripture memory cards for sale at weekend markets. To buy a new printing press, Ruth borrowed $74,000 from Barnabas and gave Barnabas a promissory note and a mortgage on the orchard parcel. Barnabas immediately and properly recorded the mortgage. Six weeks later, Ruth borrowed $41,000 from Lydia and gave Lydia a promissory note and a mortgage on the same parcel. Lydia immediately and properly recorded the mortgage. Ruth died soon afterward. Her will left the orchard parcel to Stephen for life, then to Mary. Stephen moved into the cottage. Neither Barnabas nor Lydia received the payments due on their notes. Without notifying or serving Stephen or Mary, Barnabas asks a court to order a foreclosure sale of the orchard parcel.', 'Ruth owned a private orchard cottage where she made Scripture memory cards for sale at weekend markets. To buy a new printing press, Ruth borrowed $74,000 from Barnabas and gave Barnabas a promissory note and a mortgage on the orchard parcel. Barnabas immediately and properly recorded the mortgage. Six weeks later, Ruth borrowed $41,000 from Lydia and gave Lydia a promissory note and a mortgage on the same parcel. Lydia immediately and properly recorded the mortgage. Ruth died soon afterward. Her will left the orchard parcel to Stephen for life, then to Mary. Stephen moved into the cottage. Neither Barnabas nor Lydia received the payments due on their notes. Without notifying or serving Stephen or Mary, Barnabas asks a court to order a foreclosure sale of the orchard parcel.

The court should:', 'The court should:', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19904.md","source_question_id":"19904","variant_slug":"psalm_orchard_foreclosure_notice","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"83050603","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A senior mortgagee seeks judicial foreclosure without notifying or serving the life tenant or remainderman. Should the court order a sale or deny the request?","review_truth":"Foreclosure sale waits for process: present and future interest holders must receive notice before their interests can be cut off.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-RP-FORECLOSURE-NOTICE-01","title":"Foreclosure: bring interest holders into court","signal":"Foreclosure request + life estate/remainder + no notice/service","student_move":"Cut every sale-scope answer until the service threshold is satisfied.","tiny_rule":"No notice to affected estate holders, no sale order.","trap":"Choosing the estate that can be sold instead of the process answer.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-REAL_PROPERTY-FORECLOSURE-NOTICE-01","statement":"In judicial foreclosure, holders of present or future property interests that may be cut off by the sale must receive notice and be made parties before the court orders the sale.","type":"threshold","trigger":"Foreclosure requested without notice or service on a life tenant or remainderman.","authority":"Mullane v. Central Hanover Bank & Trust Co.; Mennonite Board of Missions v. Adams.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-REAL_PROPERTY-FORECLOSURE-NOTICE-01","statement":"When a foreclosure stem says the lender did not notify or serve the estate holders, lock the call as process first and sale scope second.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"without notifying or serving","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"procedural_frame","prompt":"A foreclosure stem says the lender did not notify the life tenant or remainderman. Three choices order a sale. What is the first cut?","answer":"Cut the sale-scope choices as premature; choose the process answer."},{"drill_type":"CALL","target_skill":"threshold before merits","prompt":"Which comes first in foreclosure: identifying the estate to sell or serving the holders of affected estates?","answer":"Serving the affected estate holders."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"foreclosure necessary parties","prompt":"Who must receive notice when a judicial foreclosure may cut off a life estate and remainder?","answer":"Both the life tenant and the remainderman."}]}'),
  ('623874e0-d6be-54d0-8302-65aded0e9261', '19982_manna_warehouse', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Federal Powers', 'Spending conditions', NULL, 'After Lydia, a state emergency-services director, signed up for a federal Good Samaritan pantry-vans grant program, the federal relief agency announced a new requirement: every participating state must build refrigerated ''manna warehouses'' beside the vans. The statute authorizing the grant had said only that a state receiving the money must obey ''whatever later requirements the agency finds fitting.'' The warehouse requirement was not listed in the statute when the state took the money.', 'After Lydia, a state emergency-services director, signed up for a federal Good Samaritan pantry-vans grant program, the federal relief agency announced a new requirement: every participating state must build refrigerated ''manna warehouses'' beside the vans. The statute authorizing the grant had said only that a state receiving the money must obey ''whatever later requirements the agency finds fitting.'' The warehouse requirement was not listed in the statute when the state took the money.

What is the state''s strongest Spending Clause objection?', 'What is the state''s strongest Spending Clause objection?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19982.md","source_question_id":"19982","variant_slug":"manna_warehouse","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium_anchor","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A state accepts federal grant money under a statute reserving unspecified future agency conditions, and the agency later adds a costly condition. What is the Spending Clause objection?","review_truth":"Spending Clause grants need clear terms before the state accepts; hidden future conditions are the trap.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REMED-CONLAW-SPENDING-CLEAR-NOTICE","title":"Hidden grant terms","signal":"Federal money plus states plus later undisclosed condition.","student_move":"Ask whether the state could know the condition before accepting.","tiny_rule":"No clear notice, no knowing acceptance.","trap":"Treating federal spending as a blank check.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONLAW-SPENDING-CLEAR-NOTICE-01","statement":"Spending Clause conditions must be stated clearly enough that a state can knowingly decide whether to accept the money and the obligations. A blank-check future-condition clause is the red flag.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"state accepts federal money before the concrete condition appears","authority":"Pennhurst State School & Hospital v. Halderman; South Dakota v. Dole; Arlington Central School District Board of Education v. Murphy.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONLAW-MANNA-WAREHOUSE-01","statement":"When the call asks for the state’s Spending Clause objection, hunt for the answer that attacks the condition’s clarity at the time of acceptance.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"state took grant money before the costly condition was listed","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"Spending Clause clear notice","prompt":"A state accepts grant money before a concrete condition is stated. What objection should fire?","answer":"No clear notice for knowing acceptance."},{"drill_type":"ear_falsity","target_skill":"backwards rule","prompt":"Classify: Congress may add any undisclosed condition after a state accepts federal funds.","answer":"NOT_TRUE; backwards."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"call lock","prompt":"The call asks for a Spending Clause objection. Which facts matter most?","answer":"State acceptance, unclear condition, later costly mandate."}]}'),
  ('5944fabb-fcf2-50fe-8db9-2f05e986ff7d', '20004_dove_tokens_manna_game', 'CONTRACTS', 'Gap-Fillers, Interpretation, and the Parol Evidence Rule', 'IV', NULL, 'Stephen sued Ruth for the profit he expected from 72,000 wooden dove tokens. The written twelve-month deal had said that Ruth''s private Christian board-game company would buy from Stephen''s woodworking shop ''all wooden dove tokens required for the Manna Trail game line'' and listed 72,000 tokens as an estimate. Five months into the deal, a new child-safety rule made that small-token board-game line unprofitable. Ruth permanently canceled the line and bought no wooden dove tokens from any other source for it.', 'Stephen sued Ruth for the profit he expected from 72,000 wooden dove tokens. The written twelve-month deal had said that Ruth''s private Christian board-game company would buy from Stephen''s woodworking shop ''all wooden dove tokens required for the Manna Trail game line'' and listed 72,000 tokens as an estimate. Five months into the deal, a new child-safety rule made that small-token board-game line unprofitable. Ruth permanently canceled the line and bought no wooden dove tokens from any other source for it.

Which statement best states the parties'' rights?', 'Which statement best states the parties'' rights?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20004.md","source_question_id":"20004","variant_slug":"dove_tokens_manna_game","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020203","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"trap repair","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A buyer in a requirements contract estimated a quantity, then in good faith shut down the covered product line and bought none elsewhere. Does the estimate force a minimum purchase?","review_truth":"A requirements-contract estimate is not a purchase floor; good-faith zero requirements can be zero.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CON-REQ-01","title":"Requirements estimate is not a floor","signal":"all requirements + estimate + shutdown","student_move":"Stay in the quantity/good-faith lane before considering excuse or writing rules.","tiny_rule":"Estimate guides policing; it is not automatically a minimum.","trap":"Treating the estimate as the quantity promised.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-REQUIREMENTS-SHUTDOWN-01","statement":"In a requirements contract, a stated estimate is not a fixed minimum. Actual good-faith requirements can be zero if the buyer truly shuts down the covered operation and does not shift the same requirements elsewhere.","type":"rule","trigger":"all requirements + stated estimate + covered line shuts down + no substitute purchases","authority":"UCC 2-306(1); official-comment material on good-faith discontinuance.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-REQUIREMENTS-LANE-01","statement":"When the stem says ''all requirements'' plus an estimate, first ask whether the buyer still had covered requirements. Cut answers that jump to fixed quantity, excuse, or parol-evidence frames.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"requirements term with estimate and later shutdown","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"requirements-contract estimate","prompt":"A buyer promises to buy all requirements for one product line, estimates 40,000 units, then shuts that line down in good faith and buys none elsewhere. Is 40,000 a minimum?","answer":"No. The estimate is not automatically a floor; actual good-faith requirements may be zero."},{"drill_type":"wrong_frame_cut","target_skill":"excuse-doctrine drift","prompt":"The answer says the buyer wins only if it proves frustration. The stem asks about actual requirements after a covered-line shutdown. What is the C3 label?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE / bait_doctrine / wrong_frame."},{"drill_type":"falsity_direction","target_skill":"parol evidence timing","prompt":"A later shutdown fact is offered to measure actual requirements. Does the parol evidence rule bar it as varying the estimate?","answer":"No. That is a backwards timing use of the parol-evidence rule."}]}'),
  ('98908f9c-4937-5dfd-8db1-50868c24d0aa', '20024_loaves_fishes_crate_count', 'TORTS', 'Negligence damages', 'Negligent misrepresentation and economic loss', 'open-ended foreseeability vs known/limited class for a specific transaction', 'At a for-profit Bible-camp supply expo, Martha sold off the remaining stock from her “Loaves & Fishes” craft-kit shop. Weeks earlier, Lydia, a professional inventory consultant, had prepared a crate-count sheet solely so Martha could plan warehouse space and packing for the expo. The sheet mistakenly listed 900 sealed craft kits instead of 90. Lydia was not told that the sheet would be shown to buyers or used in any sale. Martha later slipped a copy of the sheet into a sale packet on her booth table. Timothy, who had never dealt with Lydia, used the sheet to buy the remaining kits for his own resale booth and lost money when the count proved wrong.', 'At a for-profit Bible-camp supply expo, Martha sold off the remaining stock from her “Loaves & Fishes” craft-kit shop. Weeks earlier, Lydia, a professional inventory consultant, had prepared a crate-count sheet solely so Martha could plan warehouse space and packing for the expo. The sheet mistakenly listed 900 sealed craft kits instead of 90. Lydia was not told that the sheet would be shown to buyers or used in any sale. Martha later slipped a copy of the sheet into a sale packet on her booth table. Timothy, who had never dealt with Lydia, used the sheet to buy the remaining kits for his own resale booth and lost money when the count proved wrong.

In Timothy''s negligent-misrepresentation claim against Lydia, how should the court rule?', 'In Timothy''s negligent-misrepresentation claim against Lydia, how should the court rule?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20024.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"loaves_fishes_crate_count","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"The count sheet was made only for Martha''s internal planning, and Lydia was not told it would be used for buyers or any sale.","distilled_core_question":"A professional prepared an inaccurate count sheet only for a client’s internal planning. An unknown later buyer relied on a copy in a sale packet and lost money. Is the professional liable to that buyer for negligent misrepresentation?","review_truth":"For negligent misrepresentation causing economic loss, do not stop at foreseeable reader; ask whether the plaintiff was in the known or limited group for the transaction the information was meant to guide.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-NMISREP-01","title":"Foreseeability is not the whole duty gate.","signal":"Professional information + economic loss + unknown later reader.","student_move":"Ask: targeted person/class and targeted transaction?","tiny_rule":"Foreseeability alone loses to targeted-use duty.","trap":"General negligence reflex.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-LIMITED-CLASS-01","statement":"For negligent misrepresentation by a professional information supplier, economic-loss duty is limited to a known person or limited group for a specific transaction or substantially similar transaction. It does not run to every foreseeable later reader.","type":"threshold","trigger":"professional report, economic loss, unknown third-party reader","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 552; Bily v. Arthur Young & Co.; Ultramares Corp. v. Touche.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-TARGETED-USE-01","statement":"When one finalist says ''foreseeable reader'' and another says ''known or limited class for a transaction,'' name the axis as targeted-use duty, not general foreseeability.","type":"clash","trigger":"unknown later reader relying on a client-republished report","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"No, because pure economic loss is never recoverable for negligent misrepresentation.","answer":"CUT: tiered_absolute / NOT_TRUE."},{"drill_type":"CLASH","target_skill":"targeted-use axis","prompt":"Foreseeable later reader vs known/limited class for a transaction. Which fact decides?","answer":"Whether the professional supplied the information for that person/class and transaction."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"Gold Key recall","prompt":"Professional report, economic loss, unknown third-party reader. What tiny rule?","answer":"Duty is limited to a known or limited group for a specific transaction; foreseeability alone is not enough."}]}'),
  ('c935f630-8cfc-57f8-9db0-c24b10a258fa', '20100_fish_and_loaves_keychains', 'CONTRACTS', 'Statute of Frauds', 'D', 'alleged quantity versus admitted quantity', 'Barnabas operates a private online shop that sells glow-in-the-dark ''fish and loaves'' keychains to Christian retreat organizers. He sues Lydia, alleging that she orally agreed to buy 2,400 keychains for a Bible-quiz tournament at a total price over $500. No signed writing exists. At trial, Lydia testifies, ''I did agree to buy keychains, but 750 of them, not 2,400.'' Barnabas asks the court to enforce the alleged deal for all 2,400 keychains.', 'Barnabas operates a private online shop that sells glow-in-the-dark ''fish and loaves'' keychains to Christian retreat organizers. He sues Lydia, alleging that she orally agreed to buy 2,400 keychains for a Bible-quiz tournament at a total price over $500. No signed writing exists. At trial, Lydia testifies, ''I did agree to buy keychains, but 750 of them, not 2,400.'' Barnabas asks the court to enforce the alleged deal for all 2,400 keychains.

What is the strongest Statute of Frauds result?', 'What is the strongest Statute of Frauds result?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20100.md","source_question_id":"20100","variant_slug":"fish_and_loaves_keychains","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"discriminator","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"No signed writing. Buyer admits in court to buying 750 goods, not the seller''s alleged 2,400. How much is enforceable under the UCC Statute of Frauds admission exception?","review_truth":"Under UCC 2-201(3)(b), an in-court admission can satisfy the Statute of Frauds only up to the quantity admitted.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-SOF-ADMISSION-QUANTITY-CAP","title":"Admission exception quantity cap","signal":"No writing plus in-court admission of a smaller quantity.","student_move":"Lock the admitted quantity before evaluating all-or-none answers.","tiny_rule":"Admission opens enforcement only up to the quantity admitted.","trap":"Some admitted contract means all alleged goods are enforceable.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-FISH-KEYCHAINS-01","statement":"For a UCC sale-of-goods oral contract, an in-court admission can satisfy the Statute of Frauds, but only for the quantity the party admits.","type":"exception","trigger":"No signed writing plus testimony admitting a smaller quantity than the seller alleges.","authority":"UCC 2-201(3)(b)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-FISH-KEYCHAINS-01","statement":"When the answers fight over all, none, and only the admitted amount, circle the exact admitted number first; the right answer usually sits on that number.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"A party admits one quantity but denies the larger alleged quantity.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"number_lock","target_skill":"Gold Key recall","prompt":"No writing. Buyer admits 300 units but denies 900. How many units are enforceable under the UCC admission exception?","answer":"300"},{"drill_type":"mold_identification","target_skill":"extreme_of_range","prompt":"Buyer admits 300 but seller seeks 900. Choice says all 900 because buyer admitted a contract existed. Name the mold.","answer":"extreme_of_range"},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"all/none/admitted-number scan","prompt":"Choices say all, none, market reasonable, or admitted amount. What number do you lock first?","answer":"The quantity admitted in court."}]}'),
  ('b5c7682e-9942-5089-b41f-e2efb3777e56', '20123_prayer_card_threat_writers', 'TORTS', 'Privacy', 'False light and constitutional limits', NULL, 'A Christian streaming channel released a public-concern episode about anonymous threat letters sent to several disaster-relief centers. In the episode’s opening graphic, the channel showed a close-up of a handwritten prayer card signed by Hannah under the words, “Threat writers from the mercy team.” Before release, the producers had received the graphic designer’s note stating that Hannah’s card was only a thank-you note from a volunteer lunch and that Hannah had not written any threat letter. The producers used the graphic anyway. Hannah sues for false light.', 'A Christian streaming channel released a public-concern episode about anonymous threat letters sent to several disaster-relief centers. In the episode’s opening graphic, the channel showed a close-up of a handwritten prayer card signed by Hannah under the words, “Threat writers from the mercy team.” Before release, the producers had received the graphic designer’s note stating that Hannah’s card was only a thank-you note from a volunteer lunch and that Hannah had not written any threat letter. The producers used the graphic anyway. Hannah sues for false light.

What mental-state showing most directly supports the claim?', 'What mental-state showing most directly supports the claim?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20123.md","source_question_id":"20123","variant_slug":"prayer_card_threat_writers","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62050402","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A public-concern false-light publication falsely implies a volunteer wrote threats, and the publisher had a note contradicting that implication before publication. What mental-state showing supports the claim?","review_truth":"Public-concern false light points to actual malice when the publisher had facts showing the implication was false and published anyway.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-FALSE-LIGHT-FAULT-01","title":"False-light public concern: fault before feelings","signal":"False-light claim plus public-concern publication.","student_move":"Lock the call. If it asks mental state, find the publisher''s knowledge or serious doubt.","tiny_rule":"Actual malice is knowledge of falsity or reckless disregard.","trap":"Mistaking embarrassment, special damages, or misleading graphics for the required mental-state showing.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-PUBLIC-FALSE-LIGHT-01","statement":"Public-concern false light points to actual malice: the publisher knew the false implication was false or recklessly disregarded that risk. Strict liability is the trap.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"false-light claim + public-concern publication + pre-publication contradiction","authority":"Time, Inc. v. Hill, 385 U.S. 374 (1967); New York Times Co. v. Sullivan, 376 U.S. 254 (1964)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-PRAYER-CARD-01","statement":"On a mental-state call, cut choices that describe harm, remedy, tort category, or no-fault liability before judging doctrine.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"call asks ''what mental-state showing''","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"mental-state call focus","prompt":"A false-light call asks for mental state. Choice says the plaintiff was humiliated. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut: harm, not mental state."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"public-concern false-light fault","prompt":"Public-concern false light; publisher had contrary note and published anyway. What fault phrase?","answer":"Actual malice."},{"drill_type":"TRAP_SPOTTING","target_skill":"strict liability shortcut","prompt":"Choice says strict liability because the graphic misled readers. What trap?","answer":"No-fault shortcut; public-concern false light needs constitutional fault."}]}'),
  ('7aca671e-e9cb-543b-a50f-66dd5eb7822e', '20127_jericho_trumpet', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Fifth Amendment', 'Constitutional Protection of Accused Persons', 'violation layer vs remedy layer; statement evidence vs physical evidence', 'At Paul''s theft trial, the prosecutor plans to introduce a gilded ram''s-horn trumpet prop recovered from a private rehearsal studio. The recovery traced back to police questioning: after Lydia reported that the trumpet had been taken from her Bible-themed escape-room booth, officers arrested Paul and asked him about the item before giving Miranda warnings. Paul voluntarily said, "It is inside the hollow lectern we use for the Esther skit at my rehearsal studio." Using that statement, officers obtained a valid search warrant for the studio and found the trumpet. The prosecution does not offer Paul''s statement, only the trumpet.', 'At Paul''s theft trial, the prosecutor plans to introduce a gilded ram''s-horn trumpet prop recovered from a private rehearsal studio. The recovery traced back to police questioning: after Lydia reported that the trumpet had been taken from her Bible-themed escape-room booth, officers arrested Paul and asked him about the item before giving Miranda warnings. Paul voluntarily said, "It is inside the hollow lectern we use for the Esther skit at my rehearsal studio." Using that statement, officers obtained a valid search warrant for the studio and found the trumpet. The prosecution does not offer Paul''s statement, only the trumpet.

Should the trumpet be suppressed as fruit of the Miranda violation?', 'Should the trumpet be suppressed as fruit of the Miranda violation?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20127.md","source_question_id":"20127","variant_slug":"jericho_trumpet","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"75080101","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Police use a voluntary unwarned statement to find physical evidence and offer only the physical evidence at trial. Suppress?","review_truth":"Miranda can suppress the voluntary unwarned statement, but not the physical object found because of it.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CRIM-MIRANDA-FRUIT-01","title":"Miranda Fruit Is Not Fourth-Amendment Fruit","signal":"Unwarned but voluntary statement leads police to a physical object.","student_move":"Pin the offered evidence: statement or object.","tiny_rule":"Statement out; voluntary-statement physical fruit generally in.","trap":"Importing all-fruits suppression from Fourth Amendment doctrine.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIM-MIRANDA-FRUIT-01","statement":"A voluntary unwarned statement is generally kept out of the prosecution''s case-in-chief, but physical evidence discovered from that statement need not be suppressed.","type":"exception","trigger":"The prosecution offers a physical object found through a voluntary unwarned statement.","authority":"United States v. Patane, 542 U.S. 630 (2004)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIM-MIRANDA-REMEDY-LAYER-01","statement":"Separate the warning violation from the suppression remedy: first ask what evidence the prosecution is offering.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The call says ''suppressed as fruit'' and the prosecution offers only a physical object.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"Miranda physical-fruit distinction","prompt":"Police get a voluntary unwarned statement naming a weapon location and offer only the weapon. Suppress?","answer":"No, not solely on Miranda grounds."},{"drill_type":"bait_architecture","target_skill":"violation_vs_remedy","prompt":"The call asks whether evidence is suppressed after a violation. What must you identify first?","answer":"The evidence being offered and the remedy rule."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Classify: all evidence derived from an unwarned statement is suppressed.","answer":"NOT_TRUE; tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"backwards_cut","target_skill":"voluntary does not erase Miranda","prompt":"Classify: voluntary means there was no Miranda violation.","answer":"NOT_TRUE; backwards."}]}'),
  ('8a1a1119-9877-5077-9322-c649dd57a4d3', '20153_lampstand_labs', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Individual Rights', 'First Amendment Protections - Freedom of religion', 'automatic invalidity of aid to religious schools vs neutral secular-use aid', 'Congress enacts the Lampstand Labs Act, which authorizes federal tax funds to buy robotics kits, digital microscopes, and portable planetarium projectors for qualifying private schools. The kits are available on identical terms to private schools with religious missions and to secular private schools. The Act states that every donated kit must be used only for secular instruction and secular academic activities. Mary and Peter, federal taxpayers whose children attend public schools in the state, sue in federal court for an order enjoining the federal government from spending tax funds on kits that will go to Christian and other religious private schools. They argue that the spending is unconstitutional. Should the court issue the requested injunction?', 'Congress enacts the Lampstand Labs Act, which authorizes federal tax funds to buy robotics kits, digital microscopes, and portable planetarium projectors for qualifying private schools. The kits are available on identical terms to private schools with religious missions and to secular private schools. The Act states that every donated kit must be used only for secular instruction and secular academic activities. Mary and Peter, federal taxpayers whose children attend public schools in the state, sue in federal court for an order enjoining the federal government from spending tax funds on kits that will go to Christian and other religious private schools. They argue that the spending is unconstitutional. Should the court issue the requested injunction?

Should the court issue the requested injunction?', 'Should the court issue the requested injunction?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20153.md","source_question_id":"20153","variant_slug":"lampstand_labs","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"44040501","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard anchor item","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Congress funds secular-use educational equipment for religious and nonreligious private schools on equal terms. Federal taxpayer-parents seek an injunction. Should the court stop the spending?","review_truth":"Neutral, secular-use educational aid available to religious and secular schools on equal terms is not automatically an Establishment Clause violation.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-LAMPSTAND-01","title":"Neutral aid / secular-use restriction","signal":"Religious and secular schools get the same educational aid, and the statute limits use to secular instruction.","student_move":"Do not make religious-school aid per se unconstitutional. Look for neutrality and use restriction.","tiny_rule":"Neutral + secular-use school aid usually defeats the per se Establishment Clause trap.","trap":"Public money plus religious school feels automatically invalid.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONLAW-LAMPSTAND-01","statement":"Neutral educational aid available to religious and secular private schools on equal terms, restricted to secular use, is not automatically an Establishment Clause violation.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"Public funds buy school equipment for religious and nonreligious schools on identical terms, with secular-use limits.","authority":"Mitchell v. Helms, 530 U.S. 793 (2000)","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONLAW-LAMPSTAND-02","statement":"Federal taxpayers can have standing to challenge congressional taxing-and-spending measures alleged to violate the Establishment Clause.","type":"threshold","trigger":"Federal taxpayers sue to stop congressional spending on Establishment Clause grounds.","authority":"Flast v. Cohen, 392 U.S. 83 (1968)","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONLAW-LAMPSTAND-03","statement":"Congress is not categorically barred from subsidizing education; the taxing-and-spending power reaches general-welfare education spending.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"Answer says education is solely for states.","authority":"U.S. Const. art. I, sec. 8, cl. 1","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONLAW-LAMPSTAND-01","statement":"In a 2-yes / 2-no constitutional array, do not pick by result. Grade the reason after because; same result plus false reason still dies.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Two choices say No but only one uses the statute''s secular-use fact.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"neutral aid","prompt":"A federal program gives secular-use science equipment to religious and secular private schools on equal terms. Automatic Establishment Clause violation?","answer":"No. Neutral + secular-use aid is not automatically invalid."},{"drill_type":"answer-array read","target_skill":"same-result wrong-reason","prompt":"Two choices say No. One says no standing; one says secular-use restriction. What do you check?","answer":"Grade the because; same result is not enough."},{"drill_type":"overclaim detector","target_skill":"states-only language","prompt":"Answer says education is solely a state function. What is the C3 signal?","answer":"Solely is the overclaim signal; check spending-power anchor."},{"drill_type":"threshold trap","target_skill":"Flast standing","prompt":"Federal taxpayers challenge congressional spending as violating the Establishment Clause. Standing absent?","answer":"No. Flast supplies the taxpayer-standing anchor."}]}'),
  ('1d311504-36ec-57f8-933a-a96fe30b646c', '20186_road_to_emmaus_royalty_ledgers', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Separation of Powers', 'Congressional subpoenas', 'automatic outcome vs calibrated review', 'Before taking office, President Daniel licensed a scripture-themed family board game, Road to Emmaus, and a devotional planner to Lydia''s Lamp Games LLC. A House committee, saying it is studying legislation requiring federal officials to disclose outside royalty streams and business entanglements, subpoenas the company for twelve years of Daniel''s licensing agreements, royalty statements, vendor invoices, and payment ledgers. The subpoena is sweeping. Daniel objects that the committee mainly wants to parade private details from a Christian-themed side project and that the search would burden the presidency.', 'Before taking office, President Daniel licensed a scripture-themed family board game, Road to Emmaus, and a devotional planner to Lydia''s Lamp Games LLC. A House committee, saying it is studying legislation requiring federal officials to disclose outside royalty streams and business entanglements, subpoenas the company for twelve years of Daniel''s licensing agreements, royalty statements, vendor invoices, and payment ledgers. The subpoena is sweeping. Daniel objects that the committee mainly wants to parade private details from a Christian-themed side project and that the search would burden the presidency.

What is the best description of the court''s task?', 'What is the best description of the court''s task?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20186.md","source_question_id":"20186","variant_slug":"road_to_emmaus_royalty_ledgers","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"42010105","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A House committee subpoenas a third party for broad personal records of the President while claiming a legislative-disclosure purpose. What should a court do?","review_truth":"Presidential personal records are neither automatically immune nor automatically obtainable; courts check valid legislative purpose, tailoring, and separation-of-powers burdens.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-20186-MAZARS-01","title":"Presidential subpoena = calibrated review","signal":"Congress seeks President''s personal information from a third party.","student_move":"Cut automatic extremes; then apply the Gold Key.","tiny_rule":"No automatic enforcement, no automatic immunity; court checks purpose, tailoring, and burdens.","trap":"Choosing the branch slogan instead of the court''s task.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-MAZARS-PRESIDENTIAL-01","statement":"When Congress subpoenas a President''s personal information, there is no automatic enforcement and no automatic immunity. Courts check valid legislative purpose, need, reasonable tailoring, and burdens on the presidency.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"congressional subpoena for President''s personal records held by a third party","authority":"Trump v. Mazars USA, LLP, 591 U.S. ___ (2020)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-AUTOMATIC-EXTREMES-01","statement":"When three choices give automatic outcomes and one describes a judicial evaluation, lock the call before choosing. The court-task call usually wants the calibrated evaluation, not a branch-power slogan.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"automatically / never / unlimited / only if","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"Congressional subpoena answer says the court must enforce automatically because Congress has unlimited investigative power. Name the mold.","answer":"tiered_absolute / NOT_TRUE"},{"drill_type":"CALL","target_skill":"answer_array","prompt":"Three answers give automatic outcomes; one answer gives a tailored judicial evaluation. What is the navigation move?","answer":"Use the answer-array Silver Key and prefer the calibrated court-task answer."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"Mazars trigger","prompt":"What Gold Key triggers when Congress subpoenas a President''s personal records from a third party?","answer":"No automatic enforcement and no automatic immunity; court checks purpose, need, tailoring, and burden."}]}'),
  ('2a55da74-d9d9-5573-8a8a-b267decd7049', '20213_mercy_pool_reliction', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Estates and conveyancing', 'O-4', 'adjoining owner vs public/far-side/no-private owner', 'Ruth owns a private olive-grove retreat along the east edge of Mercy Pool, a spring-fed oasis lake where her family holds Sunday hymn picnics. Barnabas owns land on the far west edge. Over 11 years, Mercy Pool’s waterline naturally crept back from Ruth’s side, little by little, leaving a dry crescent of ground between Ruth’s old waterline and the current waterline. The change was natural and gradual.', 'Ruth owns a private olive-grove retreat along the east edge of Mercy Pool, a spring-fed oasis lake where her family holds Sunday hymn picnics. Barnabas owns land on the far west edge. Over 11 years, Mercy Pool’s waterline naturally crept back from Ruth’s side, little by little, leaving a dry crescent of ground between Ruth’s old waterline and the current waterline. The change was natural and gradual.

Under the usual rule, who owns the newly exposed crescent of land?', 'Under the usual rule, who owns the newly exposed crescent of land?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20213.md","source_question_id":"20213","variant_slug":"mercy_pool_reliction","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"core diagnostic","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Water gradually and naturally recedes from the edge of a private littoral parcel, exposing attached dry land. Who owns the exposed land?","review_truth":"Gradual natural reliction moves the water boundary; the adjoining littoral owner gets the exposed land.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RP-ANCHOR-RELICTION-01","title":"Reliction: gradual water retreat adds land to the adjoining parcel","signal":"Water naturally and gradually recedes, exposing dry land.","student_move":"Ask which parcel touches the exposed strip.","tiny_rule":"Gradual natural reliction generally gives exposed land to the adjoining littoral owner.","trap":"Public-forever or far-side-owner answers.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-REAL_PROPERTY-RELICTION-01","statement":"Gradual natural recession of water generally moves the water boundary; the exposed land belongs to the adjoining riparian or littoral owner.","type":"rule","trigger":"Natural, gradual recession exposing dry land attached to a water-adjacent parcel.","authority":"Cornell Wex reliction; Nebraska v. Iowa gradual-change boundary principle.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-REAL_PROPERTY-RELICTION-01","statement":"For water-boundary ownership arrays, lock two facts before reading policy-sounding answers: gradual or sudden, and which parcel the exposed land touches.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Four choices name different owners of newly exposed waterfront land.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"reliction owner-axis","prompt":"Water naturally and gradually recedes from a private lakefront parcel and exposes land attached to that parcel. Who owns the exposed land?","answer":"The adjoining littoral owner."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"public-forever absolute","prompt":"Cut or keep: all land once covered by a lake remains public forever.","answer":"Cut: tiered_absolute overclaim."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"adjacency vs direction","prompt":"Two owners are on opposite sides of a lake. Water recedes from Owner 1’s side and exposes a strip attached to Owner 1’s land. Does the far-side owner get it because the water moved that direction?","answer":"No. The adjoining owner gets it under the reliction anchor."}]}'),
  ('aed2312b-e91f-52de-bc2b-a23d67832426', '20231_crown_above_caesars', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'First Amendment Religion', 'Free Exercise Clause', 'belief-as-such punishment vs conduct regulation', 'During a private Bible-study art class in a rented storefront, Esther teaches a creed she calls "Crown Above Caesars": that Christ''s authority is higher than any earthly government. A state statute makes it a misdemeanor to hold or teach that creed. After charging Esther, the state argues that because it may regulate unlawful acts committed by people who invoke the creed, it may also forbid the creed itself.', 'During a private Bible-study art class in a rented storefront, Esther teaches a creed she calls "Crown Above Caesars": that Christ''s authority is higher than any earthly government. A state statute makes it a misdemeanor to hold or teach that creed. After charging Esther, the state argues that because it may regulate unlawful acts committed by people who invoke the creed, it may also forbid the creed itself.

What is the best constitutional response?', 'What is the best constitutional response?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20231.md","source_question_id":"20231","variant_slug":"crown_above_caesars","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"44040501","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"moderate-anchor","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Can a state criminalize holding or teaching a religious belief because conduct inspired by that belief can be regulated?","review_truth":"Free Exercise protects religious belief as such; conduct is the separate regulatory lane.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-BELIEF-CONDUCT-01","title":"Belief lane vs conduct lane","signal":"A religion question says the state punishes holding, teaching, professing, or affirming a belief.","student_move":"Cut answers that switch to conduct, churches only, or a different Religion Clause.","tiny_rule":"Belief as such is protected; conduct is separate.","trap":"The state justifies a belief ban by pointing to regulable conduct.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONLAW-BELIEF-CONDUCT-01","statement":"Free Exercise protects religious belief and profession as such. Conduct inspired by belief is a separate lane and may be regulated under the applicable constitutional standard.","type":"distinction","trigger":"A statute punishes holding, teaching, professing, or affirming a religious belief.","authority":"Reynolds v. United States; Cantwell v. Connecticut; Employment Division v. Smith.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONLAW-BELIEF-CONDUCT-01","statement":"First lock the object of the law: belief/teaching or conduct. If the answer changes the object from belief to unlawful acts, cut it as the adjacent-call answer.","type":"cut","trigger":"Stem says hold or teach, while an answer says unlawful acts or conduct.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"belief/conduct object lock","prompt":"A statute punishes teaching a religious creed. One answer defends it by citing the state''s power to regulate unlawful acts inspired by the creed. What is the C3 cut?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE; bait_doctrine; wrong_frame."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"only-if overclaim","prompt":"A religion-clause answer says the law is invalid only if it also violates Establishment. What is the mold?","answer":"NOT_TRUE; tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"Free Exercise tiny anchor","prompt":"State the Gold Key for a statute that punishes holding or teaching a religious belief.","answer":"Belief/profession as such is protected; conduct is separate."},{"drill_type":"SILVER_KEY","target_skill":"adjacent-call defense","prompt":"What is the Silver Key when the stem says hold or teach but the answer says unlawful acts?","answer":"Lock the object of the law; cut object-switch answers."}]}'),
  ('96c7f15d-367b-5e28-a913-73dfdd4262ed', '20247_bible_memory_kit', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Appeals and Finality', 'Rule 54(b)', 'Partial dismissal alone vs Rule 54(b) certification gate.', 'Ruth filed a federal civil action after a private Bible-memory tournament kit launch fell apart. Her complaint asserted four unrelated claims against Timothy, Lydia, and Stephen. The district court dismissed every claim against Timothy and wrote, “Timothy is no longer part of this case.” The order did not direct entry of judgment, and it did not state that there was no just reason for delay. Several claims against Lydia and Stephen remain.', 'Ruth filed a federal civil action after a private Bible-memory tournament kit launch fell apart. Her complaint asserted four unrelated claims against Timothy, Lydia, and Stephen. The district court dismissed every claim against Timothy and wrote, “Timothy is no longer part of this case.” The order did not direct entry of judgment, and it did not state that there was no just reason for delay. Several claims against Lydia and Stephen remain.

May Ruth immediately appeal the dismissal under Rule 54(b)?', 'May Ruth immediately appeal the dismissal under Rule 54(b)?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20247.md","source_question_id":"20247","variant_slug":"bible_memory_kit","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A federal civil case still has claims pending. One defendant is fully dismissed, but the order lacks Rule 54(b)’s judgment direction and no-just-reason determination. Is the dismissal immediately appealable?","review_truth":"Partial dismissal is not immediately appealable under Rule 54(b) unless the district court directs entry of judgment and finds no just reason for delay.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-REM-54B-CERT-GATE","title":"Rule 54(b): partial dismissal is not automatic finality","signal":"Fewer than all claims or parties resolved.","student_move":"Ask whether the court both directed entry of judgment and found no just reason for delay.","tiny_rule":"No certification, no immediate Rule 54(b) appeal.","trap":"Full dismissal of one defendant feels final.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-RULE54B-CERT-01","statement":"Rule 54(b) is not automatic. A partial judgment in a multi-claim or multi-party civil action is immediately appealable only if the district court directs entry of judgment and determines that there is no just reason for delay.","type":"threshold","trigger":"Fewer than all claims or parties resolved; claims remain; order lacks certification language.","authority":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 54(b).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-RULE54B-CERT-01","statement":"When the stem goes out of its way to say the order lacks formal Rule 54(b) language, do not let a partial-dismissal fact do the work by itself.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"One party is out, but other claims remain and formal certification is missing.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"overclaim detection","prompt":"Rule 54(b) applies automatically in every multi-party case.","answer":"CUT: tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"Rule 54(b) certification","prompt":"A multi-party civil case dismisses one defendant; claims remain; no no-just-reason finding. Immediate appeal?","answer":"No."},{"drill_type":"issue_sense","target_skill":"wrong-element procedural answer","prompt":"Yes, because one defendant is fully dismissed.","answer":"CUT: true fact, incomplete Rule 54(b) gate."}]}'),
  ('397918a8-a061-512f-b055-8029b8ac070e', '20258_psalter_pressure', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Fifth Amendment', 'Constitutional Protection of Accused Persons', NULL, 'The source row is BARMATRIX Q# 20258, a Criminal Procedure / Fifth Amendment item testing whether coercive police threats and interrogation conditions render a confession involuntary and inadmissible in the prosecution’s case-in-chief. 

Christian divergent variation — `20258_psalter_pressure`

Investigators were questioning Martha about the theft of Stephen’s rare hand-illustrated psalter from a booth at a Christian arts festival. Before questioning, the investigators gave Martha Miranda warnings, and Martha said she understood them. Later, the investigators falsely told Martha that unless she admitted taking the psalter, they would have Barnabas, her brother, arrested that night and would call social services to remove Ruth, the niece Martha was raising. The questioning continued through the dinner hour for eight hours without food. Martha then confessed to the theft.', 'The source row is BARMATRIX Q# 20258, a Criminal Procedure / Fifth Amendment item testing whether coercive police threats and interrogation conditions render a confession involuntary and inadmissible in the prosecution’s case-in-chief. 

Christian divergent variation — `20258_psalter_pressure`

Investigators were questioning Martha about the theft of Stephen’s rare hand-illustrated psalter from a booth at a Christian arts festival. Before questioning, the investigators gave Martha Miranda warnings, and Martha said she understood them. Later, the investigators falsely told Martha that unless she admitted taking the psalter, they would have Barnabas, her brother, arrested that night and would call social services to remove Ruth, the niece Martha was raising. The questioning continued through the dinner hour for eight hours without food. Martha then confessed to the theft.

In Martha’s prosecution for theft, should the confession be admitted in the prosecution’s case-in-chief?', 'In Martha’s prosecution for theft, should the confession be admitted in the prosecution’s case-in-chief?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20258.md","source_question_id":"20258","variant_slug":"psalter_pressure","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"75080101","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Police gave warnings, then used family threats and long questioning without food; is the confession admissible in the prosecution’s case-in-chief?","review_truth":"Warnings do not save a confession if police pressure overbears the suspect’s will.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CRIM-REM-20258-01","title":"Warnings are not a pressure shield","signal":"Stem gives Miranda warnings and then coercive pressure facts.","student_move":"Separate warning/form facts from voluntariness facts.","tiny_rule":"Coercion can defeat admissibility even after warnings.","trap":"Picking the warning answer because it sounds procedurally clean.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-PSALTER-PRESSURE-01","statement":"A confession produced by coercive police threats or conditions that overbear the suspect’s will is involuntary and cannot be used in the prosecution’s case-in-chief.","type":"rule","trigger":"Warnings plus threats against family plus deprivation","authority":"Lynumn v. Illinois; Colorado v. Connelly; Mincey v. Arizona","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-MIRANDA-SEPARATE-02","statement":"Miranda waiver and due-process voluntariness are separate gates; warnings or a waiver form do not cure a coerced confession.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The stem gives warnings, then pressure facts","authority":"Mincey v. Arizona; North Carolina v. Butler","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-PSALTER-PRESSURE-01","statement":"When a stem gives one clean procedure fact and then piles on pressure facts, do not stop at the procedure fact; the answer must account for the pressure facts.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"Warnings appear before coercion facts","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true},{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-PSALTER-PRESSURE-02","statement":"In a yes/no answer set, a no answer with the wrong reason still gets cut; match both the result and the reason.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Two no answers appear","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim detector","prompt":"Choice says police may use any deception during interrogation. What is the C3 cut?","answer":"NOT_TRUE / tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"bait doctrine","prompt":"Choice says the confession is admissible because warnings were given and understood. The stem adds threats and deprivation. What is the C3 cut?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE / bait_doctrine; warnings answer the wrong layer."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"no-but-wrong-reason","prompt":"Two choices say no. One says no because coercion; one says no because no written waiver. Which survives?","answer":"The coercion answer; the form answer gives the wrong reason."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"confession voluntariness","prompt":"What Gold Key unlocks warnings-plus-pressure confession questions?","answer":"Coercion can make a confession involuntary and inadmissible even after warnings."}]}'),
  ('9f879ab2-ed02-5049-8067-38adcd91200a', '20271_lantern_handbell_evening', 'CONTRACTS', 'Expectation damages', 'A1', NULL, 'Daniel agreed to create and lead a private Scripture-themed lantern-and-handbell evening for Lydia’s family Easter celebration for $48,000. Daniel reasonably expected to spend $31,000 on lantern rentals, music assistants, and supplies. Before Daniel rented anything or began preparing, Lydia repudiated the contract without excuse. Daniel had no other losses and avoided all performance costs.', 'Daniel agreed to create and lead a private Scripture-themed lantern-and-handbell evening for Lydia’s family Easter celebration for $48,000. Daniel reasonably expected to spend $31,000 on lantern rentals, music assistants, and supplies. Before Daniel rented anything or began preparing, Lydia repudiated the contract without excuse. Daniel had no other losses and avoided all performance costs.

What is Daniel’s proper expectation recovery?', 'What is Daniel’s proper expectation recovery?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20271.md","source_question_id":"20271","variant_slug":"lantern_handbell_evening","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"51060101","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"calibration","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A service provider had a $48,000 contract, expected $31,000 in performance costs, and the client repudiated before any costs were incurred. What expectation damages does the provider recover?","review_truth":"Expectation recovery does not pay the gross price or the costs saved; it pays the net bargain lost.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-EXPECTATION-SAVED-COSTS-01","title":"Do not pay avoided costs.","signal":"The stem gives contract price and expected cost, then says the contractor avoided all costs.","student_move":"Subtract the avoided cost from the price.","tiny_rule":"Expectation protects the net bargain, not the gross price.","trap":"price/cost/zero answer array","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-COSTS-SAVED-01","statement":"When a service contract is repudiated before the contractor spends money, expectation recovery is the profit the contractor lost: contract price minus costs avoided, plus any other proven loss.","type":"rule","trigger":"fixed service price + expected costs + repudiation before performance + avoided all costs","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 347","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-PROFIT-MATH-01","statement":"When the choices are gross price, expected cost, net profit, and zero, treat the array as a formula test: keep the answer that uses price and subtracts saved costs.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"one choice equals price, one equals cost, one equals price-minus-cost, one equals zero","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"number_array","target_skill":"cost-saved offset","prompt":"Contract price $70,000; saved costs $52,000; no other loss. Recovery?","answer":"$18,000"},{"drill_type":"wrong_element","target_skill":"gross price trap","prompt":"Why is the full contract price not the recovery when all costs were avoided?","answer":"It ignores saved costs and overcompensates."},{"drill_type":"fabricated_rule","target_skill":"no-work myth","prompt":"Does a contractor lose all profit recovery because work had not begun?","answer":"No. If the contract was repudiated and profit is proved, saved costs are subtracted."}]}'),
  ('22a1dd96-613f-54ca-9810-3584de3fae19', '20291_nativity_prop_rental_freeze', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Appeals and Finality', 'Injunction Appeals', 'caption label vs practical command', 'In a federal contract dispute between two private owners of a Nativity-prop rental business, the district court enters an order captioned “trial-preparation protocol.” The order directs Ruth not to rent out the disputed angel wings, shepherd cloaks, or manger displays to any Christmas pageant customers until trial. Ruth immediately appeals, arguing that the order operates as an injunction despite its caption.', 'In a federal contract dispute between two private owners of a Nativity-prop rental business, the district court enters an order captioned “trial-preparation protocol.” The order directs Ruth not to rent out the disputed angel wings, shepherd cloaks, or manger displays to any Christmas pageant customers until trial. Ruth immediately appeals, arguing that the order operates as an injunction despite its caption.

Is Ruth’s characterization likely right?', 'Is Ruth’s characterization likely right?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20291.md","source_question_id":"20291","variant_slug":"nativity_prop_rental_freeze","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"93110100","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A district court order with a non-injunction caption tells a party not to rent disputed property until trial. Is it likely treated as an injunction for immediate appeal?","review_truth":"For injunction appealability, read practical effect; a stop-command can be treated as an injunction even if the caption says something else.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-REM-20291-FUNCTIONAL-INJUNCTION","title":"Caption vs command","signal":"A district-court order has a neutral label but tells someone to do or stop doing something.","student_move":"Ignore the caption first; identify the operative command.","tiny_rule":"Function beats label for injunction appealability.","trap":"Magic-word caption rule; every-management-order overclaim.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-FUNCTIONAL-INJUNCTION-01","statement":"For immediate appeal, an order can count as an injunction by practical effect. A command to do or stop doing something may be injunctive even when the caption uses another label.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Non-injunction caption plus a command to stop conduct until trial.","authority":"28 U.S.C. §1292(a)(1); Carson v. American Brands; Abbott v. Perez.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-FUNCTIONAL-INJUNCTION-01","statement":"In a label-vs-function appealability question, read the verb in the order before reading the caption. A stop-command is the navigation signal.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"Order caption says one thing; operative sentence commands a party not to act.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"overclaim scan","prompt":"An order is called a scheduling order. One answer says every scheduling order is immediately appealable. What is the cut?","answer":"tiered_absolute; every is too broad."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"functional injunction","prompt":"A district court labels an order a protocol but tells a party not to sell inventory until trial. What Gold Key applies?","answer":"Function beats label for injunction appealability."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"caption vs command","prompt":"The caption says protocol; the text says do not rent. Which fact controls the student move?","answer":"The command controls the navigation move."},{"drill_type":"calibration","target_skill":"anchor-assisted cut","prompt":"You cut every, only, and never, but two cuts use a Gold Key. What confidence bucket?","answer":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED."}]}'),
  ('209e8d61-21c6-57c9-b5e5-f3e9d97cc6fe', '20307_bethany_banners_transcript', 'EVIDENCE', 'Evidence Law', 'Hearsay and Circumstances of Its Admissibility', NULL, 'At a private “Songs of the Psalms” merchandise expo, Ruth was walking through an aisle when she was struck by a motorized pallet cart driven by Peter, an employee of Bethany Banners, Inc. Peter was moving boxed stage banners for Bethany Banners to its rented booth.

After the incident, Peter was charged with reckless operation of equipment and pleaded not guilty. At that criminal trial, Peter testified in his own defense. He stated that, at the time of the incident, he had looked down at the booth map to find the “Road to Emmaus” display area, and as a result he did not see Ruth walking in the aisle.

Ruth later brought a personal-injury action against Bethany Banners under a respondeat superior theory, alleging that Peter’s negligence caused her injuries. At the civil trial, Ruth proved that Peter remained employed by Bethany Banners until Peter later died from causes unrelated to the incident. Ruth then offered a transcript of Peter’s testimony from the reckless-operation trial.

Upon objection by Bethany Banners’s attorney, the transcript should be:', 'At a private “Songs of the Psalms” merchandise expo, Ruth was walking through an aisle when she was struck by a motorized pallet cart driven by Peter, an employee of Bethany Banners, Inc. Peter was moving boxed stage banners for Bethany Banners to its rented booth.

After the incident, Peter was charged with reckless operation of equipment and pleaded not guilty. At that criminal trial, Peter testified in his own defense. He stated that, at the time of the incident, he had looked down at the booth map to find the “Road to Emmaus” display area, and as a result he did not see Ruth walking in the aisle.

Ruth later brought a personal-injury action against Bethany Banners under a respondeat superior theory, alleging that Peter’s negligence caused her injuries. At the civil trial, Ruth proved that Peter remained employed by Bethany Banners until Peter later died from causes unrelated to the incident. Ruth then offered a transcript of Peter’s testimony from the reckless-operation trial.

Upon objection by Bethany Banners’s attorney, the transcript should be:

Upon objection by Bethany Banners''s attorney, the transcript should be:', 'Upon objection by Bethany Banners''s attorney, the transcript should be:', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20307.md","source_question_id":"20307","variant_slug":"bethany_banners_transcript","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"33040202","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"An employee, still employed when he testified in an earlier criminal case, admitted job-related careless conduct; the injured plaintiff later sues the employer and offers the court transcript. Is the transcript admissible?","review_truth":"Transcript questions are layer questions: clear the speaker layer and the transcript layer.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REMED-EVIDENCE-DOUBLE-HEARSAY-TRANSCRIPT","title":"Transcript = two layers","signal":"A transcript carries a statement from an earlier proceeding.","student_move":"Ask: what admits the speaker''s statement, and what admits the transcript?","tiny_rule":"Both layers need paths.","trap":"Picking the first familiar exception name.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-DOUBLE-HEARSAY-01","statement":"For hearsay within hearsay, each layer needs its own path. In an employee-transcript item, check the employee statement layer and the transcript/public-record layer separately.","type":"rule","trigger":"Transcript of earlier testimony offered for its truth.","authority":"FRE 805; FRE 801(d)(2)(D); FRE 803(8).","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-FORMER-TESTIMONY-01","statement":"Former testimony needs the current opponent, or a civil predecessor, to have had an opportunity and similar motive to develop the earlier testimony.","type":"threshold","trigger":"Earlier criminal trial; later civil defendant was not a party.","authority":"FRE 804(b)(1).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-EVIDENCE-TRANSCRIPT-LAYERS-01","statement":"When a choice says ''transcript,'' split the evidence into speaker layer and document layer before choosing an exception.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Transcript offered in a later trial.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"Double-hearsay layer split","prompt":"A transcript of earlier testimony is offered in a later civil trial. What two layers must be cleared?","answer":"The speaker''s statement layer and the transcript/document layer."},{"drill_type":"Wrong-frame cut","target_skill":"Former testimony threshold","prompt":"Prior testimony is offered against a party who was not present in the earlier case. What threshold should you check?","answer":"Whether that party or a civil predecessor had an opportunity and similar motive to develop the testimony."},{"drill_type":"Exception distinction","target_skill":"Recorded recollection vs transcript","prompt":"Why is a court transcript usually not a recorded recollection?","answer":"It is not the witness''s made-or-adopted memory record."}]}'),
  ('c4682e6a-9b89-5fca-88eb-44613808b2d0', '20310_psalms_wax_lamp', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Breach', 'later-installation use: breach proof vs limited feasibility use', 'Lydia runs a paid private Psalms calligraphy workshop in her garage studio. During the class, Ruth reached across the worktable for a sheet of parchment and brushed her forearm against an exposed brass warming lamp used to soften sealing wax. Ruth was burned. The next morning, Lydia installed a small wire guard around the lamp. Lydia admits that the guard could have been installed before Ruth was burned, but she denies that reasonable care required a guard under the conditions that existed during the class.', 'Lydia runs a paid private Psalms calligraphy workshop in her garage studio. During the class, Ruth reached across the worktable for a sheet of parchment and brushed her forearm against an exposed brass warming lamp used to soften sealing wax. Ruth was burned. The next morning, Lydia installed a small wire guard around the lamp. Lydia admits that the guard could have been installed before Ruth was burned, but she denies that reasonable care required a guard under the conditions that existed during the class.

For breach, what is the best use of the later installation?', 'For breach, what is the best use of the later installation?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20310.md","source_question_id":"20310","variant_slug":"psalms_wax_lamp","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020301","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"After a workshop injury, the defendant added a safety guard and admits the guard was feasible before the injury. For breach, does the later guard prove prior negligence, or is its only possible use feasibility if genuinely disputed?","review_truth":"A later safety fix does not prove earlier negligence; at most, it can serve a limited purpose such as feasibility if that point is genuinely disputed.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-SRM-01","title":"Later Fix Is Not Hindsight Liability","signal":"Post-injury repair, added guard, new warning, new traction, or changed procedure.","student_move":"Separate prior breach from later feasibility.","tiny_rule":"Later fix is not breach proof; feasibility only if genuinely disputed.","trap":"They fixed it later, so they must have breached earlier.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-LATER-REPAIR-01","statement":"A later safety fix is not proof that the earlier condition was negligent. Its limited use is a non-breach purpose such as feasibility, and only if that purpose is genuinely disputed.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Defendant changes the condition after the injury.","authority":"Fed. R. Evid. 407; Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Physical and Emotional Harm §3.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-LATER-REPAIR-01","statement":"Lock the call to use, not hindsight. First cut the two extremes: later fix proves everything, later fix proves nothing.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"A post-injury safety change appears in a breach question.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim detection","prompt":"A defendant repairs a hazard after an injury. One choice says the repair conclusively proves breach. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut. Conclusive proof is an overclaim."},{"drill_type":"CALL_LOCK","target_skill":"call focus","prompt":"The call asks breach by the defendant. A choice proves plaintiff contributory negligence. What filter breaks?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"later remedial measure limited use","prompt":"A later guard may be used for what limited purpose if genuinely disputed?","answer":"Feasibility, not breach proof."},{"drill_type":"TRAP_SPOTTING","target_skill":"hindsight bias","prompt":"What is the trap in ''they fixed it later, so they were negligent before''?","answer":"It uses hindsight as a substitute for breach analysis."}]}'),
  ('7847a157-2386-5de5-ba4d-82eeec2f608d', '20312_loaves_fishes_retreat', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Ownership', 'Life estates; remainders; encumbrance payment allocation', 'interest/current-use burden vs principal/future-equity burden', 'Martha holds a life estate in a private lakeside parcel called the Loaves-and-Fishes Retreat, where families rent small cabins during nearby Bible-study weekends. Stephen holds the remainder. Before the life estate was created, the parcel was already subject to a recorded mortgage securing a loan on the parcel. The parcel’s reasonable rental value is greater than the total currently needed to cover both principal and interest on that secured obligation.', 'Martha holds a life estate in a private lakeside parcel called the Loaves-and-Fishes Retreat, where families rent small cabins during nearby Bible-study weekends. Stephen holds the remainder. Before the life estate was created, the parcel was already subject to a recorded mortgage securing a loan on the parcel. The parcel’s reasonable rental value is greater than the total currently needed to cover both principal and interest on that secured obligation.

Which statement correctly allocates the payment obligation, as between Martha and Stephen, for the obligation secured by the mortgage?', 'Which statement correctly allocates the payment obligation, as between Martha and Stephen, for the obligation secured by the mortgage?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20312.md","source_question_id":"20312","variant_slug":"loaves_fishes_retreat","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"82010103","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium_high","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A life tenant is in possession. The property’s rental value can cover principal and interest on a mortgage. Who pays interest, and who pays principal?","review_truth":"Current-use burden goes to the life tenant: interest. Future-equity burden goes to the remainderman: principal.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RP-REM-LIFE-ENCUMBRANCE-01","title":"Life tenant pays interest; remainderman pays principal","signal":"life tenant + remainderman + mortgage/encumbrance","student_move":"Name the two payment components before choosing.","tiny_rule":"Interest is current-use cost; principal is future-equity cost.","trap":"Choosing whoever seems more morally responsible for the whole mortgage.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-REAL_PROPERTY-LIFE-ENCUMBRANCE-01","statement":"For an existing mortgage or encumbrance on land held in life estate, the life tenant pays interest up to the reasonable rental value; the remainderman pays principal.","type":"distinction","trigger":"life tenant + remainderman + mortgage/encumbrance + principal and interest","authority":"Thompson v. Watkins, 285 N.C. 616 (1974); N.Y. Real Property Law § 269.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-REAL_PROPERTY-LIFE-ENCUMBRANCE-01","statement":"When every choice assigns principal and interest between two estate holders, read the answers as a 2x2 allocation grid before using the anchor.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"choices distribute principal and interest between life tenant and remainderman","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"life estate encumbrance allocation","prompt":"Life tenant in possession; remainderman waiting; mortgage has principal and interest due. Allocate the burdens.","answer":"Life tenant pays interest up to rental value; remainderman pays principal."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"2x2 allocation grid","prompt":"Four choices assign two costs to two estate holders. What is the first move?","answer":"Read it as a grid: identify each cost and each holder before applying the anchor."},{"drill_type":"wrong_answer_recovery","target_skill":"avoid over-assigning burdens","prompt":"Why is ''life tenant pays both principal and interest'' tempting and why does it fail?","answer":"Tempting because the life tenant is in possession; fails because possession maps to interest, not principal."}]}'),
  ('265b586e-c0c3-5f41-a6ad-555f53f31ae4', '20313_bethlehem_olivewood_crosses', 'EVIDENCE', 'III.B.5 — Attorney-client', 'Attorney-client privilege / crime-fraud boundary', 'compliance advice versus furthering fraud', 'Lydia runs a small Bible-study gift cart at weekend retreats. Before selling a shipment of wooden crosses, she privately told her lawyer, Paul, “I am thinking about advertising these as carved from Bethlehem olivewood. Would that be legal? If that would be fraudulent, I will not use that description.” Paul researched the law and advised Lydia not to use the description.', 'Lydia runs a small Bible-study gift cart at weekend retreats. Before selling a shipment of wooden crosses, she privately told her lawyer, Paul, “I am thinking about advertising these as carved from Bethlehem olivewood. Would that be legal? If that would be fraudulent, I will not use that description.” Paul researched the law and advised Lydia not to use the description.

Are Lydia’s communications protected by attorney-client privilege?', 'Are Lydia’s communications protected by attorney-client privilege?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20313.md","source_question_id":"20313","variant_slug":"bethlehem_olivewood_crosses","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"discriminator","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"If that would be fraudulent, I will not use that description.","distilled_core_question":"Client asks lawyer whether planned conduct would be fraudulent and says she will not do it if unlawful. Lawyer advises against it. Are the communications privileged?","review_truth":"Suspicious subject matter does not defeat attorney-client privilege unless the client seeks legal advice to carry out or cover up wrongdoing.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-EVIDENCE-PRIV-CRIME-FRAUD-20313","title":"Purpose beats suspicious topic","signal":"Client asks whether a proposed act would be fraudulent.","student_move":"Ask whether the client is seeking compliance advice or help carrying out wrongdoing.","tiny_rule":"Crime-fraud needs furtherance; compliance advice remains privileged.","trap":"Fraud word = automatic exception.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-CRIME-FRAUD-01","statement":"Crime-fraud is purpose-based, not topic-based: privilege fails only when legal advice is sought to commit, continue, or cover up crime or fraud.","type":"exception","trigger":"Client asks whether planned conduct would be fraudulent and says she will not do it if unlawful.","authority":"FRE 501 common law; United States v. Zolin; Clark v. United States.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-WORKPRODUCT-DISTINCTION-01","statement":"Substantial need is a work-product concept; it is not the test for overriding attorney-client communications.","type":"distinction","trigger":"A privilege answer says ''substantial need.''","authority":"Federal privilege and work-product doctrine distinction.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-EVIDENCE-CRIME-FRAUD-01","statement":"When a privilege answer says ''fraud,'' do not stop at the topic. Name the axis: compliance advice versus using the lawyer to further fraud.","type":"clash","trigger":"Client says she will not proceed if the conduct is fraudulent.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"crime-fraud boundary","prompt":"Client asks lawyer whether a planned act would be fraudulent and says she will not do it if unlawful. Does crime-fraud automatically apply?","answer":"No. The purpose is compliance advice, not furtherance."},{"drill_type":"bait-doctrine cut","target_skill":"privilege versus work product","prompt":"A privilege answer says disclosure depends on substantial need. What is the C3 label?","answer":"bait_doctrine / wrong_frame."},{"drill_type":"Clash axis","target_skill":"purpose axis","prompt":"Two answers fight: fraud topic kills privilege versus legal advice to avoid fraud. What fact decides?","answer":"Client says she will not proceed if the conduct is fraudulent."}]}'),
  ('c9cdaca4-bcf9-57c0-9f70-9aef62c0666e', '20328_bethlehem_star_drop', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Breach proof - res ipsa loquitur', 'precise defect proof required vs res ipsa inference without direct defect proof', 'Lydia owns a for-profit Bible-story dinner theater that sells tickets to a Nativity show. During a performance, an overhead Bethlehem star prop that was supposed to glide slowly above the stage suddenly dropped from its track and struck Martha, a ticketed guest standing in the marked viewing area. Lydia had hired Timothy''s rigging company to inspect and service the overhead track, but Lydia kept responsibility for deciding whether the star prop would be used during each show and received all inspection reports. Martha did not touch the rigging, controls, or stage equipment. Martha sues Lydia for negligence but cannot prove the exact clamp, cable, or motor failure that caused the star to drop.', 'Lydia owns a for-profit Bible-story dinner theater that sells tickets to a Nativity show. During a performance, an overhead Bethlehem star prop that was supposed to glide slowly above the stage suddenly dropped from its track and struck Martha, a ticketed guest standing in the marked viewing area. Lydia had hired Timothy''s rigging company to inspect and service the overhead track, but Lydia kept responsibility for deciding whether the star prop would be used during each show and received all inspection reports. Martha did not touch the rigging, controls, or stage equipment. Martha sues Lydia for negligence but cannot prove the exact clamp, cable, or motor failure that caused the star to drop.

Will res ipsa loquitur likely be available in Martha''s negligence claim against Lydia?', 'Will res ipsa loquitur likely be available in Martha''s negligence claim against Lydia?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20328.md","source_question_id":"20328","variant_slug":"bethlehem_star_drop","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020304","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A maintained overhead prop suddenly drops during normal use; the defendant kept operational responsibility, the plaintiff did not cause it, and the plaintiff lacks direct defect proof. Is res ipsa likely available?","review_truth":"Res ipsa can supply a negligence inference from an abnormal event without direct defect proof, but it does not force judgment.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-RES-IPSA-01","title":"Res ipsa: inference, not proof shortcut to judgment","signal":"Abnormal malfunction, defendant responsibility, plaintiff non-contribution, no direct proof.","student_move":"Do not punish the plaintiff for lacking the exact defect; ask whether the event itself can speak.","tiny_rule":"No exact defect needed; no automatic judgment.","trap":"Missing direct proof feels fatal.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-RES-IPSA-INFERENCE-01","statement":"Res ipsa is an inference bridge. It can apply when the plaintiff lacks direct proof of the exact defect, and it permits but does not compel a negligence finding.","type":"rule","trigger":"The call asks whether res ipsa is available and the stem says the plaintiff lacks direct defect proof.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 328D","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-TORTS-RES-IPSA-CONTROL-01","statement":"Contractor involvement does not automatically erase res ipsa responsibility when the defendant retained operational responsibility for the instrumentality.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The stem says a contractor serviced the mechanism but the defendant retained operational responsibility and received reports.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 328D responsibility/control formulation","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-RES-IPSA-ARRAY-01","statement":"Cut the absolute contractor and automatic-judgment answers first. Then resolve the residual pair by asking whether the missing exact defect is fatal or is the reason res ipsa matters.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"One answer says no exact defect, one says inference without exact defect.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"res ipsa direct-proof trap","prompt":"Plaintiff lacks proof of the exact defect but the instrumentality fails abnormally under defendant responsibility. Fatal to res ipsa?","answer":"No. Missing exact defect proof is compatible with res ipsa."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"absolute contractor trap","prompt":"Hiring a contractor always defeats res ipsa responsibility.","answer":"Cut. Always overclaims."},{"drill_type":"remedy_precision","target_skill":"inference versus judgment","prompt":"Res ipsa requires judgment for the plaintiff once an accident happens.","answer":"Cut. It permits an inference; it does not require judgment."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"residual pair recognition","prompt":"No exact defect proof vs inference without exact defect proof. Name the axis.","answer":"Whether res ipsa requires direct proof of the precise defect."}]}'),
  ('aec55d9c-5747-5295-a4ec-284c1b2d27bb', '20351_red_sea_mini_golf', 'CONTRACTS', 'Mistake / Lack of Mutual Assent', 'F', 'mutual-mistake fact vs contractual risk allocation', 'Barnabas owns a privately run Bible-story mini-golf course called The Exodus Course. He sells it to Ruth. The sale contract says that Ruth buys the course "as is, including all hidden subsurface drainage defects, whether known or unknown." Both Barnabas and Ruth believe the underground drainage tunnels beneath the "Red Sea Crossing" hole are intact. Before signing, however, those tunnels had already collapsed from storm erosion. Ruth later discovers the collapse and seeks rescission for mutual mistake. How should the court rule?', 'Barnabas owns a privately run Bible-story mini-golf course called The Exodus Course. He sells it to Ruth. The sale contract says that Ruth buys the course "as is, including all hidden subsurface drainage defects, whether known or unknown." Both Barnabas and Ruth believe the underground drainage tunnels beneath the "Red Sea Crossing" hole are intact. Before signing, however, those tunnels had already collapsed from storm erosion. Ruth later discovers the collapse and seeks rescission for mutual mistake. How should the court rule?

How should the court rule?', 'How should the court rule?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20351.md","source_question_id":"20351","variant_slug":"red_sea_mini_golf","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"54030600","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"trap repair","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Buyer signs an as-is clause covering hidden unknown defects. Both parties are wrong about a hidden preexisting defect. Buyer seeks mutual-mistake rescission. Who wins?","review_truth":"A mutual mistake does not support rescission when the contract assigned that mistake-risk to the party seeking rescission.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-RISK-BEFORE-RESCISSION-01","title":"Risk before rescission","signal":"Mutual mistake plus as-is / unknown-defect clause.","student_move":"Do not stop at both mistaken. Ask who took the risk.","tiny_rule":"If the claimant accepted the mistake-risk, rescission is denied.","trap":"Picking the mutual-mistake-only grant answer.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-AS-IS-MISTAKE-01","statement":"A mutual mistake does not support rescission if the party seeking rescission bore the risk. An as-is clause can allocate unknown hidden-defect risk to that party.","type":"rule","trigger":"A mutual mistake stem plus an express as-is or unknown-defect risk-allocation term.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 152, 154.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-AS-IS-MISTAKE-01","statement":"In a grant/deny rescission matrix, separate the mistake fact from the risk-allocation fact. The answer that engages the explicit risk term beats the answer that only says both parties were mistaken.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Two choices grant rescission, two deny; one denial tracks the as-is clause.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"Overclaim detection","prompt":"Hidden defects are always outside an as-is clause.","answer":"Cut: tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"Fabricated rule detection","prompt":"Deny rescission only if the buyer inspected every hidden component personally.","answer":"Cut: fabricated_rule."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"Risk allocation in mutual mistake","prompt":"Both parties were mistaken, but the buyer agreed to take unknown hidden-defect risk. Rescission?","answer":"No rescission."},{"drill_type":"ANSWER_ARRAY","target_skill":"2x2 grant/deny matrix","prompt":"Two choices grant because of mistake; two deny because of risk or a proof condition. Which axis decides?","answer":"Risk allocation."}]}'),
  ('42d71c1c-8d9f-573d-9a77-6792c5eb46af', '20353_baptism_tank_burst', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Subject Matter Jurisdiction', 'Supplemental Jurisdiction - Defendant Claims', NULL, 'In a federal diversity action, Lydia, a citizen of State A, sues Barnabas, a citizen of State B, for $120,000 after Barnabas''s privately owned portable baptism-tank display burst at a Christian handbell expo and ruined Lydia''s sound equipment. Barnabas counterclaims against Lydia for $18,000 for damage to the tank display from the same burst. The counterclaim has no independent amount in controversy.', 'In a federal diversity action, Lydia, a citizen of State A, sues Barnabas, a citizen of State B, for $120,000 after Barnabas''s privately owned portable baptism-tank display burst at a Christian handbell expo and ruined Lydia''s sound equipment. Barnabas counterclaims against Lydia for $18,000 for damage to the tank display from the same burst. The counterclaim has no independent amount in controversy.

May the federal court hear the counterclaim?', 'May the federal court hear the counterclaim?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20353.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"baptism_tank_burst","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"94010300","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium_hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"In a diversity case with a proper $120,000 anchor claim, may the court hear a defendant''s $18,000 counterclaim against the plaintiff when it arises from the same incident?","review_truth":"A same-event defendant counterclaim can use supplemental jurisdiction even if it does not independently exceed $75,000.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-REM-20353-DEFENDANT-COUNTERCLAIM","title":"Defendant counterclaim under supplemental jurisdiction","signal":"Diversity anchor claim plus low-value defendant counterclaim from the same incident.","student_move":"Check whether the low-value claim is defendant-side and same occurrence before applying §1367(b).","tiny_rule":"Same-event defendant counterclaims may ride §1367(a); §1367(b) is plaintiff-side.","trap":"Treating every low-value diversity claim as independently barred.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-DEFENDANT_COUNTERCLAIM-01","statement":"In a diversity case, a defendant''s same-transaction counterclaim can use supplemental jurisdiction even below $75,000 unless a specific plaintiff-side §1367(b) bar applies.","type":"rule","trigger":"Defendant counterclaim + same incident + no independent amount in controversy.","authority":"28 U.S.C. §1367(a)-(b); Fed. R. Civ. P. 13(a)(1).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-SUPP_COUNTERCLAIM_ARRAY-01","statement":"Read the array as result plus jurisdictional reason. A ''yes'' answer is not enough; the reason must be same-event supplemental jurisdiction, not federal question.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"A counterclaim in a diversity case with no independent amount.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"A diversity anchor claim is for $110,000. The defendant asserts a $12,000 same-incident counterclaim. Cut: ''No, each claim must independently exceed $75,000.''","answer":"CUT as tiered_absolute after Gold Key."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"misfit","prompt":"A counterclaim arises from the same incident but no federal statute appears. Cut: ''Yes, because the counterclaim presents a federal question.''","answer":"CUT as misfit / wrong_frame."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"supplemental_jurisdiction_defendant_counterclaim","prompt":"State the Gold Key for a same-event defendant counterclaim below $75,000 in a diversity case.","answer":"It can use supplemental jurisdiction unless a specific plaintiff-side §1367(b) bar applies."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"result_reason_match","prompt":"Two answers say yes; one says federal question, one says same Article III case. The stem gives only same-incident facts. Which yes survives?","answer":"The same Article III case answer."}]}'),
  ('563044e0-872c-539a-b9d8-760f2b1333ab', '20370_dove_mug_notice', 'CONTRACTS', 'UCC sale of goods', 'Acceptance, rejection, notice of breach, and seller action for price', 'notice-after-acceptance vs defect-alone defense', 'Paul, a devotional-merchandise wholesaler, sued Lydia on August 9 for the price of 360 ceramic “Faith, Hope, Love” mugs. The sale began on March 7, when Lydia, who operates a Christian gift shop, ordered mugs finished with a gold dove decal, for delivery by March 18. On March 18, the mugs reached Lydia’s shop. Timothy, Lydia’s stockroom manager, opened the cartons and told Lydia both that the order had arrived and that the mugs were missing the gold dove decals. Lydia meant to text Paul that afternoon, but while preparing a children’s choir sale she forgot. On April 27, Lydia received Paul’s invoice but neither paid nor contacted him. In defense to Paul’s action, Lydia asserted that the mugs did not conform to the contract of sale.', 'Paul, a devotional-merchandise wholesaler, sued Lydia on August 9 for the price of 360 ceramic “Faith, Hope, Love” mugs. The sale began on March 7, when Lydia, who operates a Christian gift shop, ordered mugs finished with a gold dove decal, for delivery by March 18. On March 18, the mugs reached Lydia’s shop. Timothy, Lydia’s stockroom manager, opened the cartons and told Lydia both that the order had arrived and that the mugs were missing the gold dove decals. Lydia meant to text Paul that afternoon, but while preparing a children’s choir sale she forgot. On April 27, Lydia received Paul’s invoice but neither paid nor contacted him. In defense to Paul’s action, Lydia asserted that the mugs did not conform to the contract of sale.

The court should rule for', 'The court should rule for', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20370.md","source_question_id":"20370","variant_slug":"dove_mug_notice","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"56050402","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A buyer learns on delivery that goods are nonconforming, says nothing for months, and raises the defect only after the seller sues for the price. Who wins?","review_truth":"In a goods sale, a buyer who accepts goods and sits on a known defect without timely notice loses the defect remedy.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CONTRACTS-NOTICE-AFTER-ACCEPTANCE-01","title":"Accepted goods: notice keeps the remedy alive","signal":"Buyer knows goods are defective, keeps silent, then raises defect after being sued.","student_move":"Ask whether the buyer notified the seller within a reasonable time.","tiny_rule":"Known defect plus no seasonable notice means no defect remedy after acceptance.","trap":"Picking the nonconformity answer because the goods were genuinely defective.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-DOVE-NOTICE-01","statement":"After accepting goods, a buyer who discovers a defect must notify the seller within a reasonable time or lose the defect remedy.","type":"rule","trigger":"Known nonconformity on delivery plus months of silence before a price suit.","authority":"UCC §§2-602, 2-606, 2-607, 2-709","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-DOVE-ARRAY-01","statement":"Read the 2x2 by reason, not outcome: the seller-wins choice with the notice fact beats the seller-wins choice with acceptance alone.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Two choices name the seller and two name the buyer.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"same_outcome_wrong_reason","prompt":"Seller sues for price after buyer accepted defective goods. Choice says seller wins because acceptance alone always binds buyer to contract price. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut; acceptance alone is incomplete because timely notice can preserve defect remedies."},{"drill_type":"Issue-Sense","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"Buyer knew goods were defective on delivery and stayed silent for months. Choice says buyer wins because goods were defective. What is the skipped element?","answer":"Notice after discovery; defect alone is not responsive."},{"drill_type":"Anchor","target_skill":"accepted_goods_notice_rule","prompt":"After accepting goods, what must a buyer do after discovering a defect to preserve remedies?","answer":"Notify the seller within a reasonable time."},{"drill_type":"Trap Spotting","target_skill":"fabricated_rule","prompt":"Choice says seller loses the price if a reasonable pre-shipment inspection would have found the defect. What mold is this?","answer":"fabricated_rule."}]}'),
  ('b73bc1ad-be11-5921-a287-7dd3bbc81a60', '20387_psalms_chess_indictment', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Exclusionary rule', 'Remedies and limits', 'violation versus remedy', 'Detectives investigating the theft of Stephen’s personal olivewood chess set from a Christian bookstore already had the shop’s security video and written statements from two shoppers identifying Peter. Three weeks later, officers saw Peter outside the store after a Psalms-and-coffee night and arrested him without a warrant or probable cause. The arrest produced no physical evidence and no statement. Prosecutors later indicted Peter for the theft using the video and shopper statements. Peter moves to dismiss the indictment solely because the arrest was unlawful.', 'Detectives investigating the theft of Stephen’s personal olivewood chess set from a Christian bookstore already had the shop’s security video and written statements from two shoppers identifying Peter. Three weeks later, officers saw Peter outside the store after a Psalms-and-coffee night and arrested him without a warrant or probable cause. The arrest produced no physical evidence and no statement. Prosecutors later indicted Peter for the theft using the video and shopper statements. Peter moves to dismiss the indictment solely because the arrest was unlawful.

How should the court rule?', 'How should the court rule?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20387.md","source_question_id":"20387","variant_slug":"psalms_chess_indictment","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"75100000","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Police make an unlawful arrest, get no evidence or statement from it, and indict using evidence already in the file. The defendant moves to dismiss solely because of the arrest. Should dismissal be granted?","review_truth":"A bad arrest can suppress tainted evidence, but it does not automatically erase a prosecution supported by independent evidence.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CRIM-REM-ARREST-REMEDY-01","title":"Bad arrest is not automatic dismissal","signal":"Unlawful arrest + motion to dismiss + independent evidence","student_move":"Separate the violation from the requested remedy.","tiny_rule":"Suppress tainted evidence when applicable; do not dismiss charges supported by independent evidence.","trap":"Treating illegality as automatic case erasure.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-ARREST-REMEDY-01","statement":"An unlawful arrest is not itself a prosecution-ending defect. The remedy targets evidence obtained through the illegality; charges supported by untainted evidence are not dismissed merely because the arrest was bad.","type":"rule","trigger":"unlawful arrest + no arrest-derived evidence + motion to dismiss","authority":"United States v. Crews, Gerstein v. Pugh, Frisbie v. Collins.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-ARREST-REMEDY-01","statement":"Lock the remedy before applying Fourth Amendment exceptions. If the call asks for dismissal and the stem gives no arrest-produced evidence, suppression exceptions are side streets.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"motion to dismiss solely because arrest was unlawful","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"violation_vs_remedy","prompt":"Unlawful arrest; no evidence or statement obtained; independent evidence supports indictment. Motion to dismiss?","answer":"Deny; dismissal is not required."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"good-faith side street","prompt":"A choice says dismissal depends on officer good faith. What is the C3 signal?","answer":"Good faith is an evidence-admissibility lane; lock the dismissal call."},{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"unlawful arrest remedy","prompt":"What is the remedy for an unlawful arrest when no evidence was obtained from it?","answer":"No automatic dismissal; suppress tainted evidence only when applicable."},{"drill_type":"overclaim","target_skill":"automatic indictment nullity","prompt":"A choice says an unlawful arrest voids any later indictment. Name the mold.","answer":"tiered_absolute."}]}'),
  ('2469bc75-26b4-5c2d-8e41-90ec8867bd88', '20404_acts_axes_cash_loads', 'EVIDENCE', 'Materiality and probative value', 'A4', NULL, 'Mary operates a private “Acts & Axes” booth that sells scripture-engraved toy arks at summer fairs. She sues Stephen, her weekend cash-box clerk, for taking booth receipts. Before the records evidence is offered, Stephen testifies that Mary paid him $3,200 for the season and that he never took any receipts. Mary calls a prepaid-card records custodian, who will testify that during that same season Stephen made $18,600 in cash loads onto his personal prepaid card. Stephen objects that the cash loads could have come from birthday gifts or family loans.', 'Mary operates a private “Acts & Axes” booth that sells scripture-engraved toy arks at summer fairs. She sues Stephen, her weekend cash-box clerk, for taking booth receipts. Before the records evidence is offered, Stephen testifies that Mary paid him $3,200 for the season and that he never took any receipts. Mary calls a prepaid-card records custodian, who will testify that during that same season Stephen made $18,600 in cash loads onto his personal prepaid card. Stephen objects that the cash loads could have come from birthday gifts or family loans.

The custodian’s testimony is:', 'The custodian’s testimony is:', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20404.md","source_question_id":"20404","variant_slug":"acts_axes_cash_loads","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"trap repair","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A civil-theft defendant denies taking receipts and claims low seasonal pay; the plaintiff offers records showing much larger same-season cash deposits. Is that admissible even though the deposits have possible innocent explanations?","review_truth":"A clue can be admissible even when it is not a confession; weakness usually goes to weight, not admissibility.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"EVI-REM-ANY-TENDENCY-01","title":"Relevance is a clue threshold","signal":"Opponent says the evidence has innocent explanations or is not conclusive.","student_move":"Ask what fact the evidence tends to make more likely.","tiny_rule":"A clue need not be a confession.","trap":"demanding certainty before admissibility","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-ANY-TENDENCY-01","statement":"Relevant evidence does not need to prove the whole case by itself. If it has any tendency to make a consequential fact more probable, it clears the relevance threshold.","type":"threshold","trigger":"The opponent says the evidence could have another explanation.","authority":"Fed. R. Evid. 401, 402, 403; see pass-1 legal verification.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-EVIDENCE-RELEVANCE-ARRAY-01","statement":"In a relevance array, cut ''conclusive,'' ''always,'' and ''only'' before weighing the story. The survivor usually states what the evidence tends to show.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Three choices narrow admissibility; one states the fact relation.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"Classify: inadmissible because financial records are always unfairly prejudicial.","answer":"tiered_absolute; NOT_TRUE."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"relevance threshold","prompt":"Does circumstantial evidence need to conclusively prove the fact before it is admissible?","answer":"No. Any tendency is enough if the fact matters."},{"drill_type":"BAIT_ARCHITECTURE","target_skill":"offered_for_purpose","prompt":"Records contradict a witness and also support the claim. Is an only-impeachment answer safe?","answer":"No. It underclaims the substantive use."}]}'),
  ('b353cc43-edc3-5b5a-af74-6a94f7a62811', '20423_galilee_props_bias', 'EVIDENCE', 'Opinion or reputation', 'E5', 'religious-belief truthfulness use versus organizational-affiliation bias use', 'At a federal civil trial over a broken Christmas-pageant stage wagon, Peter sues Galilee Props, Inc., a private prop-rental company. Ruth testifies for Galilee. On cross-examination, Peter’s lawyer asks Ruth whether she is the paid treasurer of New Covenant Fellowship, the religious organization that owns Galilee’s voting shares and appoints its managers. Galilee objects under the rule barring religious-belief credibility evidence. Peter says he offers the question to show Ruth’s possible bias toward Galilee, not to suggest that her religious views make her truthful or untruthful. How should the court rule?', 'At a federal civil trial over a broken Christmas-pageant stage wagon, Peter sues Galilee Props, Inc., a private prop-rental company. Ruth testifies for Galilee. On cross-examination, Peter’s lawyer asks Ruth whether she is the paid treasurer of New Covenant Fellowship, the religious organization that owns Galilee’s voting shares and appoints its managers. Galilee objects under the rule barring religious-belief credibility evidence. Peter says he offers the question to show Ruth’s possible bias toward Galilee, not to suggest that her religious views make her truthful or untruthful. How should the court rule?

How should the court rule?', 'How should the court rule?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20423.md","source_question_id":"20423","variant_slug":"galilee_props_bias","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"31010505","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"trap","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A witness has a paid role in a religious organization that controls a party. The question is offered to show bias, not religious truthfulness. Should the objection be sustained?","review_truth":"Religion evidence is barred for truthfulness character, not for bias from affiliation or interest.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-EVIDENCE-R610-BIAS-01","title":"Religion Evidence: Purpose Split","signal":"Religious affiliation appears, but the proponent says bias.","student_move":"Do not stop at the religious-evidence objection. Ask the offered purpose.","tiny_rule":"Belief-as-truthfulness is barred; affiliation-for-bias is not.","trap":"Blanket religious-affiliation ban.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-RELIGION-BIAS-01","statement":"Rule 610 blocks religion-as-truthfulness evidence. It does not block religious affiliation or organizational ties when offered to show bias, interest, or relationship to a party.","type":"distinction","trigger":"religion objection plus paid role or organizational tie to a party","authority":"Federal Rule of Evidence 610 Advisory Committee Note","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-EVIDENCE-PURPOSE-AXIS-01","statement":"When an Evidence answer turns on the same fact being used for different credibility purposes, name the offered purpose first and grade only that lane.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"The stem says ''offered to show bias'' and ''not to show truthfulness.''","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"purpose_axis","target_skill":"offered_for_purpose","prompt":"A witness belongs to a religious organization connected to a party. The question is offered to show bias. Is Rule 610 a blanket bar?","answer":"No. Bias purpose is outside the belief-as-truthfulness ban."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Cut the flaw: any reference to religious affiliation is inadmissible.","answer":"Overclaim. Any erases the bias-purpose lane."},{"drill_type":"bait_doctrine_sort","target_skill":"bait_doctrine","prompt":"Truthful character may be supported only after attack. Does that govern a bias question?","answer":"No. That is Rule 608(a) truthfulness-character doctrine, not bias."},{"drill_type":"fabricated_rule_cut","target_skill":"fabricated_rule","prompt":"Bias may be shown only by reputation evidence.","answer":"Cut. The only limit is invented."}]}'),
  ('1123b3fb-ff74-5bdb-8150-29e0ff95c7ea', '20439_floating_star_driveway', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Standard of Due Care / Best Argument', NULL, 'Timothy was walking on the public sidewalk past Lydia’s house on Saturday morning. Lydia, a professional director of children’s Christmas pageants, was rehearsing a ''floating star'' scene for a neighborhood Advent program. She tested a rented battery-powered cable rig in her open driveway beside the sidewalk. A defect in the rig caused a plywood star to swing outside the driveway and strike Timothy.', 'Timothy was walking on the public sidewalk past Lydia’s house on Saturday morning. Lydia, a professional director of children’s Christmas pageants, was rehearsing a ''floating star'' scene for a neighborhood Advent program. She tested a rented battery-powered cable rig in her open driveway beside the sidewalk. A defect in the rig caused a plywood star to swing outside the driveway and strike Timothy.

If Timothy asserts a claim against Lydia, Timothy’s most effective argument in support of his claim would be that', 'If Timothy asserts a claim against Lydia, Timothy’s most effective argument in support of his claim would be that', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20439.md","source_question_id":"20439","variant_slug":"floating_star_driveway","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"moderate-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A professional pageant director uses a defective stage rig in an exposed driveway and a passerby is hurt. Which argument best supports the passerby’s tort claim?","review_truth":"A tort claim needs an operative liability basis; unreasonable conduct can supply negligence, while product defect, causation, or professional status alone does not.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-20439-01","title":"Cause/status/product are not the claim","signal":"A Torts answer names only cause, status, or product defect.","student_move":"Ask whether the answer supplies a liability basis.","tiny_rule":"For negligence, look for unreasonable conduct.","trap":"Choosing a legally flavored fragment instead of the operative ground.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-USER-SUPPLIER-01","statement":"Strict products liability for a defective product is aimed at commercial sellers or distributors, not a person who merely uses the product.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The defendant is using, testing, or operating a product rather than selling or distributing it.","authority":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Products Liability § 1.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-TORTS-FAULT-BASIS-01","statement":"Cause and status are not enough; the plaintiff’s argument must supply negligence, intent, or a strict-liability category. Unreasonable conduct supplies the negligence route.","type":"threshold","trigger":"A best-argument call with choices that name cause, status, or product defect.","authority":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Physical and Emotional Harm § 3.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-BEST-ARGUMENT-01","statement":"On a best-argument call, pick the answer that gives the plaintiff a legal route to liability; do not require the answer to prove the whole case.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The call asks for the claimant’s most effective argument.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"A Torts answer says only that the defendant’s act was a concurring cause. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut unless another part of the answer supplies negligence, intent, or strict liability."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"bait_doctrine","prompt":"A passerby is injured by a defective device used by a pageant director. The answer says the director is strictly liable for using a defective product. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut; strict products liability targets commercial sellers or distributors, not mere users."},{"drill_type":"CALL","target_skill":"call_focus","prompt":"The call asks for the plaintiff’s most effective argument. One answer gives possible unreasonable conduct; another gives only causation. Which wins?","answer":"The unreasonable-conduct answer."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"negligence reasonableness","prompt":"Which phrase best supplies negligence: professional status, concurring cause, unreasonable conduct, or product defect?","answer":"Unreasonable conduct."}]}'),
  ('f9fb9885-1afb-5e65-b06f-987ee4dd5da4', '20461_lydia_beeswax_requirements', 'CONTRACTS', 'Offer / valid offer requirements', 'II.A.1.a', NULL, 'Barnabas Wax Co. promptly accepted a signed email from Lydia, who runs a Bible-verse candle studio. The email said: “I offer to buy all the beeswax my studio requires for the next nine months at $18 per slab, shipment every other Friday.” Lydia later asserted that no contract had formed because the email did not list a fixed number of slabs.', 'Barnabas Wax Co. promptly accepted a signed email from Lydia, who runs a Bible-verse candle studio. The email said: “I offer to buy all the beeswax my studio requires for the next nine months at $18 per slab, shipment every other Friday.” Lydia later asserted that no contract had formed because the email did not list a fixed number of slabs.

Which statement is most accurate?', 'Which statement is most accurate?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20461.md","source_question_id":"20461","variant_slug":"lydia_beeswax_requirements","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020203","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"trap","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Buyer offers to buy all goods her business requires at a set unit price and delivery schedule; seller accepts; buyer says no fixed number means no contract. Is the quantity too indefinite?","review_truth":"A requirements term is a quantity term when it measures the buyer''s actual good-faith needs.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CONTRACTS-REQ-QTY-01","title":"Flexible quantity is not empty quantity","signal":"all goods the buyer requires","student_move":"Do not equate no fixed number with no quantity.","tiny_rule":"Requirements quantity is measured by good-faith needs.","trap":"Illusory-promise overread.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-REQUIREMENTS-01","statement":"A requirements term is a quantity term: it measures the buyer''s quantity by actual good-faith needs, not by a fixed number.","type":"rule","trigger":"all goods the buyer requires","authority":"UCC 2-306(1)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-REQUIREMENTS-ARRAY-01","statement":"When three choices say no contract and the stem gives a requirements phrase, cut the no-contract reasons one at a time before assuming the missing number is fatal.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"No fixed number objection after an ''all I require'' phrase.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"requirements quantity","prompt":"A buyer offers to purchase all widgets her shop requires for six months. No fixed number is stated. Is the quantity automatically too indefinite?","answer":"No. Requirements quantity can be measured by good-faith needs."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Cut or keep: ''Requirements quantities are always illusory.''","answer":"Cut: tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"wrong_frame","target_skill":"price quote vs offer","prompt":"Stem says buyer wrote ''I offer to buy'' and seller accepted. Choice says the writing was only a price quote. What is the C3 label?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE / bait_doctrine / wrong_frame."},{"drill_type":"anchor_assisted_cut","target_skill":"adequacy review","prompt":"Choice says no contract unless the court finds the stated price adequate. What is the problem?","answer":"Fabricated adequacy gate; anchor-assisted cut."}]}'),
  ('ac21ec1c-a564-5e0b-a708-f4e3d5344c03', '20476_road_to_bethlehem_sash', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Fourth Amendment', 'Constitutional Protection of Accused Persons', 'extra manipulation/immediate apparentness vs no-warrant-after-pocket-entry', 'After a city Christmas pageant called Road to Bethlehem, Officer Hannah lawfully stopped Peter on a public plaza near vendor tents where officers had recently seen illegal pill sales. Peter was still wearing his shepherd costume, including a robe with a small sash pocket. During a pat-down of Peter’s outer clothing for weapons, Hannah felt a pea-sized hard tablet in the sash pocket. She knew it was not a weapon. Curious, Hannah rolled and rubbed the tablet through the fabric for a few moments until she decided it felt like an illegal opioid pill. She then reached into the pocket and seized it. Peter moves to suppress.', 'After a city Christmas pageant called Road to Bethlehem, Officer Hannah lawfully stopped Peter on a public plaza near vendor tents where officers had recently seen illegal pill sales. Peter was still wearing his shepherd costume, including a robe with a small sash pocket. During a pat-down of Peter’s outer clothing for weapons, Hannah felt a pea-sized hard tablet in the sash pocket. She knew it was not a weapon. Curious, Hannah rolled and rubbed the tablet through the fabric for a few moments until she decided it felt like an illegal opioid pill. She then reached into the pocket and seized it. Peter moves to suppress.

Was the officer’s warrantless seizure of the pill valid under the plain-feel doctrine?', 'Was the officer’s warrantless seizure of the pill valid under the plain-feel doctrine?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20476.md","source_question_id":"20476","variant_slug":"road_to_bethlehem_sash","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"75070202","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"The officer rolled and rubbed the tablet before deciding it was contraband.","distilled_core_question":"During a lawful weapons frisk, an officer feels a nonweapon object, manipulates it to identify contraband, then seizes it. Is the seizure valid under plain feel?","review_truth":"Plain feel allows seizure only when contraband is immediately apparent during the lawful frisk; extra manipulation of a nonweapon item breaks the search.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CRIMINAL-PLAIN-FEEL-01","title":"Plain feel: immediate apparentness, not tactile investigation","signal":"Lawful frisk; nonweapon object; officer keeps feeling it to identify it.","student_move":"Stop the frisk at the moment the officer knows the item is not a weapon unless contraband is already immediately apparent.","tiny_rule":"Manipulation to identify contraband breaks plain feel.","trap":"Treating Terry frisk as permission to search for drugs.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-PLAIN-FEEL-01","statement":"Plain feel works only when the contraband identity is immediately apparent during the lawful weapons frisk. If the officer must squeeze, roll, rub, or otherwise manipulate a nonweapon item to identify it, the frisk has become a new search.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Lawful frisk + nonweapon object + extra manipulation before identification","authority":"Minnesota v. Dickerson, 508 U.S. 366 (1993); Terry v. Ohio, 392 U.S. 1 (1968)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-PLAIN-FEEL-01","statement":"When two choices both say the seizure is invalid, ask which one names the first unlawful move. Here, the first unlawful move is the extra manipulation, not the later hand-in-pocket act.","type":"clash","trigger":"Two ''No'' answers with different defect rationales","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"During a lawful frisk, the answer says any item found may be seized. Name the mold.","answer":"tiered_absolute"},{"drill_type":"CLASH","target_skill":"same-result pair","prompt":"Two answers both say suppression. One names manipulation before identification; one names lack of warrant before pocket entry. Which fact resolves it?","answer":"The officer manipulated the nonweapon object before identifying contraband."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"plain feel immediate apparentness","prompt":"What must be true before an officer may seize contraband felt during a lawful frisk?","answer":"Its contraband character must be immediately apparent by touch."},{"drill_type":"CALL","target_skill":"call focus","prompt":"A choice justifies a drug search because the area was known for drug sales. Why is it off-call?","answer":"The call asks about plain-feel seizure validity, not drug-investigation suspicion."}]}'),
  ('f83f9b86-2b38-50ad-8f92-bbe89b6dbd99', '20493_fish_mark_picnic_lot', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Conveyance by Deed — Requirements for Valid Deed', 'B1', NULL, 'Esther owned a small vacant lot that she used for neighborhood Bible-memory picnics. After one picnic, she pulled a clean paper lunch wrapper from the basket and wrote, “I convey my vacant lot at 48 Galilee Court to Timothy.” Because Esther’s hands were too stiff to write her name, she drew a small fish symbol at the bottom intending it as her signature. She gave the wrapper to Timothy and said, “This little lot belongs to you today.” Timothy accepted. Timothy paid nothing, and nobody notarized or recorded the wrapper.', 'Esther owned a small vacant lot that she used for neighborhood Bible-memory picnics. After one picnic, she pulled a clean paper lunch wrapper from the basket and wrote, “I convey my vacant lot at 48 Galilee Court to Timothy.” Because Esther’s hands were too stiff to write her name, she drew a small fish symbol at the bottom intending it as her signature. She gave the wrapper to Timothy and said, “This little lot belongs to you today.” Timothy accepted. Timothy paid nothing, and nobody notarized or recorded the wrapper.

Who owns the lot?', 'Who owns the lot?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20493.md","source_question_id":"20493","variant_slug":"fish_mark_picnic_lot","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"core diagnostic","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A landowner writes present transfer words identifying herself, the grantee, and the land, signs by an intended mark, delivers the writing, and the grantee accepts. No money is paid, and the writing is not notarized or recorded. Who owns the land?","review_truth":"A deed can be valid between grantor and grantee without a printed form, consideration, notarization, or recording if the minimal transfer formalities are present.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RP-ANCHOR-01","title":"Minimal Deed Formalities","signal":"Informal writing, present transfer words, no money, no notarization, no recording.","student_move":"Ask whether the writing hits the minimum transfer elements before adding extra gates.","tiny_rule":"Writing + parties + land + present transfer + grantor authentication + delivery + acceptance is enough between the parties.","trap":"Contract or courthouse instincts add requirements the tested rule does not require.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-REAL_PROPERTY-MINIMAL-DEED-FORMALITIES-01","statement":"A deed can be valid between grantor and grantee if it is a writing that identifies the grantor, grantee, land, and present transfer, is signed or otherwise authenticated by the grantor, and is delivered and accepted. No printed deed form, consideration, notarization, or recording is required for baseline validity between them.","type":"rule","trigger":"Who owns land after an informal written transfer with present words, mark, handoff, and acceptance?","authority":"Common-law/bar baseline; representative authorities include statutes and cases recognizing writing, subscription, delivery, signature by mark, no consideration requirement, and validity of unrecorded instruments between parties.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-REAL_PROPERTY-EXTRA-GATE-ARRAY-01","statement":"When three deed answers add formal extra gates and one answer lists the minimal transfer elements, treat the array as an extra-gate sweep after the deed-formality anchor.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"An informal writing plus present transfer words is followed by three formal-sounding objections.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"minimal deed formalities","prompt":"A landowner writes present transfer words on ordinary paper, signs by an intended mark, delivers it, and the grantee accepts. No money is paid. Does title pass between them?","answer":"Yes, if the writing identifies grantor, grantee, land, present transfer, and is delivered and accepted."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"extra-gate sweep","prompt":"Which added gate is not required for baseline deed validity: printed deed form, consideration, recording, or all three?","answer":"All three are not required under the tested baseline."},{"drill_type":"crossover","target_skill":"recording acts separation","prompt":"What does recording usually protect that deed delivery already accomplished between the parties?","answer":"Recording protects notice/priority against third parties; delivery and acceptance transfer between the parties."}]}'),
  ('b2a91b44-8dc8-5e17-9624-e558967e5fdf', '20496_loaves_prop_cart', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Damages / Collateral Source Rule', 'Who paid?', 'At Esther’s backyard “Loaves and Fishes” game night, Lydia was standing beside a table of Bible-verse prize cards when Daniel negligently lost control of a wheeled prop cart stacked with foam bread baskets. The cart struck Lydia and caused injuries that required emergency treatment. Lydia’s medical bills were substantial, but they were paid in full under a health-insurance policy that Lydia had purchased before the game night.

Lydia sued Daniel for negligence. The jury found for Lydia and awarded her $64,000. Before judgment was entered, Daniel’s lawyer made a proper motion asking the court to reduce the award by the amount Lydia had already received from her health insurer.

The motion to reduce Lydia’s damage award should be:', 'At Esther’s backyard “Loaves and Fishes” game night, Lydia was standing beside a table of Bible-verse prize cards when Daniel negligently lost control of a wheeled prop cart stacked with foam bread baskets. The cart struck Lydia and caused injuries that required emergency treatment. Lydia’s medical bills were substantial, but they were paid in full under a health-insurance policy that Lydia had purchased before the game night.

Lydia sued Daniel for negligence. The jury found for Lydia and awarded her $64,000. Before judgment was entered, Daniel’s lawyer made a proper motion asking the court to reduce the award by the amount Lydia had already received from her health insurer.

The motion to reduce Lydia’s damage award should be:

The motion to reduce Lydia''s damage award should be:', 'The motion to reduce Lydia''s damage award should be:', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20496.md","source_question_id":"20496","variant_slug":"loaves_prop_cart","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020500","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium_hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"The plaintiff''s own insurer paid her medical bills before she won a negligence verdict. May the negligent defendant reduce the verdict by that insurance payment?","review_truth":"Plaintiff-side insurance is collateral; it does not reduce the negligent defendant''s damages judgment.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"TOR-REM-COLLATERAL-SOURCE-01","title":"Who paid?","signal":"Insurance payment before or after tort verdict.","student_move":"Sort the payer: defendant-side, joint-tortfeasor-side, or plaintiff-side.","tiny_rule":"Plaintiff-side insurance does not reduce the judgment.","trap":"Double recovery feels fair but points the wrong way.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-COLLATERAL-SOURCE-01","statement":"Plaintiff-side insurance is collateral. A negligence defendant gets no credit for benefits paid by the plaintiff''s own insurer; credit is for payments by the defendant, someone acting for the defendant, or another actor subject to the same tort liability.","type":"rule","trigger":"Defendant asks to reduce a tort award because plaintiff''s own insurer already paid.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 920A.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-COLLATERAL-SOURCE-01","statement":"For setoff questions, ask who paid and whose liability the payer was tied to before reacting to double-recovery language.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"Motion to reduce damages after insurance or settlement payment.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"Collateral source rule","prompt":"A plaintiff''s own insurer paid medical bills before a tort verdict. Can the defendant reduce the verdict by that amount?","answer":"No. Plaintiff-side insurance is collateral."},{"drill_type":"Source sorting","target_skill":"Setoff payer classification","prompt":"Classify the payer: defendant, joint tortfeasor, plaintiff''s own insurer.","answer":"Only plaintiff''s own insurer is collateral."},{"drill_type":"Trap language","target_skill":"Fairness bait detection","prompt":"A choice says ''no double recovery.'' What question must you ask first?","answer":"Who paid, and whose liability was the payer tied to?"}]}'),
  ('28565236-896f-5b44-8503-93ec5ca156a9', '20516_manna_games_duress', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Defenses', 'General Principles', 'imminent threshold vs real-but-incomplete fear/family facts', 'Timothy works the evening shift in the shipping room at Manna Games, a private company that sells Bible-themed board games and collector study-Bible sets. On Monday night, Barnabas, a collector with a reputation for using hired muscle, sends Timothy a video message from a retreat center three states away: ''Before the warehouse closes on Friday, take the locked case of gold-edition study Bibles worth $18,000 and leave it behind the Noah’s Ark mini-golf sign, or after next Sunday’s hymn-sing I will have your sister Hannah badly hurt.'' Timothy believes Barnabas is serious and is terrified for Hannah. On Thursday, Timothy takes the case. Charged with theft, Timothy asserts duress.', 'Timothy works the evening shift in the shipping room at Manna Games, a private company that sells Bible-themed board games and collector study-Bible sets. On Monday night, Barnabas, a collector with a reputation for using hired muscle, sends Timothy a video message from a retreat center three states away: ''Before the warehouse closes on Friday, take the locked case of gold-edition study Bibles worth $18,000 and leave it behind the Noah’s Ark mini-golf sign, or after next Sunday’s hymn-sing I will have your sister Hannah badly hurt.'' Timothy believes Barnabas is serious and is terrified for Hannah. On Thursday, Timothy takes the case. Charged with theft, Timothy asserts duress.

Should the court accept Timothy’s duress defense?', 'Should the court accept Timothy’s duress defense?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20516.md","source_question_id":"20516","variant_slug":"manna_games_duress","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"72060206","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium_high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A worker commits theft after a remote person threatens to hurt his sister at a future time. Does duress succeed?","review_truth":"Duress is not fear alone; it requires an imminent human threat and no reasonable legal alternative.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CRIM-DURESS-IMMINENCE","title":"Duress requires now-pressure.","signal":"Future threat, remote threatener, time to seek help.","student_move":"Cut fear-only and family-only answers; keep the answer that checks imminence.","tiny_rule":"Duress is immediate-threat plus no reasonable escape, not fear alone.","trap":"Sympathy for a believable family threat.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-DURESS-IMMINENCE-01","statement":"Common-law duress needs an imminent human threat of death or serious bodily harm, directed at the defendant or a close family member, with no reasonable legal alternative.","type":"threshold","trigger":"Threat scheduled for later, threatener absent, time to seek help.","authority":"Common-law duress; United States v. Bailey and federal duress formulations.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-DURESS-SCOPE-02","statement":"Duress is not a violent-crime-only defense; theft is not excluded if the duress elements are met.","type":"exception","trigger":"Answer says duress is only for violent crimes.","authority":"Common-law duress scope; homicide limitation.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-DURESS-ARRAY-01","statement":"When two Yes answers each name one sympathetic fact and a No answer names a missing threshold, treat the array as an element-completion test.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Defense-success call with multiple partial-element answers.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"duress threshold","prompt":"A defendant steals after a threatener far away promises to hurt a sibling next week. Does duress succeed?","answer":"No. The threat is future and remote, so imminence is missing."},{"drill_type":"wrong_element cut","target_skill":"partial defense elements","prompt":"Choice says: ''Yes, because the threat was to a close family member.'' Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut as NOT_RESPONSIVE / wrong_element unless imminence and no escape are also satisfied."},{"drill_type":"tiered_absolute cut","target_skill":"scope overclaim","prompt":"Choice says: ''No, because duress is only for violent crimes.'' Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut as NOT_TRUE / tiered_absolute."}]}'),
  ('eb31e2d3-0f2f-5f47-9a19-c735af54fba0', '20523_easter_stage_guaranty', 'CONTRACTS', 'Statute of Frauds', 'D', 'extra formalities versus party-to-be-charged memorandum', 'After Peter failed to pay a private event-supply company for the portable stage and speakers used at a ticketed Easter concert at his bookstore café, the company sued Ruth. Before the rental, Ruth had orally told the company that she would pay Peter’s $18,400 rental bill if Peter did not. That evening, Ruth signed a note to Lydia, Peter’s concert scheduler, stating: “I told Galilee Gear Rentals I would cover Peter’s $18,400 stage-and-speaker rental if Peter does not pay it.” Ruth argues that the signed note cannot satisfy the Statute of Frauds because it was sent to Lydia, not to Galilee Gear Rentals.', 'After Peter failed to pay a private event-supply company for the portable stage and speakers used at a ticketed Easter concert at his bookstore café, the company sued Ruth. Before the rental, Ruth had orally told the company that she would pay Peter’s $18,400 rental bill if Peter did not. That evening, Ruth signed a note to Lydia, Peter’s concert scheduler, stating: “I told Galilee Gear Rentals I would cover Peter’s $18,400 stage-and-speaker rental if Peter does not pay it.” Ruth argues that the signed note cannot satisfy the Statute of Frauds because it was sent to Lydia, not to Galilee Gear Rentals.

Can Ruth’s signed note potentially satisfy the suretyship writing requirement?', 'Can Ruth’s signed note potentially satisfy the suretyship writing requirement?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20523.md","source_question_id":"20523","variant_slug":"easter_stage_guaranty","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"54030400","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"trap repair","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A surety signs a note to someone other than the creditor admitting she promised to pay another person’s debt if that person defaults. Can that note potentially satisfy the Statute of Frauds?","review_truth":"The Statute of Frauds memorandum test looks to the signer and content, not whether the writing was addressed to the enforcing party.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CONTRACTS-ANCHOR-SOF-MEMO-ADDRESSEE-01","title":"SOF memorandum: signer beats addressee","signal":"A signed writing admits a guaranty but was sent to someone other than the enforcing creditor.","student_move":"Map who is being sued; then ask whether that person signed a writing identifying the promise.","tiny_rule":"The memorandum need not be addressed to the enforcing party.","trap":"extra formality","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-SURETY-MEMO-THIRD-PERSON-01","statement":"For a common-law Statute of Frauds memorandum, the writing need not be addressed or delivered to the enforcing party. Ask whether it is signed by the party to be charged and whether it identifies the promise, parties, and essential terms.","type":"distinction","trigger":"signed note admits a guaranty but was sent to someone other than the creditor","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 131, 133; lawyer-confirmation only","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-SURETY-MEMO-ARRAY-01","statement":"When three answers add formalities and one answer tracks the signer-plus-content path, group the formalities first. Then cut the addressee and extra-signature conditions as the same trap family.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"choices say must be addressed, must always be signed by both, or only if debtor signed","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"SOF memorandum sufficiency","prompt":"A guarantor signs a note to the debtor admitting the guaranty. The creditor sues. Does the note fail solely because it was addressed to the debtor?","answer":"No. Addressee is not the test; signer and content are."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"extra-formality detection","prompt":"Cut the answer: ''No, because both the debtor and guarantor must always sign the guaranty memorandum.''","answer":"CUT as tiered_absolute after the Gold Key; the party being charged must sign."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"formalities grouping","prompt":"Choices add creditor-addressing, both-signature, and debtor-signature requirements. What is the shared trap?","answer":"Extra formalities beyond the party-to-be-charged signed memorandum."}]}'),
  ('7405f59f-9110-5e72-a456-4505ca50bfbe', '20542_praise_festival_cashbox', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Sixth Amendment', 'Constitutional Protection of Accused Persons', 'same-offense substance versus different label', 'Peter is indicted for armed robbery of Lydia''s traveling Scripture-calligraphy booth at a private praise-music festival, and counsel is appointed. Later, while counsel is absent, detectives visit Peter at his apartment. They say they only want to ask whether he committed ''the unarmed theft of the cash box from that same booth during the closing-night festival rush.'' They obtain no waiver. Peter admits that he took Lydia''s cash box during that incident.', 'Peter is indicted for armed robbery of Lydia''s traveling Scripture-calligraphy booth at a private praise-music festival, and counsel is appointed. Later, while counsel is absent, detectives visit Peter at his apartment. They say they only want to ask whether he committed ''the unarmed theft of the cash box from that same booth during the closing-night festival rush.'' They obtain no waiver. Peter admits that he took Lydia''s cash box during that incident.

Should Peter''s statement be suppressed under the Sixth Amendment?', 'Should Peter''s statement be suppressed under the Sixth Amendment?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20542.md","source_question_id":"20542","variant_slug":"praise_festival_cashbox","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"75090400","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"The question concerned the same booth, same cash box, and same festival incident.","distilled_core_question":"After indictment and counsel, police ask without waiver about a relabeled lesser version of the same charged incident. Suppress?","review_truth":"After indictment, the Sixth Amendment follows the charged offense in substance, not the police''s new label.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CRIM-REM-20542-SAME-OFFENSE","title":"Same offense, new label","signal":"Police say they only want to ask about a smaller version of the charged incident.","student_move":"Do not stop at the label. Compare the factual event and offense substance.","tiny_rule":"Post-charge Sixth Amendment protection follows the same offense in substance.","trap":"The label-only answer sounds precise but is too shallow.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-MASSIAH-SAME-OFFENSE-01","statement":"After formal charge, the Sixth Amendment bars deliberate elicitation about the charged offense without counsel or waiver; a lesser-included or same-Blockburger relabeling stays inside the charged offense.","type":"distinction","trigger":"indictment/counsel appointed + police ask about a renamed version of the same incident","authority":"Massiah; Fellers; McNeil; Texas v. Cobb; Brown v. Ohio","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-MASSIAH-CUSTODY-01","statement":"Sixth Amendment post-charge deliberate elicitation is not the same test as Fifth Amendment custodial interrogation.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Sixth Amendment call + post-indictment questioning","authority":"Fellers; Massiah","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-LABEL-VS-SUBSTANCE-01","statement":"When the choices fight over crime labels, go back to the stem and ask whether the officers are really asking about the same charged event in a smaller costume.","type":"clash","trigger":"charged armed robbery followed by police asking about unarmed theft from the same event","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"Sixth Amendment attachment and same-offense boundary","prompt":"After indictment, police ask without counsel about a relabeled lesser version of the charged incident. What is the trigger?","answer":"Same charged offense in substance after attachment; suppress without counsel or waiver."},{"drill_type":"Wrong-frame CUT","target_skill":"Miranda vs Massiah","prompt":"A choice says no suppression because the defendant was not in custody. The call is Sixth Amendment after indictment. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut as bait_doctrine / wrong_frame."},{"drill_type":"CLASH axis","target_skill":"same offense versus different label","prompt":"One answer says same charged offense; another says different labels. What fact decides?","answer":"Whether the questioning targets the same incident and same offense in substance."},{"drill_type":"Overclaim scan","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Police may not question an indicted defendant about any unrelated offense. What is the mold?","answer":"tiered_absolute; the Sixth Amendment right is offense-specific."}]}'),
  ('bfc302af-90b9-5aa8-b232-f6d984d4af83', '20546_jericho_wall_studio', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Premises liability / invitee dangerous condition', 'named-defendant premises breach vs adjacent product/contractor/status facts', 'Lydia owned Selah Studio, a private Bible-verse calligraphy studio open to the public for paid lettering workshops and sales of framed Scripture cards. After closing, Lydia hired Barnabas to install a motorized “Jericho Wall” display in the front room. When a participant stepped on a pressure mat, wooden panels were supposed to slide apart to reveal the day’s featured verse.

Barnabas read the manufacturer’s instructions but disregarded the warnings. When he finished, he did not test the display by stepping on the pressure mat, but told Ruth, Lydia’s night manager, that the installation was complete. The next morning, when Selah Studio opened, Mary entered and was struck by one of the moving panels. Mary asserted a claim for her injuries against Lydia.', 'Lydia owned Selah Studio, a private Bible-verse calligraphy studio open to the public for paid lettering workshops and sales of framed Scripture cards. After closing, Lydia hired Barnabas to install a motorized “Jericho Wall” display in the front room. When a participant stepped on a pressure mat, wooden panels were supposed to slide apart to reveal the day’s featured verse.

Barnabas read the manufacturer’s instructions but disregarded the warnings. When he finished, he did not test the display by stepping on the pressure mat, but told Ruth, Lydia’s night manager, that the installation was complete. The next morning, when Selah Studio opened, Mary entered and was struck by one of the moving panels. Mary asserted a claim for her injuries against Lydia.

Which one of the following additional facts or inferences, if it was the only one true, would be most likely to result in a judgment for Mary?', 'Which one of the following additional facts or inferences, if it was the only one true, would be most likely to result in a judgment for Mary?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20546.md","source_question_id":"20546","variant_slug":"jericho_wall_studio","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium_high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A public business visitor is injured by a newly installed moving device. Which added fact most supports judgment against the owner: defect, contractor non-negligence, reasonable owner inspection, or completed payment?","review_truth":"The winning premises-liability fact links the owner to a danger discoverable by reasonable inspection.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-NAMED-DEFENDANT-INSPECTION-01","title":"Owner-inspection fact beats product/contractor/status bait","signal":"Premises owner is sued after a third party installed a dangerous device.","student_move":"Ask which choice supplies breach by the named owner.","tiny_rule":"For invitees, reasonable inspection can be the breach path.","trap":"Product defect and contractor conduct feel causal but do not answer owner breach.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-JERICHO-WALL-01","statement":"For a business invitee, owner liability turns on dangerous conditions the owner knew about or would discover by reasonable care, plus failure to protect.","type":"rule","trigger":"public-facing business premises plus customer injury from a condition on the premises","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 332, 343","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-TORTS-PRODUCT-SELLER-01","statement":"A product defect alone points to strict products liability only when the defendant is in the business of selling that type of product.","type":"threshold","trigger":"injury from a defective component but defendant is sued as premises owner","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 402A","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-JERICHO-WALL-01","statement":"Lock the named defendant before reading the added facts: the winning fact must create liability for Lydia, not for Barnabas or the product chain.","type":"cut","trigger":"one defendant named in the call, but choices mention product defect, contractor conduct, and customer payment","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"named-defendant filtering","prompt":"Premises owner is sued; one choice says the contractor was careful. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut as NOT_RESPONSIVE unless it supplies owner breach."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong_frame detection","prompt":"Premises owner is sued; one choice says the device had a manufacturing defect. What is the trap?","answer":"Product frame; it needs seller status or owner-discoverability."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"invitee inspection duty","prompt":"What owner-side fact most supports an invitee premises claim?","answer":"The owner knew or reasonable inspection would have discovered the dangerous condition."},{"drill_type":"CALIBRATION","target_skill":"anchor-assisted clean cut","prompt":"Three choices miss named-defendant breach; one gives reasonable inspection by owner. Confidence?","answer":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED; commit after call/read check."}]}'),
  ('51038e24-e533-5032-bcb7-c1daf5e985cc', '20556_mustard_seed_puppet_caravan', 'CONTRACTS', 'Performance, Breach, and Discharge', 'Prospective inability to perform; reliance before cure', 'later readiness / label-only answer vs reliance before cure', 'Martha owns and operates Mustard Seed Puppet Caravan, a private for-profit company that tours Bible-story puppet shows for families. The caravan hires only one full-time performer-driver for each tour season because that person travels with the caravan, sleeps in its bunk compartment, receives meals, drives between towns, and maintains the puppets and sound equipment. On September 3, Stephen entered into a valid written contract with Martha. Under the contract, Stephen was to begin traveling with the caravan on January 10 and continue until July 10. Martha was to pay Stephen $18,500 at the end of the season, and Stephen was to receive meals and bunk space during the tour. On September 18, Stephen signed a three-year contract to join an overseas gospel recording tour. That tour was scheduled to begin on December 20. A notice of Stephen''s new tour contract appeared in the "Praise Notes" column of a local arts newsletter and was read by Martha''s managers. Martha immediately began looking for another performer-driver. On December 5, Martha entered into a valid written contract with Barnabas on terms identical to Stephen''s contract. On December 12, Stephen failed the overseas tour''s health-clearance requirement and was released from that contract. On January 10, Stephen arrived at Martha''s garage ready to travel with the caravan, but Martha told him that his services were not needed.', 'Martha owns and operates Mustard Seed Puppet Caravan, a private for-profit company that tours Bible-story puppet shows for families. The caravan hires only one full-time performer-driver for each tour season because that person travels with the caravan, sleeps in its bunk compartment, receives meals, drives between towns, and maintains the puppets and sound equipment. On September 3, Stephen entered into a valid written contract with Martha. Under the contract, Stephen was to begin traveling with the caravan on January 10 and continue until July 10. Martha was to pay Stephen $18,500 at the end of the season, and Stephen was to receive meals and bunk space during the tour. On September 18, Stephen signed a three-year contract to join an overseas gospel recording tour. That tour was scheduled to begin on December 20. A notice of Stephen''s new tour contract appeared in the "Praise Notes" column of a local arts newsletter and was read by Martha''s managers. Martha immediately began looking for another performer-driver. On December 5, Martha entered into a valid written contract with Barnabas on terms identical to Stephen''s contract. On December 12, Stephen failed the overseas tour''s health-clearance requirement and was released from that contract. On January 10, Stephen arrived at Martha''s garage ready to travel with the caravan, but Martha told him that his services were not needed.

If Stephen asserts a claim against Martha for damages resulting from breach of contract, the court should find for whom?', 'If Stephen asserts a claim against Martha for damages resulting from breach of contract, the court should find for whom?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20556.md","source_question_id":"20556","variant_slug":"mustard_seed_puppet_caravan","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"56050403","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"Martha hired Barnabas on December 5; Stephen was released on December 12.","distilled_core_question":"A service contractor made himself apparently unable to perform, the other party reasonably hired a replacement before the obstacle disappeared, and the contractor later appeared ready. Who wins the breach claim?","review_truth":"Later readiness does not save the claimant when the other party reasonably relied on apparent future inability before the obstacle disappeared.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CONTRACTS-ANCHOR-PI-01","title":"Prospective inability: reliance before cure","signal":"Future performance appears impossible, then later becomes possible.","student_move":"Ask whether the other party changed position before the cure.","tiny_rule":"Reliance before cure locks the defendant-side discharge.","trap":"Later readiness feels decisive, but it is late.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-PROSPECTIVE-INABILITY-01","statement":"If a party''s own conduct makes future performance reasonably appear impossible, the other side can be discharged when it materially changes position in reliance before the obstacle disappears.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Outside commitment begins before contract performance; replacement hired before later release.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 250, 253, 256.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-RELIANCE-TIMING-01","statement":"In a future-performance clash, prefer the answer that states the reliance/timing fact over the answer that gives only a legal label.","type":"clash","trigger":"Two defendant answers remain; one says only ''anticipatory repudiation,'' the other says reliance.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"timeline_clash","target_skill":"CLASH","prompt":"Replacement hired December 5; outside commitment released December 12. Which date controls?","answer":"December 5 controls because reliance came before cure."},{"drill_type":"label_vs_fact","target_skill":"Issue-Sense","prompt":"One answer says ''repudiation''; another says ''hired replacement in reliance.'' Which is stronger?","answer":"The reliance answer."},{"drill_type":"wrong_timing_cut","target_skill":"CUT","prompt":"A performer is ready on the start date after the company already hired a replacement. What is the trap?","answer":"Later readiness."},{"drill_type":"notice_lure","target_skill":"CUT","prompt":"The performer never personally warned the company, but the company learned from a reliable notice. What is the trap?","answer":"Direct-notice wrong element."}]}'),
  ('ecd6b6fc-0a55-5b04-86a7-6a03e19d75e9', '20565_purple_gear_hymnbook_tote', 'EVIDENCE', 'Laying a Foundation', 'II.B', 'foundation gate vs jury weight', 'In a federal products-liability action over a battery-powered fog machine used in a private Christmas pageant, Lydia offers a cracked purple plastic gear that Stephen found three weeks later inside a hymnbook tote at Martha’s rented banquet hall. Stephen did not see it come from the fog machine; no witness recognizes it; no logo, serial number, or marking ties it to the fog machine; and no evidence shows how it got into the tote. Lydia says the jury can decide what to make of it. The manufacturer objects. How should the court rule?', 'In a federal products-liability action over a battery-powered fog machine used in a private Christmas pageant, Lydia offers a cracked purple plastic gear that Stephen found three weeks later inside a hymnbook tote at Martha’s rented banquet hall. Stephen did not see it come from the fog machine; no witness recognizes it; no logo, serial number, or marking ties it to the fog machine; and no evidence shows how it got into the tote. Lydia says the jury can decide what to make of it. The manufacturer objects. How should the court rule?

How should the court rule?', 'How should the court rule?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20565.md","source_question_id":"20565","variant_slug":"purple_gear_hymnbook_tote","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"32020200","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"core","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Plaintiff offers an unidentified object with no witness, marking, or circumstance connecting it to the product or accident. Should the court admit it for the jury to weigh, or exclude it for lack of foundation?","review_truth":"A jury cannot weigh a physical object until the proponent supplies a threshold link connecting it to the case.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RM-EVI-FOUNDATION-GATE-01","title":"Gate Before Weight","signal":"Unidentified object; no witness; no marking; no chain/source fact.","student_move":"Cut weight arguments until a threshold link exists.","tiny_rule":"A jury weighs weak evidence; it does not supply the first connection.","trap":"Let the jury decide.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-FOUNDATION-GATE-01","statement":"A physical object does not reach the jury on ''maybe.'' The proponent must first supply some link supporting a finding that the object is what it claims or is connected to the case.","type":"threshold","trigger":"An object is offered, but the stem gives no identifying witness, no marks, and no source facts.","authority":"FRE 901(a); FRE 104(b).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-EVIDENCE-FOUNDATION-GATE-01","statement":"When a choice says the jury can weigh it, first ask whether the item crossed the foundation gate. No gate, no weight.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The proponent says the jury can decide what to make of an unidentified object.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"weight-before-foundation","prompt":"An unidentified object is offered with no witness, marking, or source fact. One answer says admit because the opponent can argue weight. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut. Weight comes after foundation."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"burden direction","prompt":"An answer says exclude only if the opponent proves the object came from somewhere else. What is the problem?","answer":"Backwards. The proponent must first show a connection."},{"drill_type":"EAR","target_skill":"tiered absolute","prompt":"An answer says any physical object may be considered by the jury. What is the mold?","answer":"tiered_absolute; it erases the foundation requirement."},{"drill_type":"CALL","target_skill":"threshold before weight","prompt":"Two choices survive: one says weight, one says no foundation. Which layer controls?","answer":"No foundation controls before weight."}]}'),
  ('8205c8ec-4a00-5531-9635-6921258bc689', '20567_psalm_panels_venue', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Erie Doctrine and Choice of Law', 'Federal Statute Directly Controls', 'valid federal statute directly controls vs Erie/state-law-policy bait', 'Mary, a citizen of State Jordan, runs a private business that rents hand-painted “Psalms & Parables” stage panels for paid community programs. She sued Barnabas, a citizen of State Carmel, in the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of State Carmel for breach of a paid restoration contract involving those panels. A State Carmel statute provides that a civil action may be filed only in the county where the defendant resides. Barnabas resides in a county located in the Southern District of State Carmel. Federal venue statute § 1391 provides that venue is proper in a federal district where a substantial part of the events or omissions giving rise to the claim occurred. The design meetings, payment, restoration work, and disputed delivery all occurred in the Northern District.', 'Mary, a citizen of State Jordan, runs a private business that rents hand-painted “Psalms & Parables” stage panels for paid community programs. She sued Barnabas, a citizen of State Carmel, in the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of State Carmel for breach of a paid restoration contract involving those panels. A State Carmel statute provides that a civil action may be filed only in the county where the defendant resides. Barnabas resides in a county located in the Southern District of State Carmel. Federal venue statute § 1391 provides that venue is proper in a federal district where a substantial part of the events or omissions giving rise to the claim occurred. The design meetings, payment, restoration work, and disputed delivery all occurred in the Northern District.

Which venue rule should the federal court apply?', 'Which venue rule should the federal court apply?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20567.md","source_question_id":"20567","variant_slug":"psalm_panels_venue","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"96060100","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Diversity case in federal court. State venue rule points to defendant’s county. § 1391 points to the federal district where the events occurred. The events occurred there. Which venue rule controls?","review_truth":"When a valid federal statute directly governs federal venue, apply that statute before Erie forum-shopping analysis.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-REM-ERIE-FED-STATUTE-01","title":"Federal statute on point beats Erie balancing","signal":"Diversity + state rule + federal statute directly addressing procedure.","student_move":"Ask whether the federal statute directly controls before discussing forum shopping.","tiny_rule":"Federal statute on point first; Erie twin aims only after no controlling federal law.","trap":"State-law claim language makes the state procedural rule feel controlling.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-FED-STATUTE-VENUE-01","statement":"When a valid federal statute directly controls a federal-court procedural issue, apply the federal statute before Erie twin-aims balancing.","type":"rule","trigger":"Diversity case + state procedural rule + federal statute on point.","authority":"28 U.S.C. § 1391; Hanna v. Plumer; Stewart Organization v. Ricoh.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-RULE-SOURCE-CALL-01","statement":"On a ''which rule controls'' call, do not pick the rule that creates the preferred outcome. First identify whether a federal statute directly answers the procedural question.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"Answer choices compare state rule, federal statute, and forum-shopping policy.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"Federal-statute-on-point anchor","prompt":"Diversity case; state service rule conflicts with valid Federal Rule. Which source controls?","answer":"Federal rule/statute on point controls if valid."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"State-law-claim bait","prompt":"Choice says state procedure controls because the claim arises under state law. Name the trap.","answer":"Bait doctrine / wrong frame."},{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"Rule-source call","prompt":"Call asks which rule controls. One answer picks the rule that would dismiss. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut if it answers outcome instead of source-of-rule."},{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"§ 1391 venue","prompt":"What federal district is proper under § 1391(b)(2)?","answer":"A district where a substantial part of the events or omissions giving rise to the claim occurred."}]}'),
  ('30a7b8e0-c813-5d32-a2fe-104e935f29aa', '20569_ark_float', 'CONTRACTS', 'Conditions — Express conditions', 'V-A2', 'exact stated terminating event vs neighboring contract doctrines', 'Lydia rented her hand-painted Noah’s Ark parade float to Ruth for a private neighborhood Bible-verse parade. The rental agreement stated, “The float rental remains effective unless the city engineer limits the float’s permitted load below 1,200 pounds before parade day.” The day before the parade, the city engineer issued an order limiting the float’s permitted load to 700 pounds. Lydia refused to provide the float.', 'Lydia rented her hand-painted Noah’s Ark parade float to Ruth for a private neighborhood Bible-verse parade. The rental agreement stated, “The float rental remains effective unless the city engineer limits the float’s permitted load below 1,200 pounds before parade day.” The day before the parade, the city engineer issued an order limiting the float’s permitted load to 700 pounds. Lydia refused to provide the float.

Which statement is most accurate?', 'Which statement is most accurate?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20569.md","source_question_id":"20569","variant_slug":"ark_float","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"56050102","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"core diagnostic","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A contract says an existing rental ends if a specified official order lowers a safety limit below a set threshold before the event. The order happens. Did the owner breach by refusing to provide the item?","review_truth":"An existing contract duty can be discharged by a later ''unless'' event stated in the contract.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RM-CONTRACTS-CONDITION-SUBSEQUENT-01","title":"Unless-trigger cuts off duty","signal":"Contract says ''remains effective unless'' or similar.","student_move":"Track the exact trigger and timing before defaulting to breach, impossibility, or substantial performance.","tiny_rule":"If the terminating event happened, the duty is cut off.","trap":"treating initial effectiveness as permanent","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-ARK-FLOAT-01","statement":"A condition subsequent is a later event that cuts off an existing contractual duty when the contract says that event will end the duty.","type":"rule","trigger":"contract says remains effective unless a later event occurs","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §230; condition subsequent / event that terminates a duty","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-ARK-FLOAT-01","statement":"When a contract gives an exact ''unless'' trigger, do not let choices swap in a broader doctrine like impossibility or substantial performance.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"answer uses a different doctrine than the clause''s stated event","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"wrong-frame trigger swap","prompt":"A contract says a duty remains effective unless Event X happens. Event X happens. A choice says breach unless performance was impossible. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut. It swaps Event X for impossibility."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"condition subsequent","prompt":"What does a later ''unless'' event do to an existing duty when the contract says it ends the duty?","answer":"It discharges the duty."},{"drill_type":"call_prediction","target_skill":"timeline discipline","prompt":"The contract was effective when signed, but the stated later terminating event occurred before performance. Which fact is dispositive?","answer":"The later terminating event."}]}'),
  ('c5f29e7c-8277-5dcf-bbeb-a986a8ef9652', '20586_psalms_hormone_use', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Equal Protection', 'Medical-use classifications', NULL, 'At a private ''Psalms-and-Paintbrushes'' youth retreat, Hannah brings her 16-year-old son Timothy to Dr. Luke after Timothy''s endocrinologist recommends a particular hormone medication for a diagnosis listed in the state medical code. A state statute prohibits physicians from prescribing that hormone medication to minors for that listed diagnosis, but it permits physicians to prescribe the same medication to minors for other diagnoses. The prohibition applies to every minor seeking the barred use. The text of the statute does not divide patients into male and female categories. Timothy challenges the statute under the Equal Protection Clause.', 'At a private ''Psalms-and-Paintbrushes'' youth retreat, Hannah brings her 16-year-old son Timothy to Dr. Luke after Timothy''s endocrinologist recommends a particular hormone medication for a diagnosis listed in the state medical code. A state statute prohibits physicians from prescribing that hormone medication to minors for that listed diagnosis, but it permits physicians to prescribe the same medication to minors for other diagnoses. The prohibition applies to every minor seeking the barred use. The text of the statute does not divide patients into male and female categories. Timothy challenges the statute under the Equal Protection Clause.

What is the key Equal Protection threshold question?', 'What is the key Equal Protection threshold question?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20586.md","source_question_id":"20586","variant_slug":"psalms_hormone_use","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"44040305","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium_high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A state bars one hormone medication for minors for one medical use, allows the same medication for other uses, and does not classify male/female on its face. What classification does the Equal Protection analysis start with?","review_truth":"On an Equal Protection threshold question, classify the line the law actually draws before arguing about scrutiny.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-CLASSIFICATION-FIRST-20586","title":"Classification First, Scrutiny Second","signal":"Answer choices jump to strict scrutiny, rights in every context, or automatic discrimination.","student_move":"Name the line the law draws before choosing a tier.","tiny_rule":"Equal Protection starts with classification.","trap":"Policy-disagreement and same-medication facts are not automatic scrutiny triggers.","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-MEDICAL-USE-01","statement":"A statute that bars the same medication only for a specified medical use and age group is not automatically a sex classification. Start with the classification the law actually draws.","type":"distinction","trigger":"same medication allowed for some diagnoses but barred for minors for one diagnosis","authority":"United States v. Skrmetti, 605 U.S. 495 (2025); Massachusetts Board of Retirement v. Murgia; Vacco v. Quill","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-PSALMS-HORMONE-01","statement":"On an Equal Protection threshold call, cut answers that jump straight to automatic scrutiny. The answer that names the classification actually drawn is the responsive lane.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"call asks key Equal Protection threshold question","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"automatic-trigger spotting","prompt":"Which phrase makes this answer structurally suspect: ''bad medical policy automatically triggers strict scrutiny''?","answer":"automatically triggers"},{"drill_type":"classification_first","target_skill":"Equal Protection threshold framing","prompt":"Before choosing scrutiny, what must you identify in an Equal Protection question?","answer":"The classification the law actually draws."},{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"medical-use versus sex classification","prompt":"Same medication, different permitted uses. Does that fact alone automatically prove sex discrimination?","answer":"No. Start with the classification drawn by the law."}]}'),
  ('12e57f2b-e3e4-5efe-bd60-91c1c5bea738', '20594_daniels_whale_float', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Duty; foreseeable rescuers; rescue doctrine', 'foreseeable rescue versus actual awareness', 'For a private Bible-story block party in his driveway, Daniel built a wheeled “Jonah’s whale” float. The night before the party, Daniel climbed inside the float to test the hatch on his sloped driveway, even though he had not set the brake or chocked the wheels. The float began rolling downhill toward a retention pond with Daniel trapped inside. Lydia saw the float rolling, ran to pull open the hatch before it reached the water, and the hatch spring snapped back and broke her wrist. Daniel did not see Lydia approach before she was hurt.', 'For a private Bible-story block party in his driveway, Daniel built a wheeled “Jonah’s whale” float. The night before the party, Daniel climbed inside the float to test the hatch on his sloped driveway, even though he had not set the brake or chocked the wheels. The float began rolling downhill toward a retention pond with Daniel trapped inside. Lydia saw the float rolling, ran to pull open the hatch before it reached the water, and the hatch spring snapped back and broke her wrist. Daniel did not see Lydia approach before she was hurt.

If Lydia asserts a negligence claim against Daniel for the injury she sustained, the court should find for:', 'If Lydia asserts a negligence claim against Daniel for the injury she sustained, the court should find for:', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20594.md","source_question_id":"20594","variant_slug":"daniels_whale_float","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020102","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A defendant carelessly creates danger to himself; a rescuer is injured while trying to save him. Can the rescuer recover from the defendant?","review_truth":"Danger invites rescue: negligent danger to human life can make the rescuer a foreseeable plaintiff.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-RESCUE-20594","title":"Danger invites rescue, not automatic indemnity","signal":"Rescuer injured while responding to apparent human peril.","student_move":"Ask whether the defendant''s unreasonable conduct created the danger; then reject actual-awareness and automatic-payment traps.","tiny_rule":"Rescue is foreseeable when negligent danger creates apparent human peril.","trap":"Treating rescue as either meddling or automatic reimbursement.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-DANGER-INVITES-RESCUE-01","statement":"Danger invites rescue: when a person negligently creates apparent human peril, even to himself, a reasonable rescue attempt is foreseeable and the rescuer may recover for rescue injuries caused by that negligence.","type":"rule","trigger":"rescuer injured while trying to save someone from danger the defendant negligently created","authority":"Wagner v. International Railway Co., 232 N.Y. 176 (1921); self-peril applications recognized in later rescue-doctrine cases.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-BECAUSE-CLAUSE-01","statement":"In a party-result answer set, test the because-clause before choosing the winner. A correct party with a wrong reason is still out.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"choices split between plaintiff/defendant and each uses ''because''","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"rescue doctrine","prompt":"A negligent defendant creates apparent danger to himself, and a rescuer is injured. State the rescue rule.","answer":"Danger invites rescue; reasonable rescue is foreseeable, and the rescuer may recover for injury caused by the negligent peril."},{"drill_type":"answer-array drill","target_skill":"because-clause testing","prompt":"Two answers choose the same party but give different reasons. What do you test first?","answer":"The because-clause."},{"drill_type":"wrong-element drill","target_skill":"actual awareness versus foreseeability","prompt":"The defendant did not know a rescuer would appear. Is that the rescue-doctrine element?","answer":"No. The key is whether rescue was foreseeable from the danger."}]}'),
  ('70c4dded-cb25-5940-8e6f-e80c599f237a', '20597_olivewood_nativity_craft_swap', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Defenses', 'General Principles', 'fact mistake versus law mistake', 'At a community Advent craft swap, Stephen sees a hand-carved olivewood nativity figurine on a table beside small giveaway cards printed with Bible verses. A sign on the table says, “Free verse cards—take one.” Stephen honestly believes the figurine itself has been left as part of the giveaway and takes it home intending to keep it. The figurine actually belongs to Ruth, who had set it down briefly while stacking programs. Stephen is charged with larceny, a specific intent crime requiring intent to permanently deprive another of property.

Later, at the same event, Mary sees Paul set down a similar olivewood figurine while he helps lead the closing song. Mary knows the figurine belongs to someone at the event, but she mistakenly believes state law permits a person to keep any item left unattended on a public craft-swap table after the closing song. Mary takes it intending to keep it. Mary is also charged with larceny; her belief about the law was wrong.', 'At a community Advent craft swap, Stephen sees a hand-carved olivewood nativity figurine on a table beside small giveaway cards printed with Bible verses. A sign on the table says, “Free verse cards—take one.” Stephen honestly believes the figurine itself has been left as part of the giveaway and takes it home intending to keep it. The figurine actually belongs to Ruth, who had set it down briefly while stacking programs. Stephen is charged with larceny, a specific intent crime requiring intent to permanently deprive another of property.

Later, at the same event, Mary sees Paul set down a similar olivewood figurine while he helps lead the closing song. Mary knows the figurine belongs to someone at the event, but she mistakenly believes state law permits a person to keep any item left unattended on a public craft-swap table after the closing song. Mary takes it intending to keep it. Mary is also charged with larceny; her belief about the law was wrong.

Which of the following correctly analyzes both prosecutions?', 'Which of the following correctly analyzes both prosecutions?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20597.md","source_question_id":"20597","variant_slug":"olivewood_nativity_craft_swap","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"72060103","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-high anchor-dependent","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"One larceny defendant honestly mistakes property as abandoned/giveaway; another knows it belongs to someone but misunderstands the law. Which one has a defense?","review_truth":"For specific-intent larceny, an honest fact mistake can negate intent; a law mistake usually cannot.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CRIM-REM-MISTAKE-FACT-LAW-01","title":"Fact mistake / law mistake split","signal":"One actor misunderstands ownership facts; another understands facts but misunderstands legality.","student_move":"First label each mistake as fact or law, then apply the specific-intent Gold Key.","tiny_rule":"Specific-intent fact mistake can negate intent; law mistake generally cannot.","trap":"Treating all sincere mistakes the same or requiring reasonableness for every mistake.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-MISTAKE-FACT-LAW-01","statement":"For a specific intent crime, an honest mistake of fact can negate the required specific intent even if the mistake is unreasonable.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The stem says specific intent and the defendant honestly thinks the property is abandoned or free.","authority":"Morissette v. United States; DOJ Justice Manual larceny formulation; Cornell Wex mistake-of-fact summary.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-MISTAKE-FACT-LAW-02","statement":"Mistake of law generally does not excuse criminal conduct; sincerity alone is not enough.","type":"exception","trigger":"The defendant knows the object belongs to someone but misunderstands whether taking it is legal.","authority":"Cheek v. United States; Justia mistake-of-law summary.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-FACT-LAW-MATRIX-01","statement":"Read the answer array as two switches: Stephen''s mistake is about a fact; Mary''s mistake is about the law. The credited answer must split them, not treat both defendants the same.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Two scenarios plus choices that say both guilty, both not guilty, and two split answers.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"matrix_read","target_skill":"answer_array","prompt":"One defendant mistakenly thinks property is free; another knows it belongs to someone but misunderstands the law. Which outcome pair should you expect?","answer":"Fact-mistake defendant not guilty if intent is negated; law-mistake defendant guilty."},{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"anchor","prompt":"For a specific-intent crime, must an honest fact mistake be reasonable?","answer":"No. It must honestly negate the specific intent."},{"drill_type":"trap_cut","target_skill":"wrong_frame","prompt":"Cut: ''Both are guilty because only reasonable mistakes count.''","answer":"Wrong frame. Specific-intent fact mistake does not require reasonableness."}]}'),
  ('a509b787-a938-5a66-9779-fbb8aa1aa3be', '20598_write_it_on_your_heart', 'TORTS', 'Privacy', 'Appropriation of identity', 'identity-use/commercial-consent answer vs extra-condition traps', 'Source row: BARMATRIX Q# 20598, Torts, Privacy, appropriation of identity. The source row tests whether unauthorized commercial use of a clearly recognizable identity can support appropriation even without exact name or photograph. 

Final Christian variation — `20598_write_it_on_your_heart`

Ruth is a nationally known Christian calligraphy teacher whose short devotional videos are instantly recognized by her turquoise headscarf, sand-timer necklace, whispery cadence, spiral hand-lettering flourish, and catchphrase, “write it on your heart.” Peter’s private Bible-memory app company hired Lydia, an actress and animator, to recreate those traits in a paid social-media ad for the app. The ad never used Ruth’s legal name or photograph, and Ruth did not consent. Commenters overwhelmingly understood the ad’s guide character to be Ruth. Ruth sues Peter’s company for appropriation.

Call:', 'Source row: BARMATRIX Q# 20598, Torts, Privacy, appropriation of identity. The source row tests whether unauthorized commercial use of a clearly recognizable identity can support appropriation even without exact name or photograph. 

Final Christian variation — `20598_write_it_on_your_heart`

Ruth is a nationally known Christian calligraphy teacher whose short devotional videos are instantly recognized by her turquoise headscarf, sand-timer necklace, whispery cadence, spiral hand-lettering flourish, and catchphrase, “write it on your heart.” Peter’s private Bible-memory app company hired Lydia, an actress and animator, to recreate those traits in a paid social-media ad for the app. The ad never used Ruth’s legal name or photograph, and Ruth did not consent. Commenters overwhelmingly understood the ad’s guide character to be Ruth. Ruth sues Peter’s company for appropriation.

