Should a court rule for Ruth?', 'Should a court rule for Ruth?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20598.md","source_question_id":"20598","variant_slug":"write_it_on_your_heart","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A company uses a clearly recognizable imitation of a person’s identity in a product ad without consent and without using the person’s name or photograph. Does appropriation lie?","review_truth":"Appropriation can rest on unauthorized commercial use of recognizable identity; exact name/photo, insult, and false statement are not required.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-APPROPRIATION-IDENTITY-01","title":"Name/photo trap in appropriation","signal":"The stem says no exact name or photograph, but many identity cues are copied.","student_move":"Do not require exact name/photo. Ask whether the ad clearly uses the plaintiff’s identity for commercial advantage without consent.","tiny_rule":"Recognizable commercial evocation can be enough.","trap":"Exact-name/photo-only answer.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-APPROPRIATION-IDENTITY-01","statement":"Appropriation is not limited to exact name or photograph. A clearly recognizable, unauthorized commercial evocation of the plaintiff’s identity can be enough.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Ad uses a lookalike, soundalike, persona, or identity cues to sell a product.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 652C; Midler v. Ford Motor Co., 849 F.2d 460 (9th Cir. 1988).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-APPROPRIATION-IDENTITY-01","statement":"For appropriation calls, sort the array by the three trigger facts: identity use, commercial advantage, and no consent. Extra requirements like exact name/photo, insult, or false fact are trap add-ons.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"One answer mirrors identity-use facts while others add conditions.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"fabricated_rule","prompt":"A commercial uses a distinctive soundalike and costume to evoke a celebrity but avoids the celebrity’s name and photo. Cut the answer: ''No, because no exact name or photograph was used.''","answer":"CUT as fabricated_rule once the Gold Key is applied."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"colloquialism","prompt":"A commercial respectfully imitates a performer’s identity to sell a product. Cut the answer: ''No, because the imitation was flattering.''","answer":"CUT as colloquialism; tone is not the appropriation trigger."},{"drill_type":"wrong_frame","target_skill":"bait_doctrine","prompt":"A product ad uses a person’s recognizable identity without consent. Cut the answer: ''Yes, but only if the ad stated a false fact.''","answer":"CUT as bait_doctrine / wrong_frame; false fact belongs to neighboring reputation torts."},{"drill_type":"residual","target_skill":"Gold Key application","prompt":"Pick the survivor: exact name/photo requirement, flattering-use answer, false-fact answer, or identity-use/commercial/no-consent answer.","answer":"Identity-use/commercial/no-consent answer."}]}'),
  ('40fcbd7e-d2fb-5c4b-a921-de85208e3bfa', '20599_advent_handbell_integration', 'CONTRACTS', 'CM-B — Parol evidence rule core', 'Complete integration / merger clause / consistent additional term barred', 'complete-and-exclusive integration vs mere non-contradiction', 'Stephen agreed to write and rehearse a Scripture-themed puppet play for Martha’s family Advent party. Before anyone signed, Stephen allegedly said he would also prepare a handbell cue sheet at no extra charge. The signed agreement that followed listed the fee, scenes to be written, rehearsal dates, delivery deadline, and payment schedule. It also provided, “This writing is the complete and exclusive statement of the parties’ agreement.” When a dispute arose, Martha offered evidence of the earlier handbell promise. The agreement says nothing about handbells. How should the court rule?', 'Stephen agreed to write and rehearse a Scripture-themed puppet play for Martha’s family Advent party. Before anyone signed, Stephen allegedly said he would also prepare a handbell cue sheet at no extra charge. The signed agreement that followed listed the fee, scenes to be written, rehearsal dates, delivery deadline, and payment schedule. It also provided, “This writing is the complete and exclusive statement of the parties’ agreement.” When a dispute arose, Martha offered evidence of the earlier handbell promise. The agreement says nothing about handbells. How should the court rule?

How should the court rule?', 'How should the court rule?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20599.md","source_question_id":"20599","variant_slug":"advent_handbell_integration","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"trap repair","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"The writing says it is the complete and exclusive statement.","distilled_core_question":"A signed service contract says it is the complete and exclusive agreement. One party offers a prior oral extra term that does not conflict with the writing. Admit or exclude the oral term?","review_truth":"Complete-and-exclusive beats merely consistent: a prior oral add-on inside the same deal is excluded.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-COMPLETE-INTEGRATION-01","title":"Complete beats consistent","signal":"Signed writing says complete and exclusive; offered term came before signing.","student_move":"Do not ask only whether the oral term conflicts; ask whether the writing is complete.","tiny_rule":"Complete integration excludes prior consistent add-ons.","trap":"Consistency trap","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-COMPLETE-INTEGRATION-01","statement":"A complete-and-exclusive writing is a complete integration. Prior or contemporaneous oral additions inside the same deal are excluded even if they do not contradict the writing.","type":"distinction","trigger":"complete and exclusive statement + prior oral extra term","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 210, 213, 216","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-INTEGRATION-CLASH-01","statement":"When one choice says the oral term does not contradict and another points to complete/exclusive language, clash them on integration level before judging consistency.","type":"clash","trigger":"not conflict vs complete and exclusive","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong-object Issue-Sense","prompt":"A party offers an oral side promise, but an answer excludes the signed contract. What filter breaks?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE / misfit."},{"drill_type":"CLASH","target_skill":"integration-level axis","prompt":"One answer says the oral term is consistent; another says the writing is complete and exclusive. What is the axis?","answer":"Complete integration versus mere non-contradiction."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"complete integration Gold Key","prompt":"A complete-and-exclusive writing is silent about a prior oral add-on. Does consistency alone admit it?","answer":"No. Complete integration excludes prior consistent add-ons."}]}'),
  ('e8804f9d-2dd5-5307-9b98-c05259d12527', '20602_davids_harp_projector', 'EVIDENCE', 'III.C.1 — Subsequent Remedial Measures', 'Rule 407 product defect, design defect, and need for warning or instruction', 'Barred defect/warning purpose versus general relevance/feasibility/negligence-only drift.', 'Naomi bought a portable “David’s Harp” hymn-projector kit for a neighborhood praise-night in her garage. As she unfolded the aluminum tripod, the hinge bracket snapped shut and crushed her hand. In a federal products-liability action governed by the Federal Rules of Evidence, Naomi offers evidence that, after her injury, the manufacturer replaced the hinge with a spring-loaded locking hinge and added a bold “keep hands clear / load limit” warning tag. Naomi offers the evidence to prove that the original hinge design was defective and that the original warning was inadequate.', 'Naomi bought a portable “David’s Harp” hymn-projector kit for a neighborhood praise-night in her garage. As she unfolded the aluminum tripod, the hinge bracket snapped shut and crushed her hand. In a federal products-liability action governed by the Federal Rules of Evidence, Naomi offers evidence that, after her injury, the manufacturer replaced the hinge with a spring-loaded locking hinge and added a bold “keep hands clear / load limit” warning tag. Naomi offers the evidence to prove that the original hinge design was defective and that the original warning was inadequate.

How should the court rule under Rule 407?', 'How should the court rule under Rule 407?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20602.md","source_question_id":"20602","variant_slug":"davids_harp_projector","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"trap repair","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"After a product injury, the plaintiff offers a later product redesign and stronger warning to prove the original design defect and warning defect. Under Rule 407, admissible?","review_truth":"Rule 407 blocks later fixes offered to prove defect or warning need; feasibility only matters when that is the actual disputed purpose.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REMED-EVIDENCE-407-PURPOSE","title":"Rule 407 Purpose Lock","signal":"Post-injury fix plus answer choices about relevance, feasibility, or negligence-only scope.","student_move":"Name the offered purpose before reading exceptions.","tiny_rule":"Defect/warning purpose is barred; feasibility is not automatic.","trap":"Treating feasibility or relevance as a universal admission route.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-RULE407-PURPOSE-01","statement":"Rule 407 is not negligence-only: it bars later fixes offered to prove product defect, design defect, or a need for warning or instruction. Feasibility is only a separate permitted purpose when it is genuinely disputed and actually offered for that purpose.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Post-injury redesign or stronger warning offered in a product case.","authority":"Federal Rule of Evidence 407; 1997 Advisory Committee note.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-EVIDENCE-RULE407-PURPOSE-01","statement":"When the stem tells you why evidence is offered, hold that purpose fixed. Relevance and exception words do not matter until they match the stated purpose.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The stem says the later measure is offered to prove defect or inadequate warning.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"purpose_pin","target_skill":"offered_for_purpose","prompt":"A plaintiff offers a later product redesign to prove the original product was defective. Name the offered purpose.","answer":"Product/design defect; barred under Rule 407."},{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"Rule 407 product scope","prompt":"True or false: Rule 407 applies only to negligence and culpable conduct.","answer":"False; it also covers product defect, design defect, and warning/instruction need."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Cut the phrase: ''A later redesign always proves feasibility.''","answer":"Always is the overclaim; feasibility is only a permitted purpose when disputed and actually offered."},{"drill_type":"relevance_layer","target_skill":"specific exclusion over general relevance","prompt":"Why is ''relevant evidence is generally admissible'' a bad answer to a Rule 407 call?","answer":"It answers the wrong layer; relevance does not override a specific exclusion."}]}'),
  ('68bb59a3-7ac6-5d50-94b1-38ff6ee5046e', '20603_road_to_emmaus_escape_room', 'TORTS', 'Torts', 'Negligence', 'Daniel''s negligent creation of peril vs Ruth-centered rescue facts', 'During a private, for-profit Bible-themed escape-room contest called ''The Road to Emmaus,'' Daniel, although acting with reasonable care, crawled into a narrow prop tunnel as part of the puzzle and became wedged, unconscious, and critically injured in a compartment that was difficult to reach. Ruth, an experienced stagehand who was attending the event, was seriously injured while trying to free Daniel. Ruth''s attempt failed, and Daniel died before he could be reached. Ruth brought an action against Daniel''s estate for compensation for her injuries. In this jurisdiction, traditional common-law rules relating to contributory negligence and assumption of risk remain in effect.', 'During a private, for-profit Bible-themed escape-room contest called ''The Road to Emmaus,'' Daniel, although acting with reasonable care, crawled into a narrow prop tunnel as part of the puzzle and became wedged, unconscious, and critically injured in a compartment that was difficult to reach. Ruth, an experienced stagehand who was attending the event, was seriously injured while trying to free Daniel. Ruth''s attempt failed, and Daniel died before he could be reached. Ruth brought an action against Daniel''s estate for compensation for her injuries. In this jurisdiction, traditional common-law rules relating to contributory negligence and assumption of risk remain in effect.

Is Ruth likely to prevail against Daniel''s estate?', 'Is Ruth likely to prevail against Daniel''s estate?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20603.md","source_question_id":"20603","variant_slug":"road_to_emmaus_escape_room","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"2","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A careful person gets trapped and needs rescue. The rescuer is injured in a failed rescue. Can the rescuer recover from the careful person''s estate?","review_truth":"A rescuer''s claim needs a negligent peril-maker; noble rescue alone does not create liability against a careful person.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-RESCUE-ACTOR-FAULT-01","title":"Rescue Doctrine: Actor-Fault Gate","signal":"Rescuer is hurt and sues the person rescued or that person''s estate.","student_move":"Target the defendant''s negligence before praising the rescue.","tiny_rule":"No negligent peril-maker, no rescuer recovery from that defendant.","trap":"Noble rescue facts feel dispositive.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-RESCUE-NEGLIGENT-PERIL-01","statement":"Rescue doctrine needs a negligent peril-maker. A rescuer''s injury is charged to the person whose negligence created the peril, not merely to the person who needed help.","type":"threshold","trigger":"rescuer sues the imperiled person''s estate","authority":"Wagner v. International Railway, 232 N.Y. 176, 133 N.E. 437 (N.Y. 1921); Cornell Wex rescue doctrine","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-TARGET-ACTOR-01","statement":"Lock the target actor before judging the rescue. If the rescuer sues the imperiled person''s estate, ask what that imperiled person did wrong.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"choices discuss the rescuer''s reasonableness, public policy, or failed benefit","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"colloquialism","prompt":"An answer says, ''Yes, because the law should encourage rescue.'' Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut. It is policy language, not the operative legal test."},{"drill_type":"issue_sense","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"A rescuer sues the rescued person''s estate. One answer says the rescuer acted reasonably. What is missing?","answer":"The rescued person''s negligent creation of the peril."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"rescue doctrine threshold","prompt":"When can a rescuer recover from the person rescued?","answer":"Only when that person negligently created the peril that led to the rescue injury."},{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"target actor","prompt":"The choices discuss the rescuer, policy, and failed rescue. The suit is against the estate. What is the first move?","answer":"Lock the target actor and ask what the estate''s decedent did wrong."}]}'),
  ('29c3fb4f-4f52-5084-944b-98cb02fc2153', '20605_scripture_quiz_whisper', 'TORTS', 'Privacy', 'False light', 'publicity threshold vs false-impression-only / wrong privacy-tort frames', 'Daniel first learned of Martha’s accusation when Martha later apologized to him. At a privately run Bible-character board-game tournament, Martha had pulled Lydia behind the prize table and falsely whispered, “Daniel is secretly swapping the Scripture-quiz answer cards.” Lydia kept the accusation to herself. Daniel sued Martha for false light.', 'Daniel first learned of Martha’s accusation when Martha later apologized to him. At a privately run Bible-character board-game tournament, Martha had pulled Lydia behind the prize table and falsely whispered, “Daniel is secretly swapping the Scripture-quiz answer cards.” Lydia kept the accusation to herself. Daniel sued Martha for false light.

Is Daniel likely to prevail on false light?', 'Is Daniel likely to prevail on false light?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20605.md","source_question_id":"20605","variant_slug":"scripture_quiz_whisper","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62050402","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A defendant falsely accused the plaintiff to one person in private; that person did not repeat it. Can the plaintiff win false light?","review_truth":"False light needs public-style publicity; a private one-listener accusation does not create false-light liability.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"TOR-ANCHOR-FALSE-LIGHT-PUBLICITY","title":"False light publicity gate","signal":"False-light claim after a private accusation.","student_move":"Ask whether the matter reached the public or a broad audience.","tiny_rule":"One private listener is not false-light publicity.","trap":"Treating falsity or defamation overlap as enough.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-FALSE-LIGHT-PUBLICITY-01","statement":"False light needs publicity to the public or a broad audience. A private one-listener accusation is not enough, even if it is false.","type":"threshold","trigger":"false light claim + one private listener + no repeat","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §652E; Restatement publicity commentary.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-TORTS-PRIVACY-TORT-HOMES-02","statement":"Commercial use of a person''s identity is appropriation, not false light. Do not borrow the neighboring privacy tort''s element.","type":"distinction","trigger":"false light choice using identity, likeness, or commercial use","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §652C and §652E.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-FALSE-LIGHT-NO-PAIR-01","statement":"When two answers say No, keep the No reason that matches the stem''s decisive fact. Here, private audience beats commercial-use vocabulary.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"two No choices in a privacy-tort element question","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"false-light publicity threshold","prompt":"A false accusation is made privately to one person and not repeated. False light?","answer":"No; no public-style publicity."},{"drill_type":"Wrong-frame sort","target_skill":"privacy tort homes","prompt":"Commercial use of name or likeness belongs to which privacy tort home?","answer":"Appropriation, not false light."},{"drill_type":"Wrong-element cut","target_skill":"false-impression-only trap","prompt":"Choice says yes because the statement created a false impression. What element is missing?","answer":"Publicity to the public or a broad audience."}]}'),
  ('90356905-7c86-5293-bb6c-a26dc9a13849', '20606_lydia_festival_venue', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Civil Procedure', 'Jurisdiction and Venue', 'complete venue buckets vs incomplete/poisoned venue list', 'Source row: BarMatrix Q# 20606 is a federal diversity venue question with the official credited answer listing the accident district plus both defendants’ residence districts. 

Final Christian variation

Lydia, a worship harpist domiciled in the Northern District of State G, was injured when a propane coffee cart exploded during a privately run Christian music festival in the Central District of State H. Lydia filed a federal action based on diversity against two individuals involved in setting up the cart. Peter, a stage electrician, is domiciled in the Eastern District of State J. Ruth, a carpenter who built the cart platform, is domiciled in the Western District of State J.

In which judicial districts would venue be proper for Lydia’s action?', 'Source row: BarMatrix Q# 20606 is a federal diversity venue question with the official credited answer listing the accident district plus both defendants’ residence districts. 

Final Christian variation

