In which judicial districts would venue be proper for Lydia’s action?

In which judicial districts would venue be proper for Lydia''s action?', 'In which judicial districts would venue be proper for Lydia''s action?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20606.md","source_question_id":"20606","variant_slug":"lydia_festival_venue","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"94050100","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A federal diversity tort suit has one event district and two individual defendants domiciled in different districts of the same state. Which districts are proper venue?","review_truth":"Venue list questions are bucket checks: include the event district and defendant-residence districts when all defendants reside in one state; do not add the plaintiff''s home district.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-REMEDY-VENUE-BUCKETS-20606","title":"Venue bucket checklist","signal":"The call asks which judicial districts are proper.","student_move":"Build the complete bucket list before reading combinations.","tiny_rule":"Event district plus qualifying defendant-residence districts; plaintiff home is a lure.","trap":"Stopping after the loudest venue fact.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-VENUE-BUCKETS-01","statement":"For ordinary federal civil actions, venue can sit in the event district or in a defendant-residence district when all defendants reside in that same state; the plaintiff''s home is not a venue bucket.","type":"rule","trigger":"The call asks which judicial districts are proper and gives accident location plus defendants from different districts of one state.","authority":"28 U.S.C. § 1391(b)(1), (b)(2), (c)(1)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-VENUE-BUCKETS-01","statement":"Read list answers as bucket checklists: keep every valid bucket and reject any answer that adds a false bucket or omits a true one.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Choices mix event district, defendant districts, and plaintiff home district.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"bucket_check","target_skill":"venue source sorting","prompt":"Event in District X; two defendants reside in Districts Y and Z of the same state; plaintiff resides in District P. Which buckets count?","answer":"X, Y, and Z; not P."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"plaintiff-home lure rejection","prompt":"A venue answer includes the plaintiff''s home district in an ordinary private-defendant tort suit. What is the trap?","answer":"Plaintiff residence is not a venue bucket here."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"complete-list selection","prompt":"One choice gives event only, one gives defendants only, one gives plaintiff plus event, one gives event plus defendants. Which shape usually survives?","answer":"The complete event-plus-defendants list."}]}'),
  ('008eb633-4b60-5811-960f-7a7000a2b9b5', '20627_ruth_hymn_poster_contract', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Subject Matter Jurisdiction', 'Diversity - Time of Filing', 'filing-day citizenship snapshot vs later domicile facts', 'Daniel was packing his private Scripture-verse printing booth for a Colorado craft fair when Ruth sued him in federal district court. The lawsuit, filed on April 4, was a state-law contract action seeking $144,000 for allegedly ruined illuminated hymn posters Ruth had commissioned for her private Easter pageant. When the complaint was filed, Ruth was domiciled in Arizona, and Daniel was also domiciled in Arizona. On April 25, before filing any answer, Daniel permanently moved to Colorado and changed his domicile.', 'Daniel was packing his private Scripture-verse printing booth for a Colorado craft fair when Ruth sued him in federal district court. The lawsuit, filed on April 4, was a state-law contract action seeking $144,000 for allegedly ruined illuminated hymn posters Ruth had commissioned for her private Easter pageant. When the complaint was filed, Ruth was domiciled in Arizona, and Daniel was also domiciled in Arizona. On April 25, before filing any answer, Daniel permanently moved to Colorado and changed his domicile.

Did Daniel’s Colorado move create diversity jurisdiction?', 'Did Daniel’s Colorado move create diversity jurisdiction?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20627.md","source_question_id":"20627","variant_slug":"ruth_hymn_poster_contract","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"94010100","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"2","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"P and D were citizens of the same state when P filed a $144,000 state-law case in federal court. D later moved to another state before answering. Does the move create diversity jurisdiction?","review_truth":"Diversity is a filing-day snapshot; a later domicile change does not create diversity that was missing when the complaint was filed.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CIV-DIVERSITY-FILING-SNAPSHOT","title":"Diversity snapshot","signal":"Filing-day citizenship plus later move.","student_move":"Freeze citizenship at filing before reading later procedural facts.","tiny_rule":"Diversity citizenship is checked at filing.","trap":"response-date and before-trial timing choices","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-DIVERSITY-FILING-01","statement":"For ordinary diversity jurisdiction, citizenship is measured when the complaint is filed. A later move does not create diversity that was missing at filing.","type":"rule","trigger":"A stem gives citizenship at filing and a later domicile change.","authority":"28 U.S.C. § 1332(a); Grupo Dataflux v. Atlas Global Group, L.P.; Mollan v. Torrance","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-DIVERSITY-SNAPSHOT-01","statement":"When a jurisdiction stem gives filing-day citizenship and later domicile facts, freeze the snapshot at filing before judging the later facts.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"same-state parties at filing plus a later move before answer or trial","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"diversity time of filing","prompt":"P and D are both Texas citizens when P files a $90,000 state-law case. D moves to Oklahoma before answering. Diversity?","answer":"No. Citizenship is measured at filing."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"never / any detector","prompt":"Cut the answer: ''No, because state-law claims can never be heard under diversity.''","answer":"Cut. Never is an overclaim."},{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"freeze the filing snapshot","prompt":"A stem gives citizenship at filing and then a later domicile change. What fact do you check first?","answer":"Citizenship when the complaint was filed."}]}'),
  ('a702ba1d-f4ed-5a98-91c5-1c06dd99a9ae', '20638_advent_rescue_beacons', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Relations Between Federal and State Governments', 'Supremacy Clause', 'practical interference vs geography/formality', 'Source row: QID 20638, Constitutional Law, official key B. 

Under authority granted by Congress, the United States Department of Defense hired Barnabas Metalworks, a private Advent-themed shop owned by Lydia, to manufacture rugged rescue-beacon casings for military search teams. The contract required all casting and polishing to occur at Barnabas’s old rail-depot workshop beside Mercy Brook, and it allowed the shop to release no more than four ounces per day of a polishing solvent into the brook from that production.

While producing the beacon casings under the contract, Barnabas routinely released between three and four ounces per day of the solvent into Mercy Brook. The state later prosecuted Barnabas under a state clean-water statute that prohibits any person or business entity from releasing more than three ounces per day of that solvent into any creek located in the state.

Barnabas defended on the ground that, as a federal contractor, it was immune from state regulation. Which one of the following additional facts or inferences, if it were the only one true, would most effectively support the state’s argument?', 'Source row: QID 20638, Constitutional Law, official key B. 

Under authority granted by Congress, the United States Department of Defense hired Barnabas Metalworks, a private Advent-themed shop owned by Lydia, to manufacture rugged rescue-beacon casings for military search teams. The contract required all casting and polishing to occur at Barnabas’s old rail-depot workshop beside Mercy Brook, and it allowed the shop to release no more than four ounces per day of a polishing solvent into the brook from that production.

While producing the beacon casings under the contract, Barnabas routinely released between three and four ounces per day of the solvent into Mercy Brook. The state later prosecuted Barnabas under a state clean-water statute that prohibits any person or business entity from releasing more than three ounces per day of that solvent into any creek located in the state.

Barnabas defended on the ground that, as a federal contractor, it was immune from state regulation. Which one of the following additional facts or inferences, if it were the only one true, would most effectively support the state’s argument?

Which additional fact or inference, if it were the only one true, would most effectively support the state''s argument?', 'Which additional fact or inference, if it were the only one true, would most effectively support the state''s argument?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20638.md","source_question_id":"20638","variant_slug":"advent_rescue_beacons","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"41030300","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A federal contractor violates a stricter state discharge limit. Which fact best supports the state? The stricter limit does not increase federal production cost.","review_truth":"For a federal contractor, the state’s best fact is the one showing the state rule does not interfere with the federal project.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-FED-CONTRACTOR-INTERFERENCE","title":"Federal contractor: practical interference axis","signal":"state regulation of a private federal contractor","student_move":"Ask whether the state rule burdens the federal job.","tiny_rule":"No interference, no immunity win.","trap":"geography or express-exemption formalism","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-FEDERAL-CONTRACTOR-01","statement":"A state may regulate a federal contractor unless applying the state rule would interfere with federal purposes or policies.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"state prosecutes or regulates a private contractor performing a federal contract","authority":"Supremacy Clause; Leslie Miller; Goodyear Atomic","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-FEDERAL-CONTRACTOR-01","statement":"When the call asks which fact best supports the state against federal-contractor immunity, look for the fact about practical effect on the federal job.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"choices split between location facts, express wording, and contract-performance cost","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"clash_axis","target_skill":"name the immunity axis","prompt":"State regulation of a federal contractor: express exemption or no project burden?","answer":"No project burden is the stronger state-support fact."},{"drill_type":"wrong_frame_cut","target_skill":"cut geography bait","prompt":"Federal contractor claims immunity; choice says the creek is wholly intrastate.","answer":"Cut as not responsive after federal authority is given."},{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"federal-contractor anchor","prompt":"What makes state regulation of a federal contractor vulnerable?","answer":"Interference with federal purposes or policies."}]}'),
  ('1ba61517-0bd5-5767-9c87-66876bef7172', '20649_christmas_pageant_platform', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Standard of Due Care / Contributory Negligence / Causation', 'defendant no-breach/foreseeability vs plaintiff avoidability vs other-cause blame', 'Naomi worked for Lydia''s private pageant-production company, which was staging a Christian Christmas pageant. The company hired Barnabas, an independent contractor, to adjust the idle setting on a motorized rolling platform that carried a large manger prop. Barnabas adjusted the idle setting too high. Later, Naomi stood beside the platform and pressed the start switch to move it; because the platform lacked a roll-stop interlock, it lurched and injured her. Naomi asserted a negligence claim against Barnabas.', 'Naomi worked for Lydia''s private pageant-production company, which was staging a Christian Christmas pageant. The company hired Barnabas, an independent contractor, to adjust the idle setting on a motorized rolling platform that carried a large manger prop. Barnabas adjusted the idle setting too high. Later, Naomi stood beside the platform and pressed the start switch to move it; because the platform lacked a roll-stop interlock, it lurched and injured her. Naomi asserted a negligence claim against Barnabas.

Which one of the following additional facts or inferences, if it were the only one true, would be most likely to result in a judgment for Barnabas in a jurisdiction that applies the all-or-nothing rule of contributory negligence?', 'Which one of the following additional facts or inferences, if it were the only one true, would be most likely to result in a judgment for Barnabas in a jurisdiction that applies the all-or-nothing rule of contributory negligence?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20649.md","source_question_id":"20649","variant_slug":"christmas_pageant_platform","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020201","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A contractor adjusted equipment in a way that caused injury only because a safety device was missing. Which added fact most helps the contractor win: normal safety-device custom, plaintiff avoidability, product defect, or another party''s notice failure?","review_truth":"A defendant wins a negligence claim by breaking the plaintiff''s proof of the defendant''s own unreasonable foreseeable-risk conduct, not by merely pointing to avoidability or another possible cause.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-20649-01","title":"Avoidability is not contributory negligence","signal":"Choice says plaintiff would have avoided injury if she had acted differently.","student_move":"Ask whether the choice says the actual conduct was negligent.","tiny_rule":"No plaintiff negligence, no contributory-negligence bar.","trap":"Confusing safer hypothetical with legal fault.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-FORESEEABLE-RISK-01","statement":"Negligence turns on unreasonable conduct in light of a foreseeable risk. A fact that makes the specific risk not foreseeable can defeat breach.","type":"rule","trigger":"The added fact says the missing safety device is usually present.","authority":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Physical and Emotional Harm § 3; negligence factors include foreseeable likelihood/severity and burden of precautions.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-TORTS-CONTRIB-AVOIDABILITY-01","statement":"Contributory negligence requires negligent plaintiff conduct that contributed to the harm. A fact that the plaintiff would have avoided injury by doing something else is not enough by itself.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The answer says the plaintiff would not have been hurt from another position.","authority":"Common-law contributory negligence rule; see Cornell Wex summary.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-PAGEANT-ARRAY-01","statement":"In an added-fact defense question, sort each option by what it attacks: defendant breach, plaintiff negligence, other cause, or other party. The best defense fact usually attacks an element of the plaintiff''s claim against this defendant.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Choices point in four different blame directions.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong-element","prompt":"A plaintiff would not have been injured if she had used a safer position. Does that alone prove contributory negligence?","answer":"No. It shows avoidability, not negligent conduct."},{"drill_type":"CLASH","target_skill":"tension axis","prompt":"Two answers survive: one says the device is usually present; one says the plaintiff could have stood elsewhere. Name the axis.","answer":"Defendant no-breach / unforeseeable risk versus plaintiff avoidability."},{"drill_type":"CALL","target_skill":"answer-array sorting","prompt":"In an added-fact defense call, which fact is stronger: one blaming a manufacturer, or one making the defendant''s risk unforeseeable?","answer":"The unforeseeable-risk fact, because it attacks the plaintiff''s claim against this defendant."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"contributory negligence","prompt":"What must the added fact show before contributory negligence bars recovery?","answer":"Plaintiff negligence that contributed to the harm."}]}'),
  ('5791d081-b55a-588d-85ab-ab4ef9bbfd45', '20654_nativity_ornaments_volition', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Defenses', 'General Principles', 'cognition vs volition', 'Stem / Fact Pattern

Lydia has a severe compulsion disorder that psychiatrists recognize as a legitimate mental disease. She has been examined and found to understand fully that damaging another person’s property is illegal and wrong. Even so, whenever she visits a privately run Christian craft market and sees ceramic nativity ornaments for sale, she experiences overwhelming uncontrollable urges to snap the shepherd staffs off the ornaments. She does this on repeated visits and is charged with multiple counts of criminal mischief.

The jurisdiction uses both M’Naghten and the irresistible-impulse supplement. Lydia’s expert testifies that, because of her mental disease, her urges were genuinely uncontrollable and she could not conform her conduct to the law.

Call of Question

Which of the following best states Lydia’s insanity claim?', 'Stem / Fact Pattern

Lydia has a severe compulsion disorder that psychiatrists recognize as a legitimate mental disease. She has been examined and found to understand fully that damaging another person’s property is illegal and wrong. Even so, whenever she visits a privately run Christian craft market and sees ceramic nativity ornaments for sale, she experiences overwhelming uncontrollable urges to snap the shepherd staffs off the ornaments. She does this on repeated visits and is charged with multiple counts of criminal mischief.

The jurisdiction uses both M’Naghten and the irresistible-impulse supplement. Lydia’s expert testifies that, because of her mental disease, her urges were genuinely uncontrollable and she could not conform her conduct to the law.

Call of Question

Which of the following best states Lydia’s insanity claim?

Which of the following best states Lydia''s insanity claim?', 'Which of the following best states Lydia''s insanity claim?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20654.md","source_question_id":"20654","variant_slug":"nativity_ornaments_volition","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"72060101","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium_hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A defendant knows the act is wrong but, due to mental disease, cannot conform conduct in a M''Naghten-plus-irresistible-impulse jurisdiction. Which insanity test may apply?","review_truth":"Knowing wrongfulness kills only the M''Naghten cognitive route; irresistible impulse can still matter when mental disease prevents control.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CRIM-ANCHOR-INSANITY-01","title":"Insanity tests: cognition vs volition","signal":"Stem gives M''Naghten plus irresistible impulse.","student_move":"Match facts to lanes before picking.","tiny_rule":"Know-wrong = cognition; cannot-conform = volition.","trap":"Treating one lane as the whole insanity defense.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-IRRESISTIBLE-IMPULSE-01","statement":"M''Naghten is cognition: did the defendant know the nature or wrongfulness of the act? Irresistible impulse is volition: the defendant may know wrong but still be excused if mental disease prevents control.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The stem says the jurisdiction uses M''Naghten plus irresistible impulse and gives both knowledge-of-wrongfulness and inability-to-conform facts.","authority":"M''Naghten''s Case; Model Penal Code section 4.01 conform-conduct analogue; Cornell Wex summaries.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-IRRESISTIBLE-IMPULSE-01","statement":"Read the array as three buckets: diagnosis alone, cognition, and volition. When the stem gives both ''knows wrong'' and ''cannot conform,'' the answer must choose the volition bucket.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Choices separately mention mental disease, knew wrong, M''Naghten, and irresistible impulse.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"cognition_vs_volition","prompt":"A defendant knows an act is wrong but cannot control the act due to mental disease. Which lane is this: M''Naghten or irresistible impulse?","answer":"Irresistible impulse."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"Cut the choice: ''The defendant is insane because she has a recognized mental disease.''","answer":"Wrong element: diagnosis is threshold, not sufficient."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"bucket_sorting","prompt":"Sort these facts into lanes: mental disease; knew wrong; could not conform.","answer":"Mental disease = threshold; knew wrong = cognition; could not conform = volition."}]}'),
  ('e2782e23-8183-5f9e-a192-9abc4930cb31', '20690_bethlehem_star_drone', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Personal Jurisdiction', 'Rule 12 waiver after amended pleading', NULL, 'Mary runs a private Christmas-market design studio in State A. She sued Daniel, a State B drone-show contractor, in federal court after Daniel''s ''Bethlehem Star'' drone display failed during a paid rehearsal for Mary''s Advent market. Daniel served an answer that did not object to personal jurisdiction, although he already knew every fact supporting that objection. Seven weeks later, Mary amended the complaint against Daniel only to add a lost-profit damages theory from the same failed drone display. Daniel then moved to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction.', 'Mary runs a private Christmas-market design studio in State A. She sued Daniel, a State B drone-show contractor, in federal court after Daniel''s ''Bethlehem Star'' drone display failed during a paid rehearsal for Mary''s Advent market. Daniel served an answer that did not object to personal jurisdiction, although he already knew every fact supporting that objection. Seven weeks later, Mary amended the complaint against Daniel only to add a lost-profit damages theory from the same failed drone display. Daniel then moved to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction.

How should the court rule?', 'How should the court rule?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20690.md","source_question_id":"20690","variant_slug":"bethlehem_star_drone","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"94020201","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A defendant knew all facts supporting a personal-jurisdiction objection, answered without raising it, then faced a same-defendant amended complaint adding only a new damages theory from the same transaction. Should the later personal-jurisdiction motion be granted?","review_truth":"Personal jurisdiction is waivable; a same-defendant amended complaint does not automatically revive an available personal-jurisdiction defense omitted from the first answer.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-REMEDY-R12-WAIVER-20690","title":"First response freezes waivable Rule 12 defenses","signal":"Known personal-jurisdiction facts + answer or first Rule 12 motion without PJ objection.","student_move":"Check whether the later pleading created a new defect. If not, cut fresh-start answers.","tiny_rule":"Available PJ objections are use-it-or-lose-it; same-defect amendments do not restart them.","trap":"Amended complaint = fresh Rule 12 slate.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-R12-PJ-WAIVER-01","statement":"Personal jurisdiction is a waivable Rule 12(b)(2) defense. If it was available and the defendant omitted it from the first answer or first Rule 12 motion, it is waived.","type":"rule","trigger":"A defendant answers or files a Rule 12 motion without objecting to personal jurisdiction.","authority":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(b)(2), 12(g)(2), 12(h)(1).","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-AMENDED-COMPLAINT-NO-REVIVAL-02","statement":"An amended complaint does not automatically revive a Rule 12(b)(2)-(5) defense that was available and waived earlier unless the amendment first creates the defense.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Plaintiff amends after defendant already failed to raise a known threshold defense.","authority":"Gilmore v. Shearson/American Express, Inc.; State Auto Ins. Co. v. Thomas Landscaping & Constr., Inc.; Gnotec Reftele AB order.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-JURISDICTION-WORD-01","statement":"Match the jurisdiction word to the call before doing doctrine. Personal jurisdiction is defendant-court power; supplemental jurisdiction is subject-matter power over claims.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"A choice switches from personal jurisdiction to supplemental jurisdiction.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"wrong_frame","prompt":"The call asks whether a late personal-jurisdiction motion should be granted. A choice says the plaintiff must establish supplemental jurisdiction. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut: NOT_RESPONSIVE / misfit."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"Rule 12 waiver","prompt":"A defendant knew all personal-jurisdiction facts and answered without raising PJ. What happens to the PJ defense?","answer":"Waived."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"amended complaint no revival","prompt":"Plaintiff later amends only to add damages from the same transaction against the same defendant. Does that automatically revive waived PJ?","answer":"No."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"absolute words","prompt":"A choice says every amended complaint restarts all Rule 12 defenses. What is the C3 tell?","answer":"Every/all overclaim plus fabricated Rule 12 reset."}]}'),
  ('e9d8bc59-c4f2-594d-88d2-39ad44638808', '20714_prayer_robotics_kits', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Judicial Review', 'Standing', 'Is this a general taxpayer grievance, or the narrow spending-power/Establishment-Clause exception?', 'After reports that teenagers at weekend robotics competitions are injuring themselves while soldering circuit boards, Congress enacts a provision distributing free soldering-safety kits to participants in public programs and private youth academies. The kits contain goggles, burn pads, and safety cards; they contain no prayer, worship material, or religious instruction. In many states, most private youth academies are church-affiliated. Lydia, a federal taxpayer, challenges the provision, arguing that it unconstitutionally supports religious entities.', 'After reports that teenagers at weekend robotics competitions are injuring themselves while soldering circuit boards, Congress enacts a provision distributing free soldering-safety kits to participants in public programs and private youth academies. The kits contain goggles, burn pads, and safety cards; they contain no prayer, worship material, or religious instruction. In many states, most private youth academies are church-affiliated. Lydia, a federal taxpayer, challenges the provision, arguing that it unconstitutionally supports religious entities.

Does Lydia have standing to make the challenge?', 'Does Lydia have standing to make the challenge?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20714.md","source_question_id":"20714","variant_slug":"prayer_robotics_kits","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"3","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A federal taxpayer challenges a congressional spending program that gives neutral materials to public and church-affiliated private recipients, claiming an Establishment Clause violation. Does the taxpayer have standing?","review_truth":"Federal taxpayers usually lack standing, but a challenge to congressional spending under the Establishment Clause fits the narrow Flast exception.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-FLAST-STANDING-01","title":"Taxpayer Standing: Narrow Door, Not Open Gate","signal":"Federal taxpayer challenges religious spending.","student_move":"Cut ''any taxpayer'' overclaims, then check the narrow spending-power/Establishment trigger.","tiny_rule":"General no standing; Flast exception for congressional spending plus Establishment Clause.","trap":"Treating all taxpayer challenges as allowed, or treating merits proof as standing proof.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-FLAST_SPENDING-01","statement":"Federal taxpayers generally lack standing, but they may challenge a congressional taxing-and-spending enactment when the claim alleges violation of a specific constitutional limit on that spending power; the Establishment Clause is the classic trigger.","type":"exception","trigger":"Federal taxpayer + congressional spending + Establishment Clause claim.","authority":"Flast v. Cohen, 392 U.S. 83 (1968).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-PRAYER_ROBOTICS_KITS-01","statement":"On a taxpayer-standing call, separate entry-to-court from whether the aid is unconstitutional.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The call asks whether the taxpayer has standing.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"taxpayer standing exception","prompt":"Federal taxpayer challenges a congressional spending program as violating the Establishment Clause. Standing?","answer":"Yes, under the narrow Flast exception."},{"drill_type":"overclaim cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Choice says: ''Any federal taxpayer may challenge any congressional spending.'' Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut: overclaim."},{"drill_type":"call focus","target_skill":"standing versus merits","prompt":"Choice denies standing because no religious use has been proved. What is the skipped move?","answer":"The choice answers merits proof, not standing."}]}'),
  ('1e84ae4b-d832-5ade-b7e7-3d41b806c052', '20717_ruths_hymn_recording_procedure', 'TORTS', 'Torts', 'Negligence', 'breach evidence vs breach-caused-injury evidence', 'Ruth, an adult soprano preparing a private Christmas hymns recording, had been treated by an allergist for severe medication sensitivities for four years before undergoing an elective outpatient vocal-cord procedure performed by Dr. Peter, a throat surgeon. Three days after the procedure, Ruth suffered respiratory collapse from a delayed drug reaction that developed after the procedure. The collapse put her in a coma. When doctors concluded that she was in a permanent vegetative state with no hope of recovery, the ventilator and feeding support were withdrawn at her family''s request after court approval, and Ruth died later that evening. Dr. Peter was not involved in that decision or its execution. Ruth''s estate filed a wrongful death action against Dr. Peter, alleging that he was negligent in failing to consult an allergist before the procedure. At trial, the estate offered evidence that accepted medical practice required an allergist to examine a patient before this type of procedure. In this action, the estate should', 'Ruth, an adult soprano preparing a private Christmas hymns recording, had been treated by an allergist for severe medication sensitivities for four years before undergoing an elective outpatient vocal-cord procedure performed by Dr. Peter, a throat surgeon. Three days after the procedure, Ruth suffered respiratory collapse from a delayed drug reaction that developed after the procedure. The collapse put her in a coma. When doctors concluded that she was in a permanent vegetative state with no hope of recovery, the ventilator and feeding support were withdrawn at her family''s request after court approval, and Ruth died later that evening. Dr. Peter was not involved in that decision or its execution. Ruth''s estate filed a wrongful death action against Dr. Peter, alleging that he was negligent in failing to consult an allergist before the procedure. At trial, the estate offered evidence that accepted medical practice required an allergist to examine a patient before this type of procedure. In this action, the estate should

In this action, the estate should', 'In this action, the estate should', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20717.md","source_question_id":"20717","variant_slug":"ruths_hymn_recording_procedure","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"2","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A malpractice plaintiff proves a required specialist consult was omitted but offers no evidence that the consult would have changed the outcome. Should the plaintiff prevail?","review_truth":"Breach evidence does not win malpractice unless the plaintiff also links that breach to the injury.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"TOR-REM-CAUSATION-LINK-01","title":"Breach is not causation","signal":"The stem proves what the doctor should have done but not what would have happened if the doctor had done it.","student_move":"Ask: what evidence links this specific missed step to this specific injury?","tiny_rule":"No link, no negligence win.","trap":"Choosing the answer that proves breach or the answer that proves the procedure caused harm.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-MEDMAL-CAUSATION-01","statement":"In malpractice, proof that the doctor broke a professional rule is not enough; the plaintiff must prove that the broken rule caused the injury.","type":"threshold","trigger":"Stem gives standard-of-care evidence but no evidence the missed consult would change the outcome.","authority":"Restatement (Third) of Torts §§ 26, 29; medical-malpractice causation doctrine.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-TORTS-LIFE-SUPPORT-01","statement":"When the alleged tort creates the condition requiring life support, later withdrawal of support normally does not cut off causation.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Family/court withdraws support after the medical injury creates a permanent vegetative state.","authority":"State v. Pelham; State v. Patterson.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-CAUSATION-LINK-01","statement":"Track the alleged breach, not the whole medical timeline. The question is not procedure-to-death; it is missed-consult-to-injury.","type":"clash","trigger":"One answer proves breach while another says no evidence the omitted consult would change the outcome.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"A malpractice stem proves accepted practice required a specialist consult. No evidence says the consult would have changed the outcome. Cut the breach-only answer.","answer":"Cut as wrong_element: breach without causation."},{"drill_type":"CLASH","target_skill":"tension_axis","prompt":"One answer says the doctor was negligent for omitting a consult. Another says no proof shows the consult would change the result. Name the axis.","answer":"Breach evidence vs breach-caused-injury evidence."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"life_support_superseding_cause","prompt":"The injury causes a vegetative state; family and court approve withdrawal of support. Does that usually break causation?","answer":"No, if the original breach caused the condition requiring support."}]}'),
  ('683eb2ff-5ffb-54bd-ac9a-bd1a82b387b8', '20725_emmaus_laser_cutter', 'TORTS', 'Business torts', 'Negligent misrepresentation', 'known-recipient duty boundary: no contract vs every foreseeable reader vs named recipient', 'Paul owned a small private company, Emmaus Games, that made Bible-themed board games. To decide whether to buy a used industrial laser cutter from Paul, Ruth asked for an inspection report. Paul hired Daniel, a professional equipment inspector, and told him that the report would be sent to Ruth for that purchase. Daniel prepared a report stating that the laser cutter had no major functional defects, but he carelessly failed to include that its control board was failing. Ruth reasonably relied on the report, bought the cutter, and lost money when it broke down soon after.', 'Paul owned a small private company, Emmaus Games, that made Bible-themed board games. To decide whether to buy a used industrial laser cutter from Paul, Ruth asked for an inspection report. Paul hired Daniel, a professional equipment inspector, and told him that the report would be sent to Ruth for that purchase. Daniel prepared a report stating that the laser cutter had no major functional defects, but he carelessly failed to include that its control board was failing. Ruth reasonably relied on the report, bought the cutter, and lost money when it broke down soon after.

Is Ruth likely to prevail on a negligent misrepresentation claim against Daniel?', 'Is Ruth likely to prevail on a negligent misrepresentation claim against Daniel?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20725.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"emmaus_laser_cutter","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62050200","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"moderate-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A professional supplies careless false information for a specifically known person''s reliance in a business transaction. The known person relies and loses money. Can that person recover for negligent misrepresentation?","review_truth":"Known recipient is enough; every foreseeable reader is too many; knowledge of falsity is fraud territory, not negligence.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-NM-CLASS-BOUNDARY-01","title":"Known recipient, not no one and not everyone","signal":"Professional report prepared for a named recipient''s business reliance.","student_move":"Cut the extremes: no-contract is too narrow; every-foreseeable is too broad.","tiny_rule":"Known recipient or limited known group can recover for careless false business information.","trap":"Students choose either strict privity or unlimited foreseeability.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-KNOWN-RECIPIENT-01","statement":"For negligent misrepresentation by a professional information supplier, no contract privity is required when the plaintiff is the known person, or one of a limited known group, expected to rely on the information in the transaction.","type":"rule","trigger":"professional report + named recipient or limited known group + business reliance","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §552; Private Mortgage Investment Services, Inc. v. Hotel & Club Associates, Inc.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-BRACKET-CLASS-01","statement":"When the array swings from no contract to every foreseeable reader, bracket both extremes and look for the named-recipient middle lane.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"choices include no contract, every foreseeable, and named-recipient reliance","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"Overclaim class boundary","prompt":"Choice says negligent misrepresentation reaches every foreseeable reader of a professional report. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut: tiered_absolute / too broad."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"Known recipient rule","prompt":"Professional knows a named buyer will rely on a careless report. Buyer is not in contract with professional. Does no contract alone defeat the claim?","answer":"No. Use the known-recipient Gold Key."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"Mental-state mismatch","prompt":"Choice says negligent misrepresentation requires knowledge of falsity. Stem says carelessly false information. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut: flat_misstatement / wrong mental state."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"Bracket extremes","prompt":"Array contains no contract, every foreseeable reader, and known recipient. Which lane is structurally favored after the Gold Key?","answer":"Known recipient."}]}'),
  ('a06c6b79-2a41-5f98-a315-38212d3caa4e', '20727_easter_garden_blast_hose', 'TORTS', 'Strict liability', 'Abnormally dangerous activities - harms outside abnormal risk', 'special-risk injury link vs activity/site/timing shortcut', 'Esther owned a private hillside garden where she planned to stage a Bible-themed outdoor drama. She hired Daniel’s blasting company to use explosives to carve a stone backdrop for the ''empty tomb'' scene. While the crew was checking the perimeter and before any explosive charge had been set, Lydia arrived to drop off palm-branch props. Near the refreshment tent, an ordinary folding costume rack tipped over and fractured Lydia’s ankle.', 'Esther owned a private hillside garden where she planned to stage a Bible-themed outdoor drama. She hired Daniel’s blasting company to use explosives to carve a stone backdrop for the ''empty tomb'' scene. While the crew was checking the perimeter and before any explosive charge had been set, Lydia arrived to drop off palm-branch props. Near the refreshment tent, an ordinary folding costume rack tipped over and fractured Lydia’s ankle.

Lydia sues Daniel''s company for strict liability based on the blasting. Is the company strictly liable?', 'Lydia sues Daniel''s company for strict liability based on the blasting. Is the company strictly liable?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20727.md","source_question_id":"20727","variant_slug":"easter_garden_blast_hose","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"63040100","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium_high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"The injury came from a folding costume rack, not blasting danger.","distilled_core_question":"A plaintiff is hurt by an ordinary site hazard during preparation for blasting. The injury does not come from blasting danger. Is the blaster strictly liable?","review_truth":"Strict liability for an abnormally dangerous activity covers only injuries caused by the special danger that makes the activity abnormal.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-SPECIAL-RISK-LINK","title":"Strict Liability Needs the Risk Link","signal":"Dangerous activity plus ordinary injury.","student_move":"Ask what hurt the plaintiff before choosing a strict-liability answer.","tiny_rule":"Activity label is not enough; injury must flow from the abnormal risk.","trap":"Picking the dangerous-activity label.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-SPECIAL-RISK-01","statement":"Strict liability for an abnormally dangerous activity follows the special danger, not every accident near the activity. The injury must be the kind of harm that made the activity abnormal.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The stem gives an abnormally dangerous activity plus an ordinary injury mechanism.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §519(2)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-RISK-LINK-01","statement":"In a 2-yes / 2-no strict-liability array, do not pick the outcome first. Pick the reason that links this injury to the risk named in the answer.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Two answers say yes, two say no, and only one answer talks about the injury source.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"A fireworks company is preparing a show. Before any fireworks are armed, a visitor is hurt by a collapsing picnic table. Strict liability?","answer":"No, unless the injury came from the special fireworks risk."},{"drill_type":"EAR","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Strict liability never applies before the dangerous activity begins.","answer":"Cut the absolute; timing alone is not the scope rule."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"2-yes / 2-no reason read","prompt":"Two answers say yes, two say no. One yes proves activity danger; one no proves no special-risk injury. Which reason controls?","answer":"The injury-source reason controls."},{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"special-risk limitation","prompt":"State the tiny rule for abnormally dangerous activity strict liability.","answer":"Liability covers only the kind of harm that makes the activity abnormally dangerous."}]}'),
  ('a61d5696-560d-58e3-95b4-3b8902211768', '20728_star_stable_stall', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Ownership', 'Landlord and Tenant - Assignments and subletting', NULL, 'Lydia owns a privately run Christmas craft hall called Star & Stable Market. For the season, she leased Stall 7 to Timothy to sell handmade Nativity candles. After Peter mailed a rent check for Stall 7 in his own name, Lydia deposited the check and said nothing. Timothy had assigned the remaining stall lease to Peter the week before. The lease said Timothy could not assign or sublease the stall without Lydia’s consent. When Lydia later decided she did not want Peter operating the booth, she declared the stall lease terminated.', 'Lydia owns a privately run Christmas craft hall called Star & Stable Market. For the season, she leased Stall 7 to Timothy to sell handmade Nativity candles. After Peter mailed a rent check for Stall 7 in his own name, Lydia deposited the check and said nothing. Timothy had assigned the remaining stall lease to Peter the week before. The lease said Timothy could not assign or sublease the stall without Lydia’s consent. When Lydia later decided she did not want Peter operating the booth, she declared the stall lease terminated.

May Lydia do so?', 'May Lydia do so?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20728.md","source_question_id":"20728","variant_slug":"star_stable_stall","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"82010404","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A landlord accepts rent from a known unauthorized assignee without protest, then tries to terminate the lease for the assignment. May the landlord terminate?","review_truth":"Accepting rent from a known unauthorized assignee without prompt objection may waive the right to terminate for that assignment.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REMED-RP-LEASE-WAIVER-01","title":"Original Clause vs Later Conduct","signal":"Lease restricts assignment, but landlord accepts rent from assignee.","student_move":"Do not stop at the restriction. Check whether the landlord later acted like the transfer was accepted.","tiny_rule":"Known breach + rent accepted without objection can waive forfeiture.","trap":"Choosing the original clause after the landlord''s later conduct changes the result.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-REAL_PROPERTY-STALL-WAIVER-01","statement":"A landlord who knows of an unauthorized assignment or sublease and accepts rent without promptly reserving rights generally waives the right to terminate for that transfer.","type":"rule","trigger":"The landlord accepts rent from the assignee and says nothing.","authority":"Judicial Council of California CACI No. 4324; Kern Sunset Oil Co. v. Good Roads Oil Co.; Weisman v. Clark.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-REAL_PROPERTY-STALL-WAIVER-01","statement":"When one answer uses the original lease clause and another uses later landlord conduct, mark the later conduct fact before choosing.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"The stem gives both a no-assignment clause and later acceptance of rent without protest.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"Lease-transfer waiver","prompt":"Landlord knows of an unauthorized assignment, accepts rent from the assignee, and says nothing. What is the waiver effect?","answer":"The landlord may waive the right to terminate for that transfer."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"Wrong-element cut","prompt":"Choice says: ''Yes, because the lease prohibited assignment.'' Stem says landlord accepted rent from assignee without protest. What is the defect?","answer":"It proves the original breach but does not answer the later waiver fact."},{"drill_type":"timing","target_skill":"Contradiction by sequence","prompt":"Stem says landlord deposited assignee''s rent and said nothing. Choice says landlord objected within a reasonable time. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut. The choice conflicts with the timing signal."}]}'),
  ('74cc0bef-77a8-54b4-84fa-1469ea0e573f', '20729_courtyard_stormwater', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Rights in Land', 'Takings / Landlord-Tenant', 'one-consequence answer versus combined-consequence answer', 'Lydia leased an urban courtyard lot that she owned to Timothy for a seven-year term so Timothy could run evening Bible-verse calligraphy workshops there. In the third year of the term, the city condemned the entire courtyard lot by eminent domain for a public stormwater basin.', 'Lydia leased an urban courtyard lot that she owned to Timothy for a seven-year term so Timothy could run evening Bible-verse calligraphy workshops there. In the third year of the term, the city condemned the entire courtyard lot by eminent domain for a public stormwater basin.

Which statement best describes Timothy''s rights?', 'Which statement best describes Timothy''s rights?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20729.md","source_question_id":"20729","variant_slug":"courtyard_stormwater","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"81020801","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard anchor array","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A tenant still has years left on a lease when the government condemns the entire leased parcel. Is the tenant still liable for future rent, and does the tenant receive part of the condemnation award?","review_truth":"A total eminent-domain taking of leased land ends future rent and compensates the tenant for the leasehold value taken.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RP-ANCHOR-TAKING-LEASE-01","title":"Whole taking of leased premises","signal":"Entire leased parcel is condemned during an unexpired term.","student_move":"Ask for both consequences: rent and award.","tiny_rule":"Whole taking = leasehold gone; future rent gone; leasehold value claim remains.","trap":"Picking only the rent half or only the award half.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-REAL_PROPERTY-WHOLE-TAKING-01","statement":"A total eminent-domain taking of leased land reaches the tenant''s leasehold: future rent stops, and the tenant may claim the value of the leasehold interest taken.","type":"rule","trigger":"entire leased parcel taken by eminent domain during the lease term","authority":"U.S. Const. amend. V; United States v. General Motors Corp.; Farr v. Williams; City of Vista v. Fielder","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-REAL_PROPERTY-COMPONENT-PACKAGE-01","statement":"When two choices each give one consequence and a third combines them, test each component. If both components are true, the combined choice is the complete answer.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"one answer says X, one says Y, one says X plus Y","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"total taking of leased premises","prompt":"A tenant has four years left when the entire leased parcel is condemned. Name the two tenant consequences.","answer":"Future rent stops; tenant may claim leasehold value in the award."},{"drill_type":"Silver Key answer-array","target_skill":"component package","prompt":"One choice says X, one says Y, one says X and Y. What move do you run?","answer":"Test X and Y separately; if both are true, choose the combined answer."},{"drill_type":"Trap diagnosis","target_skill":"fabricated formal condition","prompt":"A choice says a leasehold survives a taking unless named in the award. What mold is this?","answer":"fabricated_rule."}]}'),
  ('a956243f-bdf7-5c09-b771-81da86cbabbe', '20730_shepherd_sling_fair', 'TORTS', 'Torts', 'Intentional Torts', 'contact intent vs injury/risk/motive mental-state distractors', 'At a privately run Bible-story craft fair held in a rented community center, Mary brought her seven-year-old son Peter. When Lydia, a volunteer judge, gave the blue ribbon to another child''s model of Noah''s ark instead of Peter''s painted shepherd-sling display, Peter shouted, ran across the aisle, and slammed his shoulder into Lydia. Lydia fell against a table and fractured her elbow.', 'At a privately run Bible-story craft fair held in a rented community center, Mary brought her seven-year-old son Peter. When Lydia, a volunteer judge, gave the blue ribbon to another child''s model of Noah''s ark instead of Peter''s painted shepherd-sling display, Peter shouted, ran across the aisle, and slammed his shoulder into Lydia. Lydia fell against a table and fractured her elbow.

In Lydia''s battery action against Peter, which argument most strongly supports liability?', 'In Lydia''s battery action against Peter, which argument most strongly supports liability?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20730.md","source_question_id":"20730","variant_slug":"shepherd_sling_fair","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"64010102","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"2","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A child deliberately makes body contact with an adult, causing a fall and injury. In the adult''s battery action against the child, which fact most strongly supports liability?","review_truth":"For battery, aim intent at the contact, not the injury, risk, or motive.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-BATTERY-CONTACT-INTENT","title":"Battery mental-state target","signal":"Battery answer set offers contact, injury, risk, and motive.","student_move":"Lock the call, then pick the answer that targets contact.","tiny_rule":"Contact intent beats injury/risk/motive.","trap":"Choosing the more serious-sounding injury or risk answer.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-BATTERY-CONTACT-INTENT-01","statement":"Battery intent points at harmful/offensive contact or apprehension of that contact. It does not require intent to injure, appreciation of risk, or bad motive; a child can be liable if the child formed that contact-level intent.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Battery call plus choices that talk about injury, risk, age, or anger.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 13, 18; Garratt v. Dailey.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-BATTERY-ANSWER-ARRAY-01","statement":"When the answer array gives four mental states, choose the one aimed at the tort named in the call. For battery, the contact-target answer beats injury, risk, and motive answers.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Battery call with choices about contact, injury, risk, and anger.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"choice_sort","target_skill":"mental-state target sorting","prompt":"Battery call: one choice says contact intent, one says injury intent, one says risk appreciation, one says bad motive. Which target belongs to battery?","answer":"Contact intent."},{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"battery anchor","prompt":"Does battery require intent to injure?","answer":"No. It requires intent to cause harmful/offensive contact or apprehension."},{"drill_type":"crossover","target_skill":"battery vs negligence","prompt":"A child is old enough to appreciate risk. Is that the strongest battery argument?","answer":"No. Risk appreciation is a negligence-style frame; battery turns on contact intent."},{"drill_type":"motive_filter","target_skill":"motive vs element","prompt":"The defendant was unjustifiably angry before making contact. Is that the battery element?","answer":"No. It may explain motive, but the element is intentional contact."}]}'),
  ('7e606a45-314d-5e94-a04c-df1cfa2af7a5', '20733_galilee_trails_death_notice', 'TORTS', 'Negligence damages', 'Special NIED categories', 'special erroneous-death-notice category vs ordinary NIED physical-danger default', 'Galilee Trails, a private company that runs paid Christian wilderness retreats, required each participant to list an emergency contact. During a dawn prayer hike, Lydia became separated from her group. Peter, the company’s trip coordinator, mixed up two radio reports and sent Lydia’s husband, Timothy, an emergency text saying that Lydia had been found dead near the overlook. Lydia was actually alive and waiting in a ranger cabin with Ruth, a first-aid volunteer. Timothy suffered severe emotional distress from the death notice. The jurisdiction recognizes negligent erroneous death notice as a special NIED category.', 'Galilee Trails, a private company that runs paid Christian wilderness retreats, required each participant to list an emergency contact. During a dawn prayer hike, Lydia became separated from her group. Peter, the company’s trip coordinator, mixed up two radio reports and sent Lydia’s husband, Timothy, an emergency text saying that Lydia had been found dead near the overlook. Lydia was actually alive and waiting in a ranger cabin with Ruth, a first-aid volunteer. Timothy suffered severe emotional distress from the death notice. The jurisdiction recognizes negligent erroneous death notice as a special NIED category.

If Timothy sues Galilee Trails, how should a court likely rule on the emotional-distress claim?', 'If Timothy sues Galilee Trails, how should a court likely rule on the emotional-distress claim?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20733.md","source_question_id":"20733","variant_slug":"galilee_trails_death_notice","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020203","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium_high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A spouse receives a negligent false death notice, suffers severe emotional distress, and the jurisdiction recognizes erroneous death notice as a special NIED category. Is the claim viable?","review_truth":"A recognized negligent erroneous-death-notice category can support NIED without ordinary physical-danger limits.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-NIED-SPECIAL-CATEGORY-01","title":"Special category beats ordinary NIED gate","signal":"Stem gives a recognized emotional-distress category.","student_move":"Do not import zone-of-danger or bystander requirements unless the call asks for the ordinary rule.","tiny_rule":"Recognized erroneous death notice is its own NIED lane.","trap":"Physical danger sounds like the default NIED screen.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-ERRONEOUS-DEATH-NOTICE-01","statement":"When a jurisdiction recognizes negligent erroneous death notice, the claim does not fail merely because the plaintiff was outside physical danger or because the reported death was false.","type":"exception","trigger":"False death notice to close family + severe distress + jurisdiction recognizes the category.","authority":"Johnson v. State of New York, 37 N.Y.2d 378 (1975); Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Physical and Emotional Harm § 47(b).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-SPECIAL-CATEGORY-CUT-01","statement":"When the stem says the jurisdiction recognizes a special category, cut choices that answer the ordinary default lane instead of the special lane.","type":"cut","trigger":"Stem phrase: jurisdiction recognizes negligent erroneous death notice as a special NIED category.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim detection","prompt":"A choice says any negligent message that causes grief creates liability. What is the mold?","answer":"tiered_absolute"},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"bait doctrine","prompt":"The stem gives an erroneous-death-notice category, and a choice denies recovery for lack of physical danger. What is the skipped move?","answer":"Special-category lane check."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"NIED special category","prompt":"A spouse receives a negligent false death notice and suffers severe distress. The jurisdiction recognizes the category. Does lack of physical danger defeat the claim?","answer":"No."}]}'),
  ('a69c5057-5a86-5753-ba35-9c2429335cf7', '20735_bethlehem_donkey_blower', 'TORTS', 'Nuisance', 'Injunction, damages, and avoidable consequences', 'all-or-nothing remedies versus tailored abatement and avoidable damages', 'Barnabas ran a private Christmas-nativity petting-zoo business on land next to Ruth’s small prayer-retreat cottages. To dry animal bedding overnight, Barnabas installed a compost-air blower behind the donkey shed. The blower pushed a sour odor and low rumble across Ruth’s cottages, and several guests lost sleep. Ruth sued for private nuisance and requested a permanent injunction closing the petting-zoo business entirely. Before the hearing, Barnabas proved that, for a moderate cost, he could move the blower into a sealed feed room and add a scrubber, which would end nearly all odor and vibration without closing the business. Ruth refused to discuss that change and demanded closure because the guests had been miserable for months.', 'Barnabas ran a private Christmas-nativity petting-zoo business on land next to Ruth’s small prayer-retreat cottages. To dry animal bedding overnight, Barnabas installed a compost-air blower behind the donkey shed. The blower pushed a sour odor and low rumble across Ruth’s cottages, and several guests lost sleep. Ruth sued for private nuisance and requested a permanent injunction closing the petting-zoo business entirely. Before the hearing, Barnabas proved that, for a moderate cost, he could move the blower into a sealed feed room and add a scrubber, which would end nearly all odor and vibration without closing the business. Ruth refused to discuss that change and demanded closure because the guests had been miserable for months.

What is the most likely remedy in Ruth’s private nuisance claim against Barnabas?', 'What is the most likely remedy in Ruth’s private nuisance claim against Barnabas?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20735.md","source_question_id":"20735","variant_slug":"bethlehem_donkey_blower","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62050102","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"moderate-cost relocation and scrubber would end nearly all interference","distilled_core_question":"A private nuisance can be mostly fixed by reasonable abatement at moderate cost. The plaintiff wants total closure and refused to discuss the fix. What remedy is most likely?","review_truth":"A nuisance remedy is usually tailored to reasonable abatement; mitigation can limit damages, but it does not erase the nuisance or require total shutdown.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-NUISANCE-ABATEMENT-01","title":"Nuisance Remedy Oversteer","signal":"The answer says total shutdown, no remedy, or punitive-only.","student_move":"Ask which remedy fits the abatement fact.","tiny_rule":"Tailor the fix; limit avoidable damages.","trap":"All-or-nothing remedy language.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-NUISANCE-ABATEMENT-01","statement":"In nuisance remedies, a court can tailor abatement to a reasonable fix. Avoidable-consequences principles can limit damages, but they do not erase the nuisance or require total closure.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The stem gives both a substantial interference and a moderate-cost abatement option.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 822, 826; Boomer v. Atlantic Cement Co., 26 N.Y.2d 219 (1970).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-BETHLEHEM-01","statement":"When the choices are shutdown, no remedy, punitive-only, and tailored abatement, treat the first three as remedy oversteers. Match the remedy to the stem’s reasonable-fix fact.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"The stem says a moderate-cost change would end nearly all interference.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"A nuisance exists, so any substantial nuisance requires the defendant to stop the activity. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut: automatic-shutdown overclaim."},{"drill_type":"CLASH","target_skill":"remedy axis","prompt":"No remedy because plaintiff refused mitigation vs tailored abatement with limited damages. Name the axis.","answer":"Whether mitigation erases the remedy or only limits avoidable damages."},{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"avoidable consequences","prompt":"What does mitigation do in a nuisance-remedy question?","answer":"It can limit damages; it does not erase nuisance relief."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"oversteer screen","prompt":"Choices are shutdown, no remedy, punitive-only, tailored abatement. What should you look for?","answer":"The fact that supports a reasonable tailored fix."}]}'),
  ('ab9688a5-3f0e-577a-84bb-092a97570d24', '20737_jonahs_whale_lyre', 'CONTRACTS', 'Remedies', 'Specific performance for unique or highly unusual goods', 'Which fact triggers specific performance?', 'At a Bible-camp art fair, Lydia arrived on March 5 with $3,700 to collect a hand-carved olive-wood lyre shaped like Jonah’s whale. Two days earlier, Paul, who operated a booth selling Christian folk art, had agreed to sell that lyre to Lydia for that price. Paul refused to hand it over.', 'At a Bible-camp art fair, Lydia arrived on March 5 with $3,700 to collect a hand-carved olive-wood lyre shaped like Jonah’s whale. Two days earlier, Paul, who operated a booth selling Christian folk art, had agreed to sell that lyre to Lydia for that price. Paul refused to hand it over.

If Lydia sues Paul, is Lydia entitled to specific performance of the sale?', 'If Lydia sues Paul, is Lydia entitled to specific performance of the sale?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20737.md","source_question_id":"20737","variant_slug":"jonahs_whale_lyre","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A buyer agreed to buy a highly unusual chattel, and the seller refused to deliver it. Can the buyer get specific performance?","review_truth":"Specific performance for goods turns on uniqueness or high unusualness, not merchant status, ordinary personalty labels, or the seller’s hypothetical remedy.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-SP-UNIQUE-GOODS-01","title":"Specific performance for goods: find the uniqueness trigger","signal":"Buyer asks for specific performance of a chattel.","student_move":"Ask whether the object is unique or highly unusual; ignore merchant status and reciprocity.","tiny_rule":"Unique/highly unusual goods can support buyer specific performance.","trap":"Ordinary personalty damages default overstated into a never rule.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-UNIQUE-GOODS-SP-01","statement":"For goods, specific performance is exceptional but available to a buyer when the goods are unique or highly unusual.","type":"exception","trigger":"The stem describes an unusual object and asks for specific performance.","authority":"UCC 2-716(1)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-JONAH-LYRE-01","statement":"In a 3-Yes/1-No remedies array, do not accept the first Yes. Pick the answer tied to the stem’s remedy-trigger fact.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Multiple Yes answers give different reasons for specific performance.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"Gold Key recall","prompt":"Buyer seeks specific performance of a highly unusual chattel. What fact matters?","answer":"The goods are unique or highly unusual."},{"drill_type":"wrong_element_cut","target_skill":"Issue-Sense","prompt":"Choice says yes because the seller is a merchant. What filter breaks?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE; merchant status is not the remedy trigger."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"Ear plus anchor","prompt":"Choice says no specific performance is ever available for personalty. What mold?","answer":"tiered_absolute; it omits the unique-goods exception."},{"drill_type":"answer_array_navigation","target_skill":"Silver Key","prompt":"Three answers say yes for different reasons. What move?","answer":"Pick the Yes tied to the stem’s remedy-trigger fact."}]}'),
  ('68d29415-78b6-5385-91db-1b7f22744e73', '20744_bethlehem_loading_lane', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Duty / Foreseeability Target', 'foreseeability target: plaintiff/risk in the lane versus set-stage cause, exact chain, or defendant''s own breach', 'Source row used: BARMATRIX Q#20744, Torts, official key C, asking which foreseeability target controls the taxi driver’s negligence liability. 

Christian variation — final locked version

During setup for a private Christmas Bible-pageant at a rented fairground, Timothy was pushing a handcart stacked with manger scenery when he clipped Lydia and hurried away, leaving her sprawled in a marked loading lane. A few minutes later, Stephen, drowsy after knowingly taking a strong sleep aid, drove a small electric forklift down the lane and ran over Lydia’s leg.

If Lydia brings a negligence action against Stephen for damages resulting from her fractured leg, Stephen’s liability will most probably turn on whether it was foreseeable that', 'Source row used: BARMATRIX Q#20744, Torts, official key C, asking which foreseeability target controls the taxi driver’s negligence liability. 

Christian variation — final locked version

During setup for a private Christmas Bible-pageant at a rented fairground, Timothy was pushing a handcart stacked with manger scenery when he clipped Lydia and hurried away, leaving her sprawled in a marked loading lane. A few minutes later, Stephen, drowsy after knowingly taking a strong sleep aid, drove a small electric forklift down the lane and ran over Lydia’s leg.

If Lydia brings a negligence action against Stephen for damages resulting from her fractured leg, Stephen’s liability will most probably turn on whether it was foreseeable that

If Lydia brings a negligence action against Stephen for damages resulting from her fractured leg, Stephen''s liability will most probably turn on whether it was foreseeable that', 'If Lydia brings a negligence action against Stephen for damages resulting from her fractured leg, Stephen''s liability will most probably turn on whether it was foreseeable that', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20744.md","source_question_id":"20744","variant_slug":"bethlehem_loading_lane","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A vehicle operator injures a person already in the travel path. Which foreseeability target matters: a person in the path, the prior event that put the person there, the exact second accident, or the operator''s own negligence?","review_truth":"Foreseeability targets the plaintiff/risk created by the defendant''s conduct, not the exact set-stage event and not the defendant''s own negligence.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"TOR-FORESEEABILITY-TARGET-01","title":"Foreseeability target scan","signal":"All answers ask what had to be foreseeable.","student_move":"Name the object after ''foreseeable that'' before judging law.","tiny_rule":"Pick the plaintiff/risk target, not the old cause, exact chain, or defendant breach.","trap":"Students pick the most dramatic fact instead of the legal target.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-FORESEEABILITY-TARGET-01","statement":"For negligence by an active operator, the foreseeability target is the plaintiff/class/risk created by the defendant''s conduct, not the exact prior event that put the plaintiff there and not whether the defendant''s own careless act was foreseeable.","type":"distinction","trigger":"answer choices all complete ''foreseeable that ___''","authority":"Palsgraf v. Long Island R.R.; Restatement (Third) of Torts §§ 7, 29","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-BETHLEHEM-LANE-01","statement":"When every choice starts from ''foreseeable that,'' compare the object of foreseeability first. Set-stage cause, exact chain, defendant breach, and plaintiff risk are different targets.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"same grammatical stem repeated across all choices","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"target_scan","target_skill":"identify foreseeability object","prompt":"In a negligence answer choice, underline the object after ''foreseeable that.'' Is it plaintiff/risk, exact chain, prior cause, or defendant breach?","answer":"plaintiff/risk is the target"},{"drill_type":"wrong_element","target_skill":"separate breach from foreseeability","prompt":"A choice says liability turns on whether the defendant would drive while impaired. What element is it chasing?","answer":"defendant breach, not foreseeability target"},{"drill_type":"set_stage","target_skill":"identify preexisting condition lure","prompt":"A person is already in a lane before defendant drives through it. Does liability turn on whether the earlier cause of that position was foreseeable?","answer":"No; focus on whether a person in the lane was foreseeable."}]}'),
  ('dbacf306-9c18-5779-bbdc-f0d0fcde539f', '20760_rubber_stamp_mail_fraud', 'EVIDENCE', 'Types of Evidence', 'C1', NULL, 'At Daniel’s federal mail-fraud trial over forged Bible-study supply invoices, the prosecutor offers a rubber stamp marked ''PAID'' that was found in a clearance bin at Lydia’s Christian Bookshop twenty miles from the post office used in the alleged scheme. No witness connects the stamp to Daniel, any invoice, or the charged mailing. Daniel objects.', 'At Daniel’s federal mail-fraud trial over forged Bible-study supply invoices, the prosecutor offers a rubber stamp marked ''PAID'' that was found in a clearance bin at Lydia’s Christian Bookshop twenty miles from the post office used in the alleged scheme. No witness connects the stamp to Daniel, any invoice, or the charged mailing. Daniel objects.

How should the court rule?', 'How should the court rule?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20760.md","source_question_id":"20760","variant_slug":"rubber_stamp_mail_fraud","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"31010300","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"trap","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"At a federal trial, the prosecution offers a physical object with no connection to the defendant, the charged event, or any relevant issue. Should it be admitted?","review_truth":"A physical object is not relevant just because it belongs to a category that could matter; it needs a connection to a fact that matters in this case.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"EVI-REMEDY-REAL-OBJECT-CONNECTION-01","title":"Real object needs a bridge","signal":"Physical object offered, but no fact connects it to the charged event or issue.","student_move":"Ask: what fact does this object make more likely?","tiny_rule":"Object category is not a bridge.","trap":"The crime label makes the object feel relevant.","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL"},"gold_keys":[],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-EVIDENCE-RUBBER-STAMP-01","statement":"In Evidence, start with the bridge: what fact does this item help prove in this case? If the stem gives no bridge, do not jump to object category, demonstrative use, or prejudice.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"A physical object is offered, but the stem says no witness connects it to the defendant, document, event, or issue.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"A physical object is offered because all objects of that kind are relevant in this type of case. What is the C3 cut?","answer":"Tiered-absolute overclaim; category alone is not a case connection."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"offered_purpose","prompt":"The proponent offers an object as real evidence. A choice admits it as a demonstrative aid, but the stem gives no such purpose. What is the cut?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE; bait_doctrine / offered_for_purpose."},{"drill_type":"CALL_FOCUS","target_skill":"layer_order","prompt":"An object has no link to the charged event. One choice excludes under prejudice only; another excludes for no probative value. Which layer comes first?","answer":"Connection/relevance comes before prejudice."}]}'),
  ('baa7935e-f4fe-5f3f-9941-ebfe015c6722', '20805_psalm_lanterns_confirmation', 'CONTRACTS', 'Statute of Frauds', 'D', 'timely oral objection versus required written objection', 'Martha and Timothy are both merchants who wholesale supplies for Christian camp stores. At a trade fair, Timothy orally agrees to buy 180 brass lanterns with Psalm-themed labels from Martha for $18,000. Later that week, Martha mails Timothy a signed written confirmation of the deal. On the eighth day after receiving it, Timothy leaves Martha a voicemail: “I object to your confirmation.” Timothy sends no written objection and later refuses the lanterns.', 'Martha and Timothy are both merchants who wholesale supplies for Christian camp stores. At a trade fair, Timothy orally agrees to buy 180 brass lanterns with Psalm-themed labels from Martha for $18,000. Later that week, Martha mails Timothy a signed written confirmation of the deal. On the eighth day after receiving it, Timothy leaves Martha a voicemail: “I object to your confirmation.” Timothy sends no written objection and later refuses the lanterns.

Which statement best states the result?', 'Which statement best states the result?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20805.md","source_question_id":"20805","variant_slug":"psalm_lanterns_confirmation","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"54030400","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"trap repair","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Two merchants make an oral goods deal; the sender sends a signed confirmation; the recipient objects orally within 10 days but sends no writing. Does the confirmation still work against the recipient?","review_truth":"Between merchants, a confirming writing is not defeated by an oral objection; the objection must be written within 10 days.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CONTRACTS-ANCHOR-MERCHANT-CONFIRMATION-01","title":"Merchant confirmation: oral objection trap","signal":"Two merchants, oral goods deal, signed confirmation, oral objection.","student_move":"Check whether the recipient sent written notice of objection within 10 days.","tiny_rule":"Written objection required; oral objection is not enough.","trap":"Treating any timely objection as enough.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-MERCHANT-CONFIRMATION-01","statement":"Between merchants, a signed written confirmation sufficient against the sender can satisfy the UCC Statute of Frauds against the recipient if the recipient has reason to know its contents and does not give written notice of objection within 10 days. Oral objection is not enough.","type":"exception","trigger":"two merchants + oral goods deal + signed written confirmation + oral/no written objection","authority":"UCC §2-201(2)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-TIMING-VS-FORM-01","statement":"When a choice says the objection was within 10 days, ask a second question: was it in the required form? Timing does not cure the wrong method of objection.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"oral objection inside a time window","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"merchant-confirmation written objection","prompt":"Two merchants make an oral goods deal. Seller sends a signed confirmation. Buyer calls within 10 days to object but sends no writing. Does the call defeat the confirmation?","answer":"No. Written objection is required."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"timing versus form","prompt":"A choice says: ''Any objection within 10 days defeats the confirmation.'' Name the trap.","answer":"Timing-swallows-form trap; objection must be written."},{"drill_type":"mold_label","target_skill":"fabricated_rule","prompt":"A choice says the sender must prove the recipient read every word of a merchant confirmation. Mold?","answer":"fabricated_rule."},{"drill_type":"signature_myth","target_skill":"recipient signature under merchant confirmation","prompt":"A choice says the confirmation fails because the recipient merchant did not sign it. Why cut?","answer":"The merchant-confirmation rule can work without recipient signature if no timely written objection is sent."}]}'),
  ('81aea214-8e50-5183-b56e-6f5133e18d6a', '20808_jericho_press', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation', 'Consideration - preexisting legal duty', 'new legal detriment vs preexisting duty', 'Hannah owned a vintage hand-cranked letterpress called the “Jericho Press,” which Timothy liked to use to print private Bible-verse cards. Hannah transferred title to the press to Lydia, but only after Lydia agreed that Timothy could use the press whenever he wished. Lydia agreed but did not tell Timothy about that promise.

Mary lived in a coastal town and had long wanted Timothy to move there for the town’s private Scripture-calligraphy fairs. Mary wrote Timothy a letter saying that, if he moved to the town, she would leave him her estate when she died.

Timothy knew that Lydia now owned the Jericho Press. He told Lydia that, if she would let him use the press until Mary died, he would give Lydia half of whatever he received from Mary. Lydia agreed, and Timothy moved to the town and used the press.

Mary died shortly afterward. Her will left her entire estate to Timothy, but Timothy refused to share any of it with Lydia.', 'Hannah owned a vintage hand-cranked letterpress called the “Jericho Press,” which Timothy liked to use to print private Bible-verse cards. Hannah transferred title to the press to Lydia, but only after Lydia agreed that Timothy could use the press whenever he wished. Lydia agreed but did not tell Timothy about that promise.

Mary lived in a coastal town and had long wanted Timothy to move there for the town’s private Scripture-calligraphy fairs. Mary wrote Timothy a letter saying that, if he moved to the town, she would leave him her estate when she died.

Timothy knew that Lydia now owned the Jericho Press. He told Lydia that, if she would let him use the press until Mary died, he would give Lydia half of whatever he received from Mary. Lydia agreed, and Timothy moved to the town and used the press.

Mary died shortly afterward. Her will left her entire estate to Timothy, but Timothy refused to share any of it with Lydia.

If Lydia asserts a claim against Timothy for a share of the estate that Timothy received from Mary, a court should find for:', 'If Lydia asserts a claim against Timothy for a share of the estate that Timothy received from Mary, a court should find for:', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20808.md","source_question_id":"20808","variant_slug":"jericho_press","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020204","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"high","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"Lydia had already agreed that Timothy could use the press whenever he wished.","distilled_core_question":"A claimant promises to allow a use she already owed, in exchange for half of a later estate. Is the estate-sharing promise enforceable?","review_truth":"A promise to do what you already legally must do is not consideration for a new promise.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-PREEXISTING-DUTY-01","title":"Existing duty is not new consideration","signal":"A party promises to do the same thing already owed.","student_move":"Ask what new legal detriment appears in the later exchange.","tiny_rule":"Already owed is not newly paid for.","trap":"Promise-for-promise wording looks like consideration.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-PRESS-DUTY-01","statement":"A promise to do what the promisee is already legally obligated to do is not consideration for a new promise, unless the new performance is materially different or the old duty is honestly disputed.","type":"rule","trigger":"A later promise is exchanged for permission or performance already owed under an earlier deal.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 71, 73.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-PRESS-DUTY-01","statement":"When one answer says ''promise-for-promise'' and the other says ''no consideration,'' ask what new legal position the plaintiff gave up in the later deal.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"A surface bargain repeats an earlier duty.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"preexisting duty","prompt":"A promises B to do the exact act A already owes B under an earlier contract. Is A''s promise consideration for B''s new promise?","answer":"No. Existing duty is not new consideration."},{"drill_type":"CLASH axis","target_skill":"new legal detriment vs preexisting duty","prompt":"Choice 1 says promise-for-promise. Choice 2 says no consideration. What fact decides?","answer":"Whether the promisee gave up a new legal right or only repeated an existing duty."},{"drill_type":"Wrong-element cut","target_skill":"promissory estoppel reliance","prompt":"A choice says estoppel applies, but the plaintiff did not change position because of the promise. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut. Estoppel needs reliance."},{"drill_type":"Wrong-frame cut","target_skill":"condition-precedent bait","prompt":"A choice uses a hidden earlier promise to make a condition in a later promise by another person. What is the C3 label?","answer":"Wrong-frame bait doctrine."}]}'),
  ('4b2e2804-0686-5dc6-865f-546f63aa0d22', '20811_illuminated_psalms_solvent', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Personal Jurisdiction', 'Specific jurisdiction - contacts before fairness', 'defendant-created contacts vs plaintiff-side fairness/effects', 'Lydia, a Maine resident, paid to attend Barnabas’s private “Illuminated Psalms” calligraphy workshop in New Mexico. During the workshop, Barnabas handed students a solvent that he had prepared for the class. All instruction, materials use, and alleged negligent conduct occurred in New Mexico. After Lydia returned to Maine, her hands became inflamed and she missed several weeks of work on paid Advent-card commissions.

Lydia sued Barnabas in Maine. She argues that Maine is the fairest forum because she is recovering there, her family can help her there, and traveling back to New Mexico would be difficult. Barnabas has never taught workshops, advertised, shipped supplies, or taken commissions in Maine.', 'Lydia, a Maine resident, paid to attend Barnabas’s private “Illuminated Psalms” calligraphy workshop in New Mexico. During the workshop, Barnabas handed students a solvent that he had prepared for the class. All instruction, materials use, and alleged negligent conduct occurred in New Mexico. After Lydia returned to Maine, her hands became inflamed and she missed several weeks of work on paid Advent-card commissions.

Lydia sued Barnabas in Maine. She argues that Maine is the fairest forum because she is recovering there, her family can help her there, and traveling back to New Mexico would be difficult. Barnabas has never taught workshops, advertised, shipped supplies, or taken commissions in Maine.

Can fairness considerations alone create specific jurisdiction in Maine?', 'Can fairness considerations alone create specific jurisdiction in Maine?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20811.md","source_question_id":"20811","variant_slug":"illuminated_psalms_solvent","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A plaintiff travels to another state for a paid service, all defendant conduct occurs there, the harm is later felt at home, and the defendant has no home-state contacts. Can plaintiff hardship and forum fairness alone create specific jurisdiction?","review_truth":"Fairness can make jurisdiction unreasonable after contacts exist, but it cannot create minimum contacts by itself.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-REM-PJ-CONTACTS-FIRST","title":"Contacts First, Fairness Second","signal":"Plaintiff argues home forum is fairest, but defendant did all conduct elsewhere.","student_move":"Ask for defendant-created forum contacts before weighing fairness.","tiny_rule":"Fairness can defeat jurisdiction; it cannot create minimum contacts.","trap":"Letting sympathy for plaintiff hardship substitute for purposeful availment.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-CONTACTS_BEFORE_FAIRNESS-01","statement":"Specific jurisdiction starts with defendant-created forum contacts. Fairness or convenience can defeat jurisdiction after contacts exist, but it cannot create contacts by itself.","type":"threshold","trigger":"The plaintiff felt harm at home or argues the home forum is fair, while the defendant''s conduct all occurred elsewhere.","authority":"International Shoe; World-Wide Volkswagen; Walden; Ford.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-ILLUMINATED_PSALMS_SOLVENT-01","statement":"In a specific-jurisdiction array, sort contacts first and fairness second. A plaintiff-side hardship fact cannot do the work of a defendant-side contact fact.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Choices mention hardship, injury felt in the forum, and no forum-directed defendant conduct.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"A defendant did all conduct in State X; plaintiff feels injury in State Y. Is injury felt in State Y the defendant contact?","answer":"No. It is a plaintiff-side effects fact."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"misfit","prompt":"The call asks personal jurisdiction; an answer discusses federal-court exclusion. What is the cut?","answer":"Misfit: wrong procedural gate."},{"drill_type":"CALL","target_skill":"threshold ordering","prompt":"A personal-jurisdiction answer relies on fairness before defendant contacts. What is the ordered move?","answer":"Contacts first, fairness second."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"fairness-alone trap","prompt":"Plaintiff is ill and travel is hard. Defendant has no forum contacts. Can hardship create contacts?","answer":"No. Fairness cannot create minimum contacts."}]}'),
  ('e8e3e28b-b160-5f31-b8fb-4b51f858b164', '21711_foam_whale_nativity_attachment', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Personal Jurisdiction', 'Quasi-in-rem limits and relatedness', 'court power over defendant vs plaintiff/property/collection lures', 'At a privately rented Bible-story obstacle-course party in State A, Lydia and Daniel, both State A citizens, are helping set up a giant foam whale prop. Daniel negligently bumps the prop, which falls and injures Lydia. Daniel owns a hand-carved olive-wood Nativity set stored in a private storage locker in State B, but Daniel has no other contacts with State B. Lydia sues Daniel in a State B court for the prop injury and attaches the Nativity set, arguing that the property gives State B power to decide the whole injury claim.', 'At a privately rented Bible-story obstacle-course party in State A, Lydia and Daniel, both State A citizens, are helping set up a giant foam whale prop. Daniel negligently bumps the prop, which falls and injures Lydia. Daniel owns a hand-carved olive-wood Nativity set stored in a private storage locker in State B, but Daniel has no other contacts with State B. Lydia sues Daniel in a State B court for the prop injury and attaches the Nativity set, arguing that the property gives State B power to decide the whole injury claim.

What is the best analysis?', 'What is the best analysis?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ21711.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"foam_whale_nativity_attachment","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"94020302","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"The State B property is unrelated to the State A injury claim, and Daniel has no other State B contacts.","distilled_core_question":"Defendant has unrelated property in State B and no other State B contacts. Plaintiff sues there on a State A injury claim and attaches the property. Does State B have personal jurisdiction over the whole claim?","review_truth":"Unrelated forum property is not a magic key to personal jurisdiction over an unrelated claim.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-REM-QIR-RELATEDNESS-01","title":"Unrelated property is not forum power.","signal":"Plaintiff attaches defendant''s forum property for a claim arising elsewhere.","student_move":"Ask whether the property connects the defendant, forum, and litigation.","tiny_rule":"Unrelated property alone does not create jurisdiction over the personal claim.","trap":"Any-property / asset-value shortcut.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-QIR-01","statement":"Forum property is not a magic key. If the claim is unrelated to the attached property, the forum still needs a defendant-forum-litigation connection.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"A plaintiff attaches a defendant''s forum property to sue on an unrelated personal claim.","authority":"Shaffer v. Heitner, 433 U.S. 186; International Shoe Co. v. Washington, 326 U.S. 310.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-JURISDICTION-ASSET-VALUE-01","statement":"Separate court power from collection. An asset''s value may matter after a valid judgment, but it does not answer whether this court can decide the claim.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"An answer asks whether the attached property can satisfy the whole judgment.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"A plaintiff sues in State B because she was injured and prefers that forum. What is the C3 break?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE: plaintiff preference is not defendant-forum power."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"violation_vs_remedy","prompt":"An answer says the attached property is valuable enough to satisfy the judgment. What is the trap?","answer":"It answers collection, not jurisdiction."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"quasi_in_rem_relatedness","prompt":"A defendant''s unrelated property is attached in the forum for a claim arising elsewhere. Does the property alone support jurisdiction?","answer":"No. Unrelated property alone is not enough."},{"drill_type":"TRAP_SPOTTING","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Choice says any property in the forum creates personal jurisdiction. What word should ring?","answer":"Any."}]}'),
  ('12419e26-273d-5337-976c-0e398eff5f6f', '21752_olive_oil_ramp', 'TORTS', 'Vicarious liability', 'Independent contractors - nondelegable duty', 'independent-contractor default vs nondelegable-duty exception', 'Lydia operates a private, for-profit Bible-themed candle-dipping studio called Lampstand Studio. A statute requires businesses that invite the public into hands-on workshops to keep their customer walkway reasonably safe. Before opening for a paid ''Psalms and Candles'' class, Lydia hires Barnabas, an outside floor-care contractor, to cover and clean a slick olive-oil spill on the only ramp customers use to enter the studio. Barnabas negligently leaves the slick oil on the ramp, and Peter slips and falls while entering.', 'Lydia operates a private, for-profit Bible-themed candle-dipping studio called Lampstand Studio. A statute requires businesses that invite the public into hands-on workshops to keep their customer walkway reasonably safe. Before opening for a paid ''Psalms and Candles'' class, Lydia hires Barnabas, an outside floor-care contractor, to cover and clean a slick olive-oil spill on the only ramp customers use to enter the studio. Barnabas negligently leaves the slick oil on the ramp, and Peter slips and falls while entering.

What is Peter''s best argument against Lampstand Studio?', 'What is Peter''s best argument against Lampstand Studio?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ21752.md","source_question_id":"21752","variant_slug":"olive_oil_ramp","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62060101","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A public-facing business has a statutory duty to keep its customer walkway reasonably safe, hires an outside contractor to make the walkway safe, the contractor negligently leaves a hazard, and a customer falls. What is the customer''s best argument against the business?","review_truth":"A hirer may remain liable for an independent contractor''s negligence when the breached duty is nondelegable.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-NONDELEGABLE-01","title":"Independent contractor is not the end of the story","signal":"Hirer has a statutory/public safety duty and hires someone else to perform it.","student_move":"Ask whether the duty itself can be delegated.","tiny_rule":"Nondelegable duty can keep liability with the hirer.","trap":"Contractor status as total shield.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-NONDELEGABLE-01","statement":"A statutory or otherwise nondelegable safety duty stays with the hirer even when the negligent work is done by an independent contractor.","type":"exception","trigger":"statutory/public safety duty plus outside contractor","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 409, 424","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-NONDELEGABLE-01","statement":"When the stem gives a legal safety duty and an outside contractor, do not choose the all-or-nothing contractor answers; look for the duty-stays-with-the-hirer route.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"statutory duty + hired contractor + injured public entrant","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"nondelegable-duty exception","prompt":"A business has a statutory duty to keep an entrance safe and hires an independent contractor to clear it. The contractor is negligent. What argument keeps the business in the case?","answer":"The duty may be nondelegable."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Cut this answer: ''Every outside contractor working at a business is treated as the business''s employee.''","answer":"Cut as tiered_absolute; nondelegable duty is narrower than employee status."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"contractor-shield exception check","prompt":"Stem signal: statutory safety duty + independent contractor + public entrant injured. What trap should you expect?","answer":"The trap is treating contractor status as a total shield."}]}'),
  ('22ee3011-70a1-513f-a5d5-93b4a07141fd', '21848_manna_house_family_integrity', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Due Process', 'Family Liberty', 'family-integrity route vs ordinary zoning route', 'In the city of New Capernaum, the One Hearth residential code permits a one-unit rowhouse to be occupied by a parent and that parent’s children, but it does not allow an adult aunt to live in that same rowhouse with her niece and the niece’s children. Naomi, Lydia, Timothy, and Ruth share a privately owned rowhouse they call Manna House. The city orders Naomi to move out under the code. The household challenges the code as a direct interference with their family living arrangement.', 'In the city of New Capernaum, the One Hearth residential code permits a one-unit rowhouse to be occupied by a parent and that parent’s children, but it does not allow an adult aunt to live in that same rowhouse with her niece and the niece’s children. Naomi, Lydia, Timothy, and Ruth share a privately owned rowhouse they call Manna House. The city orders Naomi to move out under the code. The household challenges the code as a direct interference with their family living arrangement.

What is the best constitutional route?', 'What is the best constitutional route?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ21848.md","source_question_id":"21848","variant_slug":"manna_house_family_integrity","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"44040202","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A city directly excludes a close relative from living with family in one home. What constitutional route should the family use?","review_truth":"Direct government exclusion of close relatives from a shared household routes to substantive due process family integrity, not generic zoning or commerce.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-FAMILY-HOME-01","title":"Close-family household ≠ ordinary zoning","signal":"A city rule excludes one close relative from a shared household.","student_move":"Treat it as family-integrity due process after cutting wrong constitutional homes.","tiny_rule":"Family household first; zoning only after the family trigger is absent.","trap":"Treating every occupancy ordinance as ordinary rational-basis zoning.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-FAMILY-INTEGRITY-01","statement":"When a government housing rule directly excludes close relatives from living together as a household, route the claim to substantive due process family integrity, not ordinary zoning.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"A residential rule slices a close relative out of a family household.","authority":"Moore v. City of East Cleveland, 431 U.S. 494 (1977).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-ROUTE-CALL-01","statement":"On a route call, pick the constitutional home before grading policy strength. First cut homes that have no facts in the stem.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The call asks for the best constitutional route and the choices name different constitutional homes.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"route-call cut","target_skill":"claim-home first","prompt":"A city occupancy rule excludes an aunt from living with her niece and the niece''s children. Which route: commerce, ordinary zoning, or family-integrity due process?","answer":"Family-integrity due process."},{"drill_type":"overclaim detector","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Classify: ''Aunts are never family for constitutional purposes.''","answer":"tiered_absolute; use the family-integrity Gold Key."},{"drill_type":"misfit detector","target_skill":"wrong constitutional home","prompt":"A local family-household ordinance is challenged. The answer says Dormant Commerce Clause only. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut as misfit / NOT_RESPONSIVE."},{"drill_type":"bait-doctrine detector","target_skill":"neighboring context","prompt":"A residential code excludes a close relative from a household. The answer treats it as ordinary zoning rational basis with no family liberty concern. What is the breaker?","answer":"Neighboring zoning frame; direct family exclusion triggers the Gold Key."}]}'),
  ('8549fbd3-3cc3-59d1-9764-0eb65f4233c7', '21867_psalm_puzzle_booth', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Homicide', 'Homicide', 'misdemeanor label vs cause link', 'At a Christian homeschool co-op fair in a rented convention hall, Peter ran a paid Bible-verse puzzle booth without the required county amusement permit, a misdemeanor. Ruth bought a ticket, completed the puzzle, collected a bookmark, and left. Later that afternoon, while helping Stephen hang a banner at a bakery for a separate youth-group fundraiser, Ruth stood on a defective stepstool, fell, and died. Peter was charged with involuntary manslaughter based on operating the booth without the permit. Is Peter guilty of misdemeanor manslaughter?', 'At a Christian homeschool co-op fair in a rented convention hall, Peter ran a paid Bible-verse puzzle booth without the required county amusement permit, a misdemeanor. Ruth bought a ticket, completed the puzzle, collected a bookmark, and left. Later that afternoon, while helping Stephen hang a banner at a bakery for a separate youth-group fundraiser, Ruth stood on a defective stepstool, fell, and died. Peter was charged with involuntary manslaughter based on operating the booth without the permit. Is Peter guilty of misdemeanor manslaughter?

Is Peter guilty of misdemeanor manslaughter?', 'Is Peter guilty of misdemeanor manslaughter?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ21867.md","source_question_id":"21867","variant_slug":"psalm_puzzle_booth","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"74020102","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"moderate","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A person commits a misdemeanor by running an unpermitted paid booth. The customer later dies in a separate accident. Is that misdemeanor enough for misdemeanor manslaughter?","review_truth":"Misdemeanor manslaughter needs a cause link; a misdemeanor that merely came first is not enough.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CRIM-REM-CAUSE-LINK-01","title":"Misdemeanor label is not a death cause","signal":"A misdemeanor is followed by a death from a separate event.","student_move":"Ask whether the misdemeanor produced the death, not merely whether it came first.","tiny_rule":"No cause link, no misdemeanor manslaughter.","trap":"Counting chronology as causation.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIM-MISDEMEANOR-MANSLAUGHTER-01","statement":"Misdemeanor manslaughter still needs actual and proximate causation. A misdemeanor that merely happens before a death is not enough.","type":"threshold","trigger":"unlawful act followed by a separate death mechanism","authority":"MPC §2.03; People v. Penny; Logan/Ceasar line reflected in Oklahoma jury-instruction commentary","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIM-MISDEMEANOR-MANSLAUGHTER-01","statement":"When the array gives you a misdemeanor-label answer and a cause-link answer, lock the cause link before debating the label.","type":"cut","trigger":"one choice says the misdemeanor happened; another choice says the misdemeanor did not cause the death","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"A defendant commits a permit misdemeanor. The victim later dies in an unrelated accident. Is the misdemeanor label enough?","answer":"No; check actual and proximate cause."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"Classify: ''Any death after a misdemeanor is manslaughter.''","answer":"tiered_absolute; NOT_TRUE."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"misdemeanor manslaughter causation","prompt":"State the Gold Key for misdemeanor manslaughter.","answer":"The misdemeanor must actually and proximately cause the death."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"cause-link residual","prompt":"One answer says the misdemeanor happened. One answer says the misdemeanor did not cause the death. Which one is the safer residual?","answer":"The cause-link answer."}]}'),
  ('d70c2a6e-311a-5f75-bf94-cb8a56006557', '21869_tiny_house_hymn_sheet', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Fourth Amendment', 'Arrest warrants and home entry', NULL, 'At Lydia’s tiny house, a private psalm-singing rehearsal has turned into a hymn-sheet stapling contest. Police, holding an arrest warrant for Timothy, get a tip that Timothy is helping there. They have no search warrant for Lydia’s home, no consent, and no emergency. They enter the tiny house and arrest Timothy in the prayer nook.', 'At Lydia’s tiny house, a private psalm-singing rehearsal has turned into a hymn-sheet stapling contest. Police, holding an arrest warrant for Timothy, get a tip that Timothy is helping there. They have no search warrant for Lydia’s home, no consent, and no emergency. They enter the tiny house and arrest Timothy in the prayer nook.

Was the entry lawful?', 'Was the entry lawful?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ21869.md","source_question_id":"21869","variant_slug":"tiny_house_hymn_sheet","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"75070201","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Police have an arrest warrant for a suspect but enter someone else’s home to arrest him without a search warrant, consent, or exigency. Was the entry lawful?","review_truth":"An arrest warrant is not a search warrant for someone else’s home.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CRIM-REM-THIRD-PARTY-HOME-01","title":"Person warrant vs place warrant","signal":"Arrest warrant for suspect + suspect inside someone else''s home.","student_move":"Ask whose home is being entered.","tiny_rule":"Own home may be Payton; third-party home needs Steagald search warrant or exception.","trap":"Arrest-warrant portability.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIM-THIRD-PARTY-HOME-01","statement":"An arrest warrant lets police seize the named suspect; it does not by itself authorize entry into someone else’s home. For a third party’s home, look for a search warrant for that home, consent, or exigency.","type":"distinction","trigger":"arrest warrant + suspect located in a third party''s home","authority":"Steagald v. United States, 451 U.S. 204 (1981); Payton v. New York, 445 U.S. 573 (1980)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIM-ENTRY-FIRST-01","statement":"When the call asks whether entry into a home was lawful, decide the door before the room. A bedroom, prayer nook, or living room fact cannot supply authority to cross the threshold.","type":"cut","trigger":"answer turns on where the suspect was found after entry","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"Gold Key recall","prompt":"Police have an arrest warrant for Paul and enter Ruth’s home to find him without a search warrant, consent, or exigency. Lawful entry?","answer":"No. Third-party home requires a search warrant or valid exception."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"Overclaim detection","prompt":"Cut: ''Yes, because an arrest warrant follows the suspect into any home.''","answer":"tiered_absolute; arrest warrant does not authorize entry into any third-party home."},{"drill_type":"issue_sense","target_skill":"Wrong element / wrong timing","prompt":"Call asks whether entry into Naomi’s home was lawful. Choice says no only because suspect was found in the pantry.","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE; room fact comes after entry."}]}'),
  ('110d352f-2867-5318-b660-cc816b2d45b5', '21872_scripturequest_price_gate', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Dormant Commerce Clause', 'Extraterritorial regulation', 'in-state sales condition vs out-of-state price control', 'Ruth’s Workshop, Inc., a private company founded by Ruth, makes ScriptureQuest, a Bible-trivia board game sold through ordinary retail stores around the country. A state allows board-game makers to sell games in the state only if each month they certify that the price charged for the same game in every other state during that month is not lower than the in-state price. The state insists that it is regulating only sales inside its borders.', 'Ruth’s Workshop, Inc., a private company founded by Ruth, makes ScriptureQuest, a Bible-trivia board game sold through ordinary retail stores around the country. A state allows board-game makers to sell games in the state only if each month they certify that the price charged for the same game in every other state during that month is not lower than the in-state price. The state insists that it is regulating only sales inside its borders.

What is the company''s strongest dormant Commerce Clause objection?', 'What is the company''s strongest dormant Commerce Clause objection?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ21872.md","source_question_id":"21872","variant_slug":"scripturequest_price_gate","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"41030402","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A state says: sell here only if your out-of-state prices this month are not lower than your in-state price. What is the strongest DCC objection?","review_truth":"A state cannot condition in-state sales on the seller’s promise not to charge lower prices in other states.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CONLAW-PRICE-GATE-01","title":"Price Gate Across State Lines","signal":"Sell here only if prices elsewhere satisfy this state’s rule.","student_move":"Follow the price lever across the border.","tiny_rule":"In-state access tied to out-of-state prices points to DCC extraterritorial price control.","trap":"Accepting the state’s in-state label.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-PRICE-AFFIRMATION-01","statement":"A state may not condition in-state sales on a seller’s prices charged wholly outside the state. Price-affirmation laws tying in-state access to out-of-state prices are classic dormant Commerce Clause danger zones.","type":"rule","trigger":"in-state sales permission depends on prices in other states","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-PRICE-LEVER-01","statement":"Ignore the state’s label and follow the lever. If the in-state sales gate moves when out-of-state prices move, the DCC objection is extraterritorial control.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"certify prices in every other state","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"Cut the choice: ''The state may control all nationwide prices once the seller enters its market.''","answer":"extreme_of_range; local sales power is being pushed to nationwide prices."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"misfit","prompt":"Call asks for a DCC objection. Choice says Article IV privileges and immunities is the only doctrine. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut: misfit / wrong doctrine home."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"DCC price-affirmation Gold Key","prompt":"State conditions in-state sales on prices charged in other states. Name the DCC objection.","answer":"Extraterritorial price control / price affirmation."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"state label vs practical effect","prompt":"The state says it regulates only in-state sales. What fact should you follow?","answer":"The certification lever tied to prices in every other state."}]}'),
  ('c7b198d2-209a-514e-a43d-e47f8db35f59', '21879_noahs_ark_puppet_set', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Mutual Assent / Offer and Acceptance', 'offer vs acceptance by required performance', 'On April 3, after hearing that Lydia was organizing a neighborhood Bible-story night, Peter sent Lydia a note offering to sell his handmade Noah’s Ark puppet set. Lydia wrote back on April 10 and told Peter that she would buy the puppet set for $175 if Peter delivered it to her courtyard studio on May 6. On May 5, Lydia decided that she would borrow puppets from Naomi instead and wrote Peter a note telling him that she was no longer interested in buying the puppet set. She was about to take the note to the mail drop when Peter knocked at her door. As soon as Lydia opened the door, Peter said, “I’ll bring you that puppet set tomorrow. I’ll just have to borrow Barnabas’s cargo tricycle to carry it.” Lydia said, “Never mind. I don’t want it,” and handed Peter the note she had written but not mailed.', 'On April 3, after hearing that Lydia was organizing a neighborhood Bible-story night, Peter sent Lydia a note offering to sell his handmade Noah’s Ark puppet set. Lydia wrote back on April 10 and told Peter that she would buy the puppet set for $175 if Peter delivered it to her courtyard studio on May 6. On May 5, Lydia decided that she would borrow puppets from Naomi instead and wrote Peter a note telling him that she was no longer interested in buying the puppet set. She was about to take the note to the mail drop when Peter knocked at her door. As soon as Lydia opened the door, Peter said, “I’ll bring you that puppet set tomorrow. I’ll just have to borrow Barnabas’s cargo tricycle to carry it.” Lydia said, “Never mind. I don’t want it,” and handed Peter the note she had written but not mailed.

Peter’s statement on May 5 that “I’ll bring you that puppet set tomorrow” was probably', 'Peter’s statement on May 5 that “I’ll bring you that puppet set tomorrow” was probably', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ21879.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"noahs_ark_puppet_set","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020101","outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Buyer says she will buy only if seller delivers on a date; seller says the day before that he will bring it tomorrow. What is seller’s statement?","review_truth":"A promise to deliver later is not delivery; if delivery is the required acceptance, the promise is best labeled an offer.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-OFFER-ACCEPTANCE-MODE-01","title":"Mode-of-Acceptance Label Drill","signal":"One party says “I will buy if you deliver,” and the other says “I will deliver.”","student_move":"Ask what act the first statement required and whether that act has happened.","tiny_rule":"Words promising future delivery are not the delivery itself.","trap":"Calling the future promise an acceptance or commencement.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-MODE-ACCEPTANCE-01","statement":"If an offer requires acceptance by a specified performance or tender, a later promise to perform is not the invited acceptance; it is at most a new offer or counteroffer.","type":"distinction","trigger":"A buyer says “I will buy if you deliver,” and the seller later says “I will deliver.”","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§30, 45, 50; UCC §2-206 language/circumstances caveat.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-NOAHS-ARK-01","statement":"Classify the sentence before debating doctrine: future-tense words are not performance already begun.","type":"cut","trigger":"Choice labels a promise to bring later as performance now.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"label_classification","target_skill":"offer_acceptance_mode","prompt":"Buyer: “I will buy if you deliver Friday.” Seller Thursday: “I will deliver Friday.” Classify seller’s words.","answer":"Offer, not acceptance, if delivery was the required acceptance."},{"drill_type":"verb_tense_cut","target_skill":"future_words_vs_performance","prompt":"Choice says “commencement of performance.” Stem says “I will bring it tomorrow.” Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut; future words are not present performance."},{"drill_type":"wrong_frame","target_skill":"ratification_trap","prompt":"Can a party ratify an acceptance that never legally occurred?","answer":"No."}]}')
