  ('530d625f-a6ce-5066-9229-6ee16341fb3b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20603_road_to_emmaus_escape_room'), 'C', 'Yes, because Ruth''s rescue attempt was reasonable.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["heroism_equals_liability","rescue_success_required","rescuer_reasonableness_is_dispositive","policy_statement_is_rule"]', 'The student treats Ruth''s reasonable rescue as enough. The breaker is that the claim is against Daniel''s estate and needs Daniel-side fault.', 'The answer addresses Ruth''s rescue conduct rather than Daniel''s fault.', 'Ask whether the defendant, Daniel, failed to exercise reasonable care.', 'REM-TORTS-RESCUE-ACTOR-FAULT-01'),
  ('400c821f-ae17-5698-bd8c-575d57cabe43', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20603_road_to_emmaus_escape_room'), 'D', 'No, because Ruth''s attempt failed and therefore did not benefit Daniel.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["heroism_equals_liability","rescue_success_required","rescuer_reasonableness_is_dispositive","policy_statement_is_rule"]', 'The student treats the failed rescue as defeating the claim. The breaker is that rescue success is not the defendant-fault element.', 'The answer addresses failed benefit rather than Daniel''s negligence.', 'Ask whether failed rescue is the legal axis or only a decoy fact.', 'REM-TORTS-RESCUE-ACTOR-FAULT-01'),
  ('037535d4-2ea4-5c0b-9a87-43ebedf77ac5', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20605_scripture_quiz_whisper'), 'A', 'No, because false light requires commercial use of Daniel''s identity.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["falsity_is_enough","false_light_equals_defamation","commercial_use_required_for_all_privacy_torts"]', 'The choice is attractive because it says No and uses a real privacy-sounding commercial-use concept. The breaker is that commercial identity use belongs to appropriation, not the false-light publicity call.', 'This choice imports the commercial-use element from appropriation rather than answering the false-light publicity call.', 'Ask whether the reason belongs to false light or to a neighboring privacy tort.', 'TOR-ANCHOR-FALSE-LIGHT-PUBLICITY'),
  ('701158e3-bb60-585d-99f4-2d3e1fbc1fb7', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20605_scripture_quiz_whisper'), 'B', 'No, because the accusation was not publicized to a broad enough audience.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'B is correct. Martha made the accusation only to Lydia, in a private aside, and Lydia kept it to herself. Daniel has a false-light theory only if the false light was publicized to the public or to a broad enough audience. That publicity threshold is missing.

Gold Key: False light needs public-style publicity. A private one-listener accusation is not enough, even if the statement is false and offensive.

Silver Key: In a No/No pair, keep the No reason that tracks the decisive fact in the stem. Here the repeated fact is the private one-person communication, not commercial use.', NULL, 'TOR-ANCHOR-FALSE-LIGHT-PUBLICITY'),
  ('a38e7307-0b13-5e25-b7fa-654dc509852c', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20605_scripture_quiz_whisper'), 'C', 'Yes, because the accusation cast Daniel in a false light.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["falsity_is_enough","false_light_equals_defamation","commercial_use_required_for_all_privacy_torts"]', 'The choice is attractive because the accusation really did make Daniel look dishonest. The breaker is that false impression alone skips the broad-publicity threshold.', 'This choice proves only the false-impression element and skips the publicity gate.', 'Run the Gold Key: false light needs broad publicity.', 'TOR-ANCHOR-FALSE-LIGHT-PUBLICITY'),
  ('da1e9cbd-5dca-57bf-b6bf-34b9a88e0100', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20605_scripture_quiz_whisper'), 'D', 'Yes, because false light and defamation are the same tort.', 0, '["flat_misstatement","wrong_frame"]', '["falsity_is_enough","false_light_equals_defamation","commercial_use_required_for_all_privacy_torts"]', 'The choice is attractive because false light and defamation both involve false statements and often appear together. The breaker is that they are not identical, and false light has its own publicity gate.', 'This choice collapses false light and defamation into the same tort.', 'Treat ''same'' and ''identical'' as danger words when two neighboring torts are being collapsed.', 'TOR-ANCHOR-FALSE-LIGHT-PUBLICITY'),
  ('86a865d6-5e18-51d1-8a3f-961989533f4c', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20606_lydia_festival_venue'), 'A', 'The Eastern District of State J and the Western District of State J.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["venue_follows_plaintiff","one_proper_venue_only","event_location_only","defendant_residence_only"]', 'This choice sees that both defendants live in State J, so it grabs the residence bucket. It drops the event bucket, which the venue checklist also requires for a complete all-proper-districts answer.', 'It gives the defendant-residence bucket but omits the event bucket.', 'Add the event bucket before selecting a list answer.', 'CIV-REMEDY-VENUE-BUCKETS-20606'),
  ('156083d9-6733-597d-9871-ccc3e3a9ed00', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20606_lydia_festival_venue'), 'B', 'The Central District of State H.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["venue_follows_plaintiff","one_proper_venue_only","event_location_only","defendant_residence_only"]', 'This choice sees the accident district and stops at the most concrete fact. It skips the defendant-residence bucket that also works because both defendants reside in the same state.', 'It gives the event bucket but omits the defendant-residence bucket.', 'Ask whether all defendants live in one state and then add each defendant-residence district.', 'CIV-REMEDY-VENUE-BUCKETS-20606'),
  ('916626c2-5080-58c7-85cf-288a0ab7501d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20606_lydia_festival_venue'), 'C', 'The Central District of State H, the Eastern District of State J, and the Western District of State J.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'C is correct. Gold Key: For ordinary federal civil actions, venue can be proper in a district where a substantial part of the events occurred, and also in a district where any defendant resides if all defendants reside in that same state. Silver Key: Treat the answer choices as bucket checklists: event bucket, defendant-residence bucket, and plaintiff-home lure.

The explosion occurred in the Central District of State H, so that district is a proper event venue. Peter and Ruth both reside in State J, though in different districts, so each district where one of them resides is also a proper defendant-residence venue. The complete list is therefore the Central District of State H, the Eastern District of State J, and the Western District of State J.', NULL, 'CIV-REMEDY-VENUE-BUCKETS-20606'),
  ('2d0fc5a5-bfe3-5377-a2ab-79312c091c5b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20606_lydia_festival_venue'), 'D', 'The Northern District of State G and the Central District of State H.', 0, '["flat_misstatement","wrong_frame"]', '["venue_follows_plaintiff","one_proper_venue_only","event_location_only","defendant_residence_only"]', 'This choice rewards the impulse to include the plaintiff''s home because she chose the lawsuit. The ordinary venue checklist does not use the plaintiff''s home, so the added district poisons the list.', 'It adds the plaintiff-home district, which is not a venue bucket here.', 'Use the Gold Key: plaintiff home is not a venue bucket in this ordinary private-defendant action.', 'CIV-REMEDY-VENUE-BUCKETS-20606'),
  ('a3de0c95-4174-5703-a764-1b5894d49e31', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20627_ruth_hymn_poster_contract'), 'A', 'No, because state-law contract claims can never be heard under diversity.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["response_date_controls_diversity","pretrial_citizenship_changes_control","state_law_claims_cannot_be_diversity"]', 'The student sees a state-law contract claim and overcorrects into thinking federal court is unavailable. The breaker is the categorical word never; diversity jurisdiction can hear qualifying state-law civil actions.', 'The word never makes an overbroad category claim. State-law contract claims are not categorically excluded from diversity jurisdiction.', 'Ask whether the answer is making a categorical claim broader than diversity doctrine allows.', 'REM-CIV-DIVERSITY-FILING-SNAPSHOT'),
  ('c3c34c66-de8c-5f32-ad35-d504631a0c84', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20627_ruth_hymn_poster_contract'), 'B', 'Yes, because any change in citizenship before trial controls.', 0, '["tiered_absolute","procedural_frame"]', '["response_date_controls_diversity","pretrial_citizenship_changes_control","state_law_claims_cannot_be_diversity"]', 'The student grabs the later Colorado move and treats any pretrial citizenship change as controlling. The breaker is the filing-day Gold Key; later citizenship changes do not create diversity missing at filing.', 'The word any and the before-trial timing both fight the filing-day snapshot.', 'Circle the complaint-filing date and check citizenship there first.', 'REM-CIV-DIVERSITY-FILING-SNAPSHOT'),
  ('29f9453f-6734-56e2-b476-7864c4c3982e', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20627_ruth_hymn_poster_contract'), 'C', 'No, because Ruth and Daniel were both Arizona citizens when the complaint was filed.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is true and responsive: both parties were Arizona citizens when the complaint was filed, so the later move did not create diversity.', NULL, 'REM-CIV-DIVERSITY-FILING-SNAPSHOT'),
  ('b040cc39-e1fe-5a56-91f8-a93cf57dd697', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20627_ruth_hymn_poster_contract'), 'D', 'Yes, because diversity is measured when Daniel first answers.', 0, '["backwards","procedural_frame"]', '["response_date_controls_diversity","pretrial_citizenship_changes_control","state_law_claims_cannot_be_diversity"]', 'The student treats the answer deadline as the moment when jurisdiction becomes fixed. The breaker is that the timing runs the other way: citizenship is measured when the complaint is filed.', 'The response-date timing reverses the filing-day rule.', 'Say: filing first, response later; jurisdiction is tested at filing.', 'REM-CIV-DIVERSITY-FILING-SNAPSHOT'),
  ('78f12bcb-05c6-5561-8ea9-bde4678db7ee', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20638_advent_rescue_beacons'), 'A', 'Congress has not expressly exempted Barnabas from complying with state clean-water statutes.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["express exemption required","intrastate means state wins","navigability controls federal power"]', 'The choice sells the formal idea that express congressional wording controls. The breaker is that express wording does not answer practical interference.', 'The choice focuses on express exemption instead of the interference axis.', 'Ask whether the fact addresses practical burden on federal work.', 'REM-CONLAW-FED-CONTRACTOR-INTERFERENCE'),
  ('193f6617-3f3f-56e3-8eb8-fe70ae26bddd', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20638_advent_rescue_beacons'), 'B', 'Mercy Brook is not navigable.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["express exemption required","intrastate means state wins","navigability controls federal power"]', 'The choice sells navigability as a legal-sounding jurisdiction fact. The breaker is that navigability is not the federal-contractor immunity axis.', 'The choice shifts to navigability, a different jurisdictional frame.', 'Return to the immunity call, not admiralty or river power.', 'REM-CONLAW-FED-CONTRACTOR-INTERFERENCE'),
  ('6ec861e7-df1c-5e75-831a-e28313448382', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20638_advent_rescue_beacons'), 'C', 'Keeping the releases at three ounces or less per day would not increase the cost of producing the rescue-beacon casings required by the contract.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'After geography/formality traps are cut, C is the answer that matches the interference axis.', NULL, 'REM-CONLAW-FED-CONTRACTOR-INTERFERENCE'),
  ('f45883fc-f82e-5808-b612-df3fb92a0f03', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20638_advent_rescue_beacons'), 'D', 'Mercy Brook is located entirely within the state.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["express exemption required","intrastate means state wins","navigability controls federal power"]', 'The choice sells the creek’s intrastate location as state-control proof. The breaker is that federal authority is already supplied by the stem.', 'The choice shifts to intrastate geography after federal authority is already given.', 'Accept the stem’s federal-authority premise and look for interference.', 'REM-CONLAW-FED-CONTRACTOR-INTERFERENCE'),
  ('0e8495c7-045e-5e2b-84f5-4760c3c433d6', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20649_christmas_pageant_platform'), 'A', 'The company that previously rented the platform failed to notify Lydia''s company that the platform needed a roll-stop interlock.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["avoidability equals negligence","other cause defeats liability","other party fault defeats liability"]', 'Choice A looks useful because it blames a different business in the equipment chain. The breaker is that another actor''s lapse does not answer whether Barnabas''s own foreseeable-risk breach is defeated.', 'The choice blames a different company instead of attacking Barnabas''s own negligence.', 'Ask whether the fact defeats Barnabas''s own negligence.', 'REM-TORTS-20649-01'),
  ('c26d8e66-4f01-5ba4-b150-e3ba60b12d60', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20649_christmas_pageant_platform'), 'B', 'If Naomi had been standing on the operator step when she started the platform, she would not have been injured.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["avoidability equals negligence","other cause defeats liability","other party fault defeats liability"]', 'Choice B looks powerful because the call mentions contributory negligence and the fact says Naomi could have avoided injury. The breaker is that avoidability is not the same as negligent plaintiff conduct.', 'The choice proves a safer hypothetical, not negligent plaintiff conduct.', 'Demand a fact saying Naomi acted unreasonably.', 'REM-TORTS-20649-01'),
  ('66ac1e63-d478-598f-bc55-ae83be4ab05f', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20649_christmas_pageant_platform'), 'C', 'Rolling pageant platforms of this type are usually equipped with roll-stop interlocks.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Only C targets the contractor''s own foreseeable-risk breach.', NULL, 'REM-TORTS-20649-01'),
  ('4b4d066e-ce06-5ca8-9abe-2e2f39595b24', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20649_christmas_pageant_platform'), 'D', 'The absence of a roll-stop interlock was a manufacturing defect in the platform.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["avoidability equals negligence","other cause defeats liability","other party fault defeats liability"]', 'Choice D looks powerful because a manufacturing defect feels like a complete alternate explanation. The breaker is that another cause does not automatically defeat Barnabas''s own negligence.', 'The choice points to product-defect liability, not a defense to Barnabas''s own negligence.', 'Ask whether the fact negates Barnabas''s foreseeable risk.', 'REM-TORTS-20649-01'),
  ('4c8e1cd5-435a-54c4-b72f-00322e1d1574', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20654_nativity_ornaments_volition'), 'A', 'Lydia has an insanity defense because she has a recognized mental disease.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["m_naghten_ends_all_insanity","mental_disease_equals_insanity","impulse_overrides_cognition"]', 'This choice grabs the mental-disease fact and treats it as enough. The breaker is that the call needs a legal incapacity, not a diagnosis alone.', 'The choice proves a threshold diagnosis but does not state the legal incapacity that answers the call.', 'Mark diagnosis as threshold only, then look for cognition or volition.', 'CRIM-ANCHOR-INSANITY-01'),
  ('482f56f0-aea4-5105-af47-55daa3471cd6', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20654_nativity_ornaments_volition'), 'B', 'Lydia may have an insanity defense under the irresistible-impulse supplement because she could not conform her conduct to the law despite knowing the damage was wrong.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'B is correct. Lydia’s strongest insanity claim is not that she failed to know the act was wrong. The stem says she did know that. Her claim is that, because of a recognized mental disease, she could not conform her conduct to the law.

Gold Key: M’Naghten is the cognition lane: knowing the nature or wrongfulness of the act. Irresistible impulse is the volition lane: knowing wrong but unable to control or conform conduct.
Silver Key: In this answer array, sort every choice into one of three buckets: cognition, volition, or diagnosis alone. The stem gives “knows wrong” and “cannot conform,” so the volition bucket wins.', NULL, 'CRIM-ANCHOR-INSANITY-01'),
  ('97990ce9-c707-5fd0-b2c7-522db5eb221a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20654_nativity_ornaments_volition'), 'C', 'Lydia has no insanity defense because she knew breaking the ornaments was wrong.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["m_naghten_ends_all_insanity","mental_disease_equals_insanity","impulse_overrides_cognition"]', 'This choice correctly notices that Lydia knew the damage was wrong. The breaker is that knowledge defeats only M''Naghten, not the irresistible-impulse supplement.', 'The choice answers the cognition lane only and ignores the volitional supplement named in the stem.', 'Use the Gold Key: cognition fails, volition may survive.', 'CRIM-ANCHOR-INSANITY-01'),
  ('885a40b1-74be-528b-9302-045ae140562f', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20654_nativity_ornaments_volition'), 'D', 'Lydia has an insanity defense under M''Naghten because the impulse overrides her cognition.', 0, '["flat_misstatement","wrong_frame"]', '["m_naghten_ends_all_insanity","mental_disease_equals_insanity","impulse_overrides_cognition"]', 'This choice hears the impulse fact and tries to use it. The breaker is that impulse belongs to the irresistible-impulse lane, not the M''Naghten lane.', 'The choice places the impulse-control fact under M''Naghten, which is the wrong test label.', 'Attach impulse-control facts to irresistible impulse, not M''Naghten.', 'CRIM-ANCHOR-INSANITY-01'),
  ('19dcdf8c-c5dc-5d88-af8b-660ce3553ab1', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20690_bethlehem_star_drone'), 'A', 'Deny the motion only if Mary establishes supplemental jurisdiction over the amended damages theory.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["personal_jurisdiction_never_waivable","amended_complaint_resets_all_defenses","supplemental_jurisdiction_controls_personal_jurisdiction_motion"]', 'This choice borrows a jurisdiction word that sounds like a threshold requirement. The breaker is that supplemental jurisdiction answers subject-matter power over claims, while the call asks whether this defendant lost a personal-jurisdiction objection.', 'The choice answers supplemental jurisdiction, while the call asks personal-jurisdiction waiver.', 'Circle the jurisdiction word in the call and in the answer; cut if they do not match.', 'CIV-REMEDY-R12-WAIVER-20690'),
  ('c9497125-747b-5155-b2f0-e2cf5bd588f6', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20690_bethlehem_star_drone'), 'B', 'Deny the motion because Daniel waived the available personal-jurisdiction defense by leaving it out of his first answer.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Only B is true and responsive to the late personal-jurisdiction motion.', NULL, 'CIV-REMEDY-R12-WAIVER-20690'),
  ('f180906e-f487-5d32-b9bf-b5b5f2878bba', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20690_bethlehem_star_drone'), 'C', 'Grant the motion because lack of personal jurisdiction can never be waived.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["personal_jurisdiction_never_waivable","amended_complaint_resets_all_defenses","supplemental_jurisdiction_controls_personal_jurisdiction_motion"]', 'This choice exploits the student myth that all jurisdiction defects are never-waivable. The breaker is the personal-versus-subject-matter distinction: personal jurisdiction can be waived.', 'The choice overclaims with never; personal jurisdiction can be waived.', 'Separate subject-matter jurisdiction from personal jurisdiction before choosing.', 'CIV-REMEDY-R12-WAIVER-20690'),
  ('f93ca9cd-013f-5218-81e1-cbd4f0130132', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20690_bethlehem_star_drone'), 'D', 'Grant the motion because every amended complaint gives a defendant a fresh chance to assert all Rule 12 defenses.', 0, '["fabricated_rule","procedural_frame"]', '["personal_jurisdiction_never_waivable","amended_complaint_resets_all_defenses","supplemental_jurisdiction_controls_personal_jurisdiction_motion"]', 'This choice turns a new pleading response moment into a full procedural reset. The breaker is that a same-defendant amendment does not automatically revive Rule 12 defenses that were available and waived earlier.', 'The choice invents an automatic fresh-start rule for every amended complaint and all Rule 12 defenses.', 'Ask whether the amended complaint first created the defense; same transaction and same defendant usually means no.', 'CIV-REMEDY-R12-WAIVER-20690'),
  ('3775e95f-da1a-5c54-b714-6778066422fc', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20714_prayer_robotics_kits'), 'A', 'No, because Lydia has not shown the required nexus between her taxpayer status and the safety-kit expenditures.', 0, '["backwards","wrong_frame"]', '["all_taxpayers_can_sue","standing_requires_merits_proof","nexus_language_always_defeats_standing"]', 'The choice uses technical standing language, so it feels precise. The breaker is that the Gold Key says this spending-limit connection is supplied, not missing.', 'The choice says the standing nexus is missing, but the Gold Key shows the congressional spending and Establishment Clause facts supply the narrow exception.', 'Ask whether the challenge is to congressional spending and alleges an Establishment Clause limit.', 'REM-CONLAW-FLAST-STANDING-01'),
  ('fe071a70-d24f-5c30-afea-c3969f893e33', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20714_prayer_robotics_kits'), 'B', 'Yes, because any federal taxpayer may challenge a congressional spending authorization.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["all_taxpayers_can_sue","standing_requires_merits_proof","nexus_language_always_defeats_standing"]', 'The choice turns taxpayer status into a simple open door. The breaker is the absolute word ''any,'' because taxpayer standing is narrow.', 'The word ''any'' overstates federal taxpayer standing.', 'Mark ''any'' and replace it with ''only if the narrow exception applies.''', 'REM-CONLAW-FLAST-STANDING-01'),
  ('ff58ca1e-56b5-582a-98d5-b317486bf880', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20714_prayer_robotics_kits'), 'C', 'No, because there is no proof that any money freed up by the free kits will be used for religious purposes.', 0, '["wrong_element","violation_vs_remedy"]', '["all_taxpayers_can_sue","standing_requires_merits_proof","nexus_language_always_defeats_standing"]', 'The choice feels like it tests whether religious support has been proved. The breaker is that proof of religious use is a merits question, while the call asks standing.', 'The choice addresses proof of religious use, not taxpayer standing.', 'Separate entry-to-court from proving the Establishment Clause violation.', 'REM-CONLAW-FLAST-STANDING-01'),
  ('dd646632-a617-5845-bcf1-06a56e0a546e', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20714_prayer_robotics_kits'), 'D', 'Yes, because the challenge alleges a possible violation of a specific constitutional limit on Congress’s spending power.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'D is correct. Lydia is not relying on a general taxpayer grievance. She is challenging a congressional spending provision and claiming that the spending violates a specific constitutional limit on that spending power.

Gold Key: Federal taxpayers generally cannot sue just because tax money is being spent, but the Flast exception allows standing for a challenge to congressional taxing-and-spending action when the claim invokes a specific constitutional limit on that spending power. The Establishment Clause is the classic trigger.

Silver Key: Lock the call as standing, not merits. Do not ask whether the robotics kits are actually unconstitutional; ask whether this taxpayer is allowed into court to make that claim.', NULL, 'REM-CONLAW-FLAST-STANDING-01'),
  ('99c694d9-d023-5be5-83e0-8f6705371065', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20717_ruths_hymn_recording_procedure'), 'A', 'not prevail, because there is no evidence that an allergist would have provided advice that would have changed the outcome if one had examined Ruth before the procedure.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only choice that answers the causation gap created by the stem.', NULL, 'TOR-REM-CAUSATION-LINK-01'),
  ('efe1e166-9b68-52d5-a729-c9d6cfad4d7b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20717_ruths_hymn_recording_procedure'), 'B', 'prevail, because the respiratory collapse that caused Ruth''s death was caused by the procedure that Dr. Peter performed.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["breach_equals_liability","procedure_caused_harm_equals_breach_caused_harm","life_support_withdrawal_breaks_causation"]', 'A student sees a complete medical timeline from procedure to collapse to death. The breaker is that the alleged breach is the missed consult, not the procedure in general.', 'This choice proves the procedure was in the causal chain, but the alleged breach is the missed allergist consult.', 'Ask whether the missed allergist consult caused the collapse.', 'TOR-REM-CAUSATION-LINK-01'),
  ('71286181-829f-5b0b-9084-f7179601028b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20717_ruths_hymn_recording_procedure'), 'C', 'not prevail, because Dr. Peter had nothing to do with the withdrawal of life support, which was the cause of Ruth''s death.', 0, '["flat_misstatement","wrong_frame"]', '["breach_equals_liability","procedure_caused_harm_equals_breach_caused_harm","life_support_withdrawal_breaks_causation"]', 'A student sees that Dr. Peter did not withdraw support and treats that later decision as a clean causal break. The breaker is the life-support Gold Key: if the breach caused the need for support, withdrawal normally does not supersede causation.', 'This choice treats life-support withdrawal as the cause that defeats liability, but that move fails if the breach caused the condition requiring support.', 'Ask whether the alleged breach created the condition requiring support.', 'TOR-REM-CAUSATION-LINK-01'),
  ('a6e94a8b-8389-50f4-a68c-dd0907b3cb25', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20717_ruths_hymn_recording_procedure'), 'D', 'prevail, because Dr. Peter was negligent in failing to have Ruth examined by an allergist before the procedure.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["breach_equals_liability","procedure_caused_harm_equals_breach_caused_harm","life_support_withdrawal_breaks_causation"]', 'A student grabs the strongest plaintiff fact, the accepted-practice violation. The breaker is that breach is not enough without evidence that the breach caused the injury.', 'This choice proves breach but omits proof that the breach caused Ruth''s collapse.', 'Require evidence that the breach changed the outcome.', 'TOR-REM-CAUSATION-LINK-01'),
  ('2249d94b-959d-5eb2-a2c0-bdd4154efcad', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20725_emmaus_laser_cutter'), 'A', 'Yes, because negligent misrepresentation reaches every foreseeable purchaser who might later read the inspection report.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["professional_liability_requires_contract","foreseeability_always_defines_tort_duty","negligence_requires_knowing_falsehood"]', 'The choice flatters broad foreseeability: if anyone could be expected to read the report, liability feels fair. The word every makes the class too wide and the known-recipient Gold Key narrows the answer.', 'The choice over-expands the class with every foreseeable purchaser.', 'Bracket the class: not every foreseeable reader, only the named recipient or limited known group.', 'REM-TORTS-NM-CLASS-BOUNDARY-01'),
  ('d8e3b1d5-4ffa-556c-992b-b6577103810c', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20725_emmaus_laser_cutter'), 'B', 'No, because Ruth was not in contract with Daniel.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["professional_liability_requires_contract","foreseeability_always_defines_tort_duty","negligence_requires_knowing_falsehood"]', 'The choice sells the familiar contract boundary: Ruth never hired Daniel. The Gold Key cuts it because the known-recipient duty does not require contract privity.', 'The choice invents a contract-privity requirement for a known-recipient professional-information case.', 'Ask whether the professional knew this plaintiff or limited group would rely in the transaction.', 'REM-TORTS-NM-CLASS-BOUNDARY-01'),
  ('7a7ef91e-3937-5b7e-a5fe-64eea15edbcb', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20725_emmaus_laser_cutter'), 'C', 'No, because negligent misrepresentation requires Daniel to have known the control-board statement was false.', 0, '["flat_misstatement","wrong_frame"]', '["professional_liability_requires_contract","foreseeability_always_defines_tort_duty","negligence_requires_knowing_falsehood"]', 'The choice imports a fraud mental state, making the mistake feel more serious before liability attaches. The call says negligent misrepresentation and the facts say careless, so knowledge of falsity is the wrong mental state.', 'The choice imports a knowledge-of-falsity requirement into a negligent/careless frame.', 'Use the stem''s word careless and the call''s word negligent to reject knowledge as the required mental state.', 'REM-TORTS-NM-CLASS-BOUNDARY-01'),
  ('355770b6-5f5d-5b1c-80e7-134b04a00441', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20725_emmaus_laser_cutter'), 'D', 'Yes, because Daniel supplied false information for a known buyer''s reliance in a business transaction.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is true and responsive after the class-boundary and mental-state traps are cut.', NULL, 'REM-TORTS-NM-CLASS-BOUNDARY-01'),
  ('60957b2b-ed73-5c7c-bf94-a70a200dc4fc', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20727_easter_garden_blast_hose'), 'A', 'No, because the injury did not result from an explosion, flying rock, ground shock, or other special risk of the blasting.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Only A answers the strict-liability call through the injury-source fact.', NULL, 'REM-TORTS-SPECIAL-RISK-LINK'),
  ('d7f6d1ed-ba47-5ad4-ac8e-9a1256dbe082', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20727_easter_garden_blast_hose'), 'B', 'No, because strict liability never applies before blasting begins.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["dangerous_activity_equals_liability","site_connection_equals_liability","timing_fact_controls_all"]', 'The stem gives a timing fact, so this answer feels like a clean no-liability rule. The breaker is the absolute word ''never,'' which overstates timing and ignores the injury-source rule.', 'The word ''never'' makes timing an absolute rule. The item turns on special-risk causation, not timing alone.', 'Cut the absolute and ask whether the harm fell inside or outside the special risk.', 'REM-TORTS-SPECIAL-RISK-LINK'),
  ('85caa67e-df9e-542c-99db-8d10142d2be8', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20727_easter_garden_blast_hose'), 'C', 'Yes, because the injury occurred at the blasting site.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["dangerous_activity_equals_liability","site_connection_equals_liability","timing_fact_controls_all"]', 'The injury did occur at the blasting site, so the answer uses a true fact. The breaker is that location does not prove the injury came from the special blasting risk.', 'The site fact is true but not enough. It does not connect Lydia''s injury to the special danger of blasting.', 'Separate location from injury source.', 'REM-TORTS-SPECIAL-RISK-LINK'),
  ('bc4c65ae-ea64-53d1-ba76-83dad9036878', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20727_easter_garden_blast_hose'), 'D', 'Yes, because blasting with explosives is abnormally dangerous.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["dangerous_activity_equals_liability","site_connection_equals_liability","timing_fact_controls_all"]', 'Blasting with explosives sounds like the whole strict-liability trigger. The breaker is that the activity label omits the required link between the abnormal risk and this injury.', 'The dangerous-activity label is true but incomplete. It omits the injury-source link.', 'Ask what made the activity abnormal and whether that danger caused this injury.', 'REM-TORTS-SPECIAL-RISK-LINK'),
  ('b5aa5987-804d-5b5d-9c86-26f81c76f791', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20728_star_stable_stall'), 'A', 'Yes, because Lydia’s ownership interest in the market hall was superior to Timothy’s and Peter’s leasehold interests.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["lease_clause_always_controls","owner_interest_controls_all_lease_disputes","later_termination_equals_prompt_objection"]', 'The student reaches for the simple idea that the property owner has the stronger interest. The breaker is that ownership hierarchy does not answer whether Lydia''s later conduct waived termination.', 'The choice answers a property-hierarchy question rather than the waiver-by-conduct question.', 'Ask whether the answer explains the rent acceptance and no-protest facts.', 'REMED-RP-LEASE-WAIVER-01'),
  ('6080c4f1-2c9d-5588-854c-7cd3f011ca8d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20728_star_stable_stall'), 'B', 'No, because Lydia waived her right to terminate the lease.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only answer that accounts for the rent acceptance and no-protest facts.', NULL, 'REMED-RP-LEASE-WAIVER-01'),
  ('16e81176-2d82-5982-a12c-2b051d254e3f', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20728_star_stable_stall'), 'C', 'Yes, because Lydia objected within a reasonable time.', 0, '["contradiction"]', '["lease_clause_always_controls","owner_interest_controls_all_lease_disputes","later_termination_equals_prompt_objection"]', 'The student treats Lydia''s later declaration as an objection. The breaker is that the stem first gives rent acceptance and silence, not a prompt reservation.', 'The choice claims timely objection, but the stem first gives acceptance and silence.', 'Mark the earlier deposit-and-silence fact before evaluating the later termination.', 'REMED-RP-LEASE-WAIVER-01'),
  ('d9cda5e6-b3a6-5464-aa6c-2404207d9451', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20728_star_stable_stall'), 'D', 'Yes, because the lease prohibited assignments without Lydia’s consent.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["lease_clause_always_controls","owner_interest_controls_all_lease_disputes","later_termination_equals_prompt_objection"]', 'The student stops at the lease clause because it cleanly prohibits assignment. The breaker is the later conduct fact: Lydia accepted rent from Peter without protest, which triggers the Gold Key.', 'The choice proves the original assignment restriction but does not answer the later waiver fact.', 'Say: original clause first, later acceptance second; later fact controls the termination call.', 'REMED-RP-LEASE-WAIVER-01'),
  ('e469d784-a907-5198-9d9d-944e5185c6d6', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20729_courtyard_stormwater'), 'A', 'Timothy is entitled to a share of the condemnation award based on the value of the unexpired term of the leasehold less the rent that would have become due during that term.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["tenant_gets_only_award","tenant_gets_only_rent_release","condemnation_award_must_name_leasehold","leasehold_not_property"]', 'This choice sells the constitutional-compensation half, which sounds like the whole eminent-domain answer. The breaker is that the tenant also stops owing future rent, so the award-only statement is incomplete.', 'Award-only answer. It omits the future-rent consequence.', 'When the array offers X-only, Y-only, and X-plus-Y, test both components before choosing.', 'RP-ANCHOR-TAKING-LEASE-01'),
  ('854303b6-5a08-55f0-a28e-576fea9c9af9', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20729_courtyard_stormwater'), 'B', 'Timothy may continue to use the courtyard for the balance of the term unless his leasehold interest was specifically named in the condemnation award.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["tenant_gets_only_award","tenant_gets_only_rent_release","condemnation_award_must_name_leasehold","leasehold_not_property"]', 'This choice sells a formal-document trap by making the condemnation award sound controlling. The breaker is the Gold Key: a total taking reaches the leasehold whether or not the award names it separately.', 'It invents a requirement that the leasehold be specifically named before the taking reaches the tenant.', 'Recall that total taking of the parcel includes the leasehold.', 'RP-ANCHOR-TAKING-LEASE-01'),
  ('39d5a4ea-687a-5111-a66f-bde92837c790', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20729_courtyard_stormwater'), 'C', 'Timothy is freed of the obligation to pay rent for the balance of the term and is also entitled to a share of the condemnation award based on the value of the unexpired term of the leasehold less the rent that would have become due during that term.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'C is correct. Gold Key: when the government takes the entire leased parcel, the taking reaches the leasehold as well as the landlord’s interest. The tenant loses possession, so future rent stops, and the tenant also has a compensable leasehold interest measured by the value of the unexpired term minus the rent the tenant no longer has to pay.

Silver Key: the answer array splits the rule into two single-consequence answers and one combined answer. Once both consequences are true, the combined answer is the complete response.', NULL, 'RP-ANCHOR-TAKING-LEASE-01'),
  ('ea10bfa4-0b67-5a9d-97fb-6918080876e5', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20729_courtyard_stormwater'), 'D', 'Timothy is freed of the obligation to pay rent for the balance of the term.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["tenant_gets_only_award","tenant_gets_only_rent_release","condemnation_award_must_name_leasehold","leasehold_not_property"]', 'This choice sells the intuitive landlord-tenant half, because no rent after total ouster feels complete. The breaker is that the leasehold itself is also compensable, so the rent-only statement is incomplete.', 'Rent-only answer. It omits the compensation consequence.', 'Add the leasehold-value compensation consequence when the entire leasehold is taken.', 'RP-ANCHOR-TAKING-LEASE-01'),
  ('dd55e10b-54f5-52b9-9bf5-c78b51f3ae8c', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20730_shepherd_sling_fair'), 'A', 'Peter was old enough to appreciate the riskiness of charging across the aisle.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["intent_to_injure_required","risk_appreciation_enough","child_negligence_standard_imported","bad_motive_equals_liability"]', 'A student picks this because risk appreciation sounds like the responsible mental-state inquiry for a child. The breaker is that the battery call asks for contact intent, not negligence-style risk awareness.', 'This imports a risk-appreciation frame. The battery call is answered by contact intent.', 'Ask which choice targets contact rather than risk.', 'REM-TORTS-BATTERY-CONTACT-INTENT'),
  ('23a97504-d26a-5fcc-8ade-9502e2c2cfd1', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20730_shepherd_sling_fair'), 'B', 'Peter was old enough to appreciate that knocking Lydia into a table could inflict serious injury.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["intent_to_injure_required","risk_appreciation_enough","child_negligence_standard_imported","bad_motive_equals_liability"]', 'A student picks this because serious injury sounds more legally important than mere contact. The breaker is that battery liability does not require injury appreciation when intentional harmful or offensive contact is shown.', 'This targets injury appreciation. The credited answer targets intentional contact.', 'Apply the Gold Key: battery does not require intent to injure.', 'REM-TORTS-BATTERY-CONTACT-INTENT'),
  ('0bfc8f87-9b97-5a51-9dcb-9097c37304e5', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20730_shepherd_sling_fair'), 'C', 'Peter''s anger over the ribbon decision was not justified.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["intent_to_injure_required","risk_appreciation_enough","child_negligence_standard_imported","bad_motive_equals_liability"]', 'A student picks this because unjustified anger feels like a moral reason to impose liability. The breaker is that motive does not supply the battery element; deliberate contact does.', 'This targets motive and moral justification. The call needs the battery-contact element.', 'Ask whether the answer supplies the battery element or only explains why Peter acted.', 'REM-TORTS-BATTERY-CONTACT-INTENT'),
  ('8e7992a1-9e29-5f27-b5be-28378ed48599', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20730_shepherd_sling_fair'), 'D', 'Peter intended to slam his shoulder into Lydia.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'D is the only choice that supplies the contact-level intent required by the battery call.', NULL, 'REM-TORTS-BATTERY-CONTACT-INTENT'),
  ('92483404-6b8a-5cee-87a5-529ce5940b79', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20733_galilee_trails_death_notice'), 'A', 'Yes, because any careless emergency message that causes grief creates emotional-distress liability.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["every_negligent_message_causes_liability","physical_danger_always_required_for_NIED","false_death_notice_no_liability_if_person_alive"]', 'A picks up the real idea that the message caused grief and then broadens it into a universal rule. The breaker is the word any: the stem gives a special category, not general liability for every careless message.', 'The word any converts a special category into a universal misinformation-liability rule.', 'Circle absolute words and ask whether the stem supplies a narrower rule.', 'RC-TORTS-NIED-SPECIAL-CATEGORY-01'),
  ('f287a531-5dca-5326-b66c-8dd6b7e635f1', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20733_galilee_trails_death_notice'), 'B', 'No, because Lydia was alive, so Timothy cannot have legally cognizable distress from the message.', 0, '["contradiction"]', '["every_negligent_message_causes_liability","physical_danger_always_required_for_NIED","false_death_notice_no_liability_if_person_alive"]', 'B feels concrete because Lydia was alive, so the report was factually false. The breaker is that the false death notice is the very event the special category is built to address.', 'The fact that Lydia was alive makes the notice erroneous; it does not defeat the distress claim.', 'Ask what fact made the notice wrongful and what fact caused Timothy’s distress.', 'RC-TORTS-NIED-SPECIAL-CATEGORY-01'),
  ('b8296a60-4b66-521e-9e8c-2b3b1abfcc6b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20733_galilee_trails_death_notice'), 'C', 'Yes, under the jurisdiction’s special category for negligent erroneous death notice.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'C is correct. The stem gives the key gate: this jurisdiction recognizes negligent erroneous death notice as a special NIED category. Timothy received a negligent false death notice about Lydia, and the stem states that he suffered severe emotional distress from that notice.

Gold Key: Erroneous death notice is a special NIED category when the jurisdiction recognizes it; do not import the ordinary physical-danger or bystander screens.

Silver Key: When the stem says “special category,” cut answers that answer the ordinary default rule instead of the special-category rule.', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-NIED-SPECIAL-CATEGORY-01'),
  ('b4062b56-6692-5662-8853-271a9471e51c', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20733_galilee_trails_death_notice'), 'D', 'No, because Timothy was never in physical danger from the retreat company’s mistake.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["every_negligent_message_causes_liability","physical_danger_always_required_for_NIED","false_death_notice_no_liability_if_person_alive"]', 'D imports the ordinary zone-of-danger screen and treats physical danger as the gate. The breaker is the Gold Key: a recognized erroneous-death-notice category does not depend on Timothy being physically endangered.', 'The choice imports ordinary physical-danger NIED into a stem-given special category.', 'When the stem says special category, test whether the choice is answering the default rule instead.', 'RC-TORTS-NIED-SPECIAL-CATEGORY-01'),
  ('8668d140-f0de-54b4-9eb8-e8643654ea08', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20735_bethlehem_donkey_blower'), 'A', 'No remedy, because Ruth refused to mitigate by accepting Barnabas’s proposed relocation.', 0, '["extreme_of_range","violation_vs_remedy"]', '["nuisance always means injunction","mitigation is a complete defense","continued complaints create punitive-only recovery"]', 'The student sees Ruth refuse the proposed fix and treats that as forfeiture of the whole claim. The breaker is that avoidable consequences limit damages, not all nuisance relief.', 'The choice pushes mitigation beyond its range by making Ruth''s refusal erase all remedy.', 'Say: mitigation trims avoidable damages; it does not erase nuisance relief.', 'REM-TORTS-NUISANCE-ABATEMENT-01'),
  ('eeeff7b9-693e-5579-8372-458151f06bf3', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20735_bethlehem_donkey_blower'), 'B', 'Only punitive damages, because Barnabas kept operating after complaints.', 0, '["wrong_element","violation_vs_remedy"]', '["nuisance always means injunction","mitigation is a complete defense","continued complaints create punitive-only recovery"]', 'The student wants to punish Barnabas because he kept operating after complaints. The breaker is that the call asks for the likely nuisance remedy, and the stem points to abatement rather than punishment.', 'The choice answers a punishment frame instead of the remedy-fit call.', 'Return to the abatement fact.', 'REM-TORTS-NUISANCE-ABATEMENT-01'),
  ('62c1c106-f609-5b56-a847-0847981a37b1', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20735_bethlehem_donkey_blower'), 'C', 'A permanent shutdown injunction, because any substantial nuisance requires the defendant to stop the activity.', 0, '["tiered_absolute","violation_vs_remedy"]', '["nuisance always means injunction","mitigation is a complete defense","continued complaints create punitive-only recovery"]', 'The student sees a substantial nuisance and jumps to shutting it down. The breaker is the word ''any,'' which ignores the moderate-cost fix and makes equitable relief automatic.', 'The word ''any'' turns nuisance into automatic shutdown and ignores the moderate-cost fix.', 'Ask whether the stem gives a narrower fix.', 'REM-TORTS-NUISANCE-ABATEMENT-01'),
  ('ead80791-2dd0-5ff0-b503-ff9b310fc185', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20735_bethlehem_donkey_blower'), 'D', 'A tailored injunction requiring reasonable abatement, with damages limited to nonavoidable loss-of-use harms.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'D is correct. The facts point to a nuisance remedy that fixes the interference without overcorrecting. The blower’s odor and rumble substantially interfere with the cottages, so “no remedy” is too severe. But Barnabas has shown a moderate-cost fix that will end nearly all of the problem without shutting down the business, so total closure is too severe too.

Gold Key: In nuisance remedies, avoidable-consequences principles limit damages; they do not erase the nuisance or force an all-or-nothing remedy. Equity can tailor abatement to the reasonable fix.

Silver Key: When the answer array offers shutdown, no remedy, punitive-only, and tailored abatement, read the array as an oversteer screen. The credited answer is the one that tracks the stem’s moderate-cost abatement fact.', NULL, 'REM-TORTS-NUISANCE-ABATEMENT-01'),
  ('f2180d50-87fc-5787-b081-691833dcd265', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20737_jonahs_whale_lyre'), 'A', 'Yes, because Paul is a merchant with respect to the sale of Christian folk art.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["personalty_never_gets_specific_performance","merchant_status_controls_all_UCC_questions","equity_requires_reciprocal_seller_remedy"]', 'It grabs the real-looking UCC status word merchant and treats it as the remedy trigger. The breaker is that the call is about specific performance, and the stem-trigger is unusual goods, not seller status.', 'Merchant status is not the buyer-specific-performance trigger.', 'Ask whether the answer uses the highly unusual object.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-SP-UNIQUE-GOODS-01'),
  ('1c8bf133-1042-52cb-b000-d5d54aafcdb1', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20737_jonahs_whale_lyre'), 'B', 'Yes, because the lyre is highly unusual.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is true and responsive: the lyre’s highly unusual character is the remedy trigger.', NULL, 'REM-CONTRACTS-SP-UNIQUE-GOODS-01'),
  ('71cfd787-e7ed-56ff-bd41-066bf4f8851a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20737_jonahs_whale_lyre'), 'C', 'Yes, because Paul could have obtained specific performance if Lydia had breached.', 0, '["wrong_element","violation_vs_remedy"]', '["personalty_never_gets_specific_performance","merchant_status_controls_all_UCC_questions","equity_requires_reciprocal_seller_remedy"]', 'It makes reciprocity feel fair by asking what Paul could have obtained if Lydia had breached. The breaker is wrong party and wrong remedy; Lydia''s entitlement does not depend on Paul’s hypothetical remedy.', 'Paul’s hypothetical remedy does not answer Lydia’s actual remedy call.', 'Restate the call as Lydia’s buyer remedy.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-SP-UNIQUE-GOODS-01'),
  ('1613fbf6-9605-5d17-99f5-714bfa447dac', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20737_jonahs_whale_lyre'), 'D', 'No, because specific performance is not available as a remedy for breach of a contract for the sale of personalty.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["personalty_never_gets_specific_performance","merchant_status_controls_all_UCC_questions","equity_requires_reciprocal_seller_remedy"]', 'It starts from the normal damages default for ordinary personal property sales. The breaker is the exception for unique or highly unusual goods, which this object supplies.', 'The personalty-never statement omits the unique-goods exception.', 'Apply the Gold Key: unique or highly unusual goods can support specific performance.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-SP-UNIQUE-GOODS-01'),
  ('45adcef3-ca15-59e1-b92a-9567260208f2', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20744_bethlehem_loading_lane'), 'A', 'a person clipped by a prop cart would be involved in another collision within a few minutes.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["exact-chain-required","prior-cause-must-be-foreseeable","defendant-negligence-must-be-foreseeable"]', 'A feels precise because it tracks the whole chain from the first collision to the second collision. The breaker is that the exact chain is not the target; the person-in-lane risk is.', 'A targets the exact follow-on accident chain instead of the general person-in-lane risk.', 'Ask whether this choice names a general person/risk or an exact chain.', 'TOR-FORESEEABILITY-TARGET-01'),
  ('f1a42710-bc8a-5e11-9af5-82d247053a09', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20744_bethlehem_loading_lane'), 'B', 'a person would be in the loading lane.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is true and responsive after the Gold Key target scan.', NULL, 'TOR-FORESEEABILITY-TARGET-01'),
  ('220aecb6-404f-5fbc-8bae-4427c59a5e72', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20744_bethlehem_loading_lane'), 'C', 'Stephen would operate the forklift while drowsy after taking a strong sleep aid.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["exact-chain-required","prior-cause-must-be-foreseeable","defendant-negligence-must-be-foreseeable"]', 'C feels compelling because Stephen''s drowsy operation is the obvious bad act. The breaker is that the plaintiff need not show Stephen''s negligence itself was foreseeable.', 'C targets foreseeability of the defendant''s own breach instead of the plaintiff/risk.', 'Mark drowsy operation as breach color and return to who or what was foreseeably endangered.', 'TOR-FORESEEABILITY-TARGET-01'),
  ('511eab3d-1bbb-573b-b763-76ddd7a641d6', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20744_bethlehem_loading_lane'), 'D', 'Timothy would handle the prop cart carelessly and leave Lydia lying in the loading lane after clipping her.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["exact-chain-required","prior-cause-must-be-foreseeable","defendant-negligence-must-be-foreseeable"]', 'D feels natural because Timothy''s cart accident explains why Lydia was on the ground. The breaker is that Timothy''s prior conduct is set-stage, not Stephen''s foreseeability target.', 'D targets the prior set-stage actor''s conduct instead of the risk created by Stephen''s operation.', 'Treat facts that existed before Stephen acted as set-stage unless the call asks about the prior actor.', 'TOR-FORESEEABILITY-TARGET-01'),
  ('03391355-b22f-527a-a52d-059fe8f421f1', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20760_rubber_stamp_mail_fraud'), 'A', 'Admit the stamp only as demonstrative evidence of what rubber stamps generally look like.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","offered_for_purpose"]', '["crime_category_makes_object_relevant","demonstrative_use_can_be_invented","prejudice_is_default_exclusion_ground","exclude_result_alone_is_enough"]', 'The choice gives the student a fallback use, so the stamp feels admissible even without a case link. The breaker is that the stem never offered the stamp for that demonstrative purpose.', 'The choice invents a demonstrative use not supplied by the stem.', 'Ask whether the stem actually offered the object for that purpose.', 'EVI-REMEDY-REAL-OBJECT-CONNECTION-01'),
  ('e2a969ed-219b-5924-884f-ff5797316feb', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20760_rubber_stamp_mail_fraud'), 'B', 'Exclude the stamp only because it is unfairly prejudicial as a matter of law.', 0, '["tiered_absolute","wrong_frame"]', '["crime_category_makes_object_relevant","demonstrative_use_can_be_invented","prejudice_is_default_exclusion_ground","exclude_result_alone_is_enough"]', 'The choice reaches exclusion, so it feels close to the credited answer. The breaker is that it makes prejudice the exclusive legal reason and skips the more basic missing-connection problem.', 'The choice reaches the correct outcome but overclaims with ''only'' and ''as a matter of law'' while skipping the connection layer.', 'Cut ''only'' / ''as a matter of law'' when a more basic live layer is visible.', 'EVI-REMEDY-REAL-OBJECT-CONNECTION-01'),
  ('8101c6d8-11db-5453-9b1b-6d5a7df8fdd8', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20760_rubber_stamp_mail_fraud'), 'C', 'Admit the stamp because all office stamps are relevant in a mail-fraud trial.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["crime_category_makes_object_relevant","demonstrative_use_can_be_invented","prejudice_is_default_exclusion_ground","exclude_result_alone_is_enough"]', 'The choice lets the crime label do the work: mail fraud sounds like it could involve a stamp. The breaker is the word ''all,'' because an object category is not a connection to this case.', 'The choice treats all objects in a category as automatically relevant.', 'Treat ''all'' category language as an overclaim unless the stem supplies a connection.', 'EVI-REMEDY-REAL-OBJECT-CONNECTION-01'),
  ('7fcad970-b68d-5522-8d83-768ae4641ed7', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20760_rubber_stamp_mail_fraud'), 'D', 'Exclude the stamp because, without a connection to the charged mailing or another relevant issue, the object lacks probative value.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'D is correct. The stamp is a physical object, but the stem gives no link between the stamp and Daniel, any invoice, or the charged mailing. That means the object has no fact-path into the case. It is not enough that mail-fraud schemes can involve office supplies. The object must help prove something that matters.

Silver Key: Start with connection and purpose before object-category or prejudice. If the stem gives no bridge between the thing and the charged event, the “this kind of object fits this kind of crime” answer is bait.', NULL, 'EVI-REMEDY-REAL-OBJECT-CONNECTION-01'),
  ('fc361f3a-1f01-5b34-b77d-6de36f85af12', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20805_psalm_lanterns_confirmation'), 'A', 'The confirmation fails because Timothy did not sign it.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["any_timely_objection_suffices","recipient_must_sign","actual_reading_required","time_window_over_form"]', 'The choice activates the normal Statute of Frauds signature instinct. The breaker is that the merchant-confirmation rule can operate against the recipient without the recipient’s signature.', 'This choice imports the ordinary signature instinct into a merchant-confirmation problem. The Gold Key shows recipient signature is not required if no written objection is sent in time.', 'Ask whether the sender’s signed confirmation can substitute for recipient signature between merchants.', 'CONTRACTS-ANCHOR-MERCHANT-CONFIRMATION-01'),
  ('8618baae-2971-5904-9a44-b5d3be731016', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20805_psalm_lanterns_confirmation'), 'B', 'The confirmation fails unless Martha proves Timothy actually read every word of it before leaving the voicemail.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["any_timely_objection_suffices","recipient_must_sign","actual_reading_required","time_window_over_form"]', 'The choice sounds careful because actual reading feels like stronger notice. The breaker is that the rule uses reason to know contents, not proof of reading every word.', 'This choice invents an actual word-by-word reading requirement. The rule asks reason to know contents, not proof of reading every word.', 'Cut word-by-word reading requirements unless the Gold Key says they exist.', 'CONTRACTS-ANCHOR-MERCHANT-CONFIRMATION-01'),
  ('d42122ce-f78d-5492-9acd-ae0f2d632b6c', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20805_psalm_lanterns_confirmation'), 'C', 'Timothy’s voicemail does not prevent the merchant-confirmation rule from applying, because the objection must be written.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'C is correct. Gold Key: in a UCC merchant-confirmation problem, the receiving merchant must give a written notice of objection within 10 days after receiving the confirmation. A voicemail objection is not the required objection.

Silver Key: do not let the “within 10 days” fact swallow the “written notice” requirement. Timing and form are separate boxes.

Here, Timothy received a signed written confirmation from Martha and objected only by voicemail. Because Timothy sent no written objection, his oral objection does not stop the merchant-confirmation rule from applying.', NULL, 'CONTRACTS-ANCHOR-MERCHANT-CONFIRMATION-01'),
  ('b3f0ceab-de3a-55e7-92f3-4241156a3432', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20805_psalm_lanterns_confirmation'), 'D', 'Timothy’s voicemail prevents the confirmation from satisfying the Statute of Frauds because any objection within 10 days is enough.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["any_timely_objection_suffices","recipient_must_sign","actual_reading_required","time_window_over_form"]', 'The choice grabs the most visible fact: Timothy objected within 10 days. The breaker is that the objection must be written, so timing alone is not enough.', 'This choice turns a timely oral objection into any sufficient objection. The Silver Key separates timing from required form, and the Gold Key supplies the written-objection rule.', 'Use the Silver Key: timing and form are separate boxes.', 'CONTRACTS-ANCHOR-MERCHANT-CONFIRMATION-01'),
  ('3f59b542-8135-50c1-951e-178c62b1f546', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20808_jericho_press'), 'A', 'Lydia, because Timothy promised to share the estate with her if she promised to let him use the press until Mary’s death.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["promise_equals_consideration","estoppel_without_reliance","hidden_condition_controls_later_contract"]', 'It sees the visible promise-for-promise exchange and stops there. The breaker is the Gold Key: Lydia gave only permission she already owed, so the exchange adds no legal detriment.', 'The choice identifies a visible promise exchange but skips whether Lydia gave new legal detriment.', 'Apply the Gold Key: already owed is not new consideration.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-PREEXISTING-DUTY-01'),
  ('467fc272-b8f6-5152-a899-9a2032a03fdb', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20808_jericho_press'), 'B', 'Timothy, because his promise to Lydia was not supported by consideration.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'A, C, and D each break one filter; B is the only true-and-responsive answer.', NULL, 'REM-CONTRACTS-PREEXISTING-DUTY-01'),
  ('c70ca3d5-9622-5032-81da-d8776f596dee', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20808_jericho_press'), 'C', 'Lydia, because Timothy is estopped from denying the existence of an enforceable contract between him and Lydia.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["promise_equals_consideration","estoppel_without_reliance","hidden_condition_controls_later_contract"]', 'It reaches for promissory estoppel as a fairness rescue. The breaker is the missing reliance move: Lydia did not change legal position because of Timothy’s promise.', 'The choice reaches for promissory estoppel but lacks a Lydia reliance fact.', 'Require an induced action or forbearance before using promissory estoppel.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-PREEXISTING-DUTY-01'),
  ('8825a940-d2ad-5881-85e5-af56d5545dbd', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20808_jericho_press'), 'D', 'Timothy, because Lydia’s promise to Hannah created an implied condition precedent to Timothy’s obligation to share the estate with Lydia.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["promise_equals_consideration","estoppel_without_reliance","hidden_condition_controls_later_contract"]', 'It uses the hidden first-transfer condition to support the same winning party. The breaker is frame control: Timothy''s later promise was not made subject to a condition he never knew.', 'The choice imports a hidden earlier condition into Timothy''s later promise.', 'Cut answers that import someone else''s hidden deal into the current call.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-PREEXISTING-DUTY-01'),
  ('c1b18a1e-8198-5335-b98f-811897641070', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20811_illuminated_psalms_solvent'), 'A', 'Yes, because Lydia''s injury was felt in Maine.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["fairness_equals_jurisdiction","forum_injury_equals_contact","state_law_claim_excluded_from_federal_court","minimum_contacts_can_be_created_by_convenience"]', 'The student sees a real Maine fact: Lydia''s injury was felt there. The breaker is that personal jurisdiction turns on Barnabas''s forum contacts, not Lydia''s forum effects.', 'The answer uses the location of Lydia''s injury, but the call needs Barnabas''s forum contacts.', 'Mark injury location as plaintiff-side and return to defendant contacts.', 'CIV-REM-PJ-CONTACTS-FIRST'),
  ('414580f3-c606-5bf7-80b7-7d2695338573', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20811_illuminated_psalms_solvent'), 'B', 'No, because private workshop injury claims are excluded from federal courts.', 0, '["misfit","procedural_frame"]', '["fairness_equals_jurisdiction","forum_injury_equals_contact","state_law_claim_excluded_from_federal_court","minimum_contacts_can_be_created_by_convenience"]', 'The student hears formal federal-court language and treats it as a threshold answer. The breaker is that the call asks specific jurisdiction in Maine, not a categorical federal-court exclusion.', 'The answer switches from personal jurisdiction to a federal-court category gate.', 'Ask whether the answer addresses personal jurisdiction in Maine.', 'CIV-REM-PJ-CONTACTS-FIRST'),
  ('74f1a5ff-75e7-50c6-89f5-0e8fa8d5a3b1', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20811_illuminated_psalms_solvent'), 'C', 'Yes, because Lydia''s hardship is enough to create minimum contacts.', 0, '["backwards","wrong_frame"]', '["fairness_equals_jurisdiction","forum_injury_equals_contact","state_law_claim_excluded_from_federal_court","minimum_contacts_can_be_created_by_convenience"]', 'The student sympathizes with Lydia and wants the fair forum to win. The breaker is that fairness is second-layer and cannot create the first-layer minimum contacts.', 'The answer treats hardship and fairness as if they create minimum contacts.', 'Say: contacts first, fairness second.', 'CIV-REM-PJ-CONTACTS-FIRST'),
  ('34d21ed3-ba40-5f91-8154-5b0e0713e8fc', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '20811_illuminated_psalms_solvent'), 'D', 'No, because fairness cannot create minimum contacts absent purposeful Maine-directed conduct by Barnabas.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'D is the only answer that both answers the fairness-alone call and preserves the contacts-before-fairness threshold.', NULL, 'CIV-REM-PJ-CONTACTS-FIRST'),
  ('5d67334c-28f1-553d-a988-918ea291339b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21711_foam_whale_nativity_attachment'), 'A', 'State B may decide the claim because Lydia was injured, and plaintiffs choose the forum.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["old_property_jurisdiction_shortcut","plaintiff_chooses_forum","asset_value_confers_power"]', 'The student sees Lydia as the injured party and treats plaintiff choice as enough to pick a court. The breaker is that personal jurisdiction asks about State B''s power over Daniel, not Lydia''s preference.', 'The answer focuses on plaintiff injury and forum choice rather than State B power over Daniel.', 'Ask which party''s connection to State B matters.', 'CIV-REM-QIR-RELATEDNESS-01'),
  ('7d3675aa-40d3-57da-b920-d414b091330b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21711_foam_whale_nativity_attachment'), 'B', 'State B likely lacks personal jurisdiction over the unrelated prop-injury claim despite the attached Nativity set.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'B is the best analysis. Gold Key: Attaching a defendant’s forum property does not itself let the forum decide an unrelated personal claim; after *Shaffer*, the property must supply a jurisdictional connection among the defendant, the forum, and the litigation, or other minimum contacts must exist. Silver Key: Lock the call as court power, not collection. The attached asset is only a lure unless it connects to the claim.', NULL, 'CIV-REM-QIR-RELATEDNESS-01'),
  ('3ca72f75-d2ed-523f-8dea-a141f536386c', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21711_foam_whale_nativity_attachment'), 'C', 'State B has jurisdiction only if the Nativity set is valuable enough to satisfy the whole judgment.', 0, '["wrong_element","violation_vs_remedy"]', '["old_property_jurisdiction_shortcut","plaintiff_chooses_forum","asset_value_confers_power"]', 'The student treats a valuable attached item as practically sufficient because it could pay the judgment. The breaker is that value answers collection after a valid judgment, not the court''s power to enter one.', 'The answer focuses on the value of the attached asset, which answers collection rather than jurisdiction.', 'Decide jurisdiction before thinking about judgment satisfaction.', 'CIV-REM-QIR-RELATEDNESS-01'),
  ('614f37cd-3af8-5e4a-bf0f-530eea919e1a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21711_foam_whale_nativity_attachment'), 'D', 'State B may decide the entire injury claim because any property in the forum creates personal jurisdiction.', 0, '["tiered_absolute","wrong_frame"]', '["old_property_jurisdiction_shortcut","plaintiff_chooses_forum","asset_value_confers_power"]', 'The student remembers that property in the forum can matter and upgrades that memory into an any-property shortcut. The breaker is the Gold Key: unrelated property alone does not create jurisdiction over the whole unrelated claim.', 'The answer uses an overbroad any-property rule that the Gold Key defeats.', 'Check whether the property connects the defendant, forum, and litigation.', 'CIV-REM-QIR-RELATEDNESS-01'),
  ('b2afb3e3-fe97-51bd-b367-c4eae13f1b10', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21752_olive_oil_ramp'), 'A', 'Lampstand Studio is liable only if Lydia or one of her employees personally poured the oil on the ramp.', 0, '["fabricated_rule","wrong_frame"]', '["independent_contractor_default_is_absolute","nondelegable_duty_not_recalled","contractor_equals_employee","personal_creation_required"]', 'Students try to make the business liable only through its own hands. The Gold Key breaks that frame because a nondelegable safety duty can stay with the business even when the contractor created the hazard.', 'The answer invents a personal-creation prerequisite for business liability.', 'When a duty is statutory or public-safety based, ask whether the duty stays with the hirer.', 'REM-TORTS-NONDELEGABLE-01'),
  ('91998550-d3c3-5b22-af27-fc5876224fc6', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21752_olive_oil_ramp'), 'B', 'Lampstand Studio has no possible liability because it hired an outside company to make the ramp safe.', 0, '["extreme_of_range","wrong_frame"]', '["independent_contractor_default_is_absolute","nondelegable_duty_not_recalled","contractor_equals_employee","personal_creation_required"]', 'Students remember that hirers usually are not liable for independent contractors. The word ''possible'' overextends that default past the nondelegable-duty exception.', 'The answer pushes the independent-contractor default into a complete shield.', 'Treat independent-contractor status as a default, then check for nondelegable duty.', 'REM-TORTS-NONDELEGABLE-01'),
  ('d51f6730-7fcb-5e43-bd0c-d50a1f0307d3', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21752_olive_oil_ramp'), 'C', 'Lampstand Studio may be liable because the public-walkway safety duty is nondelegable.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is true and responsive after the overbroad and wrong-frame contractor answers are cut.', NULL, 'REM-TORTS-NONDELEGABLE-01'),
  ('2b2977dc-af38-5e46-b390-4147fa2dc4ae', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21752_olive_oil_ramp'), 'D', 'Lampstand Studio is liable because every outside company that works at a public business is treated as the business''s employee.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["independent_contractor_default_is_absolute","nondelegable_duty_not_recalled","contractor_equals_employee","personal_creation_required"]', 'Students see a business premise and convert outside workers into employees. The word ''every'' is the overclaim; nondelegable duty is a narrower route than employee status.', 'The answer says every outside company is treated as an employee.', 'Cut ''every'' and look for the narrower liability route.', 'REM-TORTS-NONDELEGABLE-01'),
  ('eaf1790c-130c-59b2-88cb-9b63108853ca', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21848_manna_house_family_integrity'), 'A', 'Resolve the case only under the Dormant Commerce Clause.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["all_zoning_is_rational_basis","extended_family_not_protected","clause_name_shopping"]', 'The choice offers a familiar constitutional clause, which can feel safe on a route question. The breaker is that the stem has no commerce facts.', 'No commerce facts appear; the answer routes to a constitutional home outside the stem.', 'Ask whether the stem contains commerce facts.', 'REM-CONLAW-FAMILY-HOME-01'),
  ('369bbbab-a412-5c6f-9bc4-f1a3d5cd8624', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21848_manna_house_family_integrity'), 'B', 'Uphold the code automatically because aunts are never family for constitutional purposes.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["all_zoning_is_rational_basis","extended_family_not_protected","clause_name_shopping"]', 'The choice gives a clean categorical cutoff and avoids hard family-liberty analysis. The breaker is the absolute word ''never,'' defeated by the family-integrity Gold Key.', 'The absolute ''never'' overclaims the family category; the Gold Key supplies the close-family household anchor.', 'Mark ''never'' and trigger the close-family household Gold Key.', 'REM-CONLAW-FAMILY-HOME-01'),
  ('e582a1c9-8bdd-531b-945e-5991fbf1f00a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21848_manna_house_family_integrity'), 'C', 'Analyze the code as a substantive due process burden on family integrity.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only choice that is both true and responsive to the route call.', NULL, 'REM-CONLAW-FAMILY-HOME-01'),
  ('115f8504-f61e-53b5-b19f-c1b7151ca681', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21848_manna_house_family_integrity'), 'D', 'Treat the code as ordinary residential zoning subject only to rational basis, with no family-liberty concern.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["all_zoning_is_rational_basis","extended_family_not_protected","clause_name_shopping"]', 'The choice borrows the real ordinary-zoning frame and pairs it with rational basis. The breaker is that a direct close-family household exclusion changes the constitutional route.', 'Ordinary zoning is a real neighboring frame, but the family-household trigger routes the item to substantive due process.', 'Ask whether the ordinance regulates unrelated occupants or directly excludes close relatives.', 'REM-CONLAW-FAMILY-HOME-01'),
  ('cbb0d406-91a1-5189-95ad-71bf204d330d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21867_psalm_puzzle_booth'), 'A', 'No, because operating the unpermitted puzzle booth did not actually or proximately cause Ruth’s fall from the stepstool.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'A is correct. The charged theory is misdemeanor manslaughter based on the unpermitted puzzle booth. The booth did not produce, set up, or increase the risk of Ruth’s later fall from a defective stepstool at a different location. Gold Key: misdemeanor manslaughter still needs actual and proximate causation; a misdemeanor that merely happened earlier is not enough. Silver Key: when one answer links the misdemeanor to the death and another only names the misdemeanor, choose the cause-link answer.', NULL, 'CRIM-REM-CAUSE-LINK-01'),
  ('c08b03d9-f04d-5ef5-b9c9-a4706e5fc972', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21867_psalm_puzzle_booth'), 'B', 'Yes, because Peter was committing a misdemeanor when he operated the puzzle booth.', 0, '["wrong_element","violation_vs_remedy"]', '["misdemeanor_is_enough","after_means_because_of","violent_only_misdemeanor_rule"]', 'The student sees a real misdemeanor and feels the unlawful-act box is checked. The breaker is that the answer never links the misdemeanor to Ruth’s death.', 'It proves the misdemeanor predicate but skips the cause link to the death.', 'Ask whether the misdemeanor produced the death.', 'CRIM-REM-CAUSE-LINK-01'),
  ('0ec4a04a-7daf-5e1b-88f7-f09ca676b9c9', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21867_psalm_puzzle_booth'), 'C', 'No, because misdemeanor manslaughter applies only to violent misdemeanors.', 0, '["fabricated_rule","wrong_frame"]', '["misdemeanor_is_enough","after_means_because_of","violent_only_misdemeanor_rule"]', 'The student reaches for a narrower category because violent-only sounds like a sophisticated limitation. The breaker is that the case turns on causation, not a violent-only threshold.', 'It substitutes a violent-only threshold for the causation problem.', 'Prefer the answer that uses the actual death mechanism.', 'CRIM-REM-CAUSE-LINK-01'),
  ('8114a752-b2c3-54ce-837b-b3bec35b8a77', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21867_psalm_puzzle_booth'), 'D', 'Yes, because any death after a misdemeanor is involuntary manslaughter.', 0, '["tiered_absolute","violation_vs_remedy"]', '["misdemeanor_is_enough","after_means_because_of","violent_only_misdemeanor_rule"]', 'The student treats sequence as enough because a misdemeanor came before a death. The breaker is the absolute word any, which erases the cause requirement.', 'It says any death after a misdemeanor is enough, which erases causation.', 'Cut absolute chronology language unless the law is truly categorical.', 'CRIM-REM-CAUSE-LINK-01'),
  ('d27f5aa8-af38-5313-b014-e156241d14aa', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21869_tiny_house_hymn_sheet'), 'A', 'Yes, because the tip gave officers reasonable suspicion that Timothy was inside.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["warrant_is_warrant","reasonable_suspicion_suffices","interior_location_controls"]', 'The tip makes the police seem specifically focused rather than fishing. The breaker is that suspicion level is not the authority needed to enter a third party’s home.', 'The choice uses reasonable suspicion as the authority for entering a third party''s home. The Gold Key requires a search warrant for that home or a valid exception.', 'Ask whether the answer supplies a warrant or valid exception for the home entered.', 'CRIM-REM-THIRD-PARTY-HOME-01'),
  ('7b380064-902a-5484-b502-7116737f83a5', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21869_tiny_house_hymn_sheet'), 'B', 'Yes, because an arrest warrant travels with the suspect into any private home.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["warrant_is_warrant","reasonable_suspicion_suffices","interior_location_controls"]', 'The answer treats an arrest warrant as portable authority that follows Timothy wherever he goes. The breaker is the Gold Key: a person warrant does not become a place warrant for Lydia’s home.', 'The phrase ''any private home'' overextends the arrest warrant. The Gold Key separates a person warrant from a place warrant.', 'Mark ''any private home'' as the overclaim and apply the Gold Key.', 'CRIM-REM-THIRD-PARTY-HOME-01'),
  ('b4c5c85a-84cc-5e98-92a0-8ba3fcc9859e', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21869_tiny_house_hymn_sheet'), 'C', 'No, but only because Timothy was seized in the prayer nook rather than at the front door.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["warrant_is_warrant","reasonable_suspicion_suffices","interior_location_controls"]', 'The prayer nook is a vivid concrete fact, so it feels legally operative. The breaker is the Silver Key: entry authority is decided at the door, not by the room where the arrest happens.', 'The prayer-nook fact concerns where Timothy was found after entry. The call asks whether entry was lawful.', 'Resolve the door before the room.', 'CRIM-REM-THIRD-PARTY-HOME-01'),
  ('2120772e-3178-5378-a377-5ead23d58804', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21869_tiny_house_hymn_sheet'), 'D', 'No, because officers needed a search warrant for Lydia’s home or a valid exception.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'D alone is true and responsive after the Gold Key cuts A and B and Issue-Sense cuts C.', NULL, 'CRIM-REM-THIRD-PARTY-HOME-01'),
  ('b54cc894-9bfc-50e4-a945-026ff6bae546', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21872_scripturequest_price_gate'), 'A', 'The law is valid because price rules never affect commerce.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["states_can_control_all_prices_after_market_entry","price_rules_do_not_affect_commerce","privileges_and_immunities_is_always_the_state_discrimination_answer"]', 'The choice flatters the belief that price regulation is local housekeeping. The breaker is the word never: prices are the substance of commerce.', 'The answer says price rules never affect commerce, which overclaims from the answer text itself.', 'Mark ''never'' and ask whether the stem itself is about commerce.', 'CONLAW-PRICE-GATE-01'),
  ('40eab928-95bd-59fb-aaab-d10191c3fa79', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21872_scripturequest_price_gate'), 'B', 'Article IV privileges and immunities is the only relevant doctrine.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["states_can_control_all_prices_after_market_entry","price_rules_do_not_affect_commerce","privileges_and_immunities_is_always_the_state_discrimination_answer"]', 'The choice reaches for a familiar state-discrimination clause and tries to end the search there. The breaker is the call itself: the item asks for a dormant Commerce Clause objection, and this price gate is commercial regulation.', 'The call asks for a dormant Commerce Clause objection; Article IV privileges and immunities is a different doctrine home.', 'Return to the call: it asks for a dormant Commerce Clause objection.', 'CONLAW-PRICE-GATE-01'),
  ('54127f35-d96d-5560-a63c-6fdd6e1942fd', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21872_scripturequest_price_gate'), 'C', 'The law directly controls prices charged in other states and risks impermissible extraterritorial regulation.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'C is correct. The statute does not merely set the price for sales inside the state. It makes access to the in-state market depend on what the seller charges in every other state during the same month.

Gold Key: A state may not condition in-state sales on a seller’s out-of-state prices. When the in-state sales gate is tied to prices charged elsewhere, the strongest dormant Commerce Clause objection is extraterritorial price control.

Silver Key: Follow the pricing lever, not the state’s label. The state says “inside our borders,” but the certification forces the seller to account for prices outside the state.', NULL, 'CONLAW-PRICE-GATE-01'),
  ('c7ed7cdc-8c47-5f8e-85df-c9a6aee46618', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21872_scripturequest_price_gate'), 'D', 'The law is valid because a state may control all nationwide prices once a seller enters its market.', 0, '["extreme_of_range"]', '["states_can_control_all_prices_after_market_entry","price_rules_do_not_affect_commerce","privileges_and_immunities_is_always_the_state_discrimination_answer"]', 'The choice starts from a true-sounding local-power idea: the state can regulate sales inside its own market. The breaker is the jump to all nationwide prices, which is the overreach the Gold Key forbids.', 'The answer stretches in-state sales power into control over all nationwide prices.', 'Separate in-state sales regulation from out-of-state price control.', 'CONLAW-PRICE-GATE-01'),
  ('743f9ad7-1529-5fc8-a53c-0cd9a0525aa2', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21879_noahs_ark_puppet_set'), 'A', 'a ratification of Lydia’s earlier acceptance of Peter’s sale offer.', 0, '["flat_misstatement","wrong_frame"]', '["agreement_words_equal_acceptance","planning_equals_performance","correspondence_chain_equals_contract"]', 'The word ratification sounds like a sophisticated way to validate the earlier exchange. The breaker is that no earlier acceptance existed to ratify.', 'It assumes an earlier acceptance existed, but the earlier letter required delivery.', 'Mark the earlier letter as delivery-conditioned before labeling Peter’s later statement.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-OFFER-ACCEPTANCE-MODE-01'),
  ('badb3400-88b2-5e7f-afb7-84895487dea8', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21879_noahs_ark_puppet_set'), 'B', 'commencement of performance.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["agreement_words_equal_acceptance","planning_equals_performance","correspondence_chain_equals_contract"]', 'Peter talks about the transport step, so the answer feels tied to action. The breaker is that he has not delivered, tendered, or begun delivering yet.', 'A future promise to bring the goods is not present commencement of performance.', 'Ask whether the goods were delivered, tendered, or physically being brought.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-OFFER-ACCEPTANCE-MODE-01'),
  ('7610a3db-793e-5b29-aaef-263f99bddc96', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21879_noahs_ark_puppet_set'), 'C', 'an offer.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only label that classifies Peter’s future-tense willingness statement without pretending delivery or an earlier acceptance already occurred.', NULL, 'REM-CONTRACTS-OFFER-ACCEPTANCE-MODE-01'),
  ('317fac25-ae0c-5ec7-a701-06caf9747c0b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '21879_noahs_ark_puppet_set'), 'D', 'an acceptance.', 0, '["flat_misstatement","wrong_frame"]', '["agreement_words_equal_acceptance","planning_equals_performance","correspondence_chain_equals_contract"]', 'Peter’s words sound like agreement, so students call them acceptance. The breaker is that the invited acceptance was delivery, not a promise to deliver later.', 'The statement promises later delivery; it is not the delivery-conditioned acceptance.', 'Ask what the buyer invited as the accepting act.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-OFFER-ACCEPTANCE-MODE-01')
